14:00:36  DEBUG    [29c3d90d8d61458d]  sse_starlette.sse  chunk: b'event: result\r\ndata: {"query": "In the meeting about onboarding a SaaS product to Google Cloud Marketplace, what did the GCP team recommend for handling delays where a new subscription entitlement is not immediately available during the customer onboarding flow?", "sections": [{"chunk_id": "0e2f452f-02d5-5d8d-bf48-5b80b9eec835", "search_unit_id": "0e2f452f-02d5-5d8d-bf48-5b80b9eec835", "block_from_id": "7e96897a-87e1-5d5f-8da7-c21e3a39abf0", "block_to_id": "57da432f-3094-5de4-bb54-97076fe76740", "section_path": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review", "document_id": "be72d63b-333f-51cc-8c3e-5b2af46aaab8", "document_version_id": "d74f559c-4a91-5a53-930c-c8b05d67f4ab", "document_name": "fireflies__dsid_6c4c1c875e704f09b4d791d64d7bc7e5.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h1-0", "heading": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review", "content": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review\\n\\nSource type: fireflies\\nDocument ID: dsid_6c4c1c875e704f09b4d791d64d7bc7e5\\nRelease: v1.0.0\\nBenchmark: EnterpriseRAG-Bench synthetic upstream data\\nGCP Marketplace onboarding + billing review (Redwood Inference)\\n\\nRedwood and the GCP Marketplace team reviewed how Redwood\xe2\x80\x99s new marketplace SKUs map to GCP billing dimensions (usage-based token/embedding meters vs subscription/commit dimensions), and what identifiers must be carried end-to-end (consumerId/entitlement) to ensure correct metering. GCP emphasized keeping dimension names stable, ensuring aggregation and rounding are consistent, and making the onboarding flow resilient to entitlement propagation delays. The group aligned on a staging test plan: subscribe with a test buyer account, validate entitlement lookup, provision/link a Redwood org, generate an API key, send test inference traffic, confirm metering events appear with the expected dimensions, and verify cancellation/plan change behavior. Redwood will send a proposed dimension list and sample metering payloads for review.\\nGCP Marketplace SKU dimensions and Cloud Commerce metering\\nEntitlement + account linking expectations\\nOnboarding UX expectations (subscription -> entitlement -> Redwood org provisioning)\\nTesting plan in staging before listing submission\\nRedwood to share final proposed GCP dimension names/IDs per SKU (Hosted/Dedicated/Private + add-ons) for partner review.\\nGCP to confirm any naming/length constraints and whether multiple dimensions per offer are acceptable for Redwood\xe2\x80\x99s packaging.\\nRedwood to run the staging procurement test end-to-end (subscribe -> entitlement -> org link -> first API call -> metering verification) and report results + logs.\\nSchedule a follow-up 30-min review focused on cancellation, plan change, and delayed entitlement propagation edge cases.\\nCole Summers: Send GCP partner team a draft mapping table (internal meter -> GCP dimension) and 2\xe2\x80\x933 example metering payloads by 2026-01-18.\\nBen Carter: Provide screenshots/wireframe of the marketplace onboarding entrypoint and error states (entitlement not found, pending propagation, wrong org) by 2026-01-19.\\nAnika Desai: Confirm recommended aggregation interval + rounding guidance for token-based meters and any API quotas to plan around by 2026-01-20.\\nMiguel Alvarez: Share a reference link / checklist for Cloud Commerce metering validation and how to verify events in the partner console by 2026-01-20.\\nChris Osei: Set up a follow-up session and circulate the staging test plan checklist by 2026-01-17.\\nMeeting header\\nDate: 2026-01-16\\nTime: 10:03 AM PT\\nDuration: 54 min\\nTitle: GCP Marketplace onboarding + billing review (Redwood Inference)\\nAttendees:\\n\\n- Redwood Inference: Stephanie Nguyen, Chris Osei, Cole Summers, Nadia Rahman, Ben Carter\\n- Google Cloud Marketplace: Priya Shah, Anika Desai, Miguel Alvarez\\n\\n[00:00] Stephanie Nguyen: Cool, thanks everyone for making time. This is Stephanie from Redwood, I run a lot of the enterprise motion, and we\xe2\x80\x99ve got Cole and Nadia on billing and metering, Ben on onboarding funnel. We wanted to do a sanity pass on the GCP marketplace requirements, specifically billing dimensions and the onboarding UX expectations before we submit.\\n\\n[00:18] Priya Shah: Sounds good. Thanks Stephanie. Priya here, partner dev manager. Anika and Miguel are the experts on the technical billing side. We saw your email about new SKUs, so today is basically make sure it\xe2\x80\x99s compatible with Cloud Marketplace and Cloud Commerce.", "score": 0.790129, "rank": 0, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "fdbbfe44-0b8d-50aa-8d99-e4bd35ccb269", "search_unit_id": "fdbbfe44-0b8d-50aa-8d99-e4bd35ccb269", "block_from_id": "d6778458-e29b-5053-8621-47f54ba3d664", "block_to_id": "5f82c632-07a0-5511-8190-382128144d99", "section_path": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review", "document_id": "be72d63b-333f-51cc-8c3e-5b2af46aaab8", "document_version_id": "d74f559c-4a91-5a53-930c-c8b05d67f4ab", "document_name": "fireflies__dsid_6c4c1c875e704f09b4d791d64d7bc7e5.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "p-6", "heading": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review", "content": "[00:33] Chris Osei: Yep, and I\xe2\x80\x99m Chris, program lead on our side for the marketplace refresh. I\xe2\x80\x99ll keep us on agenda and capture action items.\\n\\n[00:41] Anika Desai: Hi all. Anika, partner engineering. I can talk through entitlement, procurement IDs, and the typical \xe2\x80\x9cgotchas\xe2\x80\x9d we see when SaaS vendors wire up the onboarding.\\n\\n[00:50] Miguel Alvarez: Miguel here. Cloud Commerce metering / billing integrations. Happy to go deep on dimensions, aggregation, rounding, and what reviewers will look for.\\n\\n[01:01] Stephanie Nguyen: Great. Quick context: we\xe2\x80\x99re replacing legacy listings. We now have Hosted API usage-based, Dedicated capacity, and Private deployment. We want the marketplace SKUs to map cleanly to our internal billing meters. The primary issues we want to avoid are dimension mismatches and the buyer gets invoiced for something weird.\\n\\n[01:20] Miguel Alvarez: Yeah. The big thing is consistency. Dimension names and IDs in the offer need to match what you\xe2\x80\x99re sending in metering events. And be careful if you\xe2\x80\x99re changing them later\xe2\x80\x94customers don\xe2\x80\x99t like it and it can break reporting.\\n\\n[01:35] Cole Summers: That\xe2\x80\x99s exactly what we\xe2\x80\x99re trying to nail. Let me outline what we were thinking and you can tell us what\xe2\x80\x99s realistic. For Hosted, we meter \xe2\x80\x9ctokens generated\xe2\x80\x9d and \xe2\x80\x9cinput tokens\xe2\x80\x9d internally, but we can expose it as \xe2\x80\x9ctokens\xe2\x80\x9d total if that\xe2\x80\x99s simpler. We also have embeddings measured as \xe2\x80\x9cembedding units\xe2\x80\x9d (basically per 1K tokens equivalent). For Dedicated, we have reserved GPU capacity, which internally is monthly commit + overage. Private is more of a platform fee plus support tier.\\n\\n[02:05] Miguel Alvarez: Okay. So in Cloud Marketplace, you can do usage-based dimensions and you can do subscription pricing models, and you can also do committed-use style constructs depending on the product type. Most SaaS partners keep it simple: one or a small number of usage dimensions, like \xe2\x80\x9c1K tokens\xe2\x80\x9d or \xe2\x80\x9c1M tokens\xe2\x80\x9d, and then for a base subscription you do a seat or a flat monthly fee.\\n\\n[02:29] Nadia Rahman: Quick question on granularity. If we do \xe2\x80\x9c1K tokens\xe2\x80\x9d as the unit, do you see partners reporting fractional quantities? Like 123.456 units? Or is it recommended to round?\\n\\n[02:41] Miguel Alvarez: You can send decimal quantities, but you should document how you compute it and be consistent. The most common pattern is you aggregate per hour or per day and send integer quantities. But for token meters, lots of folks send decimals, just be explicit. Rounding differences between your UI and the invoice is where support tickets come from.\\n\\n[03:02] Ben Carter: On the onboarding side, we have this subscription -> entitlement -> Redwood org provisioning. One issue we see on AWS is entitlements sometimes take a few minutes to show up. Is that also a thing on GCP?\\n\\n[03:14] Anika Desai: Yep. It\xe2\x80\x99s not usually super long, but there can be propagation delay. The UX expectation is you handle \xe2\x80\x9cpending\xe2\x80\x9d gracefully. Like, don\xe2\x80\x99t show a scary error that says \xe2\x80\x9cyou are not entitled.\xe2\x80\x9d Instead say \xe2\x80\x9cwe\xe2\x80\x99re still syncing your subscription, retry in a few minutes\xe2\x80\x9d and maybe a refresh button.", "score": 0.784801, "rank": 1, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "6f4dec75-8013-58de-b9d6-c7a0b2d94d48", "search_unit_id": "6f4dec75-8013-58de-b9d6-c7a0b2d94d48", "block_from_id": "f33438e1-8f08-5d2d-bbf1-6a49aa3f4ca6", "block_to_id": "cbbe16cf-9597-52d1-998f-56ec5d653c4e", "section_path": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review", "document_id": "be72d63b-333f-51cc-8c3e-5b2af46aaab8", "document_version_id": "d74f559c-4a91-5a53-930c-c8b05d67f4ab", "document_name": "fireflies__dsid_6c4c1c875e704f09b4d791d64d7bc7e5.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "p-101", "heading": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review", "content": "[20:40] Anika Desai: Typically you\xe2\x80\x99ll have a partner test project and a test buyer project. You procure your own product as a test buyer. Then you verify that your integration receives the entitlement. If you\xe2\x80\x99re using the Cloud Commerce Partner API, you can query entitlements. If you\xe2\x80\x99re using webhooks, ensure those are wired. Since you\xe2\x80\x99re doing a console-based claim flow, you can also validate by retrieving entitlement status from your backend.\\n\\n[21:08] Cole Summers: We\xe2\x80\x99re using the partner API and a sync job, plus on-demand verify endpoint. Webhook we considered but didn\xe2\x80\x99t want to rely solely on it.\\n\\n[21:19] Miguel Alvarez: That\xe2\x80\x99s fine. Webhook can be flaky if not retried. Polling plus retry is okay, just mind quotas.\\n\\n[21:30] Nadia Rahman: For quotas, do you have ballpark? Like requests per minute? We\xe2\x80\x99re not huge but we want to be safe.\\n\\n[21:38] Miguel Alvarez: It depends on the project and API. I\xe2\x80\x99ll send the doc. But generally, if you do a sync every 5 minutes and on-demand for onboarding, you\xe2\x80\x99re fine.\\n\\n[21:53] Stephanie Nguyen: Great.\\n[21:55] (pause)\\n\\n[21:56] Ben Carter: A small UX question: when entitlement is not found, do you recommend we show \xe2\x80\x9cwait 10 minutes\xe2\x80\x9d or \xe2\x80\x9cwait 2 minutes\xe2\x80\x9d? We don\xe2\x80\x99t want to over-promise.\\n\\n[22:06] Anika Desai: I\xe2\x80\x99d say \xe2\x80\x9ca few minutes\xe2\x80\x9d and allow retry. And show a timestamp \xe2\x80\x9clast checked at\xe2\x80\x9d to reassure them you\xe2\x80\x99re doing something.\\n\\n[22:18] Ben Carter: Nice idea.\\n\\n[22:20] Chris Osei: Another edge case: procurement might buy in a different Google identity than the engineer uses (like billing@company.com). That means auto-detect entitlements won\xe2\x80\x99t work.\\n\\n[22:31] Anika Desai: Exactly. That\xe2\x80\x99s why inputting entitlement ID is helpful, or a separate \xe2\x80\x9cadmin invites user\xe2\x80\x9d workflow.\\n\\n[22:41] Stephanie Nguyen: We\xe2\x80\x99ll make sure docs call that out.\\n\\n[22:45] Miguel Alvarez: On billing, one more thing: if you\xe2\x80\x99re doing token usage, define what \xe2\x80\x9ctoken\xe2\x80\x9d means. Different models count differently. You should base it on what you count internally, but documentation should say it\xe2\x80\x99s per model tokenizer.\\n\\n[23:01] Cole Summers: Good point. Our API already reports token usage per request in the response metadata. We can use that as source of truth.\\n\\n[23:10] Nadia Rahman: And we can expose daily usage summaries in console to match marketplace billing.\\n\\n[23:15] Miguel Alvarez: That\xe2\x80\x99s helpful for customer trust.\\n\\n[23:19] Stephanie Nguyen: Okay. Let\xe2\x80\x99s talk about dimension naming and plan structure. It sounds like recommended is:\\n\\n- Hosted plan with usage dimensions (tokens, embeddings)\\n- Dedicated plan as subscription, possibly with an overage usage dimension\\n- Private plan as subscription\\n- Compliance add-on as bundle plans (Hosted+Compliance etc)\\n\\nIs that fair?\\n[23:44] Priya Shah: Yes.\\n\\n[23:46] Miguel Alvarez: Yes, that would be a clean structure.\\n\\n[23:49] Cole Summers: For Dedicated overage, would we meter tokens or something else? Because customers might still care about tokens, not GPU hours.\\n\\n[23:59] Miguel Alvarez: If the overage is \xe2\x80\x9cextra usage beyond included\xe2\x80\x9d, tokens is okay. If it\xe2\x80\x99s \xe2\x80\x9cextra capacity\xe2\x80\x9d, maybe hours. But tokens is more straightforward given your product.\\n\\n[24:12] Cole Summers: Great. We\xe2\x80\x99ll think through included vs overage.", "score": 0.748576, "rank": 2, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "9779c77f-d77a-56a5-b5c4-488b853cc42f", "search_unit_id": "9779c77f-d77a-56a5-b5c4-488b853cc42f", "block_from_id": "c8bf63f2-1ba9-5155-bf2d-182772e16db0", "block_to_id": "013559f2-b7d3-5236-843a-3d3a852a45cd", "section_path": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review", "document_id": "be72d63b-333f-51cc-8c3e-5b2af46aaab8", "document_version_id": "d74f559c-4a91-5a53-930c-c8b05d67f4ab", "document_name": "fireflies__dsid_6c4c1c875e704f09b4d791d64d7bc7e5.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "p-124", "heading": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review", "content": "[24:16] Chris Osei: I want to make sure we leave with concrete outputs. Cole will send a proposed mapping table and example payloads. Anika and Miguel will send docs and constraints. Ben will share screenshots/wireframes.\\n\\n[24:31] Stephanie Nguyen: One question: in GCP marketplace, do customers expect to get credits / trial? We offer free credits on our website; does marketplace support that?\\n\\n[24:41] Priya Shah: Marketplace trials exist in some contexts but not universally. Many partners handle trials outside marketplace or do a low-cost starter plan. If you want a trial, we can discuss, but it may complicate approval.\\n\\n[24:56] Stephanie Nguyen: We\xe2\x80\x99ll keep it simple for now.\\n\\n[24:59] Ben Carter: Another thing: we have a dedicated onboarding docs page. Is it okay to link external docs from the listing?\\n\\n[25:07] Priya Shah: Yes, you can link to documentation. Just make sure it\xe2\x80\x99s stable URLs.\\n\\n[25:12] Chris Osei: We\xe2\x80\x99re finalizing vanity redirects for marketplace. Okay.\\n\\n[25:16] (minor audio glitch)\\n\\n[25:18] Nadia Rahman: Can we ask about time window for metering submissions? Like can we send usage events delayed by a day?\\n\\n[25:26] Miguel Alvarez: There is a window; don\xe2\x80\x99t send extremely late. A few hours delay is okay. A day is sometimes okay but not ideal. Send as close to real time as possible. Again, I\xe2\x80\x99ll send guidance.\\n\\n[25:43] Nadia Rahman: Got it.\\n\\n[25:45] Cole Summers: For retry logic, if API errors, we retry with exponential backoff. If it fails for a long time, we queue. We can cap backlog.\\n\\n[25:54] Miguel Alvarez: That\xe2\x80\x99s fine. But make sure you don\xe2\x80\x99t exceed the allowed backfill window.\\n\\n[26:02] Stephanie Nguyen: Okay.\\n\\n[26:04] Priya Shah: Any other questions from Redwood before we wrap?\\n\\n[26:09] Stephanie Nguyen: I have one more: procurement question. Some buyers will ask \xe2\x80\x9cdoes buying through marketplace change the support terms / SLAs?\xe2\x80\x9d We have dedicated support tiers. Do you see any constraints on what we can say?\\n\\n[26:23] Priya Shah: You can state support terms clearly. Just avoid promising things you can\xe2\x80\x99t deliver. If there\xe2\x80\x99s a premium SLA, you can make it a plan or an add-on, but it should be consistent.\\n\\n[26:37] Ben Carter: We\xe2\x80\x99ll align with our support team.\\n\\n[26:40] Chris Osei: Okay. Let\xe2\x80\x99s do a quick recap and next steps.\\n\\n[26:45] Chris Osei: Recap:\\n\\n- Dimension strategy: keep Hosted usage-based with a small set of dimensions (tokens, embeddings), use subscription plans for Dedicated/Private, consider bundled plans for compliance add-ons.\\n- Use stable IDs, don\xe2\x80\x99t key logic off display names.\\n- Handle entitlement propagation delays with friendly UI + retry.\\n- Implement idempotency for metering events and alert on 4xx errors.\\n- Run staging test: subscribe -> entitlement -> org link -> API key -> sample traffic -> verify metering -> cancellation/plan change.\\n\\n[27:21] Stephanie Nguyen: And action items: Cole will send draft mapping + payload examples, Ben will send UX screens, Anika/Miguel will send docs on quotas/rounding/validation.\\n\\n[27:33] Anika Desai: Yep.\\n\\n[27:35] Miguel Alvarez: Yep, I\xe2\x80\x99ll send the metering validation checklist and rounding recommendations.", "score": 0.716978, "rank": 3, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "98997e59-4396-56a3-8ca6-923da1a39a85", "search_unit_id": "98997e59-4396-56a3-8ca6-923da1a39a85", "block_from_id": "49e91202-e695-532f-ae20-ef4aed919ce2", "block_to_id": "bcaa1799-9702-5bd2-8ddc-92489ec23c08", "section_path": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review", "document_id": "be72d63b-333f-51cc-8c3e-5b2af46aaab8", "document_version_id": "d74f559c-4a91-5a53-930c-c8b05d67f4ab", "document_name": "fireflies__dsid_6c4c1c875e704f09b4d791d64d7bc7e5.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "p-32", "heading": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review", "content": "[06:55] Miguel Alvarez: Dedicated capacity tends to fit better as subscription / contract. Usage metering for \xe2\x80\x9cgpu hours\xe2\x80\x9d works, but you need to align it with what you\xe2\x80\x99re actually providing. If it\xe2\x80\x99s truly reserved, then a monthly subscription fee is clearer. If there\xe2\x80\x99s burst/overage, you can meter overage separately.\\n\\n[07:16] Nadia Rahman: We can do either. Internally we track reserved pool size and then tokens anyway, but the contract is the key. If you recommend subscription for reserved, then our metering is mostly for overage.\\n\\n[07:29] Priya Shah: From a procurement standpoint, enterprise buyers usually prefer a predictable subscription line item. Then usage-based Hosted is more PLG.\\n\\n[07:41] Stephanie Nguyen: That matches what we\xe2\x80\x99re seeing. One thing we\xe2\x80\x99re worried about: we want three clear \xe2\x80\x9cpaths\xe2\x80\x9d in the listing\xe2\x80\x94Hosted, Dedicated, Private. But under the hood GCP constructs are not always \xe2\x80\x9cthree products.\xe2\x80\x9d It\xe2\x80\x99s one listing with plans, right?\\n\\n[07:57] Priya Shah: Exactly. You typically have one product listing and multiple plans. You can describe the three modes in the copy, but technically it\xe2\x80\x99s one product with pricing plans.\\n\\n[08:12] Ben Carter: On the onboarding, would you expect different landing pages for different plans? Like, if someone buys Private, they shouldn\xe2\x80\x99t be dropped into \xe2\x80\x9ccreate API key and call the hosted endpoint\xe2\x80\x9d because their next step is networking and deployment.\\n\\n[08:26] Anika Desai: That\xe2\x80\x99s a good point. Ideally yes: after entitlement, route them to the appropriate next steps. But you can also have a general landing page that asks \xe2\x80\x9cwhat did you purchase?\xe2\x80\x9d or reads the plan from the entitlement.\\n\\n[08:43] Cole Summers: Reading the plan from entitlement is doable if the entitlement includes plan name. We just need to be careful with plan rename.\\n\\n[08:52] Miguel Alvarez: Plan rename is okay if you keep the underlying plan ID stable. Don\xe2\x80\x99t key logic off display name.\\n\\n[09:01] Chris Osei: Noted. We\xe2\x80\x99ll key off ID.\\n\\n[09:05] Stephanie Nguyen: Let\xe2\x80\x99s talk about reviewer expectations on the metering payload. Miguel, can you share what they check?\\n\\n[09:14] Miguel Alvarez: Sure. They\xe2\x80\x99ll check: (1) you\xe2\x80\x99re using the correct API endpoints, (2) the consumerId or entitlement is valid, (3) the dimension is a valid one configured in your offer, (4) the quantity is non-negative and within reason, (5) timestamps are sane (not future by a day), (6) idempotency\xe2\x80\x94you don\xe2\x80\x99t double bill on retry.\\n\\n[09:40] Nadia Rahman: On idempotency: we have an internal \xe2\x80\x9cusage_event_id\xe2\x80\x9d and we can keep a ledger. If we retry, we send the same event id. Is that a pattern you like to see?\\n\\n[09:52] Miguel Alvarez: Yes. Exactly. Store the request ID and mark it as sent. Most of the \xe2\x80\x9cbilling mismatch\xe2\x80\x9d issues are duplicate sends during outages.\\n\\n[10:05] Ben Carter: Do you have any advice on how to validate end-to-end that metering is going to show up for the customer? Like, in AWS we can see it in the buyer account pretty quickly.", "score": 0.669441, "rank": 4, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "d63fa582-1069-5ed0-949c-73ae9c029e46", "search_unit_id": "d63fa582-1069-5ed0-949c-73ae9c029e46", "block_from_id": "1bc0c0e5-5337-5055-b204-3ff01a9d5ec8", "block_to_id": "d06c3d39-2866-571c-ab50-c35f03c87c79", "section_path": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review", "document_id": "be72d63b-333f-51cc-8c3e-5b2af46aaab8", "document_version_id": "d74f559c-4a91-5a53-930c-c8b05d67f4ab", "document_name": "fireflies__dsid_6c4c1c875e704f09b4d791d64d7bc7e5.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "p-61", "heading": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review", "content": "[13:22] Chris Osei: Yeah. Proposed test checklist:\\n1) Subscribe to plan in GCP Marketplace using test buyer\\n2) Verify entitlement exists (via API/console)\\n3) In Redwood console, run entitlement verification and link/create org\\n4) Generate an API key\\n5) Send a small set of inference requests (chat/text and embeddings)\\n6) Verify metering calls succeed and appear under correct dimension\\n7) Cancel subscription and verify access behavior (grace period?)\\n8) Re-subscribe / plan change and verify entitlements update\\n9) Negative tests: wrong entitlement, cross-org linking, delayed propagation\\n\\n[14:03] Miguel Alvarez: That\xe2\x80\x99s good. On cancellation: define your policy. Some partners cut off immediately, some allow a grace period. But it needs to match your listing terms and how you interpret the entitlement state.\\n\\n[14:18] Cole Summers: Our default is to stop accepting billable traffic if entitlement is inactive, but we may allow a short grace if the cancellation is accidental. For marketplace we probably keep it strict.\\n\\n[14:31] Anika Desai: During review, they\xe2\x80\x99ll test cancel and see if your UI reflects it. If there\xe2\x80\x99s a grace, document it.\\n\\n[14:40] Nadia Rahman: On entitlement state changes, how quickly do we see \xe2\x80\x9cactive -> cancelled\xe2\x80\x9d? That\xe2\x80\x99s a potential race.\\n\\n[14:49] Anika Desai: It\xe2\x80\x99s usually quick, but again, you should poll or have a scheduled sync. Don\xe2\x80\x99t assume immediate.\\n\\n[15:00] Ben Carter: We have a sync job that runs every few minutes and on-demand verification. We can tighten it during onboarding.\\n\\n[15:08] Miguel Alvarez: Just be careful with rate limits. If you have everyone spamming \xe2\x80\x9cverify entitlement\xe2\x80\x9d you might hit quotas. But for normal scale it\xe2\x80\x99s okay.\\n\\n[15:19] Stephanie Nguyen: One more thing: our SKUs include compliance add-ons (like extended audit log retention, compliance pack). Is it common to have add-ons as separate dimensions or separate plans?\\n\\n[15:34] Priya Shah: It depends. If it\xe2\x80\x99s truly an add-on, separate plan can be complicated. Some do \xe2\x80\x9cbundle plans\xe2\x80\x9d (base + add-on). Others handle add-ons off-marketplace. If you want it procured via marketplace, bundling plans is usually easiest.\\n\\n[15:54] Miguel Alvarez: If you do it as a usage dimension, that\xe2\x80\x99s weird for a monthly compliance pack. Better as subscription line item. But you can create a plan called \xe2\x80\x9cHosted + Compliance\xe2\x80\x9d and so on.\\n\\n[16:09] Cole Summers: That increases plan combinatorics. We were trying to keep it minimal.\\n\\n[16:14] Stephanie Nguyen: For enterprise procurement, it might be okay. We can do a small number: Hosted, Hosted+Compliance, Dedicated, Dedicated+Compliance, Private. Something like that.\\n\\n[16:26] Priya Shah: Exactly. Keep it manageable.\\n\\n[16:29] Chris Osei: Let\xe2\x80\x99s capture that as an open decision: add-ons as bundles vs separate plans.\\n\\n[16:35] Ben Carter: If it\xe2\x80\x99s bundled, onboarding can still read plan ID and show the right \xe2\x80\x9cfeatures enabled\xe2\x80\x9d banner.\\n\\n[16:42] Anika Desai: Yep.\\n\\n[16:44] Miguel Alvarez: For dimensions, fewer is better. If you have too many, it\xe2\x80\x99s harder to validate.\\n\\n[16:52] Nadia Rahman: Another metering nuance: we do streaming responses. Tokens get generated over time. We aggregate at end of request. That means we may emit one usage record per request or per batch. Is that okay?", "score": 0.665834, "rank": 5, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "a83d0680-34b9-5735-92f6-b5aca34f901c", "search_unit_id": "a83d0680-34b9-5735-92f6-b5aca34f901c", "block_from_id": "cfacdd9d-af81-5bf7-8ec8-4b2a29d3b767", "block_to_id": "fb1eabd7-2b32-53c5-877e-bf2ca901eb72", "section_path": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review", "document_id": "be72d63b-333f-51cc-8c3e-5b2af46aaab8", "document_version_id": "d74f559c-4a91-5a53-930c-c8b05d67f4ab", "document_name": "fireflies__dsid_6c4c1c875e704f09b4d791d64d7bc7e5.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "p-80", "heading": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review", "content": "[17:07] Miguel Alvarez: That\xe2\x80\x99s okay. The marketplace doesn\xe2\x80\x99t care if you batch, as long as it\xe2\x80\x99s accurate and you can explain your aggregation. Many partners aggregate per hour to reduce volume.\\n\\n[17:19] Cole Summers: We can aggregate per hour per org per dimension. That\xe2\x80\x99s probably a good default.\\n\\n[17:26] Miguel Alvarez: Yes.\\n\\n[17:28] Stephanie Nguyen: Any pitfalls with multi-region? Our hosted API runs in multiple regions. If a GCP marketplace buyer uses us in EU, does billing care?\\n\\n[17:39] Priya Shah: Billing doesn\xe2\x80\x99t care, but listing and security docs might. If you claim data residency, make sure it\xe2\x80\x99s accurate. The marketplace reviewers may look at your data handling statements.\\n\\n[17:52] Chris Osei: We\xe2\x80\x99re preparing a security packet; we\xe2\x80\x99ll align wording.\\n\\n[17:57] Anika Desai: From onboarding side, if you route them to a region selection, ensure it\xe2\x80\x99s clear. Some customers think marketplace implies region-specific. It doesn\xe2\x80\x99t necessarily.\\n\\n[18:10] Ben Carter: We plan to ask region at org creation and default to the closest region, but allow override.\\n\\n[18:18] Stephanie Nguyen: Great.\\n[18:20] (brief crosstalk)\\n\\n[18:22] Stephanie Nguyen: Miguel, can we ask about \xe2\x80\x9cdimension mismatch\xe2\x80\x9d issues you\xe2\x80\x99ve seen? We had one on AWS where our internal meter name didn\xe2\x80\x99t match the marketplace dimension and we billed wrong. We\xe2\x80\x99re trying to prevent it.\\n\\n[18:37] Miguel Alvarez: Yeah, common. It\xe2\x80\x99s usually one of: using display name instead of dimension ID, or typos, or changing the offer and not updating code. The best practice: keep a single source config file for mapping, version it, and add automated tests that validate the dimension list against what\xe2\x80\x99s configured in marketplace.\\n\\n[18:57] Cole Summers: We have a billing catalog config in code. We can add a test that asserts \xe2\x80\x9cthese dimensions are the only allowed ones.\xe2\x80\x9d But we can\xe2\x80\x99t programmatically query the marketplace config easily.\\n\\n[19:10] Miguel Alvarez: You can still do a manual gate before launch: export the dimension list and compare. And once stable, don\xe2\x80\x99t change often.\\n\\n[19:21] Nadia Rahman: We also emit telemetry events in our pipeline. We can alert on unknown dimension errors.\\n\\n[19:28] Miguel Alvarez: Perfect. Set alerts on 4xx responses from metering API.\\n\\n[19:35] Chris Osei: Okay. Let\xe2\x80\x99s do a quick pass on what GCP needs for submission beyond billing: screenshots, support, security docs, etc. Priya?\\n\\n[19:46] Priya Shah: You\xe2\x80\x99ll need: final listing copy, pricing plans configured, support contact info, a privacy policy link, and security documentation depending on the category. For enterprise oriented products, we often see SOC 2 statements, encryption, retention, audit logging. You don\xe2\x80\x99t need to paste the whole report, but your claims must be accurate.\\n\\n[20:10] Stephanie Nguyen: Understood. We\xe2\x80\x99ll provide a marketplace security overview and pointers to evidence under NDA.\\n\\n[20:18] Anika Desai: Reviewers like diagrams. Even a simple data flow diagram helps.\\n\\n[20:23] Ben Carter: We have a diagram showing subscription -> entitlement -> Redwood control plane -> inference runtime. We\xe2\x80\x99ll use it.\\n\\n[20:31] Chris Osei: Back to testing. Anika, you mentioned a test buyer flow. Can you outline quickly? Just so we know if we need special accounts.", "score": 0.632355, "rank": 6, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "4e1de271-3922-5983-9192-38028c593426", "search_unit_id": "4e1de271-3922-5983-9192-38028c593426", "block_from_id": "797bdded-0a3a-51b1-9a43-046c8e6562de", "block_to_id": "216bf5e6-513b-5732-b614-9d58c70f87f6", "section_path": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review", "document_id": "be72d63b-333f-51cc-8c3e-5b2af46aaab8", "document_version_id": "d74f559c-4a91-5a53-930c-c8b05d67f4ab", "document_name": "fireflies__dsid_6c4c1c875e704f09b4d791d64d7bc7e5.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "p-47", "heading": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review", "content": "[10:15] Miguel Alvarez: On GCP, you can see metering records in the partner console and via reports, but it\xe2\x80\x99s not always instant. For testing, you want to confirm your API calls succeed and then use the console to verify the usage events are recorded. Anika can share the checklist.\\n\\n[10:33] Anika Desai: Yeah, I can send a link. There\xe2\x80\x99s a \xe2\x80\x9ctest buyer\xe2\x80\x9d flow too. You procure with a test account, then you validate that entitlement is created and your backend can resolve it.\\n\\n[10:47] Stephanie Nguyen: Great. Another question: dimension naming constraints. We saw some limitations around character length and allowed characters.\\n\\n[10:56] Miguel Alvarez: Keep it short, alphanumeric plus underscore usually. Avoid spaces. If you have to include a version, don\xe2\x80\x99t. Better to keep the same dimension and change the price model rather than changing the dimension name.\\n\\n[11:13] Cole Summers: That\xe2\x80\x99s helpful. We were going to do \xe2\x80\x9chosted_tokens_1k\xe2\x80\x9d rather than \xe2\x80\x9chosted_input_tokens_1k\xe2\x80\x9d and \xe2\x80\x9chosted_output_tokens_1k\xe2\x80\x9d to keep it one dimension. We can still show the breakdown in our console for observability, but bill as total tokens.\\n\\n[11:32] Miguel Alvarez: That\xe2\x80\x99s a good compromise.\\n\\n[11:35] Stephanie Nguyen: And embeddings we can keep separate.\\n\\n[11:38] Miguel Alvarez: Yes.\\n\\n[11:40] Chris Osei: I want to make sure we cover onboarding UX expectations. Ben, can you describe what we\xe2\x80\x99re building and get GCP feedback?\\n\\n[11:49] Ben Carter: Sure. So in our console, when you click \xe2\x80\x9cGet started\xe2\x80\x9d, there will be a \xe2\x80\x9cPurchased via marketplace?\xe2\x80\x9d path. If they choose GCP marketplace, we ask them to sign in. Then we try to auto-detect entitlements tied to that user, and if we can\xe2\x80\x99t, we allow them to paste an entitlement ID. If entitlement is found, we show \xe2\x80\x9clink to existing org\xe2\x80\x9d or \xe2\x80\x9ccreate new org.\xe2\x80\x9d After that, we prompt them to create first API key for Hosted, or if the plan is Dedicated/Private, we route to request provisioning steps.\\n\\n[12:24] Anika Desai: That\xe2\x80\x99s pretty aligned with what we like to see. Two things: (1) make it very clear what to do if they\xe2\x80\x99re not the person who purchased. Sometimes procurement buys and engineering uses. So include instructions like \xe2\x80\x9cask the billing admin to invite you\xe2\x80\x9d or \xe2\x80\x9cforward this entitlement ID.\xe2\x80\x9d (2) include a retry experience for pending entitlements.\\n\\n[12:50] Ben Carter: Yep. We have an error state \xe2\x80\x9cEntitlement not found\xe2\x80\x9d that currently suggests contacting support. We can change that to \xe2\x80\x9cmay take a few minutes\xe2\x80\x9d and include refresh.\\n\\n[13:02] Priya Shah: Also include support SLA information and the right support channel for marketplace customers. It doesn\xe2\x80\x99t have to be complex, but the listing should say how to get help.\\n\\n[13:12] Stephanie Nguyen: We\xe2\x80\x99ll incorporate that into listing FAQs. On testing: we need a clear test plan. Chris, we have some draft steps; can we align with GCP?", "score": 0.483532, "rank": 7, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "09c91ae1-fa9b-5864-aa27-572727b20925", "search_unit_id": "09c91ae1-fa9b-5864-aa27-572727b20925", "block_from_id": "6618f247-727d-5ae0-b594-829a8b76da67", "block_to_id": "c39da020-3371-55c3-aa3e-eba8f98e8abf", "section_path": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review", "document_id": "be72d63b-333f-51cc-8c3e-5b2af46aaab8", "document_version_id": "d74f559c-4a91-5a53-930c-c8b05d67f4ab", "document_name": "fireflies__dsid_6c4c1c875e704f09b4d791d64d7bc7e5.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "p-17", "heading": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review", "content": "[03:32] Stephanie Nguyen: That\xe2\x80\x99s helpful. We\xe2\x80\x99ve got an entitlement verification endpoint and a sync job; we can make sure the UI does the \xe2\x80\x9cpending\xe2\x80\x9d messaging.\\n\\n[03:41] Priya Shah: Also important: the marketplace reviewers will click through the onboarding. If they hit a dead end, that\xe2\x80\x99s friction during review. Provide a support link and clear instructions.\\n\\n[03:53] Chris Osei: Got it. Maybe we jump into identifiers. Anika, can you talk about what we should store and what we can expect to receive? We\xe2\x80\x99ve seen \xe2\x80\x9cconsumer ID\xe2\x80\x9d in docs but there are like multiple names for it.\\n\\n[04:07] Anika Desai: Yeah. So, you\xe2\x80\x99ll have a procurement account / buyer account. When a customer subscribes, there\xe2\x80\x99s an entitlement created. Depending on your integration, you\xe2\x80\x99ll get a consumerId and entitlement name or resource. Your system should treat that as the source of truth for \xe2\x80\x9cis this customer subscribed\xe2\x80\x9d and also for mapping metering events to the right buyer.\\n\\n[04:30] Cole Summers: For metering, we need a stable key to associate usage to the marketplace customer. We also have our internal \xe2\x80\x9corg_id\xe2\x80\x9d. So our thought was: marketplace entitlement maps to Redwood org, and then that org has one or more projects / API keys. Usage is aggregated at org level and then billed.\\n\\n[04:49] Miguel Alvarez: That\xe2\x80\x99s typical. Just ensure you don\xe2\x80\x99t mix multiple entitlements into a single org without clear logic. If a customer buys two subscriptions, you need to decide whether that\xe2\x80\x99s two entitlements attached to one org or two orgs. Reviewers will look for correct allocation.\\n\\n[05:06] Ben Carter: We have an edge case: existing Redwood customers may subscribe later through marketplace for procurement reasons. So they want to link the marketplace subscription to an already-existing org.\\n\\n[05:17] Anika Desai: That\xe2\x80\x99s also common. UX expectation: you allow linking to an existing account. But ensure authentication is done. And avoid a scenario where someone can claim an entitlement that isn\xe2\x80\x99t theirs.\\n\\n[05:31] Stephanie Nguyen: Right. We require sign-in, then they paste an entitlement or we detect via login? We were thinking detect via a \xe2\x80\x9cclaim token\xe2\x80\x9d page in our console.\\n\\n[05:42] Anika Desai: Either works. Most partners do a redirect back with parameters, or they ask the user to input the entitlement ID. Redirect flows are nicer but more work. For review, either is acceptable as long as it\xe2\x80\x99s documented.\\n\\n[06:00] Chris Osei: For this phase we\xe2\x80\x99re doing a marketplace-specific entrypoint in our console. It will prompt for \xe2\x80\x9cGCP marketplace subscription\xe2\x80\x9d and then run entitlement verification.\\n\\n[06:10] Miguel Alvarez: That\xe2\x80\x99s fine. On billing: I want to zoom in on the dimension list. Can you share what you propose?\\n\\n[06:18] Cole Summers: We don\xe2\x80\x99t have final IDs yet, but draft is something like:\\n\\n- Hosted: \xe2\x80\x9chosted_tokens_1k\xe2\x80\x9d and \xe2\x80\x9chosted_embeddings_1k\xe2\x80\x9d\\n- Rerank: \xe2\x80\x9crerank_requests_1k\xe2\x80\x9d (maybe)\\n- Dedicated: \xe2\x80\x9cdedicated_gpu_hour\xe2\x80\x9d or \xe2\x80\x9cdedicated_capacity_unit\xe2\x80\x9d\\n- Private: \xe2\x80\x9cprivate_platform_month\xe2\x80\x9d\\n- Add-ons: \xe2\x80\x9ccompliance_pack_month\xe2\x80\x9d, \xe2\x80\x9cextended_audit_log_retention_month\xe2\x80\x9d\\n\\nThe big question is whether \xe2\x80\x9cDedicated\xe2\x80\x9d should be a usage dimension or a subscription SKU.", "score": 0.425825, "rank": 8, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "ab05e4e3-abcf-5374-bad9-e7b306108d95", "search_unit_id": "ab05e4e3-abcf-5374-bad9-e7b306108d95", "block_from_id": "c531a362-20d4-57d6-971d-13c94aa06d77", "block_to_id": "5bb4312f-a726-5731-9e5f-fe8840fb16f2", "section_path": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review", "document_id": "be72d63b-333f-51cc-8c3e-5b2af46aaab8", "document_version_id": "d74f559c-4a91-5a53-930c-c8b05d67f4ab", "document_name": "fireflies__dsid_6c4c1c875e704f09b4d791d64d7bc7e5.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "p-148", "heading": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review", "content": "[27:40] Priya Shah: And once you\xe2\x80\x99ve done the staging test, we can do a short follow-up to review results and any issues.\\n\\n[27:48] Stephanie Nguyen: Perfect. Thanks everyone.\\n[27:51] (call continues with scheduling chatter)\\n\\n[28:00] Chris Osei: I\xe2\x80\x99ll send the notes and propose a follow-up next week.\\n\\n[28:05] Priya Shah: Great.\\n[28:07] Meeting ended (recording continued briefly)\\n[53:58] (end of recording)", "score": 0.398513, "rank": 9, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}], "events": [], "entities": [], "relations": [], "source_hits": [], "summary": "", "citations": [], "answer_status": "pending", "no_answer_reason": null, "exploration_id": null, "stats": {"canonical_index": true, "ready_versions": 81, "requested_top_k": null, "candidate_top_k": 32, "candidate_unit_pool_limit": 1024, "candidate_pool_saturated": true, "candidate_units_before_cap": 74, "candidate_units_after_cap": 74, "candidate_units_truncated": 0, "semantic_candidates": 45, "lexical_candidates": 40, "candidates": 74, "relevant": 47, "filtered_irrelevant": 27, "fusion_method": "rrf", "selection_method": "authorized_content_dedup_mmr", "cross_scope_merge": "rank_interleave", "coverage": {"candidate_count": 64, "deduplicated_count": 0, "diversity_weight": 0.5, "near_duplicate_similarity": 0.98, "required_anchor_count": 0, "covered_anchor_count": 0, "anchor_coverage": "not_required", "status": "unknown", "canonical_locator_verified": true, "semantic_answerability": "unknown"}, "routing": {"planner": {"planner_version": "qsp-v1", "requested_strategy": "AUTO", "effective_strategy": "TEMPORAL", "modifiers": [], "reason_codes": ["TEMPORAL_CUE_PRESENT"], "multi_hop_eligible": false}, "request_snapshot_id": "f4f5c7fc-8204-4e4c-8655-e0bab981f0eb", "groups": [{"project_id": "b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c", "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "partition_id": "b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c", "snapshot_id": "3e934e6f-db48-44e9-92f0-bfeab1b8548c", "request_snapshot_id": "f4f5c7fc-8204-4e4c-8655-e0bab981f0eb", "acl_scope_fingerprint": "328ac326f64690733d385248a99659bc5ed25e83991108887c7a3367d2d5c51e", "tree_version": "tree-50bdd85ee0f1111bfecc7840", "routing_slot": "SLOT_A", "search_epoch": 2, "selected_nodes": ["56053360af7bd0c00b3c23b570340db9", "5aacc2b21b3f97707f284f0fa55f0052", "d48df15e10c1588c77a8ea414162a6c5"], "membership_count": 168, "entropy": 0.9998614749137494, "margin": 0.01063089234895831, "broad_route": true, "reason_code": "ROUTE_UNCERTAIN_BROADENED", "fallback_reason": null, "branch_local_candidates": 56, "global_candidates": 56, "escape_recovered_candidates": 18, "escape_attempted": true, "escape_trigger": "reserved_parallel_global_escape", "local_failure": null, "escape_failure": null, "blackhole_detected": false}], "coverage": {"candidate_count": 64, "deduplicated_count": 0, "diversity_weight": 0.5, "near_duplicate_similarity": 0.98, "required_anchor_count": 0, "covered_anchor_count": 0, "anchor_coverage": "not_required", "status": "unknown", "canonical_locator_verified": true, "semantic_answerability": "unknown"}, "selection_fallback_reason": null, "multi_hop": {"eligible": false, "effective": "TEMPORAL", "graph_expansion_attempted": false, "graph_expanded_candidates": 0, "graph_expansion_skip_reason": null}, "fallback_used": true}, "selection_fallback_reason": null, "stage_latencies_ms": {"auth_scope": 24.52, "tree_route": 539.08, "candidate_retrieval": 1930.23, "evidence_hydration": 16.41, "rank_fusion": 36.13, "graph_expansion": 0.0, "diversity_selection": 1904.52, "total_retrieval": 4460.73}, "latency_total_ms": 4460.73, "automatic_top_k": true, "effective_top_k": 10, "adaptive_waves": 1, "retrieval_stop_reason": "relevance_plateau", "budget_exhausted": false, "has_more": false, "acl_filtered_candidates": 0, "query_route": {"version": "query-flow-v1", "query_original": "In the meeting about onboarding a SaaS product to Google Cloud Marketplace, what did the GCP team recommend for handling delays where a new subscription entitlement is not immediately available during the customer onboarding flow?", "scope_source_ids": ["b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159"], "coarse_intent": "KNOWLEDGE", "confidence": 0.8677, "model": "Kev-4b", "suggested_strategy": "vector", "requested_strategy": "auto", "effective_strategy": "vector", "retrieval": "required", "fallback_used": false, "fallback_reason": null, "reason_codes": ["KNOWLEDGE_INTENT", "TEMPORAL_CUE"], "query_analysis": {"normalized_phrase": "inthemeetingaboutonboardingasaasproducttogooglecloudmarketplacewhatdidthegcpteamrecommendforhandlingdelayswhereanewsubscriptionentitlementisnotimmediatelyavailableduringthecustomeronboardingflow", "lookup_terms": ["meeting", "onboarding", "saas", "product", "google", "cloud", "marketplace", "gcp", "team", "recommend", "handling", "delays", "new", "subscription", "entitlement", "not"], "features": {"exact_terms": [], "identifier_terms": [], "path_terms": [], "temporal_cues": ["during"], "relation_cues": [], "global_cues": [], "multi_hop": false}}, "strategy_plan": {"planner_version": "qsp-v1", "requested_strategy": "AUTO", "effective_strategy": "TEMPORAL", "modifiers": [], "reason_codes": ["TEMPORAL_CUE_PRESENT"], "multi_hop_eligible": false}, "retrieval_fallback_used": false}}}\r\n\r\n'
