14:04:27  DEBUG    [03c1d077cf794f24]  sse_starlette.sse  chunk: b'event: result\r\ndata: {"query": "What was the high-percentile latency concern reported after a smoke test of about 50 concurrent streaming chat sessions for Satellite Grove?", "sections": [{"chunk_id": "9535c1ad-9e50-5d72-a44d-15448d666949", "search_unit_id": "9535c1ad-9e50-5d72-a44d-15448d666949", "block_from_id": "2f7181e9-79f9-524f-b06b-de815b454823", "block_to_id": "1d44c726-2cf3-5c9b-90e7-749505d4429f", "section_path": "Company Satellite Grove Assist", "document_id": "4ff17d7e-33e3-5ee4-9a0d-01424fe43fe8", "document_version_id": "397413d3-bc75-5433-a674-c76bd935f11c", "document_name": "hubspot__dsid_e75bbf328fe349d2bb39e29b1ed7c9d5.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h1-0", "heading": "Company Satellite Grove Assist", "content": "Company Satellite Grove Assist\\n\\nSource type: hubspot\\nDocument ID: dsid_e75bbf328fe349d2bb39e29b1ed7c9d5\\nRelease: v1.0.0\\nBenchmark: EnterpriseRAG-Bench synthetic upstream data\\nSatellite Grove\\n\\nLead source: self-serve signup (product-led). Team: CTO (Aisha Kaur), Eng Lead (Mateo Lopez), PM (Rina Shah). Early discovery call 2026-03-02 via Fireflies ff-20260302-0934. Summary: building an in-app conversational assistant for their B2B workflow SaaS; must feel instant (streaming responses) and run on public cloud.\\n\\nKey requirements:\\n\\n- Streaming websocket support + chunked tokens (UX requires UI tokens arrive in <50ms increments).\\n- p50 target ~40-80ms, p95 <120ms, p99 <200ms for short responses (avg 60 tokens).\\n- Concurrent active users at launch ~100-200, expected steady-state 20-50.\\n- Cost-sensitive: want guidance on batching vs streaming tradeoffs and token/unit price impact.\\n- Model preferences: prefer smaller fast open models for latency (we mentioned quantized Llama-family / Mistral-lite), but open to Redwood verified models for throughput.\\n- Security: SOC2 on roadmap, need SSO (SAML) + audit logging for partner beta.\\n\\nPOC/workflow discussed:\\n\\n- They used free credits to wire up a quick websocket chat to Redwood hosted API. Observed good tail latencies but worried about unit cost when scaling. Did a smoke test with ~50 concurrent short chats (avg reply length 60 tokens) \xe2\x80\x94 saw 120ms median, 250ms p99 in one run.\\n- Interested in Redwood Optimize suggestions (batching hints, prefix caching) to reduce cost without hurting streaming UX.\\n\\nQuotes / call notes (shorthand):\\n\\n- \\"Streaming must feel instant \xe2\x80\x94 if agents lag, users drop off.\\" \xe2\x80\x94 Aisha (CTO).\\n- \\"We need a clear cost model for streaming usage; tickets are tight for first 6 months.\\" \xe2\x80\x94 Mateo (Eng Lead).\\n\\nTimeline & activities: see activity_timeline field for discrete items.\\n\\nRisk / next-stage signals: if we can show a predictable cost/latency plan and a simple SSO flow, they\'ll move to a 3-month paid pilot and open a dedicated usage seat in April.\\n2026-02-28: Product signup + free credits \xe2\x80\x94 initial test with websocket streaming\\n2026-03-02: Discovery call (Fireflies ff-20260302-0934) \xe2\x80\x94 introduced hosted API streaming endpoints\\n2026-03-04: Eng follow-up email with repro of p99 spikes (gt-20260301-dev-inbound-7a9)\\n2026-03-06: SE triage \xe2\x80\x94 Priya reproduced with 50 concurrent sessions, flagged batching vs concurrency tradeoff\\n2026-03-09: Sent preliminary cost/latency delta matrix + link to pricing deck (drive:/deals/SatelliteGrove/pricing-deck-v2.pdf)\\n2026-03-10: Customer asked for p99 benchmarks on region us-west and token-based cost example at 200 concurrent users", "score": 0.817042, "rank": 0, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "7d31c784-c1a1-5c50-a8e3-7018a27a2f5d", "search_unit_id": "7d31c784-c1a1-5c50-a8e3-7018a27a2f5d", "block_from_id": "a06af6d3-809b-5e1d-8af6-3e4209ab6787", "block_to_id": "010f8512-acbf-5040-89bd-f1872a5a3679", "section_path": "Sup 18421 Enterprise 429 Spike After Priority Routing Rollout > Impact", "document_id": "89c06a4c-9104-5c7e-b766-1b1af3c247dd", "document_version_id": "c6465792-6750-5999-b72c-4196eb71504b", "document_name": "jira__dsid_1980f45c3fb4455f943a2b030892ae90.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-4", "heading": "Sup 18421 Enterprise 429 Spike After Priority Routing Rollout > Impact", "content": "Impact\\n\\n- Customer batch + online traffic intermittently fails with 429s, causing retry storms on their side.\\n- Reported elevated end-user latency due to retries; some requests fail after their max-retry budget.\\n- Customer concern: enterprise tier expected fewer 429s, not more.", "score": 0.669271, "rank": 1, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "243aa4e1-ec00-57ad-bd06-c6f6b5c7ef5a", "search_unit_id": "243aa4e1-ec00-57ad-bd06-c6f6b5c7ef5a", "block_from_id": "780d9ed0-d169-5a86-9b48-f746b3595b01", "block_to_id": "cf0d9903-36e3-5187-bf17-795b8600e5b7", "section_path": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > SLOs (targets) > Latency SLOs by route and tier", "document_id": "12efb103-6e2e-5955-be3e-431b65ac2bca", "document_version_id": "78cfdc0b-41fe-5614-b19f-c7d639aaa33b", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_49fa5cfd95624dbeb6eaa535f9959470.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-46", "heading": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > SLOs (targets) > Latency SLOs by route and tier", "content": "Latency SLOs by route and tier\\n\\nLatency objectives are defined in terms of:\\n\\n- **Chat streaming:** TTFB p95/p99\\n- **Non-streaming:** E2E p95/p99\\n\\n| Route | Tier | Latency SLO (primary) | Latency SLO (secondary) | Measurement |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---|\\n| `/v1/chat/completions` (streaming) | Enterprise (Protected) | TTFB p95 \xe2\x89\xa4 450ms | TTFB p99 \xe2\x89\xa4 900ms | Gateway TTFB |\\n| `/v1/chat/completions` (streaming) | Best-effort | TTFB p95 \xe2\x89\xa4 900ms | (informational) p99 \xe2\x89\xa4 1800ms | Gateway TTFB |\\n| `/v1/chat/completions` (non-streaming) | Enterprise (Protected) | E2E p95 \xe2\x89\xa4 1.8s | E2E p99 \xe2\x89\xa4 3.5s | Gateway E2E |\\n| `/v1/embeddings` | Enterprise (Protected) | E2E p95 \xe2\x89\xa4 350ms | E2E p99 \xe2\x89\xa4 800ms | Gateway E2E |\\n| `/v1/embeddings` | Best-effort | E2E p95 \xe2\x89\xa4 700ms | (informational) p99 \xe2\x89\xa4 1.5s | Gateway E2E |\\n| `/v1/rerank` | Enterprise (Protected) | E2E p95 \xe2\x89\xa4 550ms | E2E p99 \xe2\x89\xa4 1.2s | Gateway E2E |\\n| `/v1/rerank` | Best-effort | E2E p95 \xe2\x89\xa4 1.2s | (informational) p99 \xe2\x89\xa4 2.5s | Gateway E2E |\\n\\n**Notes / caveats**\\n\\n- Latency SLOs assume nominal prompt and response sizes. Excessive prompt length or extreme max_tokens increases may be subject to policy limits; those requests are still included in latency metrics unless explicitly rejected by policy.\\n- Where fallback is permitted (route/model policy), latency SLI measures the actual served path; dashboards must annotate fallback activations.", "score": 0.654259, "rank": 2, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "cd16a46d-74cc-580a-875c-465401a1b3e9", "search_unit_id": "cd16a46d-74cc-580a-875c-465401a1b3e9", "block_from_id": "ae8b6cdf-9bf6-5bbb-a815-9990fe1395ba", "block_to_id": "d1964765-27dd-5c46-aab3-4e24005cd79a", "section_path": "Sup 18510 Streaming Throughput Metric Mismatch", "document_id": "d164ca2a-1fef-5580-b76a-e8640c05b61e", "document_version_id": "9403367b-6c10-5fbb-bd00-38bc695b7e78", "document_name": "jira__dsid_a5a5235d973e421c870e11ceea4887ed.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h1-0", "heading": "Sup 18510 Streaming Throughput Metric Mismatch", "content": "Sup 18510 Streaming Throughput Metric Mismatch\\n\\nSource type: jira\\nDocument ID: dsid_a5a5235d973e421c870e11ceea4887ed\\nRelease: v1.0.0\\nBenchmark: EnterpriseRAG-Bench synthetic upstream data\\nStreaming throughput metric mismatch (customer dashboard vs Redwood export) \xe2\x80\x94 suspected token accounting double-count in streaming\\n\\nCustomer (Vidorama) reports inconsistent throughput numbers for their Dedicated deployment when using streaming responses.\\n\\nIssue summary\\n\\n- Vidorama is comparing:\\n  1) Redwood Console throughput panel (tokens/sec) for their Dedicated deployment, and\\n  2) Their internal measurement based on streamed tokens (client-side) + Redwood usage export.\\n- They see Redwood Console tokens/sec ~1.8\xe2\x80\x932.1x higher than their client-side computed throughput for the same time windows.\\n- Discrepancy appears only for streaming (non-streaming requests match within ~5\xe2\x80\x9310%).\\n\\nImpact\\n\\n- Customer confusion + concern about Dedicated throughput SLO reporting accuracy.\\n- Potential false positives/negatives in SLO compliance if the canonical throughput metric is inflated for streaming.\\n\\nCustomer-provided details\\n\\n- Workload: chat completions, streaming enabled (server-sent events).\\n- Typical request: prompt 1\xe2\x80\x932k tokens, completion 150\xe2\x80\x93400 tokens, 20\xe2\x80\x9360 concurrent streams.\\n- Sampling window: 5-min rolling, comparing against their Prometheus counters.\\n\\nRedwood initial checks (Support)\\n\\n- Confirmed customer is hitting the correct deployment_id and region (us-east).\\n- Confirmed they are not mixing model variants in the comparison window (pinned to llama-3.1-70b-instruct; occasional mixtral bursts were excluded by customer).\\n- Confirmed they are not counting input tokens in their client throughput metric (they only count generated tokens).\\n\\nSteps to reproduce (internal)\\n1) Run load test against a Dedicated deployment with streaming enabled.\\n2) Compare:\\n\\n   - streaming token counter used by throughput dashboard (server-side), vs\\n   - sum(completion_tokens) from request final usage events (server-side) and/or reconstructed deltas.\\n\\n3) Observe that for streaming requests, the dashboard-aligned metric increases faster than final usage totals.\\n\\nInvestigation notes (SRE/Obs)\\n\\n- Hypothesis A: double-counting streamed completion tokens (both per-chunk deltas and final usage event contribute to the same counter).\\n- Hypothesis B: retries/resumes on stream reconnects cause duplicated deltas.\\n- Hypothesis C: mixed attribution labels (route vs deployment) cause aggregation overlap.\\n\\nFindings\\n\\n- We can reproduce consistent ~2x inflation when streaming is enabled and client consumes the full stream.\\n- Root cause identified in telemetry emission path:\\n  - For streaming responses, we emit per-chunk token deltas (used for near-real-time throughput).\\n  - At stream termination, a final usage event is emitted.\\n  - In the current pipeline, the dashboard throughput counter was incremented by BOTH the per-chunk deltas and the final usage event for the same request.\\n- This does NOT affect billing totals (billing uses finalized usage only). It affects the real-time throughput metric used in dashboards and SLO evaluation.\\n\\nWorkaround (until fix rollout)\\n\\n- For customer reporting, use finalized completion token metrics (request end) for streaming workloads:\\n  - completion_tokens_total (finalized) / wall time\\n- Avoid using real-time streaming throughput panel for contractual/SLO comparisons until patched.\\n\\nNext actions", "score": 0.615056, "rank": 3, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "50cf7910-8278-59c2-a02e-53ac321e5dfd", "search_unit_id": "50cf7910-8278-59c2-a02e-53ac321e5dfd", "block_from_id": "5a698eaa-0691-536a-9270-379ee96a445c", "block_to_id": "5a698eaa-0691-536a-9270-379ee96a445c", "section_path": "Sup 864999 Sliced Ingest Priority Class Throttle Config", "document_id": "d9afbdee-1747-5299-966e-78fdaa3c1cf3", "document_version_id": "7d1ff66b-6a87-5e79-a4fb-df1cc60b4124", "document_name": "jira__dsid_5f3a672da4974781a5577b0f3d4993e9.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "p-13", "heading": "Sup 864999 Sliced Ingest Priority Class Throttle Config", "content": "1) Use the customer\'s traffic shape: 100 concurrent short chat requests (avg 50ms) + 20 parallel multipart embedding uploads (avg 45s each).\\n2) Route both traffic shapes through the dedicated pool dp-132-usw with current priority-class config (default: interactive=high, background=low).\\n3) Start background uploads in a tight window to simulate a scheduled flush (cron at 09:00).\\n4) Observe 429s on interactive calls between 09:00-09:30 when background uploads ramp up.\\n2026-03-10T09:02:14Z - 200 - /v1/chat - key=acct-streamly-01 - latency=62ms\\n2026-03-10T09:02:19Z - 429 - /v1/chat - key=acct-streamly-01 - throttle: route_window=0/100\\n2026-03-10T09:02:20Z - 429 - /v1/embed - key=acct-streamly-01 - chunk=12/20 - err=quota_exhausted\\n2026-03-10T09:05:01Z - 429 - /v1/chat - key=acct-streamly-01 - no-rate-headers (opaque)\\nSampler trace: https://observability.redwood.internal/trace/ingest/trace-9832\\nHigh - user-visible errors for real-time chat during scheduled bulk ingest; customer is on dedicated tier and requests urgent guidance (P1).\\nInstructed customer to stagger background multipart uploads and introduce jitter in their flush window. Recommended reducing concurrency of background upload workers to < 6 during peak windows as a temporary mitigation.\\n2026-03-10 09:20 - Support (Ava Chen): Acknowledged ticket and requested full request IDs and example request traces. Asked customer to confirm whether they use signed URLs for chunk upload callbacks.\\n2026-03-10 10:05 - Customer (Streamly AI - ops@streamly.ai): Provided request IDs and stated they use signed URLs for chunk completion callbacks and run a cron flush at 09:00 local. Attached sample batch IDs: batch-20260310-0919.\\n2026-03-10 11:00 - SRE (Liam O\'Neill): Checked rate-limiter logs and saw route-level token depletion aligning with the cron flush. Noted some refill events lagged due to burst-pool-weight recalculation on pool attach.\\n2026-03-11 08:45 - Eng (Maya Patel): Observed a potential priority-class weight inversion in canary release 1.14.0-canary where background ingestion borrow logic could erroneously draw from interactive burst credits under certain attach sequences.\\n2026-03-12 09:00 - Support (Ava Chen): Shared recommended config changes and rollout plan. Scheduling a brief call with customer for 2026-03-12 14:00 PDT to apply non-breaking adjustments and observe behavior.\\n2026-03-12 13:30 - Customer (Streamly AI): Agreed to the windowed-stagger plan and will apply client-side jitter for the March 12 trial.\\n1) Correlation between cron flush start times and onset of 429s is strong (observability traces + rate-limiter counters).\\n2) Rate-limiter metric rlim.route.tokens.available shows transient negative deltas when background workers register multiple concurrent signed-url callbacks (double-counting observed in edge proxy earlier; needs validation).\\n3) Canary release 1.14.0 has a code path that recalculates burst-pool weights when a route attaches to a new pool; race condition can allow an attach to read stale bucket state and permit borrow that appears to zero out another pool\'s credits.\\n4) Some 429s are opaque because edge proxy returns before rate-limiter headers are populated when under heavy callback fanout. Need to instrument edge path to surface X-RateLimit headers even under backpressure.\\n5) No evidence of dedicated pool GPU exhaustion; autoscaler metrics are stable.\\n1) Short-term (apply during call): adjust per-route burst allocation to reserve 20% of interactive burst credits exclusively for priority=high routes on dp-132-usw.\\n2) Short-term: recommend customer stagger flush windows and add randomized jitter to callback fanout.", "score": 0.611992, "rank": 4, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "6907986d-ae14-5167-af96-fb20158abed9", "search_unit_id": "6907986d-ae14-5167-af96-fb20158abed9", "block_from_id": "f8735775-a5c2-55df-9eee-44825a4ca655", "block_to_id": "3e381144-1521-5137-b4bb-9113bc2ac936", "section_path": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > Retry & backoff guidance (what we should recommend) > Streaming-specific guidance", "document_id": "d1667e56-b0c3-5fb4-a769-9f13a8679e2f", "document_version_id": "a9986e4a-2cf0-5373-a739-d5611a7a824c", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_ca42a8f18d784c879c5a6fcfe3a484c0.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-41", "heading": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > Retry & backoff guidance (what we should recommend) > Streaming-specific guidance", "content": "Streaming-specific guidance\\n\\nStreaming workloads can worsen overload if clients open many concurrent streams.\\n\\n- Recommend **lower streaming concurrency** during peak periods.\\n- If the customer can tolerate it, recommend **shorter outputs** (reduce `max_tokens`) and/or **shorter prompts**.\\n- If a stream fails mid-response with an overload error on reconnect attempts, advise them to back off and resume with a bounded number of attempts.", "score": 0.601384, "rank": 5, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "e4c50399-72c5-540a-b18c-d7afd92e9a11", "search_unit_id": "e4c50399-72c5-540a-b18c-d7afd92e9a11", "block_from_id": "dd9bca12-3593-500a-bac8-e6ce74c5ef78", "block_to_id": "faef3e43-3217-593f-83c4-7be4153ef3b3", "section_path": "Sliced Ingest 429S Dedicated Pool Priority Class Debug Notes", "document_id": "f554b405-fa78-5662-b771-484dbdbab9f3", "document_version_id": "38a32ec0-f3b7-5fcb-86a6-461c5475d650", "document_name": "google_drive__dsid_8ffbd9fe82df457ca67d4fbc9ff1090c.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h1-0", "heading": "Sliced Ingest 429S Dedicated Pool Priority Class Debug Notes", "content": "Sliced Ingest 429S Dedicated Pool Priority Class Debug Notes\\n\\nSource type: google_drive\\nDocument ID: dsid_8ffbd9fe82df457ca67d4fbc9ff1090c\\nRelease: v1.0.0\\nBenchmark: EnterpriseRAG-Bench synthetic upstream data\\nSliced ingest 429s on dedicated pool after migration \xe2\x80\x94 priority class + burst pool debug notes (Streamly AI)\\n\\nContext / why this doc exists\\n\\n- This is a working scratchpad for SRE/Eng while debugging Streamly AI\xe2\x80\x99s sustained 429s during sliced ingest after dedicated pool migration.\\n- Source ticket: SUP-864999 (customer-support). This doc is meant to be the \xe2\x80\x9cnewer truth\xe2\x80\x9d vs the older ticket narrative since we\xe2\x80\x99ve learned more post-call + after patch testing.\\n\\nCustomer + impact recap (same as ticket, but condensed)\\n\\n- Customer: Streamly AI (dedicated tier)\\n- Environment: prod us-west, dedicated pool dp-132-usw\\n- Traffic mix:\\n  - Interactive: short chat calls (priority=high)\\n  - Background: multipart embedding uploads (priority=background/low) with signed URL chunk completion callbacks\\n- Observed on 2026-03-10 ~09:00\xe2\x80\x9311:30 PDT:\\n  - ~30% of short chat requests returned 429 coincident with the scheduled bulk ingest flush\\n  - Customer saw retry amplification and user-visible errors\\n\\nVersions / components\\n\\n- Dedicated pool: dp-132-usw\\n- Ingest coordinator version (at time of incident): 2026.02.3\\n- Rate-limiter version (at time of incident): 1.14.0-canary\\n- Edge / API gateway involved due to opaque 429 behavior (missing headers)\\n\\nSymptoms (what we saw)\\n\\n- Persistent 429s for /v1/chat during background upload ramp.\\n- Throttle headers when present indicated per-route window exhaustion.\\n- A subset of 429s were \xe2\x80\x9copaque\xe2\x80\x9d (no X-RateLimit headers) especially during callback fanout peaks.\\n\\nExample log snippets (from ticket; kept here for reference)\\n\\n- 2026-03-10T09:02:14Z - 200 - /v1/chat - key=acct-streamly-01 - latency=62ms\\n- 2026-03-10T09:02:19Z - 429 - /v1/chat - key=acct-streamly-01 - throttle: route_window=0/100\\n- 2026-03-10T09:02:20Z - 429 - /v1/embed - key=acct-streamly-01 - chunk=12/20 - err=quota_exhausted\\n- 2026-03-10T09:05:01Z - 429 - /v1/chat - key=acct-streamly-01 - no-rate-headers (opaque)\\n\\nSteps to reproduce (traffic shape)\\n1) 100 concurrent short chat requests (avg 50ms) + 20 parallel multipart embedding uploads (avg 45s each).\\n2) Route both traffic shapes through dp-132-usw with priority classes: interactive=high, background=low.\\n3) Start background uploads in tight window (cron-style flush ~09:00 local).\\n4) Observe interactive 429s during the first 20\xe2\x80\x9340 minutes of background ramp.\\n\\nWhat changed vs initial hypothesis (updated findings)\\n\\n- Initial hypothesis in SUP-864999: \xe2\x80\x9cpriority class mismatch / weight inversion in 1.14.0-canary allows background to borrow interactive burst credits under certain attach sequences.\xe2\x80\x9d\\n- Updated after deeper review (2026-03-13 to 2026-03-20):\\n  - There are TWO overlapping issues:\\n    (A) Rate-limiter burst-pool attach race (real) that can temporarily mis-attribute available burst credits across pools.\\n    (B) Edge proxy early-return path under backpressure causing missing X-RateLimit headers and (worse) collapsing distinct upstream 429 reasons into a single opaque 429 surface.\\n  - The attach race (A) increases the probability that interactive tokens are temporarily depleted during the exact window when callback fanout is max.\\n  - The edge behavior (B) makes it look like \xe2\x80\x9crandom 429s\xe2\x80\x9d and prevents the customer from applying correct retry/backoff logic.\\n\\nRoot cause (current best explanation)", "score": 0.574485, "rank": 6, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "4eecf490-a4a3-586f-bb21-48209953879c", "search_unit_id": "4eecf490-a4a3-586f-bb21-48209953879c", "block_from_id": "0a02dc26-a711-542c-b9e7-3f18dfaa1d2b", "block_to_id": "2a40507b-21a0-5d8e-9a1c-e7e43a5f312e", "section_path": "Sup 3812 Northwind Dedicated Latency Spike During Interview Window", "document_id": "29cff449-edef-5f43-8bdf-540f01e450ce", "document_version_id": "13b1791c-7c70-53fe-bbcc-1a5e8649a6e6", "document_name": "jira__dsid_0c504258cd0c432d898e6711fb90eaf6.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h1-0", "heading": "Sup 3812 Northwind Dedicated Latency Spike During Interview Window", "content": "Sup 3812 Northwind Dedicated Latency Spike During Interview Window\\n\\nSource type: jira\\nDocument ID: dsid_0c504258cd0c432d898e6711fb90eaf6\\nRelease: v1.0.0\\nBenchmark: EnterpriseRAG-Bench synthetic upstream data\\nNorthwind Commerce (Dedicated): transient p95 latency spike during case study measurement/interview window\\n\\nIssue summary\\n\\n- Northwind Commerce noticed an elevated p95 latency window on their Dedicated cluster while Marketing/SE were collecting performance numbers for a planned public case study.\\n- The spike was transient (~18 minutes) but would materially change any \\"single-window\\" p95 figure if captured during that interval.\\n\\nCustomer impact\\n\\n- Endpoint: /v1/chat.completions (streaming + non-streaming)\\n- Impact: p95 increased from baseline ~430\xe2\x80\x93520ms to ~1.6\xe2\x80\x932.1s; p99 peaked ~3.8s.\\n- Error rate: no sustained 5xx increase; brief uptick in 429s due to queue protection (max in-flight).\\n- User-visible symptoms: slower first-token latency on some requests; a few retries observed by Northwind.\\n\\nContext (why ticket was opened)\\n\\n- This was surfaced during case study preparation (interview + metric capture). We need to:\\n  1) confirm whether the spike was a platform issue vs workload change,\\n  2) ensure mitigations prevent recurrence during future measurement windows,\\n  3) document the event so published metrics are measured over an appropriate window (e.g., 7\xe2\x80\x9330 days) and/or exclude known maintenance events.\\n\\nTimeline (UTC)\\n\\n- 2025-10-09 16:42: Northwind reports \\"latency feels off\\" in Slack via CSM; SE flags risk to published p95.\\n- 16:46: Support opens SUP-3812; on-call begins triage.\\n- 16:50\xe2\x80\x9317:08: p95 elevated; queue depth rises; autoscaler events noted.\\n- 17:10: Tail latency returns to baseline without customer changes.\\n- 17:35: Initial hypothesis shared with SE/Marketing; agree to avoid quoting that window.\\n\\nObservability / dashboards\\n\\n- Dedicated overview (Grafana): https://grafana.redwoodinference.com/d/ded-nw/northwind-dedicated-overview?from=2025-10-09T16:30:00Z&to=2025-10-09T18:00:00Z\\n- API p95 by route (Datadog): https://app.datadoghq.com/dashboard/3xq-9qk-7eu/northwind-api-latency?from_ts=1760027400000&to_ts=1760032800000\\n- Scheduler queue depth: https://grafana.redwoodinference.com/d/sched-q/scheduler-queue-depth?var=cluster=northwind-dedicated-us-east\\n- Node events (maintenance/drain): https://grafana.redwoodinference.com/d/k8s-ev/k8s-events?var=namespace=nw-dedicated\\n\\nWhat we checked\\n\\n- Workload: No evidence of a step-function traffic increase from Northwind; request rate remained within typical burst bounds (+8% vs same weekday baseline).\\n- Model/version: No model rollout; pinned model version unchanged.\\n- Platform: K8s events showed a single GPU node drain/replace in the Dedicated pool coincident with the spike.\\n\\nInvestigation notes (most likely cause)\\n\\n- Root cause: transient capacity reduction during a node drain (kernel security patch) combined with conservative surge settings for the deployment, leading to fewer ready replicas for ~10\xe2\x80\x9315 minutes.\\n- Contributing factors:\\n  - Warm-up for the replacement node took longer than expected (image pull + cache warm), increasing request queue depth.\\n  - Autoscaler reacted, but scale-up lag (GPU provisioning) meant tail latency increased before capacity stabilized.\\n\\nMitigation applied\\n\\n- Short-term (same day):\\n  - Increased min_replicas for Northwind Dedicated deployment from 6 -> 8 during business hours.\\n  - Adjusted pod disruption budget to reduce simultaneous unready capacity during drains.\\n  - Coordinated with Infra to pause non-urgent maintenance on Northwind\xe2\x80\x99s Dedicated pool during the case study measurement week.\\n- Follow-up hardening:\\n  - Added an alert on \\"ready replicas < desired\\" + queue depth correlation for Dedicated clusters.\\n  - Documented recommended measurement window guidance for Marketing/SE: use rolling 7-day p95 (excluding maintenance/drain events) or present a range with a footnote.\\n\\nCustomer comms", "score": 0.559193, "rank": 7, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "829619c6-5bb2-5336-b599-41394e4d318e", "search_unit_id": "829619c6-5bb2-5336-b599-41394e4d318e", "block_from_id": "14f1eecf-4873-5552-ac97-9814fbb2085d", "block_to_id": "25fdbf57-8bd8-5b95-81cc-c2f2996c61b9", "section_path": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Metrics Covered", "document_id": "c055d063-a555-5cdf-b541-fe036031cb1b", "document_version_id": "57c83493-8d9e-5467-903c-46c7977b91fb", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a3f94972391244f38ef773c05926af83.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-32", "heading": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Metrics Covered", "content": "Metrics Covered\\n\\nBaselines and regressions are computed for:\\n\\n- End-to-end latency: p50, p95, p99 (ms)\\n- Runtime-only latency: p50, p95, p99 (ms) (excludes network/client)\\n- Prefill latency and decode latency (ms)\\n- Queueing time (ms) (scheduler-level)\\n- Tokens/sec (overall, and decode tokens/sec where available)\\n- Batch size distribution (for attribution)\\n- KV cache hit rate (where applicable; informational at v1)", "score": 0.544489, "rank": 8, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "7bcbb5c3-7dbe-5883-b0d9-09dc2a1b9982", "search_unit_id": "7bcbb5c3-7dbe-5883-b0d9-09dc2a1b9982", "block_from_id": "416cc4ea-c623-5815-9be2-4828dd785b72", "block_to_id": "39c5304a-822e-5481-ba29-585b5ecfe142", "section_path": "Usage Based Pricing Legal Terms Playbook > Clause library (approved language) > C. Streaming responses \xe2\x80\x94 how to describe", "document_id": "45f6686b-12fc-5fae-9c11-9abaeb20a3e5", "document_version_id": "a4bad94b-544e-5a78-88ce-d2659b6c4f79", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_c52c609c0cdf4211a7e8c57bb1da72e4.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-47", "heading": "Usage Based Pricing Legal Terms Playbook > Clause library (approved language) > C. Streaming responses \xe2\x80\x94 how to describe", "content": "C. Streaming responses \xe2\x80\x94 how to describe\\n\\n**Approved clause (standard):**\\n\xe2\x80\x9cFor streaming responses, usage is measured based on tokens processed and reported by Redwood for the request. If a streaming connection is interrupted, Redwood may report usage for tokens processed up to the point of interruption.\xe2\x80\x9d\\n\\n**Negotiation notes:**\\n\\n- Avoid promising that interrupted streams are always free; we can grant credits case-by-case.", "score": 0.526234, "rank": 9, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "660e88bd-e521-55ea-bc15-65cd7f246e03", "search_unit_id": "660e88bd-e521-55ea-bc15-65cd7f246e03", "block_from_id": "0f40f801-80ed-5a24-8da9-6825990c7bd0", "block_to_id": "0f40f801-80ed-5a24-8da9-6825990c7bd0", "section_path": "Company Horizon Quay Ai Infrastructure", "document_id": "793f4cbe-a368-50ef-97dd-b2cdec25c3ce", "document_version_id": "610434b5-ade4-532e-b110-feed870ccba2", "document_name": "hubspot__dsid_03af1e44970d4d6db6e85bc2c5fce8de.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "list-2", "heading": "Company Horizon Quay Ai Infrastructure", "content": "- Profile: Horizon Quay is a global maritime logistics SaaS (port ops + scheduling). Heavy real-time chat+assistant for ops centers; embeddings for manifest search; reranking for ETA predictions.\\n- Capacity ask: Reserved pool sized for 8 sustained A100-equivalent GPUs per primary region (NA + EU). Expected steady-state: ~350\xe2\x80\x93600 RPS for short payload chat (avg token window 256). Burst to ~1k RPS during shift changes.\\n- Performance targets: P95 text-gen latency <= 80ms, P99 <= 220ms for chat on dedicated pool; throughput guarantees of 500 RPS sustained per region.\\n- SLA position (customer asks): 99.95% uptime across dedicated pools; 30% credit at 99.9\xe2\x80\x9399.95; 50% credit below 99.9; request for monthly credit cap and cumulative measurement window.\\n- Redwood counterproposals discussed: baseline 99.95 SLA with quarterly reconciliation; credit schedule tiered: 99.9\xe2\x80\x9399.95 = 10% service credit, 99.5\xe2\x80\x9399.9 = 30%, <99.5 = 60% (proposal to legal). Redwood wants max cap at 6 months\' fee.\\n- Maintenance windows: customer requires predictable maintenance windows not during shift-change windows (06:00\xe2\x80\x9309:00 UTC). Proposed weekly 02:00\xe2\x80\x9304:00 region-local maintenance with 72h advance notice; emergency maintenance with 48h notice and rolling failover.\\n- Support / escalation: Horizon Quay requires 24/7 hotline for Sev1 with 15-minute acknowledgement, 2-hour remediate SLA target for critical infrastructure, 4-hour for Sev2. Redwood proposes 15min ack, 1h initial response, remediation time objective to be defined per incident severity in SOW.\\n- Throughput guarantees & reserved GPU planning: plan includes capacity buffer for 1.5x expected steady-state; autoscaling burst into shared pool allowed with pre-authorized fallback; need explicit routing policy for latency-first vs cost-first (customer wants latency-first for ops-critical calls).\\n- Security/compliance: customer requires SOC2 Type II report, KMS integration for customer-managed keys, audit logs retained 12 months, option for EU-only residency for EU traffic.\\n- POC summary (3 weeks):\\n  * Week 0: onboarding, network peering, SSO test (SAML).\\n  * Week 1: model import + inference validation, baseline latency/throughput profiling.\\n  * Week 2: load testing to 1.2x forecast, KV/prefix cache tuning, batch tuning.\\n  * Week 3: SLA stress scenarios, failover drill, cost/perf report and recommended reserved sizing.\\n- POC status: onboarding complete; peering and SSO in staging done; load tests scheduled 2026-03-15. Initial latency targets met on small-scale tests; sustained throughput test pending.\\n- Procurement notes: finance wants committed capacity discount tiers and an option to convert to Dedicated Reserved on annual renewal. Legal flagged service credit language and liability cap.\\n- Quote history: pricing deck sent 2026-02-28 (drive link). Redwood gave two sizing options: 8x reserved (latency-priority) and 12x with burst (cost amortized). Horizon Quay prefers 8x + autoscale to shared.\\n- Direct quotes from stakeholders: \\"We cannot accept blackouts during port shift windows \xe2\x80\x94 any maintenance must be out of peak or have a clear failover plan.\\" \xe2\x80\x94 Head of Ops.\\n- Next steps (concrete): Redwood to produce SLA redline + SOW by 2026-03-17; schedule legal review call 2026-03-22; run scheduled load test 2026-03-15; finalize forecasted ARR and draft PO template after pricing revision.\\n- Internal AE reminders: capture agreed uptime measurement buckets, document monthly reporting cadence, confirm escrow/backup plan for on-prem fallbacks.", "score": 0.486677, "rank": 10, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "4d87ca2d-5ad6-5a3a-951e-e2b15f54aa63", "search_unit_id": "4d87ca2d-5ad6-5a3a-951e-e2b15f54aa63", "block_from_id": "602a47a2-0976-53f9-9b0b-1f572b45bf68", "block_to_id": "fb4cbd91-c76e-55fe-9ddc-d078b2ebb11f", "section_path": "1740726110 Rollout Split Mismatch Us East", "document_id": "40e64c2c-c3b1-53ab-af31-fa4991ca7697", "document_version_id": "dd2c254e-5b35-5a94-bb25-a57db2c82b03", "document_name": "slack__dsid_8ae540be8e4c458ca15e122040ea2418.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h1-0", "heading": "1740726110 Rollout Split Mismatch Us East", "content": "1740726110 Rollout Split Mismatch Us East\\n\\nSource type: slack\\nDocument ID: dsid_8ae540be8e4c458ca15e122040ea2418\\nRelease: v1.0.0\\nBenchmark: EnterpriseRAG-Bench synthetic upstream data\\nincidents\\n\\nIncident Bot: :rotating_light: *INCIDENT* (beta) - \\"rollout split mismatch\\" reported by Northstar in us-east. Expected canary=10%, observed ~35-45% over last 10m. Route: `hosted/chat/northstar-support-prod`. Rollout: `rlt_7f2c`.\\n\\nSean Gallagher: seeing the same in our cohort dashboard. us-west looks ~10% +/- noise, eu-central ~9-11%. us-east is way off.\\n\\nAisha Bello: pulling quick numbers from metrics.\\n`sum(rate(rw_infer_requests_total{route=\\"hosted/chat/northstar-support-prod\\",region=\\"us-east\\",cohort=\\"canary\\"}[5m])) / sum(rate(rw_infer_requests_total{route=\\"hosted/chat/northstar-support-prod\\",region=\\"us-east\\"}[5m]))`\\n= 0.39 right now.\\n\\nRafael Mendes: ack. is this impacting latency/error? customer-facing impact?\\n\\nSean Gallagher: error rate stable, but this is a safety issue if the new model is bad. We should treat as sev2-ish for beta. Let\'s contain.\\n\\nMina Haddad: on it. This is traffic-splitting enforcement in data plane. If it\xe2\x80\x99s only us-east, could be hash input mismatch on that cluster or a config propagation issue.\\n\\nRavi Menon: +1. First check: are we using the same `cohort_key` in us-east? It\xe2\x80\x99s supposed to be `tenant_id + route_id + stable_user_key` with fallback to request_id if missing.\\n\\nEmily Zhao: I can check the us-east router build / config digest. Might be running slightly different commit?\\n\\nVikram Singh: grabbing router logs for rlt_7f2c in us-east. any recent deploy?\\n\\nAisha Bello: latency p95 is slightly up on canary vs baseline (not huge): baseline 820ms, canary 910ms in us-east over 15m. errors flat.\\n\\nRafael Mendes: mitigation: can we pause rollout / force canary=0 in us-east only?\\n\\nHannah Rivera: orchestrator supports pause globally; region-scoped override is behind flag but I think Smart Routing has region weights per rollout? Mina confirm.\\n\\nMina Haddad: yes, routing primitive has per-region enforcement weights. Quickest is set canary weight to 0 in us-east (keep 10% elsewhere). Alternatively pause the rollout which sets all regions to baseline.\\n\\nSean Gallagher: do region 0% if safe, to minimize disruption. But only if we\xe2\x80\x99re confident it won\xe2\x80\x99t wedge state.\\n\\nElena Popov: I can push a config patch to set `regions.us-east.canary_pct=0` for rlt_7f2c. Will take ~2-3 mins to propagate.\\n\\nDylan Brooks: FYI Console shows rollout at step 10% with soak timer running. If we do region override, Console won\xe2\x80\x99t reflect it yet (known gap). Marissa: heads up.\\n\\nMarissa Cole: yep, understood. For beta customer safety > UI accuracy. We\xe2\x80\x99ll message Northstar through support channel.\\n\\nRafael Mendes: +1. Let\xe2\x80\x99s do it.\\nElena Popov: applying patch now.\\n\\nIncident Bot: Change requested by @Elena Popov: \\"Set rlt_7f2c canary_pct=0 in us-east\\" (config pipeline: `routing-prod`).\\n\\nVikram Singh: while that propagates, here\xe2\x80\x99s log sample from us-east router:\\n\\n```\\nrollout_id=rlt_7f2c route=hosted/chat/northstar-support-prod region=us-east\\ncohort_key_source=header:x-rw-user-key\\ncohort_key_present=false\\nfallback_source=ip\\nhash_seed=route_id_only\\nassigned=canary\\n```\\n\\nThat looks suspicious: `hash_seed=route_id_only`??? should include tenant_id at least.\\n\\nRavi Menon: whoa. That `hash_seed` field indicates the \\"v0\\" cohorting code path. That would skew based on NAT IP distribution too.\\n\\nEmily Zhao: checking build stamp in us-east: it\xe2\x80\x99s on `router@1.38.2` but others are `1.38.3`. us-east missed the rollout during last deploy window.\\n\\nMina Haddad: ok so we have version skew. 1.38.2 uses legacy cohorting fallback behavior (route-only seed when user key missing). That can absolutely produce mismatch.", "score": 0.484957, "rank": 11, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "77aeddcb-cd7b-5e17-b86a-007450d674de", "search_unit_id": "77aeddcb-cd7b-5e17-b86a-007450d674de", "block_from_id": "f7839eb8-e349-5145-a947-acb587ffe9d9", "block_to_id": "0286a2cd-6a4e-5c5b-8db3-2aa376a12271", "section_path": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Escalation > Paging and escalation triggers (when to page SRE / Infra) > Page SRE On-Call immediately when any of the following is true", "document_id": "2aced3e3-1ed9-50b2-9ef8-a037c40fcfaf", "document_version_id": "5b0b49bf-5c10-5f9e-b6f9-1ae199865977", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_409618a3958944f9b78362ed7e01bb33.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-38", "heading": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Escalation > Paging and escalation triggers (when to page SRE / Infra) > Page SRE On-Call immediately when any of the following is true", "content": "Paging and escalation triggers (when to page SRE / Infra)\\n\\nPage SRE On-Call immediately when any of the following is true\\n\\n- Premium tier customer: reported throughput below committed floor for **>10 minutes**, AND they confirm they are not client-throttled.\\n- Any tier: a Dedicated Throughput SLO **burn-rate alert** is firing for the deployment.\\n- Any tier: repeated or escalating customer timeouts/5xx correlated with throughput drop.\\n- Any tier: throughput drop correlates with recent Redwood changes (rollout, routing, driver/runtime update).", "score": 0.481689, "rank": 12, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "3f2bce47-f169-5eca-bf30-e0a6bc91727f", "search_unit_id": "3f2bce47-f169-5eca-bf30-e0a6bc91727f", "block_from_id": "49bbd8a8-51ed-592a-9f4e-751e9ba4fa8f", "block_to_id": "49bbd8a8-51ed-592a-9f4e-751e9ba4fa8f", "section_path": "Sup 28463 Double Charged For Retries", "document_id": "f32b26b3-6dd1-521a-8be7-4ece35353c67", "document_version_id": "87f5b416-505d-5ae8-8d73-fff770f45c01", "document_name": "jira__dsid_2f1f0358b85148f19d38f619891bfb15.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "p-12", "heading": "Sup 28463 Double Charged For Retries", "content": "2026-02-11 10:05 PT \xe2\x80\x94 Cole Summers (Billing Hooks) \xe2\x80\x94 PR pr-27463 merged to main. Change: do not finalize billable usage event for aborted streaming attempt if a retry/fallback is triggered and an idempotency key is present; instead, emit attempt record with billable=false and tie to retry_chain_id. Also tightened dedupe key (account_id + idempotency_key + operation + request_start_minute).\\n2026-02-12 16:48 PT \xe2\x80\x94 Aisha Bello (SRE/Observability) \xe2\x80\x94 Deployed to staging and ran shadow-billing compare: delta went from +6\xe2\x80\x9310% (old > new) on reproduced dataset to <0.3% expected variance. No increase in missing usage events observed.\\n2026-02-13 09:37 PT \xe2\x80\x94 Connor O\'Brien (Release) \xe2\x80\x94 Rolled out to prod us-east (10% -> 50% -> 100%) with shadow-billing enabled for 48h. Monitoring duplicate rate + drift dashboards; no alert triggers so far.\\n2026-02-14 12:20 PT \xe2\x80\x94 Cole Summers (Billing Hooks) \xe2\x80\x94 Quantification for Acme AI window 2026-02-06: estimated overstatement 7.9% of billed_tokens for streaming chat requests in us-east only. Scoped to retry/fallback path; non-streaming unaffected. Recommending Finance-approved credit; not doing retroactive invoice re-issue unless required. Looping in Emily Stone for credit guidance.\\n2026-02-14 15:05 PT \xe2\x80\x94 Emily Stone (RevRec/Accounting) \xe2\x80\x94 Approved: issue customer a one-time service credit equal to estimated overbilled amount for the impacted period, documented as \\"metering correction credit\\". Do not re-open posted invoices. Attach query evidence and PR references to the internal audit trail per playbook.\\n2026-02-15 09:12 PT \xe2\x80\x94 Monica Patel (Support) \xe2\x80\x94 Informed Acme AI: bug confirmed + fixed; credit will be applied on next invoice. Shared customer-safe explanation: duplicate token charge could occur when streaming request aborted and Redwood retried/fell back; fix ensures a single billable event per idempotency key and bills only the final successful stream. Customer acknowledged and asked for a short written note for their finance team.\\n2026-02-16 11:40 PT \xe2\x80\x94 Cole Summers (Billing Hooks) \xe2\x80\x94 Closing as resolved. Follow-ups: (1) ensure usage API exposes billed_tokens vs raw_tokens and retry attribution consistently (tracked in related PRs), (2) add alert on duplicate billable events per idempotency key (owned by telemetry).\\nConfirmed double-counting of billed tokens on streaming retry/fallback path when idempotency keys were present. Fix shipped via pr-27463 with shadow-billing validation and perf-canary regression coverage. Customer remediation: Finance-approved service credit for estimated overbilled amount (no retroactive invoice re-issue).", "score": 0.458822, "rank": 13, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "c5b4a601-736f-5571-93cf-9f8d798aa176", "search_unit_id": "c5b4a601-736f-5571-93cf-9f8d798aa176", "block_from_id": "64b71eb2-acf0-5873-bd86-8196a12ccdb4", "block_to_id": "d6f32d2a-0056-5287-a4ad-bd0c859db1cb", "section_path": "Go Live Protocols Cross Org Communications Escalation 2027", "document_id": "c1cb92b0-f42c-5fdf-9753-22cf1f569b80", "document_version_id": "a96eb210-903b-56d1-a33c-9bd4347e8b15", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_9c8cb10d65324c03a8ededbd98c1b058.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h1-0", "heading": "Go Live Protocols Cross Org Communications Escalation 2027", "content": "Go Live Protocols Cross Org Communications Escalation 2027\\n\\nSource type: confluence\\nDocument ID: dsid_9c8cb10d65324c03a8ededbd98c1b058\\nRelease: v1.0.0\\nBenchmark: EnterpriseRAG-Bench synthetic upstream data\\nGo-Live Protocols: Cross-Org Communications & Escalation\\nSummary\\n-------\\nThis document defines the cross-organizational go-live protocol used by Redwood Inference for enterprise tenant cutovers. It focuses on communications, stakeholder coordination, and an escalation ladder tailored for high-risk or high-visibility customers. Use this runbook as the canonical checklist for the 72 hours before, during, and 72 hours after a cutover where multiple teams (Customer Success, SRE, Solutions Engineering, Product, and Sales) must be coordinated.\\n\\nScope\\n-----\\n\\n- Applies to: Dedicated and Private deployment go-lives and tenant migrations with SLA commitments (any customer with an enterprise contract or > $50k monthly spend).\\n- Out of scope: self-serve trial accounts, routine model-only updates without routing changes.\\n\\nGoals and SLOs to Protect\\n-------------------------\\n\\n- Availability SLA target to protect during cutover: 99.9% (monthly) for production routes.\\n- Immediate remediation thresholds (trigger escalation):\\n  - Error rate spike: sustained >5% errors for 5 minutes across tenant endpoints.\\n  - Latency spike: P95 latency >2000ms for 10 minutes.\\n  - Token cost anomaly: >200% of projected token spend in 30 minutes.\\n  - KV cache miss-rate >30% sustained for 15 minutes (for cache-sensitive workloads).\\n\\nPre-Cutover (T-72 to T-1 hours)\\n-------------------------------\\n\\n1. Confirm stakeholders and channels\\n   - Primary CSM (Customer Success Manager): name + mobile number.\\n   - Tech lead (Customer): contact + escalation path.\\n   - Solution Engineer (SE): responsible for ingress, API contract verification.\\n   - Oncall SRE/Platform owner: PagerDuty schedule and runbook owner.\\n   - Slack channels: #cs-<customer>-go-live (private channel), #oncall-redwood, #se-ops.\\n2. Telemetry sanity checks \xe2\x80\x94 must pass before proceeding:\\n   - Synthetic journey (happy path) passes end-to-end: <customer> -> Redwood API -> response < 1s.\\n   - PromQL checks: error_rate/instance < 0.005 for 10m; p95_latency_query < 1500ms.\\n3. Canary plan and acceptance criteria\\n   - Canary traffic: route 1% of production for 30 minutes with zero critical errors.\\n   - Acceptance: no P0/P1 incidents during canary and no regression in model quality metrics (if applicable).\\n4. Whitelist and firewall confirmations\\n   - Verify ingress IP ranges, customer egress rules, and TLS client certs.\\n\\nDuring Cutover (T0 to +4 hours) \xe2\x80\x94 Immediate Checklist\\n----------------------------------------------------\\n\\n- Primary communications protocol:\\n  - All updates posted to #cs-<customer>-go-live every 10 minutes during the first hour, then every 30 minutes until stable.\\n  - The CSM posts customer-facing status updates; technical details posted to private channel only.\\n- Live monitoring dashboard (owner: Oncall SRE):\\n  - Tenant-specific dashboard showing throughput, p50/p95/p99 latency, error rate, KV cache hits, token consumption.\\n- Escalation triggers (first responder must escalate within 5 minutes if triggered):\\n  1) P0: service unavailable for customer routes \xe2\x80\x94 immediate page to P1 oncall and notify Engineering Director.\\n  2) P1: sustained SLO breach (see thresholds above) \xe2\x80\x94 page SE and PM, start RCA and mitigation steps.\\n  3) P2: model quality regression detected \xe2\x80\x94 notify Applied ML and SE; consider traffic switch to verified model snapshot.\\n\\nEscalation Ladder (contact order and responsibilities)\\n----------------------------------------------------", "score": 0.433919, "rank": 14, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "9e33e5e2-7d10-5e0a-884e-75f1afefb84a", "search_unit_id": "9e33e5e2-7d10-5e0a-884e-75f1afefb84a", "block_from_id": "774947be-8537-52f8-81af-2dcd815df5ab", "block_to_id": "e70fa823-90fe-5ba9-b899-43a9acb1573b", "section_path": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Definition > Canonical SLI and SLO > Canonical throughput metric (SLI)", "document_id": "19c5a655-72be-5ec3-9113-0a8f67b72c9f", "document_version_id": "2e39e4c6-4649-5fd6-9e3d-ebf763331245", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_e33c122264214846a8b7df7701d31482.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-13", "heading": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Definition > Canonical SLI and SLO > Canonical throughput metric (SLI)", "content": "Canonical SLI and SLO\\n\\nCanonical throughput metric (SLI)\\n\\n**SLI name:** Dedicated Throughput Floor Attainment\\n**SLI definition (per deployment):**\\nFor each `deployment_id`, compute the fraction of eligible 5-minute windows in which the **observed output token rate** meets or exceeds the committed floor.\\n\\nA 5-minute window is considered **good** if:\\n\\n- `observed_tps(deployment_id, window) >= committed_tps(deployment_id) * 1.00`\\n\\nA window is **bad** if:\\n\\n- `observed_tps(deployment_id, window) < committed_tps(deployment_id) * 1.00`\\n\\n**Why tokens/sec:** Token throughput is the most stable cross-model, cross-request indicator for generation routes and aligns with customer-perceived capacity for streaming/chat.", "score": 0.431907, "rank": 15, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "d618e705-cd98-5910-b62e-6c00f9d6341f", "search_unit_id": "d618e705-cd98-5910-b62e-6c00f9d6341f", "block_from_id": "fa48d691-c612-53c7-80f9-2bfc85ec0671", "block_to_id": "fa48d691-c612-53c7-80f9-2bfc85ec0671", "section_path": "Int 9821 Crucible Inference Latency Spike And 5Xx Inc 9821", "document_id": "2b07e75b-b8a4-5578-b4d1-5dad113848fe", "document_version_id": "62c69ecf-53d2-534c-96c8-261df54adfb3", "document_name": "jira__dsid_0c1f1c83e8d44d2fa3a9f6c7b2d7b9f1.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "p-7", "heading": "Int 9821 Crucible Inference Latency Spike And 5Xx Inc 9821", "content": "Impact (finalized): peak 5xx rate revised to ~6\xe2\x80\x938% (varied by minute) with ~10\xe2\x80\x9312 minute period of elevated errors; throughput dipped to ~60% of baseline at trough. No evidence of data integrity or security issues.\\n2026-08-28 (Stephanie Nguyen): Forwarded urgent report from Crucible. Asks: infra/serving triage immediately; CS to own comms + schedule exec sync 09:00 PDT; finance to pre-approve priority burst capacity if needed. Customer attachments: request_logs_20260828.zip, detailed_kpi_snapshot.csv.\\n2026-08-28 (Marcus Lin): Initial hypotheses: autoscaler oscillation under sudden burst + failing shard causing head-of-line blocking. Immediate actions: emergency scale dedicated pool (+20%), temporarily increase max concurrency, route new requests to secondary model variant (lower context), isolate/drain problematic instance(s). ETA for measurable improvement: ~8\xe2\x80\x9312 minutes after scale. Will update INC-9821.\\n2026-08-28 (Hannah Schmitt): On comms. Proposed external update: mitigation in flight; expect improvement within ~10 minutes; reconvene 09:00 PDT. Requested temporary SLA exception window + demo support. Asked finance to greenlight burst billing pending postmortem.\\n2026-08-28 (Stephanie Nguyen -> customer): Sent external update: emergency scaling, drain suspected degraded instance, fallback routing enabled. Set expectation of measurable improvement in 8\xe2\x80\x9312 minutes and 09:00 PDT exec sync.\\n2026-08-29 (Marcus Lin): Investigation summary shared internally/with customer on thread: reverted autoscaler policy update; added cooldown to prevent oscillation; drained/reprovisioned affected instance; hotfix to prevent recurrence. Impact noted as ~8% peak 5xx and ~10 minute window. Action items: customer postmortem by EOD; harden autoscaler testing + canary for autoscaler changes; product routing knob for rapid cross-model fallback; finance confirm priority-burst billing terms.\\n2026-09-01 (Marcus Lin): Correction after deeper node telemetry review: primary node issue was intermittent driver/kernel launch stalls (no sustained OOM). Updated remediation: rolled new base image + added driver health-check gate to remove degraded nodes faster; autoscaler change now includes 30m canary + enforced cooldown floor. Linked PRs: serving-runtime#18422 (health gate + faster eviction), infra-autoscaler#771 (cooldown floor + canary checklist).\\n2026-09-03 (Stephanie Nguyen): Closed internal ticket after customer postmortem delivered and follow-up technical review scheduled for next week. Finance confirmed no additional burst-capacity charges for incident window; goodwill credit handled via CS.", "score": 0.370908, "rank": 16, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "08b652e7-ed1b-5af6-a301-e8adca25ae33", "search_unit_id": "08b652e7-ed1b-5af6-a301-e8adca25ae33", "block_from_id": "d419ba2c-2b99-503d-8e8e-b7f396413c52", "block_to_id": "58651ba9-2cf7-5cb4-82ec-89419329e4e9", "section_path": "20260828 Crucible Inference Sitrep", "document_id": "f3f0dff2-64f2-519c-8ff2-bb1bb02e1099", "document_version_id": "738452de-9c17-5e45-975f-409192701b08", "document_name": "gmail__dsid_5611f05242ae4e6b9ae68d3c3e91bd54.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "p-22", "heading": "20260828 Crucible Inference Sitrep", "content": "Attachment: scaling_action_plan.md (ops stub)\\n\xe2\x80\x94 Marcus Lin\\nPrincipal Engineer, Serving Runtime\\nmarcus_lin@redwood.com\\n\\n> On Aug 28, 2026, at 08:25, Stephanie Nguyen <stephanie_nguyen@redwood.com> wrote:\\n> > Team \xe2\x80\x94 forwarding urgent customer report from Crucible Health (Julia).\\n\\nFrom: Hannah Schmitt <hannah_schmitt@redwood.com>\\nTo: Stephanie Nguyen <stephanie_nguyen@redwood.com>, Marcus Lin <marcus_lin@redwood.com>, Laura Bennett <laura_bennett@redwood.com>\\nCc: Sergio Alvarez <sergio_alvarez@redwood.com>\\nDate: 2026-08-28T08:40:00-07:00\\nSubject: Re: Fwd: Critical: sudden latency spike and 500s on inference endpoints\\n\\nThanks \xe2\x80\x94 I\'m on comms. Stephanie, please send the short external status below by 08:50 PDT. I\'ll run the 09:00 exec sync and include Marcus for the technical update.\\n\\nProposed external update to send to Julia (short):\\nHi Julia \xe2\x80\x94 we see the spike and have enacted emergency scaling and a fallback routing policy. We expect measurable latency reduction within ~10 minutes and will reconvene with a full status at 09:00 PDT. Apologies for the disruption; prioritizing now.\\n\\nWe should open a temporary SLA exception window and offer demo support. Laura, can we greenlight priority burst capacity billing for this account pending postmortem?\\n\\n\xe2\x80\x94 Hannah Schmitt\\nHead of Customer Success\\nhannah_schmitt@redwood.com\\n\\n> On Aug 28, 2026, at 08:30, Marcus Lin <marcus_lin@redwood.com> wrote:\\n> > Looking at the log snippet and metrics in the linked Drive...\\n\\nFrom: Stephanie Nguyen <stephanie_nguyen@redwood.com>\\nTo: Julia Adams <julia@cruciblehealth.com>, Ravi Patel <ravi@cruciblehealth.com>\\nCc: Marcus Lin <marcus_lin@redwood.com>, Hannah Schmitt <hannah_schmitt@redwood.com>, Laura Bennett <laura_bennett@redwood.com>\\nDate: 2026-08-28T08:50:00-07:00\\nSubject: Update: Redwood is actively mitigating the latency issue (ETA ~10m)\\n\\nHi Julia / Ravi \xe2\x80\x94 quick update from Redwood:\\n\\n- We see the anomaly and have enacted emergency scaling for your dedicated pool; SRE is draining a suspected degraded instance.\\n- We have enabled a fallback routing policy that will send new traffic to a compatible model variant with lower context length to reduce tail latency.\\n- Marcus estimates 8\xe2\x80\x9312 minutes for a measurable improvement. We will join a 09:00 PDT exec sync to share full context and next steps.\\n\\nWe appreciate the heads-up \xe2\x80\x94 we\'re treating this as high priority. I\'ll be on the 09:00 sync and will keep you updated in the thread.\\n\\nAttachments: request_logs_20260828.zip (we received your logs and propagated to ops)\\n\\nBest,\\nStephanie Nguyen\\nAccount Executive, Redwood Inference\\nstephanie_nguyen@redwood.com\\n\\n> On Aug 28, 2026, at 08:12, Julia Adams <julia@cruciblehealth.com> wrote:\\n> > We started seeing a large increase in end-to-end latency...\\n\\nFrom: Julia Adams <julia@cruciblehealth.com>\\nTo: Stephanie Nguyen <stephanie_nguyen@redwood.com>, Hannah Schmitt <hannah_schmitt@redwood.com>\\nDate: 2026-08-28T09:05:00-07:00\\nSubject: Re: Update: Redwood is actively mitigating the latency issue (ETA ~10m)\\n\\nThanks Stephanie \xe2\x80\x94 appreciate the fast response. We\'ll stand by for the 09:00 PDT update and let our demo team know the mitigations are in flight.\\n\\nJulia\\nHead of Integrations, Crucible Health\\n\\n> On Aug 28, 2026, at 08:50, Stephanie Nguyen <stephanie_nguyen@redwood.com> wrote:\\n> > Hi Julia / Ravi \xe2\x80\x94 quick update from Redwood...\\n\\nFrom: Marcus Lin <marcus_lin@redwood.com>\\nTo: Stephanie Nguyen <stephanie_nguyen@redwood.com>, Hannah Schmitt <hannah_schmitt@redwood.com>, Laura Bennett <laura_bennett@redwood.com>, Julia Adams <julia@cruciblehealth.com>\\nCc: Sergio Alvarez <sergio_alvarez@redwood.com>\\nDate: 2026-08-29T16:45:00-07:00\\nSubject: Incident update & postmortem summary \xe2\x80\x94 latency spike (INC-9821)\\n\\nAll \xe2\x80\x94 final update from the investigation and mitigation performed yesterday. Summary:", "score": 0.358412, "rank": 17, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "278c554f-2f3c-5117-8fca-18819f8ba7f9", "search_unit_id": "278c554f-2f3c-5117-8fca-18819f8ba7f9", "block_from_id": "3326fd7c-938f-5b5e-a1e1-fb9f594e1119", "block_to_id": "5634704f-f1ed-5d72-aca3-7d8230fd7429", "section_path": "Postmortem Perf Canary False Positive Alert Storm > Impact > Customer impact", "document_id": "020f768f-b114-5ca7-94ee-64447a4e421c", "document_version_id": "aac8c399-c8c4-5179-91b0-c93e8328a769", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_58bdb9f3bb4241e089f4f2b102765db4.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-5", "heading": "Postmortem Perf Canary False Positive Alert Storm > Impact > Customer impact", "content": "Impact\\n\\nCustomer impact\\n\\n- **None confirmed.** Runtime and customer SLO dashboards did not show correlated sustained p95 latency increases for customer traffic.", "score": 0.35809, "rank": 18, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "880290d9-359d-5970-8535-74a961750bfc", "search_unit_id": "880290d9-359d-5970-8535-74a961750bfc", "block_from_id": "9c83414f-d43d-5fd0-8992-947ec68d5b35", "block_to_id": "8fc5133a-72ef-580b-a4f0-4989cec0950c", "section_path": "Burst Capacity Retro Notes", "document_id": "055d3522-4e6b-5ab7-a0f7-0224afbd508e", "document_version_id": "f52eceb0-167c-5e90-84d2-0bc653ae49c8", "document_name": "google_drive__dsid_95c7aa5238f94570a8e0a8684736b666.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "list-39", "heading": "Burst Capacity Retro Notes", "content": "- Total burst attempts: dominated by 2 tenants with scheduled batch jobs.\\n- Denial rate: ranged 0\xe2\x80\x9318% by tenant depending on time-of-day; spikes correlated with telemetry lag and cooldown behavior.\\n- Admission controller p99: stable in us-east; spiky in eu-west (timeouts).\\n- Neighbor impact: one validated event (SUP-28455) where non-burst p99 regressed ~12\xe2\x80\x9318% for ~20 minutes before mitigation.\\n- Billing reconciliation: identified retry double-counting bug; now fixed but we need ongoing automated checks.\\n\\nWhat we changed during beta (already merged or in progress)\\n\\n- Telemetry smoothing / prevent starvation under lag: GitHub:redwood/pr-23944\\n- Global circuit breaker for burst pools: GitHub:redwood/pr-23919\\n- SLO-based auto-gating (reduce/disable burst on non-burst regression): GitHub:redwood/pr-23988\\n- Billing idempotency + prevent double-counting: GitHub:redwood/pr-24108\\n\\nGA readiness action items (must do)\\nP0 (blockers)\\n1) Telemetry freshness as a first-class signal\\n\\n- Add metric + alert: \xe2\x80\x9ctelemetry freshness seconds\xe2\x80\x9d by region/hardware + admission controller decision mode (fresh vs degraded).\\n- In degraded mode: enforce safer decisions (lower caps) + include explicit denial reason \xe2\x80\x9cTELEMETRY_STALE_DEGRADED_MODE\xe2\x80\x9d.\\n- Owner: Allison Grant + Logan Wright\\n- Target: by 2025-06-07\\n\\n2) EU-West admission controller latency hardening\\n\\n- Bound dependency calls with strict timeouts; add cached snapshots; add load shedding in controller.\\n- Define routing behavior when controller is slow: deterministic fallback decision + do not hammer controller.\\n- Owner: Jordan Lee + Mina Haddad\\n- Target: by 2025-06-10\\n\\n3) Retry guidance + \xe2\x80\x9cRetry-After\xe2\x80\x9d semantics tied to reason codes\\n\\n- Standardize: which denials are immediate (cap reached) vs transient (pool saturated) and include a backoff hint.\\n- Update docs + support macros.\\n- Owner: Peter Holtz + Kira Thompson\\n- Target: by 2025-06-12\\n\\n4) Billing reconciliation automation\\n\\n- Nightly job: compare metering export vs dashboard aggregates within tolerance; page billing on drift > X%.\\n- Owner: Nadia Rahman + Cole Summers\\n- Target: by 2025-06-12\\n\\nP1 (strongly recommended for GA)\\n5) Cooldown redesign proposal\\n\\n- Experiment with sustained-threshold cooldown vs proportional cooldown.\\n- Add simulation + real-world replay tests.\\n- Owner: Keiko Yamamoto\\n- Target: proposal by 2025-06-14; implement behind flag if time.\\n\\n6) Neighbor impact circuit breaker thresholds\\n\\n- Define concrete thresholds: p95 delta, tail amplification, and error rate deltas for non-burst tenants.\\n- Ensure breaker annotations show \xe2\x80\x9cwhy\xe2\x80\x9d and which tenant(s) triggered.\\n- Owner: Sean Gallagher\\n- Target: 2025-06-14\\n\\n7) Priority tier fairness tests\\n\\n- Extend multi-tenant integration tests to include tier mixes; assert starvation bounds.\\n- Owner: Jordan Lee\\n- Target: 2025-06-15\\n\\n8) Console/config propagation SLO + fix\\n\\n- Set expectation: config changes visible within N minutes; instrument end-to-end propagation time.\\n- Owner: Chloe Martin\\n- Target: 2025-06-15\\n\\nOperational notes / runbook improvements\\n\\n- Add a \xe2\x80\x9cfirst 5 minutes\xe2\x80\x9d checklist for burst-related pages:\\n  - Check global circuit breaker status\\n  - Check telemetry freshness + telemetry lag panels\\n  - Check admit/deny by reason code\\n  - Check pool saturation + queue depth\\n  - Check non-burst tenant latency deltas\\n  - Decide: tighten per-tenant cap vs disable region/hardware allow list\\n- Ensure runbook has a customer comms template (what happened, what we did, what to expect next).\\n\\nOpen questions (need decision)\\n1) Queue vs immediate denial for pool saturation", "score": 0.348386, "rank": 19, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "5f87a7ec-6a84-5077-9637-43a2d538e29d", "search_unit_id": "5f87a7ec-6a84-5077-9637-43a2d538e29d", "block_from_id": "37cd6cb3-8c0a-50f7-ab06-abc75d1a1f54", "block_to_id": "bcde8b1d-1858-5d86-8ade-9c985f7b4164", "section_path": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > SLIs (what we measure) > 2) Latency SLI", "document_id": "12efb103-6e2e-5955-be3e-431b65ac2bca", "document_version_id": "78cfdc0b-41fe-5614-b19f-c7d639aaa33b", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_49fa5cfd95624dbeb6eaa535f9959470.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-32", "heading": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > SLIs (what we measure) > 2) Latency SLI", "content": "2) Latency SLI\\n\\nLatency is measured at the gateway edge (request receive to first byte / completion) and reported as:\\n\\n- **TTFB (time-to-first-byte)** for streaming endpoints.\\n- **E2E response time** for non-streaming.\\n\\nWe maintain distinct latency SLO targets per tier and route (see tables below).", "score": 0.584513, "rank": 20, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "d96a4c9a-c4f3-514a-8e5f-f194656bffc5", "search_unit_id": "d96a4c9a-c4f3-514a-8e5f-f194656bffc5", "block_from_id": "d2a7e8aa-5b93-5d40-af91-71ca51524293", "block_to_id": "eed600ac-cb08-5515-a9a0-b5944bc54dbf", "section_path": "Sup 18421 Enterprise 429 Spike After Priority Routing Rollout > Steps to reproduce (customer)", "document_id": "89c06a4c-9104-5c7e-b766-1b1af3c247dd", "document_version_id": "c6465792-6750-5999-b72c-4196eb71504b", "document_name": "jira__dsid_1980f45c3fb4455f943a2b030892ae90.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-12", "heading": "Sup 18421 Enterprise 429 Spike After Priority Routing Rollout > Steps to reproduce (customer)", "content": "Steps to reproduce (customer)\\n\\n1) Send ~800\xe2\x80\x931200 RPS bursts to /v1/chat/completions with streaming disabled.\\n2) Concurrently send embedding bursts (~2k RPS) during the same window.\\n3) Observe 429 rates rising from <0.2% to 6\xe2\x80\x9312% during bursts.", "score": 0.588096, "rank": 21, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "9d89662d-3914-5df3-b0c7-68c3e008f6fe", "search_unit_id": "9d89662d-3914-5df3-b0c7-68c3e008f6fe", "block_from_id": "d317fcfc-8ec5-5ce4-a3b4-ddaf4f730dce", "block_to_id": "4b794544-a5e2-5379-bfa8-fafab3c36f60", "section_path": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers", "document_id": "12efb103-6e2e-5955-be3e-431b65ac2bca", "document_version_id": "78cfdc0b-41fe-5614-b19f-c7d639aaa33b", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_49fa5cfd95624dbeb6eaa535f9959470.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h1-0", "heading": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers", "content": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers\\n\\nSource type: confluence\\nDocument ID: dsid_49fa5cfd95624dbeb6eaa535f9959470\\nRelease: v1.0.0\\nBenchmark: EnterpriseRAG-Bench synthetic upstream data\\nHosted API SLOs: Enterprise Route Tiers (Hot-route capacity protection)", "score": 0.575748, "rank": 22, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "027545ed-544d-59a4-ac84-be54d2a402a7", "search_unit_id": "027545ed-544d-59a4-ac84-be54d2a402a7", "block_from_id": "ec70ec29-2014-565d-af3f-a4647a389fab", "block_to_id": "199d7ca2-70e2-5a0c-bc52-0dbf99c4216f", "section_path": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Error budgets > Budget stop-the-line triggers", "document_id": "12efb103-6e2e-5955-be3e-431b65ac2bca", "document_version_id": "78cfdc0b-41fe-5614-b19f-c7d639aaa33b", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_49fa5cfd95624dbeb6eaa535f9959470.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-65", "heading": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Error budgets > Budget stop-the-line triggers", "content": "Budget stop-the-line triggers\\n\\nFor enterprise protected routes, any of the following triggers a \xe2\x80\x9cstop the line\xe2\x80\x9d review in the weekly reliability sync (or async in #eng-sre if urgent):\\n\\n- Availability error budget burn \xe2\x89\xa5 **50%** in 7 days\\n- p99 latency SLO violated for \xe2\x89\xa5 **2 hours** in a day (sustained)\\n- Overload shedding observed for enterprise traffic (non-zero sustained) outside planned game day / load test windows", "score": 0.569475, "rank": 23, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "58dc01cf-7b4b-5977-978a-bae4f559d671", "search_unit_id": "58dc01cf-7b4b-5977-978a-bae4f559d671", "block_from_id": "6dbc399d-d826-56ff-a253-455ff6822a28", "block_to_id": "3684c4d9-c2ae-55b6-bb87-e5caa82776e9", "section_path": "Sup 18421 Enterprise 429 Spike After Priority Routing Rollout > Investigation notes (internal)", "document_id": "89c06a4c-9104-5c7e-b766-1b1af3c247dd", "document_version_id": "c6465792-6750-5999-b72c-4196eb71504b", "document_name": "jira__dsid_1980f45c3fb4455f943a2b030892ae90.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-20", "heading": "Sup 18421 Enterprise 429 Spike After Priority Routing Rollout > Investigation notes (internal)", "content": "Investigation notes (internal)\\n\\nHypothesis: us-east protected-route capacity protection enabled for Proxima allowlisted accounts; per-route/per-tier admission control is active and may be gating bursts more aggressively than intended for this customer\xe2\x80\x99s contractual traffic shape.\\n\\nChecks performed:\\n\\n- Correlated spike with protection annotations in hot-route dashboard (SRE): overload protection activated for route-group \\"chat\\" + \\"embeddings\\" during a concurrent regional pressure event.\\n- Confirmed most 429s for this account carry reason=overload_protection_admission (not quota).\\n- Verified no corresponding 5xx spike; p99 latency for enterprise class remained within SLO during event, indicating protections are working for fleet health but customer\xe2\x80\x99s burst profile is being clipped.\\n\\nOpen questions\\n\\n- What is Proxima\xe2\x80\x99s contracted peak/burst behavior (RPS and token/s) for these routes?\\n- Is Proxima being routed to a stricter per-route budget due to route-group defaults?", "score": 0.556326, "rank": 24, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "f8d4590f-6d5f-56a1-8f95-17cb8cbf70d5", "search_unit_id": "f8d4590f-6d5f-56a1-8f95-17cb8cbf70d5", "block_from_id": "bf91ceec-bcda-59fd-b0ab-92bc9bb46071", "block_to_id": "e560db75-82d2-5766-a424-9cefdf581a82", "section_path": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Purpose", "document_id": "12efb103-6e2e-5955-be3e-431b65ac2bca", "document_version_id": "78cfdc0b-41fe-5614-b19f-c7d639aaa33b", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_49fa5cfd95624dbeb6eaa535f9959470.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-2", "heading": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Purpose", "content": "Purpose\\n\\nThis document defines **per-route SLOs and error budget policy** for Redwood Inference\xe2\x80\x99s **Hosted API** as it relates to **hot-route capacity protection** (tier-aware quotas, priority routing, and controlled shedding).\\n\\nThe intent is to:\\n\\n- Explicitly define **enterprise vs best-effort** expectations for the highest-traffic routes.\\n- Make overload behavior **measurable and supportable** (clear SLIs + dashboards + alerts).\\n- Align alerting to overload protection so we page on **enterprise degradation**, not expected best-effort shedding.", "score": 0.55612, "rank": 25, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "0e9be2a7-c39f-5762-b7c6-a08b499e3bfc", "search_unit_id": "0e9be2a7-c39f-5762-b7c6-a08b499e3bfc", "block_from_id": "e2679d07-03c8-5704-a619-95bfdee31b55", "block_to_id": "40ac20d9-b556-5a2e-9dbc-7b20581770ee", "section_path": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Scope", "document_id": "12efb103-6e2e-5955-be3e-431b65ac2bca", "document_version_id": "78cfdc0b-41fe-5614-b19f-c7d639aaa33b", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_49fa5cfd95624dbeb6eaa535f9959470.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-6", "heading": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Scope", "content": "Scope\\n\\n**In scope**\\n\\n- Hosted API routes: `/v1/chat/completions`, `/v1/embeddings`, `/v1/rerank`, `/v1/completions`.\\n- Tiered behavior: **Enterprise (Protected)** vs **Best-effort (Standard/Self-serve)**.\\n- SLOs for **availability**, **latency**, and **overload responses (429)**.\\n- Alert thresholds and error budget burn policy.\\n\\n**Out of scope**\\n\\n- Dedicated and Private SLOs (tracked separately).\\n- Model-quality or semantic correctness SLIs.", "score": 0.536492, "rank": 26, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "1bed7f84-10e9-5901-9e71-655b5a96d238", "search_unit_id": "1bed7f84-10e9-5901-9e71-655b5a96d238", "block_from_id": "de04aadd-8eb6-52aa-babb-9e97778ad93a", "block_to_id": "5fb120e4-395a-5358-8250-00d3116fd776", "section_path": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Alerting (paging + ticketing) > Enterprise latency alerts", "document_id": "12efb103-6e2e-5955-be3e-431b65ac2bca", "document_version_id": "78cfdc0b-41fe-5614-b19f-c7d639aaa33b", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_49fa5cfd95624dbeb6eaa535f9959470.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-74", "heading": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Alerting (paging + ticketing) > Enterprise latency alerts", "content": "Enterprise latency alerts\\n\\nFor each protected route x region:\\n\\n- **Page (Sev2):** p99 latency SLO violated for **15 minutes** AND admission control active (or queue depth high)\\n- **Ticket:** p95 latency SLO violated for **60 minutes**", "score": 0.535663, "rank": 27, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "cd49e8e6-bdc5-5c01-a8f0-37450d10a51b", "search_unit_id": "cd49e8e6-bdc5-5c01-a8f0-37450d10a51b", "block_from_id": "1f31c549-698e-5181-95cb-c16fcb8db805", "block_to_id": "01945f51-74a3-5691-9964-001d14b8dc4a", "section_path": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Definitions > Route tiers", "document_id": "12efb103-6e2e-5955-be3e-431b65ac2bca", "document_version_id": "78cfdc0b-41fe-5614-b19f-c7d639aaa33b", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_49fa5cfd95624dbeb6eaa535f9959470.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-11", "heading": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Definitions > Route tiers", "content": "Definitions\\n\\nRoute tiers\\n\\nWe classify requests into one of the following tiers at the gateway/policy engine:\\n\\n- **Enterprise (Protected):** SLA-bearing tenants and allowlisted enterprise accounts for protected routes. These requests receive priority admission and are protected during fleet pressure.\\n- **Best-effort:** all other traffic. Best-effort may be throttled earlier and more aggressively to preserve fleet health and enterprise budgets.", "score": 0.516865, "rank": 28, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "78666fbe-0b98-571c-820d-c25aeb5777d8", "search_unit_id": "78666fbe-0b98-571c-820d-c25aeb5777d8", "block_from_id": "3799b33b-76e4-542c-ba64-13c225825417", "block_to_id": "a690b4fe-539c-5274-aaf5-5aa5a3eec022", "section_path": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > Purpose", "document_id": "d1667e56-b0c3-5fb4-a769-9f13a8679e2f", "document_version_id": "a9986e4a-2cf0-5373-a739-d5611a7a824c", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_ca42a8f18d784c879c5a6fcfe3a484c0.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-2", "heading": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > Purpose", "content": "Purpose\\n\\nThis page helps Support/CS respond to customer reports of new \xe2\x80\x9coverload protection\xe2\x80\x9d errors (circuit breakers + load shedding) on Redwood Hosted API.\\n\\nGoals:\\n\\n- Explain what the customer is seeing (stable semantics vs ambiguous timeouts/5xx).\\n- Provide canonical retry/backoff guidance to prevent retry storms.\\n- Show how to interpret correlation IDs so Engineering can quickly trace requests.\\n- Define escalation criteria and the minimum information to collect.", "score": 0.511176, "rank": 29, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "f10ec658-a2c4-508e-b896-ff4b52f494be", "search_unit_id": "f10ec658-a2c4-508e-b896-ff4b52f494be", "block_from_id": "2b20b8b5-5dc5-568d-9de5-96449a47865b", "block_to_id": "f90024af-24e7-5a09-9f45-2619f274ab90", "section_path": "Sup 18421 Enterprise 429 Spike After Priority Routing Rollout > Initial support response (guidance provided)", "document_id": "89c06a4c-9104-5c7e-b766-1b1af3c247dd", "document_version_id": "c6465792-6750-5999-b72c-4196eb71504b", "document_name": "jira__dsid_1980f45c3fb4455f943a2b030892ae90.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-18", "heading": "Sup 18421 Enterprise 429 Spike After Priority Routing Rollout > Initial support response (guidance provided)", "content": "Initial support response (guidance provided)\\n\\n- Confirmed that the new system returns distinct reason codes and customers should key their retry/backoff behavior on:\\n  - \\"quota_exceeded\\" (respect quota windows; reduce steady-state rate)\\n  - \\"overload_protection_admission\\" (transient overload; respect Retry-After; use exponential backoff + jitter)\\n- Recommended changes:\\n  - Use exponential backoff with full jitter; cap concurrency; do not retry immediately.\\n  - Honor Retry-After; treat 429 as a signal to shed load client-side.", "score": 0.508646, "rank": 30, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "c00b44bb-1e2d-5032-98ac-eabb643e37ce", "search_unit_id": "c00b44bb-1e2d-5032-98ac-eabb643e37ce", "block_from_id": "43a0161c-c137-5e12-bf34-deb5614df57c", "block_to_id": "b07b5e8d-2bba-59b5-9b42-aa9ccbbe528b", "section_path": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > SLIs (what we measure) > 1) Availability SLI (server success)", "document_id": "12efb103-6e2e-5955-be3e-431b65ac2bca", "document_version_id": "78cfdc0b-41fe-5614-b19f-c7d639aaa33b", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_49fa5cfd95624dbeb6eaa535f9959470.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-26", "heading": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > SLIs (what we measure) > 1) Availability SLI (server success)", "content": "1) Availability SLI (server success)\\n\\n**Definition:** percentage of requests that return an eligible success response.\\n\\n- Success codes: `2xx` (and `3xx` if any internal redirects apply; typically none)\\n- Failures: `5xx`, `408`, `499` (client disconnect) are counted as failures unless explicitly excluded by route-specific rules below.\\n\\n**Tier handling of 429:**\\n\\n- **Enterprise:** `429` due to *overload shedding/admission control* counts as **unavailable** (because enterprise is protected; overload shed should be rare and indicates under-provisioning or misconfiguration).\\n- **Best-effort:** `429` due to overload shedding/admission control does **not** count as unavailable (it is expected behavior under pressure). Quota/rate-limit 429s are not counted as availability failures for either tier.\\n\\n**Note:** 429 classification must be based on the reason code emitted by gateway/policy engine, not inferred from rate.", "score": 0.497237, "rank": 31, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "a66ee084-511a-5b59-8f86-bcca1c76c679", "search_unit_id": "a66ee084-511a-5b59-8f86-bcca1c76c679", "block_from_id": "2e882f9f-c43a-52b8-b1d6-1b908759cc49", "block_to_id": "3faf634a-ef9c-5adc-b883-d7fc93dbf320", "section_path": "Sup 18421 Enterprise 429 Spike After Priority Routing Rollout > Environment", "document_id": "89c06a4c-9104-5c7e-b766-1b1af3c247dd", "document_version_id": "c6465792-6750-5999-b72c-4196eb71504b", "document_name": "jira__dsid_1980f45c3fb4455f943a2b030892ae90.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-6", "heading": "Sup 18421 Enterprise 429 Spike After Priority Routing Rollout > Environment", "content": "Environment\\n\\n- Hosted API, prod\\n- Region: us-east\\n- Routes: /v1/chat/completions, /v1/embeddings\\n- Auth: enterprise API key (account: PROXIMA-ENT-014)", "score": 0.528724, "rank": 32, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "3ac0f03e-be13-5959-bc3b-21b5800a2fb3", "search_unit_id": "3ac0f03e-be13-5959-bc3b-21b5800a2fb3", "block_from_id": "467c8227-b91c-541b-8dd6-a586c95c651f", "block_to_id": "6cba830f-b454-5c55-88a5-9939d11008a7", "section_path": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Operational guidance > Change management", "document_id": "12efb103-6e2e-5955-be3e-431b65ac2bca", "document_version_id": "78cfdc0b-41fe-5614-b19f-c7d639aaa33b", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_49fa5cfd95624dbeb6eaa535f9959470.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-89", "heading": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Operational guidance > Change management", "content": "Change management\\n\\nChanges that affect enterprise SLOs require:\\n\\n- Feature flag + region canary\\n- Dashboard annotation\\n- Rollback plan\\n- Confirmed alert routing and runbook link in incident-bot message", "score": 0.497237, "rank": 33, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "160727a0-7a58-5eea-83bf-d0020ec58327", "search_unit_id": "160727a0-7a58-5eea-83bf-d0020ec58327", "block_from_id": "b621a115-2020-5f95-8ce9-f923147dc88d", "block_to_id": "cffcedec-bc33-5d20-83e5-cb0482e5dcea", "section_path": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Ownership", "document_id": "12efb103-6e2e-5955-be3e-431b65ac2bca", "document_version_id": "78cfdc0b-41fe-5614-b19f-c7d639aaa33b", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_49fa5cfd95624dbeb6eaa535f9959470.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-92", "heading": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Ownership", "content": "Ownership\\n\\n- SLO definitions and alert policy: **SRE (Rafael Mendes / Aisha Bello)**\\n- Metric correctness and tagging: **Telemetry (Logan Wright)**\\n- Enforcement behavior (429 reason codes, gateway semantics): **API Gateway (Amara Diallo)**\\n- Priority routing/admission control behavior: **Smart Routing (Mina Haddad)**", "score": 0.497237, "rank": 34, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "4a3acd45-1a9a-517b-a605-39ba12ab2180", "search_unit_id": "4a3acd45-1a9a-517b-a605-39ba12ab2180", "block_from_id": "17ce7afa-bed8-5956-941f-ef0f72e58b09", "block_to_id": "ffab9b77-5166-5149-8d05-5f6621fe0c55", "section_path": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Error budgets > Error budget policy (what we do when we burn)", "document_id": "12efb103-6e2e-5955-be3e-431b65ac2bca", "document_version_id": "78cfdc0b-41fe-5614-b19f-c7d639aaa33b", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_49fa5cfd95624dbeb6eaa535f9959470.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-60", "heading": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Error budgets > Error budget policy (what we do when we burn)", "content": "Error budget policy (what we do when we burn)\\n\\n**Enterprise (Protected) routes**\\n\\n- If burn is elevated, the default stance is **protect SLOs over throughput**.\\n- Allowed mitigations (in priority order):\\n  1) Tighten best-effort admission control thresholds (shed earlier)\\n  2) Temporarily reduce best-effort burst allowances\\n  3) Increase enterprise reserved capacity / pool weights (if available)\\n  4) Enable guarded fallback policies where contractually allowed\\n  5) As last resort: temporary enterprise throttling with exec/on-call approval and proactive comms\\n\\n**Best-effort routes**\\n\\n- Best-effort can be shed under pressure; we do not page purely on best-effort 429 shedding.\\n- If best-effort availability drops due to 5xx (not 429 overload-shed), we investigate as a normal reliability regression.", "score": 0.497237, "rank": 35, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "57b96fdd-0f5e-53b4-a310-daead3454531", "search_unit_id": "57b96fdd-0f5e-53b4-a310-daead3454531", "block_from_id": "0c3e2cf6-07f3-5d32-a0c7-a3ecaf18f79a", "block_to_id": "35a0efd4-14b1-5149-8872-366364ecc72c", "section_path": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > What changed (customer-facing)", "document_id": "d1667e56-b0c3-5fb4-a769-9f13a8679e2f", "document_version_id": "a9986e4a-2cf0-5373-a739-d5611a7a824c", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_ca42a8f18d784c879c5a6fcfe3a484c0.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-6", "heading": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > What changed (customer-facing)", "content": "What changed (customer-facing)\\n\\nWhen the platform is saturated (GPU capacity, scheduler backlog, downstream dependency brownout), Redwood may reject requests *early* and predictably rather than allowing them to time out or generate random 5xx.\\n\\nCustomers may now see:\\n\\n- **429** responses for admission / quota / per-tenant caps.\\n- **503** responses when a regional/model circuit breaker is open or the system is actively shedding load.\\n\\nThese responses:\\n\\n- Include stable **error codes** and a machine-readable JSON payload.\\n- Typically include a **Retry-After** header to indicate safe retry timing.\\n- Include Redwood request/correlation IDs for tracing.", "score": 0.493135, "rank": 36, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "ff3757e1-0f21-5dd7-aba5-64ae45c41dc1", "search_unit_id": "ff3757e1-0f21-5dd7-aba5-64ae45c41dc1", "block_from_id": "a3ec1d56-3a0f-5ebe-8586-0bd60428ded1", "block_to_id": "fce94957-09da-5f78-a49c-7776db4c356e", "section_path": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Error budgets > Error budget calculation", "document_id": "12efb103-6e2e-5955-be3e-431b65ac2bca", "document_version_id": "78cfdc0b-41fe-5614-b19f-c7d639aaa33b", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_49fa5cfd95624dbeb6eaa535f9959470.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-52", "heading": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Error budgets > Error budget calculation", "content": "Error budgets\\n\\nError budget calculation\\n\\nFor an SLO target **T** over a 28-day window:\\n\\n- Error budget = **1 \xe2\x88\x92 T**\\n\\nExamples:\\n\\n- 99.95% availability \xe2\x87\x92 0.05% error budget (\xe2\x89\x88 21.6 minutes of downtime equivalent over 28 days)\\n- 99.5% availability \xe2\x87\x92 0.5% error budget\\n\\nWe track budgets separately per:\\n\\n- route\\n- region\\n- tier", "score": 0.497237, "rank": 37, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "f678312e-2816-51f5-8a37-a4d28bc0d31e", "search_unit_id": "f678312e-2816-51f5-8a37-a4d28bc0d31e", "block_from_id": "bcb5e610-4b5c-5a54-91be-23fcb89c4f92", "block_to_id": "0127f5ba-b07b-5d07-8c63-e3fc5f61a574", "section_path": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Operational guidance > How to interpret 429s for SLOs", "document_id": "12efb103-6e2e-5955-be3e-431b65ac2bca", "document_version_id": "78cfdc0b-41fe-5614-b19f-c7d639aaa33b", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_49fa5cfd95624dbeb6eaa535f9959470.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-86", "heading": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Operational guidance > How to interpret 429s for SLOs", "content": "Operational guidance\\n\\nHow to interpret 429s for SLOs\\n\\n- **Enterprise 429 overload-shed:** treat as incident-worthy and investigate capacity signals, admission thresholds, route budgets, and pool weights.\\n- **Best-effort 429 overload-shed:** expected under pressure; use this to validate the protection is working (enterprise should remain stable).\\n- **Quota 429 (any tier):** not an SLO failure; route to Support/Solutions for policy adjustment if needed.", "score": 0.497237, "rank": 38, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "83ec62b5-f7e7-526f-8aac-42d5f516142e", "search_unit_id": "83ec62b5-f7e7-526f-8aac-42d5f516142e", "block_from_id": "50860677-0e07-5a22-b304-2a91252ff67f", "block_to_id": "dea4a6da-eac4-510f-a038-e3b04f86a397", "section_path": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Revision history", "document_id": "12efb103-6e2e-5955-be3e-431b65ac2bca", "document_version_id": "78cfdc0b-41fe-5614-b19f-c7d639aaa33b", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_49fa5cfd95624dbeb6eaa535f9959470.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-94", "heading": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Revision history", "content": "Revision history\\n\\n- 2025-12-10: Initial version drafted for hot-route protection MVP (enterprise vs best-effort split).\\n- 2026-01-12: Updated latency targets for embeddings after eu-west canary measurements.\\n- 2026-01-29: Finalized burn-rate thresholds and enterprise shed-rate paging criteria for GA.", "score": 0.497237, "rank": 39, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "e534946f-6b15-55fa-94d0-63dd0bfb990e", "search_unit_id": "e534946f-6b15-55fa-94d0-63dd0bfb990e", "block_from_id": "0b5ad9a5-e47d-5b04-a4f8-5f31762fdd88", "block_to_id": "4474e74d-d55e-50e0-a7ee-0d9ef36ee6b9", "section_path": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > SLIs (what we measure) > 3) Overload activation SLI (operational)", "document_id": "12efb103-6e2e-5955-be3e-431b65ac2bca", "document_version_id": "78cfdc0b-41fe-5614-b19f-c7d639aaa33b", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_49fa5cfd95624dbeb6eaa535f9959470.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-36", "heading": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > SLIs (what we measure) > 3) Overload activation SLI (operational)", "content": "3) Overload activation SLI (operational)\\n\\nNot a customer-facing SLO, but required for on-call safety.\\n\\nWe track:\\n\\n- **shed_rate** (fraction of requests rejected due to overload by tier)\\n- **admission_control_active** (boolean/ratio time in state)\\n- **queue_depth / scheduler_lag** for the backing pools\\n\\nThese drive alerts and annotations on the enterprise SLO dashboards.", "score": 0.497237, "rank": 40, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "d6786536-5f2f-5d06-98f8-5dfe2ab73f60", "search_unit_id": "d6786536-5f2f-5d06-98f8-5dfe2ab73f60", "block_from_id": "732d1e55-a761-52fa-9091-a660c7a4dd39", "block_to_id": "71060f8f-3d29-554f-87bf-4df097c47116", "section_path": "Sup 18421 Enterprise 429 Spike After Priority Routing Rollout > Expected behavior", "document_id": "89c06a4c-9104-5c7e-b766-1b1af3c247dd", "document_version_id": "c6465792-6750-5999-b72c-4196eb71504b", "document_name": "jira__dsid_1980f45c3fb4455f943a2b030892ae90.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-14", "heading": "Sup 18421 Enterprise 429 Spike After Priority Routing Rollout > Expected behavior", "content": "Expected behavior\\n\\n- Enterprise priority should protect them during fleet pressure; customer expects either stable acceptance or clearly defined, predictable throttling limits.", "score": 0.508646, "rank": 41, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "0e220222-b88b-5e64-9355-bb13bd6432ab", "search_unit_id": "0e220222-b88b-5e64-9355-bb13bd6432ab", "block_from_id": "1097e02f-bddd-5b59-800e-4e309a05ea34", "block_to_id": "00662f96-ca52-524b-95ed-582364c94065", "section_path": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Definitions > Overload / throttling semantics", "document_id": "12efb103-6e2e-5955-be3e-431b65ac2bca", "document_version_id": "78cfdc0b-41fe-5614-b19f-c7d639aaa33b", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_49fa5cfd95624dbeb6eaa535f9959470.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-20", "heading": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Definitions > Overload / throttling semantics", "content": "Overload / throttling semantics\\n\\nRequests may be rejected with HTTP **429** for:\\n\\n- **Quota/rate limit enforcement** (tenant policy)\\n- **Overload shedding/admission control** (fleet protection)\\n\\nWe distinguish causes via standardized reason code headers/payloads (see Support doc for exact fields). For SLO accounting, we treat quota-throttled and overload-shed requests differently by tier (see SLI rules below).", "score": 0.497237, "rank": 42, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "a3e54e4f-7341-58c6-b673-790f8dc189c5", "search_unit_id": "a3e54e4f-7341-58c6-b673-790f8dc189c5", "block_from_id": "3c3dbe92-f436-5016-b51c-d7546d9b8f00", "block_to_id": "85593ccd-fe4e-592c-acbf-78f8a7d921ad", "section_path": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Alerting (paging + ticketing) > Alert principles", "document_id": "12efb103-6e2e-5955-be3e-431b65ac2bca", "document_version_id": "78cfdc0b-41fe-5614-b19f-c7d639aaa33b", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_49fa5cfd95624dbeb6eaa535f9959470.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-68", "heading": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Alerting (paging + ticketing) > Alert principles", "content": "Alerting (paging + ticketing)\\n\\nAlert principles\\n\\n- Page on **enterprise** impact (availability/latency) and on conditions that predict imminent enterprise impact.\\n- Do not page on expected best-effort shedding unless it indicates misconfiguration or runaway protection (e.g., shedding at low utilization).\\n- Use multi-window burn rate to avoid flapping.", "score": 0.497237, "rank": 43, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "65608456-3a44-5f00-9233-749e94d694a7", "search_unit_id": "65608456-3a44-5f00-9233-749e94d694a7", "block_from_id": "ff85e2bc-47fa-56eb-9cb8-f7704dcc70fd", "block_to_id": "95bc4171-61c8-584a-b5a0-d1ed1b5fb0a8", "section_path": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Definitions > Protected routes", "document_id": "12efb103-6e2e-5955-be3e-431b65ac2bca", "document_version_id": "78cfdc0b-41fe-5614-b19f-c7d639aaa33b", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_49fa5cfd95624dbeb6eaa535f9959470.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-15", "heading": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Definitions > Protected routes", "content": "Protected routes\\n\\nProtected routes are selected based on:\\n\\n- Fleet-wide traffic concentration (hot routes)\\n- High enterprise SLO/SLA coupling\\n- Known overload blast radius\\n\\nCurrent protected route set:\\n\\n- `/v1/chat/completions` (includes streaming)\\n- `/v1/embeddings`\\n- `/v1/rerank`\\n- `/v1/completions` (legacy; still used by some enterprise SDKs)", "score": 0.497237, "rank": 44, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "42378ef4-57c2-5454-b9a7-8e90730f3c5e", "search_unit_id": "42378ef4-57c2-5454-b9a7-8e90730f3c5e", "block_from_id": "dfb0dcb7-7641-50f8-890e-1cca9cb70242", "block_to_id": "a73aed1b-1158-50c2-9629-fd346db666ff", "section_path": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > SLIs (what we measure)", "document_id": "12efb103-6e2e-5955-be3e-431b65ac2bca", "document_version_id": "78cfdc0b-41fe-5614-b19f-c7d639aaa33b", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_49fa5cfd95624dbeb6eaa535f9959470.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-24", "heading": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > SLIs (what we measure)", "content": "SLIs (what we measure)\\n\\nAll SLIs are computed per **route x region x tier** using the standard metrics set.", "score": 0.497237, "rank": 45, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "ea913aa2-74a9-5230-91bf-983d0d58c6d9", "search_unit_id": "ea913aa2-74a9-5230-91bf-983d0d58c6d9", "block_from_id": "ed7c7ef6-7291-59dd-8bb5-06c6afb34224", "block_to_id": "810265e3-ce3f-5313-9cf4-0a7e2f41513a", "section_path": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > SLOs (targets) > Availability SLOs by route and tier", "document_id": "12efb103-6e2e-5955-be3e-431b65ac2bca", "document_version_id": "78cfdc0b-41fe-5614-b19f-c7d639aaa33b", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_49fa5cfd95624dbeb6eaa535f9959470.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-44", "heading": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > SLOs (targets) > Availability SLOs by route and tier", "content": "Availability SLOs by route and tier\\n\\n| Route | Tier | Availability SLO | Notes |\\n|---|---:|---:|---|\\n| `/v1/chat/completions` | Enterprise (Protected) | 99.95% | 429 overload-shed counts as failure. Quota 429 excluded. |\\n| `/v1/chat/completions` | Best-effort | 99.5% | 429 overload-shed excluded from availability; still tracked separately. |\\n| `/v1/embeddings` | Enterprise (Protected) | 99.95% | Batch requests included. |\\n| `/v1/embeddings` | Best-effort | 99.5% | 429 overload-shed excluded. |\\n| `/v1/rerank` | Enterprise (Protected) | 99.9% | Lower traffic; still protected. |\\n| `/v1/rerank` | Best-effort | 99.5% | 429 overload-shed excluded. |\\n| `/v1/completions` | Enterprise (Protected) | 99.9% | Legacy route; fewer dependencies but included for enterprise compatibility. |\\n| `/v1/completions` | Best-effort | 99.0% | Best-effort only; deprecation path in progress. |", "score": 0.497237, "rank": 46, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "a494e024-71d0-5d97-9144-bc164ae7f865", "search_unit_id": "a494e024-71d0-5d97-9144-bc164ae7f865", "block_from_id": "003b87dc-2fa7-50de-bdd3-a58e90891f06", "block_to_id": "4275183e-19cc-5054-b98a-4c75a73a265f", "section_path": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Dashboard requirements (minimum)", "document_id": "12efb103-6e2e-5955-be3e-431b65ac2bca", "document_version_id": "78cfdc0b-41fe-5614-b19f-c7d639aaa33b", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_49fa5cfd95624dbeb6eaa535f9959470.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-82", "heading": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Dashboard requirements (minimum)", "content": "Dashboard requirements (minimum)\\n\\nEvery protected route must have:\\n\\n- Availability by tier (enterprise vs best-effort)\\n- Latency p95/p99 by tier (and TTFB for streaming)\\n- 429 rate split by reason code (quota vs overload)\\n- shed_rate and admission_control_active\\n- queue depth and scheduler lag for backing pools\\n- Annotations for config changes, feature flag toggles, fallback activation\\n\\nSee the dashboard spec for panel/query naming conventions.", "score": 0.497237, "rank": 47, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "708e5a9c-86f5-506e-8da1-295220bce219", "search_unit_id": "708e5a9c-86f5-506e-8da1-295220bce219", "block_from_id": "4161f767-6da3-52a1-88f6-d87da4574795", "block_to_id": "b9fa1aed-e707-55e1-8810-924abf1845c1", "section_path": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > Quick triage checklist (Support)", "document_id": "d1667e56-b0c3-5fb4-a769-9f13a8679e2f", "document_version_id": "a9986e4a-2cf0-5373-a739-d5611a7a824c", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_ca42a8f18d784c879c5a6fcfe3a484c0.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-12", "heading": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > Quick triage checklist (Support)", "content": "Quick triage checklist (Support)\\n\\n1) Confirm this is an overload-protection response (see \xe2\x80\x9cHow to recognize\xe2\x80\x9d below).\\n2) Verify the customer is following recommended retry/backoff (or advise them to change).\\n3) Collect required debug info (IDs, time window, region/model, response headers).\\n4) Determine if this is expected transient overload vs a bug/misconfig (missing Retry-After, incorrect status mapping, fallback not triggering).\\n5) Escalate to Eng Oncall when criteria are met (see \xe2\x80\x9cWhen to escalate\xe2\x80\x9d).", "score": 0.475093, "rank": 48, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "94f8473e-6fa7-5ca7-b28d-fd0d06dc1ff1", "search_unit_id": "94f8473e-6fa7-5ca7-b28d-fd0d06dc1ff1", "block_from_id": "5924c214-0cba-5898-8186-88e78be523d2", "block_to_id": "f7410cc1-70a0-522d-ae36-40ed819db9ab", "section_path": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Alerting (paging + ticketing) > Overload protection operational alerts", "document_id": "12efb103-6e2e-5955-be3e-431b65ac2bca", "document_version_id": "78cfdc0b-41fe-5614-b19f-c7d639aaa33b", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_49fa5cfd95624dbeb6eaa535f9959470.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-77", "heading": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Alerting (paging + ticketing) > Overload protection operational alerts", "content": "Overload protection operational alerts\\n\\nThese are predictive/guardrail alerts. Configure as:\\n\\n- **Page (Sev2):** enterprise overload shedding sustained\\n  - enterprise `shed_rate` > **0.1%** for **10 minutes** (any protected route)\\n- **Ticket:** best-effort shedding unexpectedly high at low utilization\\n  - best-effort `shed_rate` > **20%** for **15 minutes** AND GPU utilization < **65%** (indicates policy mis-tuning or signal errors)\\n- **Ticket:** queue depth / scheduler lag sustained without corresponding shedding\\n  - queue depth > threshold for **10 minutes** with `admission_control_active=false` (indicates missing trigger wiring)", "score": 0.497237, "rank": 49, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "32edec19-5981-5717-8440-1c0ecfedf3bd", "search_unit_id": "32edec19-5981-5717-8440-1c0ecfedf3bd", "block_from_id": "fb923860-bcb3-5033-81df-9e48baa2e320", "block_to_id": "001e456b-ee95-577b-ba26-27a8b5ab4208", "section_path": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Alerting (paging + ticketing) > Paging routing", "document_id": "12efb103-6e2e-5955-be3e-431b65ac2bca", "document_version_id": "78cfdc0b-41fe-5614-b19f-c7d639aaa33b", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_49fa5cfd95624dbeb6eaa535f9959470.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-80", "heading": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Alerting (paging + ticketing) > Paging routing", "content": "Paging routing\\n\\n- Primary: **#eng-oncall** and SRE pager rotation\\n- Secondary: **#eng-platform** for policy/gateway issues, **#eng-runtime** for scheduler lag/queue depth anomalies", "score": 0.497237, "rank": 50, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "9171dac8-c4bb-5bd4-9d28-a6e76a3c14be", "search_unit_id": "9171dac8-c4bb-5bd4-9d28-a6e76a3c14be", "block_from_id": "9d4bc5c5-5344-5e1a-905d-2c59ccf76f64", "block_to_id": "4afa2a28-c7ef-559a-874a-d4e15c21c1cd", "section_path": "Sup 864999 Sliced Ingest Priority Class Throttle Config", "document_id": "d9afbdee-1747-5299-966e-78fdaa3c1cf3", "document_version_id": "7d1ff66b-6a87-5e79-a4fb-df1cc60b4124", "document_name": "jira__dsid_5f3a672da4974781a5577b0f3d4993e9.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h1-0", "heading": "Sup 864999 Sliced Ingest Priority Class Throttle Config", "content": "Sup 864999 Sliced Ingest Priority Class Throttle Config\\n\\nSource type: jira\\nDocument ID: dsid_5f3a672da4974781a5577b0f3d4993e9\\nRelease: v1.0.0\\nBenchmark: EnterpriseRAG-Bench synthetic upstream data\\nSustained 429s during sliced ingest with priority class mismatch after dedicated pool migration\\n\\nIssue summary:\\nCustomer reports sustained 429 responses across a set of sliced ingest workers after migrating ingestion to a Dedicated pool. The traffic mix is: low-latency chat calls (priority=high) and large multi-part embedding uploads (priority=background). After the migration, background uploads are intermittently exhausting burst credits at the per-route layer and causing opaque 429s for high-priority short calls during peak windows.\\n\\nImpact:\\n\\n- ~30% of short chat requests returned 429 between 09:00-11:30 PDT on 2026-03-10 for Streamly AI.\\n- Customer reports user-facing errors and retry amplification in their orchestrator.\\n- Business impact: partial degradation of their real-time features during peak ingest windows.\\n\\nEnvironment:\\n\\n- Dedicated pool: dp-132-usw\\n- Region: us-west\\n- Ingest coordinator version: 2026.02.3\\n- Rate-limiter service version: 1.14.0-canary\\n\\nObserved behaviour:\\n\\n- Persistent 429s reported by customer for short-lived chat requests coincident with high concurrency uploads.\\n- Throttle headers (when present) show per-route-window exhaustion; some requests show no X-RateLimit headers (opaque 429).\\n\\nExpected behaviour:\\n\\n- High-priority interactive calls should be isolated into a small burst pool and not experience 429s while background uploads borrow burst credit from a separate pool (or be rate-limited gracefully).\\n\\nCustomer asks:\\n\\n- Why are small chat calls getting 429s when their load is relatively small?\\n- How to configure priority classes and burst pools for dedicated pools to prevent this?", "score": 0.487231, "rank": 51, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "3c29ae8f-333e-571e-99bd-aa3c7ff9aeab", "search_unit_id": "3c29ae8f-333e-571e-99bd-aa3c7ff9aeab", "block_from_id": "6342bbc2-b5e5-53b5-b00c-de7baf7de1a6", "block_to_id": "587fa100-5b3c-50ef-870a-e03488252f55", "section_path": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Status", "document_id": "c055d063-a555-5cdf-b541-fe036031cb1b", "document_version_id": "57c83493-8d9e-5467-903c-46c7977b91fb", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a3f94972391244f38ef773c05926af83.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-2", "heading": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Status", "content": "Status\\n\\nPublished.", "score": 0.462816, "rank": 52, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "d93d2bc8-b21e-5022-ab81-2171d5a9fc0a", "search_unit_id": "d93d2bc8-b21e-5022-ab81-2171d5a9fc0a", "block_from_id": "c63a6ebd-1b90-576f-a654-dab9c22a1640", "block_to_id": "2bf54639-15d2-5100-bd4f-2433409d5e0c", "section_path": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > Internal-only: where to look (for Support with access)", "document_id": "d1667e56-b0c3-5fb4-a769-9f13a8679e2f", "document_version_id": "a9986e4a-2cf0-5373-a739-d5611a7a824c", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_ca42a8f18d784c879c5a6fcfe3a484c0.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-78", "heading": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > Internal-only: where to look (for Support with access)", "content": "Internal-only: where to look (for Support with access)\\n\\n- Overload dashboards: shed rate, breaker-open rate, queue depth, saturation signals by region/model.\\n- Incident channels: #eng-oncall, #incidents (look for breaker announcements).\\n- Runbook: \xe2\x80\x9cHosted overload / circuit breaker runbook\xe2\x80\x9d (linked above).", "score": 0.457052, "rank": 53, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "139b27e1-b120-500b-8afb-187e49be95e0", "search_unit_id": "139b27e1-b120-500b-8afb-187e49be95e0", "block_from_id": "4d0b355d-cd97-5fc3-9b5a-3e0d47653a45", "block_to_id": "fc7e5142-2524-5128-96f5-869cfa6333e8", "section_path": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > Canonical mapping: status \xe2\x86\x92 meaning \xe2\x86\x92 what we tell customers", "document_id": "d1667e56-b0c3-5fb4-a769-9f13a8679e2f", "document_version_id": "a9986e4a-2cf0-5373-a739-d5611a7a824c", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_ca42a8f18d784c879c5a6fcfe3a484c0.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-26", "heading": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > Canonical mapping: status \xe2\x86\x92 meaning \xe2\x86\x92 what we tell customers", "content": "Canonical mapping: status \xe2\x86\x92 meaning \xe2\x86\x92 what we tell customers\\n\\nUse this table to drive consistent customer guidance.\\n\\n| HTTP | Typical meaning | What Support should say | Customer action |\\n|------|------------------|--------------------------|-----------------|\\n| 429 | Admission control / rate limiting / tier cap hit | \xe2\x80\x9cYour request was rejected to protect system stability. This is not a model failure; it indicates you are sending requests faster than the current allowed rate/concurrency.\xe2\x80\x9d | Respect Retry-After; implement exponential backoff + jitter; reduce concurrency; consider batching; if persistent, discuss tier/capacity options. |\\n| 503 | Regional/model breaker open or load shedding | \xe2\x80\x9cRedwood is temporarily overloaded in the requested region/model. We are rejecting early to avoid timeouts and protect overall reliability.\xe2\x80\x9d | Respect Retry-After; backoff + jitter; optionally route to another region/model if configured; reduce max_tokens/streaming concurrency; retry with bounded attempts. |\\n\\nNotes:\\n\\n- If the customer reports **timeouts** with no JSON error, treat as a separate issue (possible client/network or upstream timeouts) and escalate based on impact.\\n- If the customer gets **5xx** without the overload payload, collect details; it may be an unrelated incident.", "score": 0.457052, "rank": 54, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "cdbb6d08-8ba1-5356-89c6-5f1be0d7a0d6", "search_unit_id": "cdbb6d08-8ba1-5356-89c6-5f1be0d7a0d6", "block_from_id": "465c7502-6eb1-50f3-862d-75e9129549d1", "block_to_id": "05603d51-0610-5c82-a53a-43ee96ba9c3e", "section_path": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > Escalation template (copy/paste)", "document_id": "d1667e56-b0c3-5fb4-a769-9f13a8679e2f", "document_version_id": "a9986e4a-2cf0-5373-a739-d5611a7a824c", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_ca42a8f18d784c879c5a6fcfe3a484c0.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-75", "heading": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > Escalation template (copy/paste)", "content": "Escalation template (copy/paste)\\n\\nSubject: Hosted API overload errors \xe2\x80\x93 customer impact\\n\\n- Customer / tenant: \\n- Tier: (Self-serve Hosted / Enterprise Hosted / Dedicated)\\n- Primary route(s): (e.g., /v1/chat/completions)\\n- Model(s): \\n- Region(s): requested / observed\\n- Time window (with timezone): \\n- Error HTTP status: 429 / 503\\n- Error code(s) from body: \\n- Retry-After present? value(s): \\n- x-redwood-correlation-id(s): \\n- x-redwood-request-id(s): \\n- Customer retry behavior (describe): \\n- Business impact: (prod down / degraded / batch delay)\\n- Attachments: sample responses (headers + body), client logs", "score": 0.457052, "rank": 55, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "d0ba3a34-6d9c-5187-8982-318b01a053c0", "search_unit_id": "d0ba3a34-6d9c-5187-8982-318b01a053c0", "block_from_id": "54b2b35e-ddfa-55af-9885-fab6dcddb921", "block_to_id": "ce4aa47e-1831-5ec1-a44f-6b21166e2f56", "section_path": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > Common customer questions + approved responses > \xe2\x80\x9cIs Redwood down?\xe2\x80\x9d", "document_id": "d1667e56-b0c3-5fb4-a769-9f13a8679e2f", "document_version_id": "a9986e4a-2cf0-5373-a739-d5611a7a824c", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_ca42a8f18d784c879c5a6fcfe3a484c0.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-54", "heading": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > Common customer questions + approved responses > \xe2\x80\x9cIs Redwood down?\xe2\x80\x9d", "content": "Common customer questions + approved responses\\n\\n\xe2\x80\x9cIs Redwood down?\xe2\x80\x9d\\n\\nApproved response:\\n\xe2\x80\x9cNot necessarily. These errors indicate Redwood is protecting service reliability during temporary saturation in a specific region/model. We reject early with clear retry guidance to avoid timeouts and degraded performance. Please follow the Retry-After header and exponential backoff. If the issue persists beyond a short window, share correlation IDs and we\xe2\x80\x99ll investigate.\xe2\x80\x9d", "score": 0.457052, "rank": 56, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "fcf908f4-3acc-5496-bdaa-657f90a3cad2", "search_unit_id": "fcf908f4-3acc-5496-bdaa-657f90a3cad2", "block_from_id": "d026b7e6-a9d0-52b2-b51b-ecdea495ce10", "block_to_id": "a0b81b9f-bd1c-5ec3-89dc-80aab513ae28", "section_path": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > Revision history", "document_id": "d1667e56-b0c3-5fb4-a769-9f13a8679e2f", "document_version_id": "a9986e4a-2cf0-5373-a739-d5611a7a824c", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_ca42a8f18d784c879c5a6fcfe3a484c0.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-80", "heading": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > Revision history", "content": "Revision history\\n\\n- 2025-11-10: Initial publication for overload error rollout (Support readiness).\\n- 2025-12-05: Updated retry guidance; clarified correlation IDs and escalation thresholds.", "score": 0.457052, "rank": 57, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "6ec7073f-b628-5f9d-9589-eaaedd0e1c04", "search_unit_id": "6ec7073f-b628-5f9d-9589-eaaedd0e1c04", "block_from_id": "0b7e415a-871b-5ee2-9715-3a8c6a09800b", "block_to_id": "48ae9f4c-5300-53b0-ad8c-54c4bda1a255", "section_path": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > Error payload: what customers see", "document_id": "d1667e56-b0c3-5fb4-a769-9f13a8679e2f", "document_version_id": "a9986e4a-2cf0-5373-a739-d5611a7a824c", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_ca42a8f18d784c879c5a6fcfe3a484c0.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-21", "heading": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > Error payload: what customers see", "content": "Error payload: what customers see\\n\\nOverload errors are additive and JSON-compatible with existing response parsing.\\n\\nTypical shape (example):\\n\\n- `error.type`: `overload_error` (or `rate_limit_error` in some cases)\\n- `error.code`: stable machine code\\n- `error.message`: human-readable\\n- `error.request_id`: mirrors `x-redwood-request-id` where available\\n- `error.correlation_id`: mirrors `x-redwood-correlation-id` where available\\n\\nExample snippet (illustrative only):\\n{\\n  \\"error\\": {\\n    \\"type\\": \\"overload_error\\",\\n    \\"code\\": \\"BREAKER_OPEN\\",\\n    \\"message\\": \\"The service is temporarily overloaded. Please retry after the specified delay.\\",\\n    \\"request_id\\": \\"req_01J9...\\",\\n    \\"correlation_id\\": \\"corr_01J9...\\"\\n  }\\n}", "score": 0.457052, "rank": 58, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "8fbf98ac-7f3c-5420-b5f9-f2b57fd5b758", "search_unit_id": "8fbf98ac-7f3c-5420-b5f9-f2b57fd5b758", "block_from_id": "56349e95-55f8-548a-9ce0-9740e41b3f1b", "block_to_id": "a8e2c43d-8b94-507e-b7c4-45bd307f72c0", "section_path": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > When to escalate to Engineering (Eng Oncall / SRE)", "document_id": "d1667e56-b0c3-5fb4-a769-9f13a8679e2f", "document_version_id": "a9986e4a-2cf0-5373-a739-d5611a7a824c", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_ca42a8f18d784c879c5a6fcfe3a484c0.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-72", "heading": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > When to escalate to Engineering (Eng Oncall / SRE)", "content": "When to escalate to Engineering (Eng Oncall / SRE)\\n\\nEscalate immediately if any of the following are true:\\n\\n- **Enterprise customer** reporting sustained overload errors impacting production.\\n- Overload errors persist for **>15 minutes** with correct backoff behavior.\\n- **No Retry-After** observed on multiple responses for the same route/region/model.\\n- Suspected **misclassification** (e.g., 500s that should be 429/503 overload, or overload codes returned with 200/stream interruptions).\\n- **Fallback routing configured** but not activating.\\n- Customer reports sharp regression (new errors) coincident with a canary/rollout and provides IDs.", "score": 0.457052, "rank": 59, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "bc401c57-33c3-50bd-a410-5c9ee1c0fc4a", "search_unit_id": "bc401c57-33c3-50bd-a410-5c9ee1c0fc4a", "block_from_id": "5ea22243-12c2-530f-9cb8-add8278b88ee", "block_to_id": "871e0af5-521e-5c9d-b5b2-18b6f26b0243", "section_path": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > How to recognize overload-protection responses > Expected headers (may vary by route)", "document_id": "d1667e56-b0c3-5fb4-a769-9f13a8679e2f", "document_version_id": "a9986e4a-2cf0-5373-a739-d5611a7a824c", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_ca42a8f18d784c879c5a6fcfe3a484c0.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-18", "heading": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > How to recognize overload-protection responses > Expected headers (may vary by route)", "content": "Expected headers (may vary by route)\\n\\n- **Retry-After**: integer seconds is preferred (e.g., `Retry-After: 2`).\\n- **x-redwood-request-id**: unique ID for a single request attempt.\\n- **x-redwood-correlation-id**: groups related attempts across services (gateway \xe2\x86\x92 routing \xe2\x86\x92 runtime). Some clients/logs only show one of these; either is helpful.\\n\\nIf the response is 429/503 *and* includes a Redwood overload error code in the JSON body, treat it as overload protection.", "score": 0.457052, "rank": 60, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "ac71ef7c-7162-548f-ae1c-cef9b82ec04e", "search_unit_id": "ac71ef7c-7162-548f-ae1c-cef9b82ec04e", "block_from_id": "d9331753-256a-5d3f-8366-5f2b891886af", "block_to_id": "6ea5aca9-f46d-5988-aab8-1689931ea184", "section_path": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > Known issues (Support-facing)", "document_id": "d1667e56-b0c3-5fb4-a769-9f13a8679e2f", "document_version_id": "a9986e4a-2cf0-5373-a739-d5611a7a824c", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_ca42a8f18d784c879c5a6fcfe3a484c0.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-65", "heading": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > Known issues (Support-facing)", "content": "Known issues (Support-facing)\\n\\n1) **Retry-After header missing on some overload responses**\\n\\n- Symptom: Customer receives 429/503 overload payload but no Retry-After.\\n- Workaround: Recommend exponential backoff with jitter as above.\\n- Action: Escalate if reproducible; include route + correlation IDs.\\n\\n2) **Fallback model not triggering as expected**\\n\\n- Symptom: Customer has configured fallback but still sees repeated 503 without failover.\\n- Workaround: Customer can temporarily specify an alternate model/region explicitly (if acceptable) while we investigate.\\n- Action: Escalate; include tenant ID, configured fallback policy (what they intended), and correlation IDs.\\n\\n3) **Customer interprets 503 as \xe2\x80\x9cplatform outage\xe2\x80\x9d**\\n\\n- Guidance: Use the approved response language; direct them to Retry-After and backoff guidance.", "score": 0.457052, "rank": 61, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "bdee7da0-de24-59e3-ab10-ea1d75bbaf31", "search_unit_id": "bdee7da0-de24-59e3-ab10-ea1d75bbaf31", "block_from_id": "ef3688e8-5268-5b0e-8ca9-1ca569e5f244", "block_to_id": "19329754-f309-5978-a5e3-d866fc0872fa", "section_path": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > How to interpret and use correlation IDs > Where customers can find them", "document_id": "d1667e56-b0c3-5fb4-a769-9f13a8679e2f", "document_version_id": "a9986e4a-2cf0-5373-a739-d5611a7a824c", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_ca42a8f18d784c879c5a6fcfe3a484c0.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-49", "heading": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > How to interpret and use correlation IDs > Where customers can find them", "content": "Where customers can find them\\n\\n- Response headers in their HTTP client.\\n- SDK exception fields (Python/TypeScript SDKs surface request IDs; newer versions surface correlation IDs when present).\\n- Their application logs if they log response headers.", "score": 0.457052, "rank": 62, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "5b491687-2e16-584c-a3e6-cab774cb2c89", "search_unit_id": "5b491687-2e16-584c-a3e6-cab774cb2c89", "block_from_id": "d1abd9f9-8d65-588d-b637-ea5cc6743aaf", "block_to_id": "58f5162d-c731-51a7-890d-1d874d9614c1", "section_path": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > Retry & backoff guidance (what we should recommend) > Recommended algorithm (support-approved)", "document_id": "d1667e56-b0c3-5fb4-a769-9f13a8679e2f", "document_version_id": "a9986e4a-2cf0-5373-a739-d5611a7a824c", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_ca42a8f18d784c879c5a6fcfe3a484c0.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-34", "heading": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > Retry & backoff guidance (what we should recommend) > Recommended algorithm (support-approved)", "content": "Recommended algorithm (support-approved)\\n\\nIf `Retry-After` is present:\\n\\n- Sleep `Retry-After` seconds, then retry.\\n\\nIf `Retry-After` is missing:\\n\\n- Backoff schedule suggestion: 0.5s, 1s, 2s, 4s, then stop (max 4 retries), each with \xc2\xb120\xe2\x80\x9340% jitter.\\n\\nBounds:\\n\\n- Maximum total retry time budget: **~10\xe2\x80\x9320 seconds** for interactive apps, **~60 seconds** for batch jobs.\\n- Maximum concurrent retries: keep to a small fraction of steady-state concurrency (e.g., 10\xe2\x80\x9320%).", "score": 0.457052, "rank": 63, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "4ca0acae-f5f5-56d8-99ec-9ea26a528585", "search_unit_id": "4ca0acae-f5f5-56d8-99ec-9ea26a528585", "block_from_id": "d4f780a7-3c72-5e6e-81f7-5c439b0f3d47", "block_to_id": "121de8c0-61c4-5ff8-90ad-ad8a51ce21e8", "section_path": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > Common customer questions + approved responses > \xe2\x80\x9cShould I fail over to another model/region?\xe2\x80\x9d", "document_id": "d1667e56-b0c3-5fb4-a769-9f13a8679e2f", "document_version_id": "a9986e4a-2cf0-5373-a739-d5611a7a824c", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_ca42a8f18d784c879c5a6fcfe3a484c0.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-59", "heading": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > Common customer questions + approved responses > \xe2\x80\x9cShould I fail over to another model/region?\xe2\x80\x9d", "content": "\xe2\x80\x9cShould I fail over to another model/region?\xe2\x80\x9d\\n\\nGuidance:\\n\\n- If they have **fallback routing configured** (Dedicated/enterprise Hosted tiers or configured routing policies), yes\xe2\x80\x94recommend they enable/verify it.\\n- Otherwise, advise them to implement client-side fallback only if they can validate output compatibility and safety.", "score": 0.457052, "rank": 64, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "4a7e3cce-c9c8-55b9-905f-703e61a38352", "search_unit_id": "4a7e3cce-c9c8-55b9-905f-703e61a38352", "block_from_id": "162248f9-343a-50bc-b5e0-c92001696295", "block_to_id": "ce6d9c24-4360-50c7-818e-46b0a9526337", "section_path": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > How to recognize overload-protection responses > Common HTTP statuses", "document_id": "d1667e56-b0c3-5fb4-a769-9f13a8679e2f", "document_version_id": "a9986e4a-2cf0-5373-a739-d5611a7a824c", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_ca42a8f18d784c879c5a6fcfe3a484c0.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-14", "heading": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > How to recognize overload-protection responses > Common HTTP statuses", "content": "How to recognize overload-protection responses\\n\\nCommon HTTP statuses\\n\\n- **429 Too Many Requests**\\n  - Used for admission controls such as per-tenant concurrency caps, token buckets, or tier limits.\\n  - Usually safe to retry with backoff if Retry-After is provided.\\n\\n- **503 Service Unavailable**\\n  - Used when the platform is overloaded at the regional/model level (breaker open) or actively shedding load to protect SLOs.\\n  - Usually safe to retry with backoff and/or reduce load. May persist until capacity recovers.", "score": 0.457052, "rank": 65, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "c6a94887-e648-5d8d-9abe-f68b4bcca04e", "search_unit_id": "c6a94887-e648-5d8d-9abe-f68b4bcca04e", "block_from_id": "75c93691-1e39-59d3-8027-f907aa615e53", "block_to_id": "a92e60f5-8ecd-51cc-9b33-5a747158fb2b", "section_path": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > Common customer questions + approved responses > \xe2\x80\x9cWhy am I seeing 503 if this is overload?\xe2\x80\x9d", "document_id": "d1667e56-b0c3-5fb4-a769-9f13a8679e2f", "document_version_id": "a9986e4a-2cf0-5373-a739-d5611a7a824c", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_ca42a8f18d784c879c5a6fcfe3a484c0.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-57", "heading": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > Common customer questions + approved responses > \xe2\x80\x9cWhy am I seeing 503 if this is overload?\xe2\x80\x9d", "content": "\xe2\x80\x9cWhy am I seeing 503 if this is overload?\xe2\x80\x9d\\n\\nApproved response:\\n\xe2\x80\x9c503 is used when the platform is temporarily unable to serve the request due to load shedding or an open circuit breaker. It\xe2\x80\x99s a deliberate, controlled response (with stable error codes) to prevent cascading failures and long tail latency.\xe2\x80\x9d", "score": 0.457052, "rank": 66, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "6a555b8d-f11b-51e1-8bd3-9079a846097a", "search_unit_id": "6a555b8d-f11b-51e1-8bd3-9079a846097a", "block_from_id": "3800f0b7-5683-5bb7-bf2a-9fb1804d1876", "block_to_id": "23bb2a3a-ae5c-5f88-aeaa-1e1bc35ce7e4", "section_path": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > How to interpret and use correlation IDs > How Support should use them internally", "document_id": "d1667e56-b0c3-5fb4-a769-9f13a8679e2f", "document_version_id": "a9986e4a-2cf0-5373-a739-d5611a7a824c", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_ca42a8f18d784c879c5a6fcfe3a484c0.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-51", "heading": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > How to interpret and use correlation IDs > How Support should use them internally", "content": "How Support should use them internally\\n\\n- Paste the ID(s) into the internal log search / tracing tool.\\n- Capture:\\n  - the **error.code**\\n  - the **route** (e.g., `/v1/chat/completions`, `/v1/responses`, embeddings endpoint)\\n  - the **region** (requested + served)\\n  - whether **Retry-After** was present and its value\\n\\nIf Support cannot access dashboards/tracing, escalate with the IDs and all details in the escalation template below.", "score": 0.457052, "rank": 67, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "032225b4-4174-53d9-88dc-f54e70fe0c00", "search_unit_id": "032225b4-4174-53d9-88dc-f54e70fe0c00", "block_from_id": "63f9d991-c068-50ac-b7b3-df4816644d74", "block_to_id": "5157e419-583c-5b22-977d-03cd517a3125", "section_path": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Summary", "document_id": "c055d063-a555-5cdf-b541-fe036031cb1b", "document_version_id": "57c83493-8d9e-5467-903c-46c7977b91fb", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a3f94972391244f38ef773c05926af83.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-4", "heading": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Summary", "content": "Summary\\n\\nThis ADR defines how the production performance canary computes baselines and evaluates regressions for per-model serving performance (primarily p95 latency and tokens/sec). It documents:\\n\\n- Baseline computation approach (rolling vs fixed) and when each is used.\\n- Regression thresholds (warning vs paging) and how they are applied.\\n- Minimum sample sizes and confidence requirements to avoid noisy alerts.\\n- Anti-flap / dedupe logic to prevent alert storms during drift, telemetry delays, or small-sample variance.", "score": 0.446481, "rank": 68, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "aa0f59b2-aca1-5600-bb3d-4cf5046b5503", "search_unit_id": "aa0f59b2-aca1-5600-bb3d-4cf5046b5503", "block_from_id": "cf85636a-e739-574b-8011-19c00953672a", "block_to_id": "08f43229-8e9e-508c-aade-f7961cd4576d", "section_path": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Alerting (paging + ticketing) > Enterprise availability burn alerts (recommended)", "document_id": "12efb103-6e2e-5955-be3e-431b65ac2bca", "document_version_id": "78cfdc0b-41fe-5614-b19f-c7d639aaa33b", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_49fa5cfd95624dbeb6eaa535f9959470.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-71", "heading": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > Alerting (paging + ticketing) > Enterprise availability burn alerts (recommended)", "content": "Enterprise availability burn alerts (recommended)\\n\\nFor each protected route x region:\\n\\n- **Page (Sev2):** 28d SLO burn rate with multi-window thresholds\\n  - Fast burn: 5m and 1h windows > **14x** budget burn\\n  - Slow burn: 30m and 6h windows > **6x** budget burn\\n- **Ticket:** 2h and 1d windows > **2x** burn", "score": 0.497237, "rank": 69, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "ab43dfc2-4541-55ca-83c4-b538991c0e67", "search_unit_id": "ab43dfc2-4541-55ca-83c4-b538991c0e67", "block_from_id": "f4567963-1d41-5eca-8075-72fdc54367a7", "block_to_id": "e78b061b-f023-578f-8842-03c5ebd267e4", "section_path": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > Retry & backoff guidance (what we should recommend) > Golden rules", "document_id": "d1667e56-b0c3-5fb4-a769-9f13a8679e2f", "document_version_id": "a9986e4a-2cf0-5373-a739-d5611a7a824c", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_ca42a8f18d784c879c5a6fcfe3a484c0.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-31", "heading": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > Retry & backoff guidance (what we should recommend) > Golden rules", "content": "Retry & backoff guidance (what we should recommend)\\n\\nGolden rules\\n\\n- **Always respect Retry-After** when provided.\\n- **Use exponential backoff with jitter**; do not retry immediately.\\n- **Cap retries** to avoid amplifying overload.\\n- **Reduce concurrency** rather than increasing it during overload.\\n- Prefer **idempotency keys** for safe retries on non-streaming requests.", "score": 0.457052, "rank": 70, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "2ecd7cd1-11cd-5948-9e75-9bcae5ba8648", "search_unit_id": "2ecd7cd1-11cd-5948-9e75-9bcae5ba8648", "block_from_id": "272e810a-0a1e-50fe-b260-3b60e2f93d3c", "block_to_id": "35af38c2-5431-554a-9cba-c854c0dd62f0", "section_path": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance", "document_id": "d1667e56-b0c3-5fb4-a769-9f13a8679e2f", "document_version_id": "a9986e4a-2cf0-5373-a739-d5611a7a824c", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_ca42a8f18d784c879c5a6fcfe3a484c0.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h1-0", "heading": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance", "content": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance\\n\\nSource type: confluence\\nDocument ID: dsid_ca42a8f18d784c879c5a6fcfe3a484c0\\nRelease: v1.0.0\\nBenchmark: EnterpriseRAG-Bench synthetic upstream data\\nHosted API overload errors: support guidance, retry/backoff, and escalation", "score": 0.529218, "rank": 71, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "4e8c1606-f72d-5136-9ce8-fabf763777a9", "search_unit_id": "4e8c1606-f72d-5136-9ce8-fabf763777a9", "block_from_id": "ede7f74b-3902-5925-8fcd-a7d922089b06", "block_to_id": "b5d12f89-a1c5-5889-b71c-39ea90694fee", "section_path": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Context / Problem", "document_id": "c055d063-a555-5cdf-b541-fe036031cb1b", "document_version_id": "57c83493-8d9e-5467-903c-46c7977b91fb", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a3f94972391244f38ef773c05926af83.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-7", "heading": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Context / Problem", "content": "Context / Problem\\n\\nRedwood\xe2\x80\x99s serving runtime changes frequently (kernel selection updates, scheduler heuristics, model config changes, GPU fleet changes, and staged rollouts). We need an always-on canary that:\\n\\n- Detects real p95 latency regressions and throughput drops early.\\n- Produces actionable signals for attribution (kernel vs scheduler vs infra).\\n- Avoids paging on expected variance and baseline drift (e.g., slow fleet mix changes or regional differences).\\n\\nThe key design tension is baseline choice:\\n\\n- A **fixed baseline** is stable and easy to reason about during rollouts, but can become stale as hardware/driver stacks and workloads evolve.\\n- A **rolling baseline** adapts over time, but can drift (masking regressions) or create false positives if drift is misinterpreted as regression.", "score": 0.430146, "rank": 72, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "d5c35f69-884a-5602-a3e7-e3b293289508", "search_unit_id": "d5c35f69-884a-5602-a3e7-e3b293289508", "block_from_id": "40f58b84-843e-58a9-80b8-ecdba4e8d23b", "block_to_id": "28699223-e704-5fd3-9de2-e027bfc184f2", "section_path": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > How to interpret and use correlation IDs > Which IDs to ask for", "document_id": "d1667e56-b0c3-5fb4-a769-9f13a8679e2f", "document_version_id": "a9986e4a-2cf0-5373-a739-d5611a7a824c", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_ca42a8f18d784c879c5a6fcfe3a484c0.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-46", "heading": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > How to interpret and use correlation IDs > Which IDs to ask for", "content": "How to interpret and use correlation IDs\\n\\nWhich IDs to ask for\\n\\nAsk the customer for (in order of preference):\\n1) `x-redwood-correlation-id` (best for cross-service tracing)\\n2) `x-redwood-request-id` (single request attempt)\\n3) Timestamp + region + model + route if IDs are not available", "score": 0.457052, "rank": 73, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "ccfb0303-74f9-5f6e-8453-2020a0773c5b", "search_unit_id": "ccfb0303-74f9-5f6e-8453-2020a0773c5b", "block_from_id": "86f27c7d-f46e-5b7c-a28c-65cdf007658d", "block_to_id": "65b7d661-0827-5da3-b943-5e2ade9df157", "section_path": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Decision", "document_id": "c055d063-a555-5cdf-b541-fe036031cb1b", "document_version_id": "57c83493-8d9e-5467-903c-46c7977b91fb", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a3f94972391244f38ef773c05926af83.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-14", "heading": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Decision", "content": "Decision\\n\\nWe adopt a **hybrid baseline strategy**:\\n\\n1) **Primary baseline = Fixed, versioned baseline per (model, model_version, runtime_build, kernel_set_id, hardware_sku) with optional region override**\\n\\n- Baselines are generated from controlled canary runs (prod-like load) and stored as immutable artifacts.\\n- Baselines are explicitly tied to the runtime build and kernel selection identifiers so comparisons remain meaningful.\\n- This is the baseline used for rollout gating and for paging.\\n\\n2) **Secondary baseline = Rolling \xe2\x80\x9chealth baseline\xe2\x80\x9d used for drift monitoring and threshold calibration (not for paging)**\\n\\n- We compute a rolling reference from recent stable periods (default: last 7 days) to detect slow environmental drift and to inform recalibration.\\n- Rolling baseline is not used to auto-suppress a fixed-baseline regression; it is used as a triage and tuning signal.\\n\\n3) **Baseline freeze rules**\\n\\n- During a runtime rollout (canary/gradual), the baseline used for alerts is **frozen** to the most recent fixed baseline for the currently active runtime build on that ring.\\n- If an incident is declared for a perf regression, baseline updates are **paused** for affected keys until incident closure.\\n\\n4) **Threshold evaluation requires minimum sample size + sustained breach + hysteresis**\\n\\n- Alerts require both a minimum number of canary requests and sustained breach across multiple evaluation windows.\\n- Alert clear conditions are stricter than trigger conditions (hysteresis) to reduce flapping.", "score": 0.413812, "rank": 74, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "83d18350-c1bb-5872-ac05-5fbc8c02f296", "search_unit_id": "83d18350-c1bb-5872-ac05-5fbc8c02f296", "block_from_id": "a5acc818-750a-5c18-bb02-9bf043739249", "block_to_id": "e844c72c-f808-5d8b-b173-b3c0a0341314", "section_path": "Go Live Protocols Cross Org Communications Escalation 2027", "document_id": "c1cb92b0-f42c-5fdf-9753-22cf1f569b80", "document_version_id": "a96eb210-903b-56d1-a33c-9bd4347e8b15", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_9c8cb10d65324c03a8ededbd98c1b058.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "list-24", "heading": "Go Live Protocols Cross Org Communications Escalation 2027", "content": "- CSM: customer comms and executive escalation.\\n- SE: onboarding technical lead, API contract signoff, whitelist verification.\\n- Oncall SRE: monitoring, mitigation, and platform config changes in emergencies.\\n- Product/Applied ML: model quality regressions and rollbacks.\\n- Finance Ops: post-cutover cost anomalies and quota adjustments.\\n\\nAppendix A \xe2\x80\x94 Playbook Checklist (copyable)\\n-----------------------------------------\\n\\n- [ ] Confirm private channel and all stakeholder contacts pinned\\n- [ ] Telemetry smoke test passed (synthetic happy path)\\n- [ ] Canary plan scheduled and accepted by SE and SRE\\n- [ ] Firewall/whitelist config validated by Network team\\n- [ ] PagerDuty rotations confirmed and escalation contacts available\\n- [ ] Customer-facing status template approved by CSM\\n- [ ] Post-cutover QBR slot scheduled with customer (within 30 days)\\n\\nAppendix B \xe2\x80\x94 Links and References\\n--------------------------------\\n\\n- Tenant cutover checklist (internal): /confluence/customer-success-and-support/enterprise-onboarding/onboarding-handback-and-first-90-days-ops-guide-2026\\n- Incident comms templates: /confluence/eng-sre/incident-communication-templates\\n- PagerDuty runbook: https://pagerduty.com/orgs/redwood-incidents (private)\\n\\nAppendix C \xe2\x80\x94 Escalation Contact Quickview\\n----------------------------------------\\n\\n- Oncall SRE: pagerduty (PD ID: PD12345), Slack: @oncall-sre, mobile: +1-555-0102\\n- SE on duty: Slack: @se-ops, mobile: +1-555-0148\\n- CSM (Primary): Priya Patel, Slack: @priya.cs, mobile: +1-555-0190\\n\\nRevision History\\n----------------\\n\\n- 2027-02-10: Created by Priya Patel.\\n- 2027-03-02: Reviewed and clarified rollback steps; added expense reconciliation task (Marisol Gomez).\\n\\nNotes and governance\\n--------------------\\nKeep this page updated after every enterprise go-live. If the customer contract contains additional communication obligations (e.g., weekly executive summaries), add them to the post-cutover tasks and pin them in the customer channel.", "score": 0.417444, "rank": 75, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "4d7afc92-5ba0-53c2-a694-97e612d826d5", "search_unit_id": "4d7afc92-5ba0-53c2-a694-97e612d826d5", "block_from_id": "5c4aa2bf-81ed-523c-af27-8fd745dc2af1", "block_to_id": "92efbd3f-2398-549e-b0e0-8089a7e21326", "section_path": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Decision Drivers", "document_id": "c055d063-a555-5cdf-b541-fe036031cb1b", "document_version_id": "57c83493-8d9e-5467-903c-46c7977b91fb", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a3f94972391244f38ef773c05926af83.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-12", "heading": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Decision Drivers", "content": "Decision Drivers\\n\\n1. **Low noise / high trust**: warnings should be useful; pages should be rare and high-confidence.\\n2. **Fast detection**: detect regressions within ~10\xe2\x80\x9330 minutes for top models in covered regions.\\n3. **Attribution**: baseline keys must align to dimensions needed for root cause (runtime build, kernel tags, SKU).\\n4. **Operational simplicity**: SRE/on-call should have a predictable interpretation of \xe2\x80\x9cregression vs baseline,\xe2\x80\x9d including during ongoing rollouts.\\n5. **Guardrails against drift and telemetry issues**: avoid alert storms caused by baseline drift, delayed metric ingestion, and label cardinality errors.", "score": 0.413812, "rank": 76, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "0bab4b61-2347-5b3a-845d-6f3bba6297b1", "search_unit_id": "0bab4b61-2347-5b3a-845d-6f3bba6297b1", "block_from_id": "09185fbc-2dd2-59de-843f-99f5f6a2f45f", "block_to_id": "9cc7b2e5-b278-5170-b574-b901af5c2821", "section_path": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Baseline Keys (What defines \xe2\x80\x9clike-for-like\xe2\x80\x9d)", "document_id": "c055d063-a555-5cdf-b541-fe036031cb1b", "document_version_id": "57c83493-8d9e-5467-903c-46c7977b91fb", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a3f94972391244f38ef773c05926af83.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-24", "heading": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Baseline Keys (What defines \xe2\x80\x9clike-for-like\xe2\x80\x9d)", "content": "Baseline Keys (What defines \xe2\x80\x9clike-for-like\xe2\x80\x9d)\\n\\nBaseline artifacts and regression evaluation are keyed by the following dimensions:\\n\\n**Required baseline key fields (v1):**\\n\\n- model_id (canonical model identifier)\\n- model_version (registry version / hash)\\n- runtime_build_id (serving runtime build tag)\\n- kernel_set_id (kernel selection/version identifier)\\n- hardware_sku (e.g., H100-80GB, A100-80GB)\\n\\n**Optional fields (only when variance requires it):**\\n\\n- region (enabled for regions with materially different fleet/network characteristics)\\n- capacity_tier (hosted vs dedicated pools) when pools differ materially\\n\\nNotes:\\n\\n- We do NOT key baselines by customer, endpoint, or prompt content (privacy + cardinality constraints).\\n- Scheduler configuration flags that materially change behavior must be reflected either in runtime_build_id or an explicit scheduler_profile tag (introduced only if needed; kept off by default to control cardinality).", "score": 0.413812, "rank": 77, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "2eebb20a-591d-506c-b85a-40583bdabfb8", "search_unit_id": "2eebb20a-591d-506c-b85a-40583bdabfb8", "block_from_id": "6534390e-d337-5fa5-867c-5f7d360901cd", "block_to_id": "5d87204c-d905-5235-a7bd-28e6127d93ce", "section_path": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Regression Definition > Core comparison method (v1)", "document_id": "c055d063-a555-5cdf-b541-fe036031cb1b", "document_version_id": "57c83493-8d9e-5467-903c-46c7977b91fb", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a3f94972391244f38ef773c05926af83.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-42", "heading": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Regression Definition > Core comparison method (v1)", "content": "Core comparison method (v1)\\n\\nFor each metric M and key K in each evaluation window W:\\n\\n- Compute observed metric value O(K, W).\\n- Fetch baseline value B(K) and baseline variability estimates when available.\\n- Compute percent change: \xce\x94 = (O - B) / B.\\n\\nWe use percent change thresholds with guardrails (min samples + sustained breach). Percent thresholds are chosen because:\\n\\n- Different models have different absolute latencies.\\n- p95 is the primary SLO-relevant tail metric.", "score": 0.413812, "rank": 78, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "23d59747-a1af-515c-8f79-528d93c99d09", "search_unit_id": "23d59747-a1af-515c-8f79-528d93c99d09", "block_from_id": "9cd681e5-97cb-5376-8e9e-4eebb60ae8c3", "block_to_id": "5ea39e5e-dfbb-5c06-b745-2e0fbead0881", "section_path": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Baseline Computation > Rolling health baseline (calibration only)", "document_id": "c055d063-a555-5cdf-b541-fe036031cb1b", "document_version_id": "57c83493-8d9e-5467-903c-46c7977b91fb", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a3f94972391244f38ef773c05926af83.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-38", "heading": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Baseline Computation > Rolling health baseline (calibration only)", "content": "Rolling health baseline (calibration only)\\n\\n- Window: trailing 7 days, excluding periods marked as \xe2\x80\x9crollout\xe2\x80\x9d or \xe2\x80\x9cincident.\xe2\x80\x9d\\n- Aggregation: daily aggregates with robust statistics (median-of-daily-p95) to reduce sensitivity to short spikes.\\n- Purpose:\\n  - detect slow drift (e.g., fleet changes) that may warrant generating a new fixed baseline\\n  - suggest region/SKU overrides when variance is consistently higher", "score": 0.413812, "rank": 79, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "048dcbdb-3ef2-5adb-aaa1-7b3d09b59784", "search_unit_id": "048dcbdb-3ef2-5adb-aaa1-7b3d09b59784", "block_from_id": "19c4bfbb-325e-5713-b50c-5c5bc52048b7", "block_to_id": "29816627-69c4-5de1-9925-50d061e7922c", "section_path": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds", "document_id": "c055d063-a555-5cdf-b541-fe036031cb1b", "document_version_id": "57c83493-8d9e-5467-903c-46c7977b91fb", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a3f94972391244f38ef773c05926af83.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h1-0", "heading": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds", "content": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds\\n\\nSource type: confluence\\nDocument ID: dsid_a3f94972391244f38ef773c05926af83\\nRelease: v1.0.0\\nBenchmark: EnterpriseRAG-Bench synthetic upstream data\\nADR-0142: Perf Canary Baselines, Regression Thresholds, Min Sample Sizes, and Anti-Flap Logic", "score": 0.47915, "rank": 80, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "62fcc83f-b414-51cb-bcb2-209bbd1a09df", "search_unit_id": "62fcc83f-b414-51cb-bcb2-209bbd1a09df", "block_from_id": "7bb3447a-50dd-5a6f-be82-70df9ce725ae", "block_to_id": "e9103e26-8b73-51b7-911b-0f8f9f58012a", "section_path": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > Retry & backoff guidance (what we should recommend) > What NOT to recommend", "document_id": "d1667e56-b0c3-5fb4-a769-9f13a8679e2f", "document_version_id": "a9986e4a-2cf0-5373-a739-d5611a7a824c", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_ca42a8f18d784c879c5a6fcfe3a484c0.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-44", "heading": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > Retry & backoff guidance (what we should recommend) > What NOT to recommend", "content": "What NOT to recommend\\n\\n- \xe2\x80\x9cJust retry in a tight loop.\xe2\x80\x9d\\n- \xe2\x80\x9cRetry immediately with the same concurrency.\xe2\x80\x9d\\n- \xe2\x80\x9cSpin up many parallel retries to get through.\xe2\x80\x9d", "score": 0.457052, "rank": 81, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "88c5d01c-3c5f-5fa7-90a8-6dc5fafff25b", "search_unit_id": "88c5d01c-3c5f-5fa7-90a8-6dc5fafff25b", "block_from_id": "37c58785-6dda-52d4-840e-3f394dea5e3f", "block_to_id": "6be03658-1bd7-5836-8f16-cd17ebcdc45e", "section_path": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Minimum Sample Sizes and Windowing > Minimum samples (per key K, per window)", "document_id": "c055d063-a555-5cdf-b541-fe036031cb1b", "document_version_id": "57c83493-8d9e-5467-903c-46c7977b91fb", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a3f94972391244f38ef773c05926af83.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-64", "heading": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Minimum Sample Sizes and Windowing > Minimum samples (per key K, per window)", "content": "Minimum samples (per key K, per window)\\n\\n- **Latency metrics**: minimum **200 successful canary requests** per window.\\n- **Tokens/sec**: minimum **200 successful canary requests** per window AND minimum **50,000 total generated tokens** per window.\\n\\nIf sample requirements are not met:\\n\\n- No alert state change is allowed (prevents flapping in low-coverage periods).\\n- Dashboard panels display \xe2\x80\x9cinsufficient sample\xe2\x80\x9d annotation.\\n\\nRationale:\\n\\n- Tail latency (p95/p99) is unreliable with very small N.\\n- Tokens/sec needs sufficient token volume to smooth per-request variance.", "score": 0.413812, "rank": 82, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "dce37664-4c7a-5cd2-aff9-2b8b05d93b21", "search_unit_id": "dce37664-4c7a-5cd2-aff9-2b8b05d93b21", "block_from_id": "6d4588a0-f081-5ee0-9394-bddc1b49fd75", "block_to_id": "648b9fdc-1229-5e63-b445-0ffa22b1fc1a", "section_path": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Default Thresholds (v1) > Runtime-only attribution signals (non-paging by default)", "document_id": "c055d063-a555-5cdf-b541-fe036031cb1b", "document_version_id": "57c83493-8d9e-5467-903c-46c7977b91fb", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a3f94972391244f38ef773c05926af83.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-56", "heading": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Default Thresholds (v1) > Runtime-only attribution signals (non-paging by default)", "content": "Runtime-only attribution signals (non-paging by default)\\n\\nThese are emitted to dashboards and included in alert payloads, but do not page unless explicitly enabled for a model:\\n\\n- Queueing time p95 +25% (suggests scheduler/fleet saturation)\\n- Prefill p95 +15% (suggests kernel/model config regressions)\\n- Decode p95 +15% (suggests kernel/model config regressions)\\n\\nRationale:\\n\\n- Paging should be driven by end-to-end customer-impact proxy (E2E p95 and tokens/sec).\\n- Breakdown metrics improve triage but are often noisier and more sensitive to load mix.", "score": 0.413812, "rank": 83, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "f37db93e-b81a-5ba4-8d45-7c2934d4367c", "search_unit_id": "f37db93e-b81a-5ba4-8d45-7c2934d4367c", "block_from_id": "c64b2220-dfdd-542d-9218-86e48adf63ea", "block_to_id": "91573533-ff3d-56bc-8378-b75fd72b0c0d", "section_path": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Regression Definition > Threshold levels", "document_id": "c055d063-a555-5cdf-b541-fe036031cb1b", "document_version_id": "57c83493-8d9e-5467-903c-46c7977b91fb", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a3f94972391244f38ef773c05926af83.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-47", "heading": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Regression Definition > Threshold levels", "content": "Threshold levels\\n\\nTwo alert levels exist:\\n\\n- **Warning**: actionable signal for runtime team triage; routed to #eng-runtime.\\n- **Paging**: sustained, high-confidence regression on top models; routed to on-call rotation.", "score": 0.413812, "rank": 84, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "11e07925-978c-5589-a759-600b153ded4d", "search_unit_id": "11e07925-978c-5589-a759-600b153ded4d", "block_from_id": "be65d402-742a-5e74-b3dd-89badbc8f639", "block_to_id": "ec1f642b-4a53-5e57-a104-739d0f9859df", "section_path": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Anti-Flap, Dedupe, and Hysteresis > Alert clear (hysteresis)", "document_id": "c055d063-a555-5cdf-b541-fe036031cb1b", "document_version_id": "57c83493-8d9e-5467-903c-46c7977b91fb", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a3f94972391244f38ef773c05926af83.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-74", "heading": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Anti-Flap, Dedupe, and Hysteresis > Alert clear (hysteresis)", "content": "Alert clear (hysteresis)\\n\\nAlerts clear only when performance returns closer to baseline:\\n\\n- Warning clears when \xce\x94 < +6% (latency) and \xce\x94 > -5% (tokens/sec) for 3/3 windows.\\n- Paging clears when \xce\x94 < +12% (latency) and \xce\x94 > -10% (tokens/sec) for 4/4 windows.", "score": 0.413812, "rank": 85, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "66d7594b-8436-5080-9aef-e0793b071050", "search_unit_id": "66d7594b-8436-5080-9aef-e0793b071050", "block_from_id": "b054f4e8-bbac-5499-945b-0a4e35e10b2a", "block_to_id": "ced36613-e0ec-5017-b3a5-8e6157be9809", "section_path": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Minimum Sample Sizes and Windowing > Evaluation window", "document_id": "c055d063-a555-5cdf-b541-fe036031cb1b", "document_version_id": "57c83493-8d9e-5467-903c-46c7977b91fb", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a3f94972391244f38ef773c05926af83.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-61", "heading": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Minimum Sample Sizes and Windowing > Evaluation window", "content": "Minimum Sample Sizes and Windowing\\n\\nEvaluation window\\n\\n- Default evaluation window: **10 minutes**.\\n- Windows are evaluated continuously and require sustained breach rules (see anti-flap).", "score": 0.413812, "rank": 86, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "2492981b-b2d9-5b18-ae30-ecc8af9d3a18", "search_unit_id": "2492981b-b2d9-5b18-ae30-ecc8af9d3a18", "block_from_id": "2017ef93-6d74-5202-9e6a-751770578531", "block_to_id": "0a422d4e-f17d-5077-9a27-980e547a2c74", "section_path": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Escalation > Triage checklist (Support/CS) > 7) Mitigation options you can offer immediately (tier-dependent)", "document_id": "2aced3e3-1ed9-50b2-9ef8-a037c40fcfaf", "document_version_id": "5b0b49bf-5c10-5f9e-b6f9-1ae199865977", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_409618a3958944f9b78362ed7e01bb33.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-65", "heading": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Escalation > Triage checklist (Support/CS) > 7) Mitigation options you can offer immediately (tier-dependent)", "content": "7) Mitigation options you can offer immediately (tier-dependent)\\n\\nThese are customer-facing actions CS/Support may propose, with SRE confirmation where possible:\\n\\n- Temporarily reduce max context / max output tokens (if within acceptable product behavior).\\n- Temporarily shift traffic to the contracted primary route only (avoid experimental routes).\\n- Enable/disable streaming based on their client constraints (note: streaming affects perceived latency but should not invalidate throughput).\\n- If the customer has multi-deployment setup: fail over to a secondary deployment (Premium: may be Redwood-assisted).\\n\\nDo not promise failover across regions unless the tier/contract explicitly includes it.", "score": 0.390762, "rank": 87, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "27698a5d-d93e-55bc-9e85-1ea0b026d5f6", "search_unit_id": "27698a5d-d93e-55bc-9e85-1ea0b026d5f6", "block_from_id": "52c4cb61-c56f-5cf9-a932-5f55b94704eb", "block_to_id": "4d4a53fe-b57d-5204-82ee-bd71a6c3810e", "section_path": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Regression Definition", "document_id": "c055d063-a555-5cdf-b541-fe036031cb1b", "document_version_id": "57c83493-8d9e-5467-903c-46c7977b91fb", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a3f94972391244f38ef773c05926af83.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-40", "heading": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Regression Definition", "content": "Regression Definition\\n\\nWe define regression as a statistically and operationally meaningful deviation from the fixed baseline.", "score": 0.413812, "rank": 88, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "82e5de62-ab2f-52ee-a327-f8ae85a98506", "search_unit_id": "82e5de62-ab2f-52ee-a327-f8ae85a98506", "block_from_id": "f2167597-0d78-51c4-98ac-6e6b5709a336", "block_to_id": "ee939171-e136-5ca2-917d-48f955d2b50a", "section_path": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Default Thresholds (v1) > Throughput (tokens/sec)", "document_id": "c055d063-a555-5cdf-b541-fe036031cb1b", "document_version_id": "57c83493-8d9e-5467-903c-46c7977b91fb", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a3f94972391244f38ef773c05926af83.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-54", "heading": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Default Thresholds (v1) > Throughput (tokens/sec)", "content": "Throughput (tokens/sec)\\n\\n- Warning: tokens/sec **-8%** vs baseline for **2 out of 3** windows.\\n- Paging: tokens/sec **-15%** vs baseline for **3 out of 4** windows.", "score": 0.413812, "rank": 89, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "727341b2-7485-5498-b317-52936582e1ca", "search_unit_id": "727341b2-7485-5498-b317-52936582e1ca", "block_from_id": "72526615-4309-5311-b017-ea8575fcc639", "block_to_id": "461f557f-a9ae-56b2-a8f6-4cdb7deb0121", "section_path": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Anti-Flap, Dedupe, and Hysteresis > Sustained breach logic", "document_id": "c055d063-a555-5cdf-b541-fe036031cb1b", "document_version_id": "57c83493-8d9e-5467-903c-46c7977b91fb", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a3f94972391244f38ef773c05926af83.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-70", "heading": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Anti-Flap, Dedupe, and Hysteresis > Sustained breach logic", "content": "Anti-Flap, Dedupe, and Hysteresis\\n\\nSustained breach logic\\n\\nWe use \xe2\x80\x9cM out of N windows\xe2\x80\x9d to reduce sensitivity to transient spikes:\\n\\n- Warning: 2/3 consecutive windows breach\\n- Paging: 3/4 consecutive windows breach", "score": 0.413812, "rank": 90, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "84971e12-3b85-562f-a47a-0616d6f1282d", "search_unit_id": "84971e12-3b85-562f-a47a-0616d6f1282d", "block_from_id": "0fcf622d-1f62-51a2-a7a7-d75470f483a7", "block_to_id": "ede8256a-24a2-5244-87e0-c2190dbea2b1", "section_path": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Default Thresholds (v1)", "document_id": "c055d063-a555-5cdf-b541-fe036031cb1b", "document_version_id": "57c83493-8d9e-5467-903c-46c7977b91fb", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a3f94972391244f38ef773c05926af83.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-50", "heading": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Default Thresholds (v1)", "content": "Default Thresholds (v1)\\n\\nThresholds are configurable per model, with these platform defaults used unless overridden.", "score": 0.413812, "rank": 91, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "f1fb4888-4d60-56f5-8687-0461431f49f1", "search_unit_id": "f1fb4888-4d60-56f5-8687-0461431f49f1", "block_from_id": "87a564e7-e30d-501b-90ed-13454fef143f", "block_to_id": "59c35bd7-8d0e-579e-90ed-21b4f04209b4", "section_path": "Int 9821 Crucible Inference Latency Spike And 5Xx Inc 9821", "document_id": "2b07e75b-b8a4-5578-b4d1-5dad113848fe", "document_version_id": "62c69ecf-53d2-534c-96c8-261df54adfb3", "document_name": "jira__dsid_0c1f1c83e8d44d2fa3a9f6c7b2d7b9f1.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h1-0", "heading": "Int 9821 Crucible Inference Latency Spike And 5Xx Inc 9821", "content": "Int 9821 Crucible Inference Latency Spike And 5Xx Inc 9821\\n\\nSource type: jira\\nDocument ID: dsid_0c1f1c83e8d44d2fa3a9f6c7b2d7b9f1\\nRelease: v1.0.0\\nBenchmark: EnterpriseRAG-Bench synthetic upstream data\\nCrucible Health: inference latency spike + transient 5xx on /v1/generate (INC-9821)\\n\\nCustomer escalation relayed by Stephanie Nguyen. Crucible Health reported sudden latency spike and burst of 5xx on inference endpoint /v1/generate beginning ~2026-08-28 07:55 PDT. Reported impact: median latency increased from ~120ms to ~900ms; throughput dropped from ~250 req/s to ~150 req/s for Crucible production key; initial customer-observed 5xx rate ~8% during the window; multiple batches timed out and downstream throttles triggered. Customer had a critical demo at 10:00 PDT.\\n\\nImmediate hypotheses (serving): autoscaler oscillation under burst + degraded single shard causing head-of-line blocking.\\n\\nImmediate mitigations executed: emergency scale-up of dedicated pool (target +20% capacity) + temporary concurrency limit increase + drain suspected bad instance(s) + enable routing of new requests to compatible fallback model variant (lower context) to reduce per-token work.\\n\\nOutcome (updated after investigation): improvements observed within ~9\xe2\x80\x9312 minutes; throughput returned to baseline and tail latency normalized after draining the degraded node(s) and reverting autoscaler policy.\\n\\nUpdated root cause (post-incident): the triggering event was an autoscaler policy update that reduced stabilization/cooldown and interacted poorly with a burst in queue depth, causing scale oscillation. One GPU instance concurrently entered a degraded driver state (intermittent kernel launch stalls rather than a hard OOM), amplifying tail latency and increasing request timeouts which surfaced as 5xx at the gateway. The instance replacement lag plus oscillation extended the incident duration.", "score": 0.365148, "rank": 92, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "0d6e9203-9f98-5a99-85ca-6a76573ddf53", "search_unit_id": "0d6e9203-9f98-5a99-85ca-6a76573ddf53", "block_from_id": "f7267fbe-e16b-54a9-8f7b-8624803d5b66", "block_to_id": "4b321e57-30fd-5d83-9eeb-3b1e7b298e07", "section_path": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Definition > Change log", "document_id": "19c5a655-72be-5ec3-9113-0a8f67b72c9f", "document_version_id": "2e39e4c6-4649-5fd6-9e3d-ebf763331245", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_e33c122264214846a8b7df7701d31482.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-108", "heading": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Definition > Change log", "content": "Change log\\n\\n- 2025-03-14: Clarified \xe2\x80\x9cmeaningfully loaded\xe2\x80\x9d exclusion threshold and reporting language.\\n- 2025-04-02: Documented streaming token accounting fix boundary (PR-19422) and added minimum eligible window suppression for burn-rate alerts.", "score": 0.353159, "rank": 93, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "eed9fa3f-4200-5bfb-8ae2-7eeb70b03277", "search_unit_id": "eed9fa3f-4200-5bfb-8ae2-7eeb70b03277", "block_from_id": "70290ae3-2269-565d-9ba7-051707666a4e", "block_to_id": "7d3b1e7b-4c85-53b7-b8c8-60dde669744a", "section_path": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Escalation > Common failure modes and quick routing hints", "document_id": "2aced3e3-1ed9-50b2-9ef8-a037c40fcfaf", "document_version_id": "5b0b49bf-5c10-5f9e-b6f9-1ae199865977", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_409618a3958944f9b78362ed7e01bb33.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-136", "heading": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Escalation > Common failure modes and quick routing hints", "content": "Common failure modes and quick routing hints\\n\\n- **Customer sees lower throughput than Redwood reports:** often a measurement mismatch (streaming token accounting, end-to-end latency vs throughput, counting input tokens). Engage Observability if persistent.\\n- **Throughput drops with GPU pegged:** suspect contention, node health, thermal throttling, network bottleneck; page Infra.\\n- **Throughput drops with low utilization:** suspect admission control limiting, customer throttling, or workload shift to longer contexts; page SRE; ask customer for concurrency/workload details.\\n- **Regional capacity constraint / failover behavior:** check tier failover posture; page Infra and SRE; do not promise cross-region without tier/contract confirmation.\\n\\n---", "score": 0.354952, "rank": 94, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "f01e1322-a143-570f-b42b-445ad769f16f", "search_unit_id": "f01e1322-a143-570f-b42b-445ad769f16f", "block_from_id": "c01b4777-5362-543a-8f35-af538b2ca709", "block_to_id": "c5d100e9-1b17-5124-a1e5-377252248c20", "section_path": "Sup 18510 Streaming Throughput Metric Mismatch", "document_id": "d164ca2a-1fef-5580-b76a-e8640c05b61e", "document_version_id": "9403367b-6c10-5fbb-bd00-38bc695b7e78", "document_name": "jira__dsid_a5a5235d973e421c870e11ceea4887ed.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "list-21", "heading": "Sup 18510 Streaming Throughput Metric Mismatch", "content": "- Engineering to patch streaming accounting to ensure final usage does not also increment the streaming delta counter (or to separate metrics cleanly).\\n- Backfill: no backfill planned; we will annotate the dashboard for the affected time range and document the known issue.\\n- Customer comms: provide written explanation that billing is unaffected; commit to patch timeline and a short validation report post-deploy.\\n\\n2025-02-21 10:14 PT \xe2\x80\x94 Owen Phillips (Support): Customer ticket opened after Vidorama noticed Redwood Console tokens/sec ~2x their internal measurement on streaming. Non-streaming looks normal. Requesting SRE/Obs help; tagging Aisha Bello.\\n2025-02-21 12:02 PT \xe2\x80\x94 Aisha Bello (SRE): Acknowledged. Pulling metrics for deployment_id provided by customer. Initial glance: tokens/sec is unusually spiky and high during periods dominated by streaming traffic.\\n2025-02-22 09:37 PT \xe2\x80\x94 Sergio Costa (Observability): I can reproduce in staging with a scripted SSE client. Suspect dashboard is aggregating both stream deltas and final usage. Need confirm on emitter path in serving-runtime.\\n2025-02-23 14:18 PT \xe2\x80\x94 Logan Wright (Telemetry): Found candidate: token counter increments on chunk delta AND again on finalize handler. This would produce ~2x under full consumption. Filing fix with serving-runtime owners.\\n2025-02-24 11:26 PT \xe2\x80\x94 Aisha Bello (SRE): Confirmed billing exports are correct (final usage only). The problematic series is the near-real-time throughput metric used in the dashboard and SLO evaluator. Recommending we temporarily treat streaming throughput dashboard values as best-effort until patch.\\n2025-02-25 16:40 PT \xe2\x80\x94 Owen Phillips (Support): Sent customer interim update: billing unaffected; mismatch is in streaming throughput visualization/metric; offered workaround using finalized usage tokens. Customer asked whether this impacts Dedicated SLO credits\xe2\x80\x94told them we will validate SLO evaluator inputs and share fix ETA.\\n2025-02-27 13:05 PT \xe2\x80\x94 Aisha Bello (SRE): Fix PR is up: sources/github/redwood/pr-19422-fix-throughput-metric-double-counting-streaming.json. Plan: canary in us-east Dedicated pools first, then global rollout if stable.\\n2025-03-01 09:22 PT \xe2\x80\x94 Aisha Bello (SRE): Patch deployed to prod/us-east. Verified in internal load test: streaming tokens/sec now matches finalized usage within ~3\xe2\x80\x936% over 5m windows. Monitoring for 24h; no regressions so far.\\n2025-03-02 10:11 PT \xe2\x80\x94 Owen Phillips (Support): Customer confirmed their numbers now align with Redwood Console within expected variance. Closing ticket. Follow-up: add dashboard annotation for 2025-02-10 through 2025-03-01 and link to known issue in Dedicated throughput SLO FAQ.\\nResolved by patching streaming token accounting to prevent double-counting (per-chunk deltas + final usage). Deployed to prod/us-east on 2025-03-01 and validated against load tests and customer confirmation. Billing was not impacted. Ticket closed after customer confirmed metrics alignment.", "score": 0.353718, "rank": 95, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "4071c582-b115-5585-92ac-842f0c1d3103", "search_unit_id": "4071c582-b115-5585-92ac-842f0c1d3103", "block_from_id": "f5108182-44ac-56e8-be87-0c6cc420ca25", "block_to_id": "f5108182-44ac-56e8-be87-0c6cc420ca25", "section_path": "Sup 18421 Enterprise 429 Spike After Priority Routing Rollout > Requested outcome", "document_id": "89c06a4c-9104-5c7e-b766-1b1af3c247dd", "document_version_id": "c6465792-6750-5999-b72c-4196eb71504b", "document_name": "jira__dsid_1980f45c3fb4455f943a2b030892ae90.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "p-28", "heading": "Sup 18421 Enterprise 429 Spike After Priority Routing Rollout > Requested outcome", "content": "2026-01-20 09:26 PT - Owen Phillips (Support): Customer reports 10x increase in 429s since this morning. Asking if we changed rate limiting / routing. Provided immediate guidance: honor Retry-After, implement exponential backoff + jitter, and send us 10 sample X-Redwood-Request-Id values from 429s and 200s around the same time window.\\n2026-01-20 10:02 PT - Customer (Proxima Bank): Shared 12 request IDs and headers. Majority show X-Redwood-Throttle-Reason=overload_protection_admission; a few quota_exceeded. They confirm current retry policy is fixed 100ms, no jitter, up to 10 retries.\\n2026-01-20 10:18 PT - Rafael Mendes (SRE): Acknowledged correlation with overload protection activation in us-east. Confirmed this is not a billing quota-only issue; most are admission control during regional pressure. Asked Platform to confirm policy config for PROXIMA-ENT-014 and whether they\xe2\x80\x99re on canary allowlist.\\n2026-01-20 10:41 PT - Jordan Lee (Policy Engine): PROXIMA-ENT-014 is in enterprise priority class, but route-group defaults include a conservative burst multiplier. Their combined chat+embeddings bursts can exceed the enterprise route budget during pressure windows (designed to preserve latency SLO). Decision logs show deny reason=admission_over_budget with retry_after_seconds=1\xe2\x80\x932.\\n2026-01-20 11:07 PT - Amara Diallo (API Gateway): Confirmed gateway is emitting new reason codes/headers from PR-27441. For this account, 429 payloads are consistent. Suggested customer should branch retry logic by reason: quota_exceeded (longer wait; smooth traffic) vs overload/admission (short retry-after, but exponential backoff and reduced concurrency).\\n2026-01-20 11:32 PT - Mina Haddad (Routing): During 09:10\xe2\x80\x9309:30 PT, us-east had elevated scheduler lag and queue depth on shared pools; best-effort traffic was shed first, but enterprise admission control still clips if the enterprise route budget is exceeded. No evidence of misrouting or fallback. This appears like expected behavior but thresholds may be too tight for Proxima\xe2\x80\x99s contract.\\n2026-01-20 12:10 PT - Owen Phillips (Support): Sent customer explanation of reason codes and concrete retry guidance. Asked for their target peak RPS and max prompt/response sizes during market-open events. Offered a call with TAM (Liam O\'Rourke) to align on traffic shape and burst needs.\\n2026-01-21 08:55 PT - Liam O\'Rourke (TAM): Spoke with customer. They need up to ~1,500 RPS chat for 20\xe2\x80\x9340 seconds and ~3,000 RPS embeddings for 60 seconds during market-open. They can accept some smoothing but want fewer hard 429s. They requested a temporary exception for 2 weeks while they deploy client-side backoff improvements.\\n2026-01-21 09:24 PT - Cole Summers (Quotas): Noted some quota_exceeded events are due to embeddings daily quota window nearing cap; separate from overload. For overload/admission, we can grant a time-boxed burst multiplier increase for the specific route-group, but must monitor shed rates and enterprise latency burn.\\n2026-01-21 10:03 PT - Rafael Mendes (SRE): Approved a guarded exception: increase burst multiplier for PROXIMA-ENT-014 on chat+embeddings in us-east only, 14-day expiry, automatic rollback if enterprise p99 latency burn > threshold or shed rate sustained > 2%. Added monitoring annotation + incident-bot notice rule. Customer must implement exponential backoff + jitter within 7 days.", "score": 0.349445, "rank": 96, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "54d01ba8-1f0e-5a03-9de3-8cb01f94c1c9", "search_unit_id": "54d01ba8-1f0e-5a03-9de3-8cb01f94c1c9", "block_from_id": "dbfda5c5-5d2f-5966-8c87-d9eefc6ad7ca", "block_to_id": "381ceb0d-432e-5e39-81ea-6d242d958db3", "section_path": "Burst Capacity Retro Notes", "document_id": "055d3522-4e6b-5ab7-a0f7-0224afbd508e", "document_version_id": "f52eceb0-167c-5e90-84d2-0bc653ae49c8", "document_name": "google_drive__dsid_95c7aa5238f94570a8e0a8684736b666.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h1-0", "heading": "Burst Capacity Retro Notes", "content": "Burst Capacity Retro Notes\\n\\nSource type: google_drive\\nDocument ID: dsid_95c7aa5238f94570a8e0a8684736b666\\nRelease: v1.0.0\\nBenchmark: EnterpriseRAG-Bench synthetic upstream data\\nDedicated Burst Capacity \xe2\x80\x94 Post-beta retro notes (SRE)\\nDoc goal\\nCapture what we learned from the Dedicated Burst Capacity beta (May 2025): what broke, which guardrails were too strict/too lax, what we changed during beta, and what must be completed before GA.\\n\\nRetro logistics\\n\\n- Date: 2025-05-28 (60 min)\\n- Facilitator: Rafael Mendes (SRE)\\n- Notetaker: Peter Holtz (Routing SRE)\\n- Beta window covered: 2025-05-06 to 2025-05-24\\n- Scope: burst pools (regional/hardware), admission controller, routing integration, SLO gates, dashboards/alerts, metering/billing hooks, supportability\\n\\nAttendees (high signal contributors)\\n\\n- SRE/Obs: Sean G., Rafael M., Peter H., Allison G., Sergio C.\\n- Infra/Fleet: Vanessa O., Keiko Y.\\n- Control plane/policy: Jordan L.\\n- Routing: Mina H.\\n- Billing/metering: Cole S., Nadia R.\\n- CS feedback: Rachel K.\\n\\nWhat went well (keep)\\n1) Global safety cap + circuit breaker concept worked in practice\\n\\n- We hit the cap once in staging and once \xe2\x80\x9cnear miss\xe2\x80\x9d in prod beta; breaker prevented runaway neighbor impact.\\n- The playbook of \xe2\x80\x9creduce caps -> drain queues -> disable burst for lowest tiers first\xe2\x80\x9d is operationally sound.\\n\\n2) Denial reason codes improved triage materially\\n\\n- Support/CS could distinguish \xe2\x80\x9ccap reached\xe2\x80\x9d vs \xe2\x80\x9cpool saturated\xe2\x80\x9d vs \xe2\x80\x9ccooldown\xe2\x80\x9d vs \xe2\x80\x9ctelemetry stale\xe2\x80\x9d.\\n- Routing logs + reason codes made it clear where to look first.\\n\\n3) Dashboards were mostly the right primitives\\n\\n- Saturation, queue depth, admit/deny rates, and p95 deltas by tenant were enough to debug 80% of issues.\\n- SLO gate signal (non-burst tenant latency regression) is a good north star even if thresholds need tuning.\\n\\n4) Rollout gating/feature flags prevented blast radius\\n\\n- Per-region allow list + per-tenant enablement meant we could pause EU-West issues without impacting US-East.\\n\\nWhat broke / painful incidents\\nA) Telemetry lag caused unfair denials + occasional starvation\\n\\n- Symptom: a few tenants saw denial spikes while pool appeared underutilized; the \xe2\x80\x9claggy\xe2\x80\x9d telemetry made the controller think they were still consuming burst tokens.\\n- Customer-visible effect: burst requests denied unexpectedly; some clients retried aggressively and amplified load.\\n- Root cause (likely): our admission controller relied on near-real-time usage signals; when signals were delayed, the smoothing window + token bucket refill behavior effectively penalized active tenants.\\n- Fix during beta: hotfix added smoothing + guard conditions + conservative fallback when telemetry freshness is below threshold.\\n- Ref: GitHub:redwood/pr-23944\\n\\nB) EU-West burst admission timeouts (controller dependency chain)\\n\\n- Symptom: admission calls intermittently timed out in eu-west under high request rate; routing fell back but increased gateway error rates briefly.\\n- Root cause: admission controller p99 latency spiked due to dependency (policy evaluation + fleet telemetry query) not being bounded; threadpool saturation.\\n- Mitigation: enable circuit breaker + temporarily tighten per-tenant caps + disable burst in eu-west allow list until patched.\\n- Ref: Jira:SUP-28579\\n\\nC) Billing: burst usage double-counted on retry paths", "score": 0.345806, "rank": 97, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "1c1211f3-dfc6-5a5e-8c3c-69e3558ddde0", "search_unit_id": "1c1211f3-dfc6-5a5e-8c3c-69e3558ddde0", "block_from_id": "6e8758de-6730-514b-860d-e68a310fe836", "block_to_id": "911dc9b1-d297-52fd-85d0-a9c7430e4fc9", "section_path": "Sup 3812 Northwind Dedicated Latency Spike During Interview Window", "document_id": "29cff449-edef-5f43-8bdf-540f01e450ce", "document_version_id": "13b1791c-7c70-53fe-bbcc-1a5e8649a6e6", "document_name": "jira__dsid_0c504258cd0c432d898e6711fb90eaf6.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "list-19", "heading": "Sup 3812 Northwind Dedicated Latency Spike During Interview Window", "content": "- We informed Northwind that we identified a transient capacity event (node drain) and applied safeguards to reduce the chance of recurrence. No action required on their side.\\n\\nResolution\\n\\n- Latency returned to baseline at 2025-10-09 17:10 UTC.\\n- Preventative changes are in place (min replicas + PDB + maintenance pause).\\n- Guidance provided to Marketing/SE to avoid quoting p95 captured during the impacted interval; use an agreed measurement window for publication.\\n\\nNext steps / action items\\n1) Infra: validate node warm-up time on Dedicated pools; ensure image pre-pull + model cache warm path for replacement nodes (linked PR: redwood-inference/infra#19344).\\n2) Runtime: add \\"pre-warm\\" hook to reduce first-request penalty after replica replacement (linked PR: redwood-runtime#8821).\\n3) Support Ops: add a checklist item for case study metric capture: confirm no maintenance/drain events in the selected interval.\\n\\nAttachments\\n\\n- Screenshots of queue depth + p95 charts saved in #support-internal thread (ref: Slack link in comments).\\n\\n2025-10-09 16:48 UTC \xe2\x80\x94 Miguel Santos (Reporter): Opening this because Marketing is collecting Northwind p95 for a public case study this week. Need to confirm whether this is a platform blip and whether to exclude this interval from any published metric.\\n2025-10-09 16:55 UTC \xe2\x80\x94 Simran Kaur (Assignee): Looking at Dedicated overview now. Seeing queue depth climb and ready replicas briefly below desired. No model rollout. Pulling k8s events next.\\n2025-10-09 17:06 UTC \xe2\x80\x94 Simran Kaur: K8s events show a gpu-node drain in nw-dedicated-us-east at ~16:49 UTC. One replica pending longer than usual (image pull). This aligns with p95 spike. Will coordinate with Infra to confirm patch/drain reason and ensure we avoid repeats during measurement.\\n2025-10-09 17:18 UTC \xe2\x80\x94 Markus Klein (Solutions Engineering): Thanks. For the case study we\xe2\x80\x99ll use a rolling 7-day window p95 and add a metrics disclaimer; we will not use the 16:45\xe2\x80\x9317:10 window. Please confirm once mitigations are in place so we can proceed with the interview summary.\\n2025-10-09 17:42 UTC \xe2\x80\x94 Simran Kaur: Mitigation proposal: temporarily raise min_replicas and tighten PDB for Northwind pool; also ask Infra to pause non-urgent maintenance through next Wednesday. Posting details in #support and will update ticket after change.\\n2025-10-10 14:05 UTC \xe2\x80\x94 Simran Kaur: Changes applied: min_replicas 6->8 (business hours), PDB updated. Infra confirmed drain was part of standard kernel patch cycle; they\xe2\x80\x99ve added Northwind pool to a temporary maintenance freeze list through 2025-10-16.\\n2025-10-12 09:30 UTC \xe2\x80\x94 Miguel Santos: Northwind confirmed latency has been stable since Thursday. Closing the loop for Marketing: safe to proceed with publication using rolling window + disclaimer.\\nResolved after identifying a transient Dedicated pool capacity reduction due to a GPU node drain/replacement, which temporarily reduced ready replicas and increased queue depth. Applied mitigations (higher min_replicas during business hours, PDB tightening, temporary maintenance freeze) and documented guidance to exclude the affected interval from case study metric capture. No further customer action required.", "score": 0.347404, "rank": 98, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "459ab52f-ad02-5124-a093-298963521de1", "search_unit_id": "459ab52f-ad02-5124-a093-298963521de1", "block_from_id": "695cf9e1-61e9-5d41-ba54-7b9c1379c661", "block_to_id": "695cf9e1-61e9-5d41-ba54-7b9c1379c661", "section_path": "Sliced Ingest 429S Dedicated Pool Priority Class Debug Notes", "document_id": "f554b405-fa78-5662-b771-484dbdbab9f3", "document_version_id": "38a32ec0-f3b7-5fcb-86a6-461c5475d650", "document_name": "google_drive__dsid_8ffbd9fe82df457ca67d4fbc9ff1090c.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "list-31", "heading": "Sliced Ingest 429S Dedicated Pool Priority Class Debug Notes", "content": "- Interactive endpoints (/v1/chat) see <0.1% 429 during scheduled flush windows for 3 consecutive days.\\n- Any remaining 429s are limited to background endpoints and include consistent headers / error codes enabling correct retry behavior.", "score": 0.336844, "rank": 99, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "73269d97-65e7-5254-b30b-a9475de2449d", "search_unit_id": "73269d97-65e7-5254-b30b-a9475de2449d", "block_from_id": "53bf8d3b-2114-59af-bb9a-5099e9665938", "block_to_id": "d8ca49fe-295d-56fa-9119-0eab79d8ab74", "section_path": "Sup 18421 Enterprise 429 Spike After Priority Routing Rollout > Actual behavior", "document_id": "89c06a4c-9104-5c7e-b766-1b1af3c247dd", "document_version_id": "c6465792-6750-5999-b72c-4196eb71504b", "document_name": "jira__dsid_1980f45c3fb4455f943a2b030892ae90.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-16", "heading": "Sup 18421 Enterprise 429 Spike After Priority Routing Rollout > Actual behavior", "content": "Actual behavior\\n\\n- Customer sees throttling during burst windows and requests clarification on:\\n  1) Why enterprise traffic is being rejected\\n  2) Whether the 429s are overload shedding vs quota\\n  3) Whether Redwood can grant a temporary burst exception / larger budget", "score": 0.356473, "rank": 100, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "38c6113b-64a6-5e6e-9740-cc3d24045319", "search_unit_id": "38c6113b-64a6-5e6e-9740-cc3d24045319", "block_from_id": "bdbceb1a-9cb0-5dd6-a170-217d38eca8c5", "block_to_id": "2a8a7ef0-605c-56a9-9aa4-0882536d6ed5", "section_path": "Sliced Ingest 429S Dedicated Pool Priority Class Debug Notes", "document_id": "f554b405-fa78-5662-b771-484dbdbab9f3", "document_version_id": "38a32ec0-f3b7-5fcb-86a6-461c5475d650", "document_name": "google_drive__dsid_8ffbd9fe82df457ca67d4fbc9ff1090c.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "list-16", "heading": "Sliced Ingest 429S Dedicated Pool Priority Class Debug Notes", "content": "- A burst-pool weight recalculation occurs on route attach to a dedicated pool.\\n- Under high fanout (multipart chunk completion callbacks + interactive requests), an attach sequence can read stale bucket state and allow background borrow to temporarily draw from the interactive pool\xe2\x80\x99s credits.\\n- Separately, when the edge proxy is under callback-related backpressure, it can return 429 before the normal header-enrichment path runs, producing opaque 429s.\\n\\nMitigations applied / tested (new since ticket)\\nTemporary customer-facing workaround (still valid)\\n\\n- Stagger background multipart uploads and add jitter to the flush window.\\n- Reduce background upload concurrency (Streamly tested <= 6 workers) during peak interactive usage.\\n\\nConfig change (applied on 2026-03-12 call)\\n\\n- On dp-132-usw we reserved burst capacity explicitly for interactive routes.\\n- Updated reservation target: reserve 30% (previous internal suggestion was 20%) of interactive burst credits exclusively for priority=high routes.\\n- Also separated the route groups so callbacks cannot share the same per-route window as interactive endpoints.\\n\\nPatch status (updated)\\n\\n- Rate-limiter fix was originally tracked as \xe2\x80\x9ctarget 1.14.1\xe2\x80\x9d.\\n- As of 2026-03-19: fix is in 1.14.1-rc2 and deployed to canary in us-west dedicated pools (including dp-132-usw) for validation.\\n- Early results: eliminates the negative token deltas we were seeing during attach bursts; interactive 429 rate decreased materially during the flush window.\\n\\nResults from customer validation (new)\\n\\n- 2026-03-12 (trial during scheduled flush):\\n  - Interactive error rate improved; remaining 429s clustered around callback spikes.\\n  - Customer confirmed jitter reduced the \xe2\x80\x9call-at-once\xe2\x80\x9d failure mode.\\n- 2026-03-20 (post 1.14.1-rc2 canary + reserved burst config still in place):\\n  - No sustained 429s for /v1/chat during the 09:00\xe2\x80\x9310:00 window.\\n  - A few 429s remain on background /v1/embed chunk callbacks (expected; acceptable if retried with backoff).\\n\\nOpen questions / TODO\\n\\n- Edge proxy: ensure X-RateLimit headers are emitted even under backpressure for 429s originating from rate-limiter.\\n- Confirm whether signed URL callback endpoints should be assigned their own priority class (e.g., \xe2\x80\x9ccallback-low\xe2\x80\x9d) distinct from generic background.\\n- Add a regression test: attach burst + high fanout + two priority classes should never allow cross-pool borrow that depletes interactive tokens.\\n\\nRecommended next steps (updated plan)\\n1) Keep the reserved interactive burst split (30%) for dedicated pools with mixed interactive + bulk ingest traffic.\\n2) Promote rate-limiter 1.14.1 from rc to stable after 48h canary observation (watch: rlim.route.tokens.available deltas, interactive 429 rate, attach timing).\\n3) Edge proxy change: always populate rate-limit headers (or at least a stable error code) for 429s; avoid \xe2\x80\x9copaque 429\xe2\x80\x9d.\\n4) Update the support playbook page with explicit guidance for signed URL callback fanout (jitter + concurrency caps + route separation).\\n5) Schedule a controlled load test with Streamly AI for 3 consecutive peak windows and collect traces + request IDs for closure criteria.\\n\\nNotes / timeline crumbs (for internal use)\\n\\n- 2026-03-10: incident window; strong correlation with cron flush.\\n- 2026-03-11: Eng suspected weight inversion / attach sequence sensitivity.\\n- 2026-03-12: customer call; implemented burst reservation + client-side jitter.\\n- 2026-03-19: 1.14.1-rc2 canary deployed to us-west dedicated.\\n- 2026-03-20: customer reports no sustained interactive 429s during flush.\\n\\nClosure criteria (for SUP-864999)", "score": 0.339465, "rank": 101, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "88bfcf97-3010-5c72-be76-1a321d0b50c6", "search_unit_id": "88bfcf97-3010-5c72-be76-1a321d0b50c6", "block_from_id": "def0563d-82be-502d-8930-598dfc6881b3", "block_to_id": "d2df9593-73ac-5d13-9344-e67f1d0a3838", "section_path": "Burst Capacity Retro Notes", "document_id": "055d3522-4e6b-5ab7-a0f7-0224afbd508e", "document_version_id": "f52eceb0-167c-5e90-84d2-0bc653ae49c8", "document_name": "google_drive__dsid_95c7aa5238f94570a8e0a8684736b666.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "list-61", "heading": "Burst Capacity Retro Notes", "content": "- Today we mostly deny quickly; some customers want queuing.\\n- SRE view: queuing can hide fleet pain and increase tail; if we add queueing it must be bounded with clear max wait + cancellation and must not break determinism.\\n\\n2) How much \xe2\x80\x9cdegraded mode\xe2\x80\x9d behavior is acceptable?\\n\\n- If telemetry is stale, do we:\\n  a) deny all burst? (safe but may be overly strict)\\n  b) allow small burst with conservative cap? (better UX)\\n  c) continue as-is with risk? (not acceptable)\\n- Tentative: (b) with tight global caps + explicit surfaced state.\\n\\n3) GA scope: region/hardware allow list\\n\\n- Recommendation: GA in us-east H100 first; add eu-west after EU-West latency fix + game day rerun.\\n\\nAppendix: customer support learnings (from tickets)\\n\\n- SUP-28411 (unexpected denials): customers interpret \xe2\x80\x9cburst enabled\xe2\x80\x9d as \xe2\x80\x9cguaranteed extra capacity.\xe2\x80\x9d We must emphasize \xe2\x80\x9cbest-effort within guardrails,\xe2\x80\x9d with clear reasons.\\n- SUP-28503 (429s): customers need explicit guidance on client-side backoff, concurrency control, and how to detect \xe2\x80\x9cyou are at cap.\xe2\x80\x9d\\n- SUP-28488 (billing question): customers will audit the burst line item against their own job schedule; we need accurate exports + transparent calculation.\\n\\nNext steps\\n\\n- Rafael to turn P0s into a GA gate checklist and review in SRE staff meeting (2025-06-04).\\n- Schedule a short follow-up retro after GA week 1 to validate assumptions and update runbooks.", "score": 0.314424, "rank": 102, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "f46c4ba8-acff-59bb-8c27-9220cdeddcb0", "search_unit_id": "f46c4ba8-acff-59bb-8c27-9220cdeddcb0", "block_from_id": "3369b0e3-ccd4-536d-ad1f-a8f23c9764ac", "block_to_id": "3c097507-1408-5e77-886c-dbbc236d4a30", "section_path": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Escalation > Templates (copy/paste) > Template A \xe2\x80\x94 Initial acknowledgement (suspected throughput miss)", "document_id": "2aced3e3-1ed9-50b2-9ef8-a037c40fcfaf", "document_version_id": "5b0b49bf-5c10-5f9e-b6f9-1ae199865977", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_409618a3958944f9b78362ed7e01bb33.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-83", "heading": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Escalation > Templates (copy/paste) > Template A \xe2\x80\x94 Initial acknowledgement (suspected throughput miss)", "content": "Templates (copy/paste)\\n\\nTemplate A \xe2\x80\x94 Initial acknowledgement (suspected throughput miss)\\n\\nSubject: Dedicated deployment throughput investigation (Deployment {{deployment_id}})\\n\\nHi {{customer_name}},\\n\\nThanks for flagging this. We\xe2\x80\x99re investigating reduced throughput on your Dedicated deployment ({{deployment_id}}) in {{region}}.\\n\\nTo help us confirm impact and correlate logs/metrics, could you share:\\n\\n- The approximate start time and whether the issue is ongoing\\n- The model + version you\xe2\x80\x99re using\\n- A representative request ID (or a small set) from the impacted window\\n- Any recent traffic/concurrency changes on your side\\n\\nWe\xe2\x80\x99ll provide the next update by {{time}}.\\nBest,\\n{{agent_name}}", "score": 0.308178, "rank": 103, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "a6065907-1903-5b5e-bbdf-2d8f3e83c940", "search_unit_id": "a6065907-1903-5b5e-bbdf-2d8f3e83c940", "block_from_id": "0c2b8211-3c07-529a-838b-580603568a6b", "block_to_id": "9555bfc3-8e5e-5c74-857a-d7c54358bdbc", "section_path": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > SLOs (targets) > General notes", "document_id": "12efb103-6e2e-5955-be3e-431b65ac2bca", "document_version_id": "78cfdc0b-41fe-5614-b19f-c7d639aaa33b", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_49fa5cfd95624dbeb6eaa535f9959470.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-41", "heading": "Hosted Api Slos Enterprise Route Tiers > SLOs (targets) > General notes", "content": "SLOs (targets)\\n\\nGeneral notes\\n\\n- SLOs are evaluated over a **rolling 28-day window**.\\n- Unless specified, targets apply per region (e.g., us-east, eu-west).\\n- We define both **p95 and p99** latency objectives for enterprise; best-effort uses p95 as primary.", "score": 0.312588, "rank": 104, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "d18f361d-677e-5ddd-83c6-85d124171be6", "search_unit_id": "d18f361d-677e-5ddd-83c6-85d124171be6", "block_from_id": "6b0aa3a3-4a20-50e1-af05-8c82c7819cb3", "block_to_id": "76a4f7c2-cda8-5771-b381-884d8e758c25", "section_path": "Sup 18421 Enterprise 429 Spike After Priority Routing Rollout > Issue summary", "document_id": "89c06a4c-9104-5c7e-b766-1b1af3c247dd", "document_version_id": "c6465792-6750-5999-b72c-4196eb71504b", "document_name": "jira__dsid_1980f45c3fb4455f943a2b030892ae90.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-2", "heading": "Sup 18421 Enterprise 429 Spike After Priority Routing Rollout > Issue summary", "content": "Issue summary\\n\\nCustomer (Proxima Bank) reports a sharp increase in HTTP 429 responses starting ~2026-01-20 09:10 PT on /v1/chat/completions and /v1/embeddings. They believe this correlates with Redwood\xe2\x80\x99s recent \xe2\x80\x9cpriority routing / capacity protection\xe2\x80\x9d changes.", "score": 0.338068, "rank": 105, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "d4d28dbf-f9ec-5e64-81cc-730e9fc10ed1", "search_unit_id": "d4d28dbf-f9ec-5e64-81cc-730e9fc10ed1", "block_from_id": "82335435-9c35-5b4e-8472-b32d274cf408", "block_to_id": "44984730-99d8-5d84-a2fe-54d8b24e2cd5", "section_path": "Go Live Protocols Cross Org Communications Escalation 2027", "document_id": "c1cb92b0-f42c-5fdf-9753-22cf1f569b80", "document_version_id": "a96eb210-903b-56d1-a33c-9bd4347e8b15", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_9c8cb10d65324c03a8ededbd98c1b058.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "tbl-11", "heading": "Go Live Protocols Cross Org Communications Escalation 2027", "content": "| Level | Trigger example | Primary contact | Secondary contact | Action required |\\n|-------|-----------------|-----------------|-------------------|-----------------|\\n| L1 (CSM) | Customer questions, minor errors | CSM | SE | Acknowledge to customer within 15m, open internal thread |\\n| L2 (SRE) | Error rate >5% | Oncall SRE | SRE Lead | Execute diagnostics, apply mitigations (rate-limit, route switch) |\\n| L3 (SE/PM) | Persistent degradation >30m | Solutions Engineer | Product Manager | Coordinate config changes, model fallback, communicate to customer executives |\\n| L4 (Exec) | SLA breach or major incident | Head of Customer Success | Head of Engineering | Declare incident, engage legal/finance if SLA credits likely |\\n\\nNote: The table above is intended as a quick reference. The full escalation paging list with phone numbers and PagerDuty IDs is stored in /confluence/people-ops/rosters and in the private channel pinned messages.\\n\\nCommon Mitigations (ordered by lowest customer impact first)\\n----------------------------------------------------------\\n\\n- Increase routing to a higher-capacity pool (if Dedicated: burst pool)\\n- Apply tenant-level throttles to protect shared resources (quota reduce to 30% of projected steady-state)\\n- Enable model fallback: route to last-known-stable model snapshot\\n- Temporarily switch customer to hosted API endpoint (if allowed by contract)\\n- Quiesce long-running batch jobs from the customer side\\n\\nRollback and Safe-fail Strategies\\n--------------------------------\\n\\n1. Safe rollback to previous routing config (SE executes):\\n   - Command: curl -X POST https://ops-api.internal.redwood/v1/route/revert -d \'{\\"tenant\\":\\"<tenant-id>\\",\\"to_config\\":\\"<previous-config-id>\\"}\' \\n   - Confirm routing table via: kubectl get svc ingress-<tenant> -o yaml (requires ops role)\\n2. Tenant quiesce API (customer-facing graceful shutdown):\\n   - POST https://api.redwood.com/v1/tenants/<tenant>/quiesce?actor=ops\\n   - Expected behavior: existing requests drain, new requests return 429 with Retry-After header.\\n3. Emergency switchback to hosted region: update DNS TTL and apply traffic policy TTL=30s for faster re-routing.\\n\\nCommunication Templates\\n-----------------------\\n\\n- Customer-facing 1-line (acknowledge): \\"We are investigating increased latency for your production routes and will provide an update within 15 minutes. No action required from your side unless we ask.\\"\\n- Customer-facing status update (10m cadence): include short bullet list: observed metric, action taken, owner, ETA for next update.\\n- Internal P0 page message sample:\\n  - \\"P0: <customer> production routes returning 503s since 08:12 UTC. Oncall SRE investigating. Initial hypothesis: ingress proxy thread exhaustion. Paging SRE Lead and SE. Next update in 5 minutes\\"\\n\\nTelemetry checks / Queries (examples)\\n----------------------------------\\n\\n- PromQL (errors): sum(rate(http_requests_total{tenant=\\"<tenant>\\",status=~\\"5..\\"}[5m])) / sum(rate(http_requests_total{tenant=\\"<tenant>\\"}[5m])) > 0.05\\n- Token consumption anomaly (example):\\n  - Query: increase(token_count_total{tenant=\\"<tenant>\\"}[30m]) / expected_token_rate > 2.0\\n- Synthetic validation command (curl):\\n  - curl -s -X POST https://api.redwood.com/v1/generate -H \\"Authorization: Bearer <key>\\" -d \'{\\"input\\":\\"synthetic health check\\"}\' | jq \'.latency_ms\'\\n\\nPost-Cutover (T+4 to T+72 hours)\\n--------------------------------\\n\\n- Stability window monitoring: SRE to keep watch for 72 hours; escalate on any SLO breach.\\n- Cost and quota reconciliation: Finance Ops and CSM to review token consumption, forecast delta, and apply quota protections if needed.\\n- Customer QBR prep: collect first-week metrics and any incident notes for the 30-day QBR.\\n\\nRunbook Ownership and RACI\\n-------------------------", "score": 0.302813, "rank": 106, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "e4fb0db7-0d72-513c-864b-54ea9c98779a", "search_unit_id": "e4fb0db7-0d72-513c-864b-54ea9c98779a", "block_from_id": "aa4fd05e-834d-5c0d-94cf-fbfffbf445a6", "block_to_id": "d6208276-c1f2-59e6-8701-5418155a267e", "section_path": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Escalation > Triage checklist (Support/CS) > 2) Confirm the customer is driving enough load", "document_id": "2aced3e3-1ed9-50b2-9ef8-a037c40fcfaf", "document_version_id": "5b0b49bf-5c10-5f9e-b6f9-1ae199865977", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_409618a3958944f9b78362ed7e01bb33.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-51", "heading": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Escalation > Triage checklist (Support/CS) > 2) Confirm the customer is driving enough load", "content": "2) Confirm the customer is driving enough load\\n\\nAsk:\\n\\n- Current request concurrency\\n- Any client-side rate limiting (explicit QPS cap, worker pool limit)\\n- Any recent application deploy or traffic change\\n\\nIf the customer is under-driving load, throughput may be low without being an SLO failure.", "score": 0.303322, "rank": 107, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "d74399fb-275f-5fdf-8f6b-ee7950d57fd6", "search_unit_id": "d74399fb-275f-5fdf-8f6b-ee7950d57fd6", "block_from_id": "904875f5-0603-5f66-b912-4a16526b6a7f", "block_to_id": "808f8a13-a2a5-5c77-a49d-285a0f26dfa4", "section_path": "Postmortem Perf Canary False Positive Alert Storm > Timeline (UTC)", "document_id": "020f768f-b114-5ca7-94ee-64447a4e421c", "document_version_id": "aac8c399-c8c4-5179-91b0-c93e8328a769", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_58bdb9f3bb4241e089f4f2b102765db4.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-14", "heading": "Postmortem Perf Canary False Positive Alert Storm > Timeline (UTC)", "content": "Timeline (UTC)\\n\\n- **2026-02-12 16:05** \xe2\x80\x93 Expanded canary coverage from 3 \xe2\x86\x92 8 models in us-east; enabled paging rule for \xe2\x80\x9ctop models\xe2\x80\x9d.\\n- **16:12** \xe2\x80\x93 First warning alerts fire for 3 models (p95 latency regression > threshold). Incident-bot starts threads.\\n- **16:16** \xe2\x80\x93 Paging alerts begin for a top model; on-call acknowledges.\\n- **16:18** \xe2\x80\x93 Additional paging alerts fire for multiple models; dedupe fails and creates multiple parallel threads.\\n- **16:23** \xe2\x80\x93 Runtime on-call compares canary runtime-only latency vs customer e2e; no matching regression observed.\\n- **16:28** \xe2\x80\x93 Telemetry team notices time series fragmentation: unexpected label combinations, very low samples per series.\\n- **16:34** \xe2\x80\x93 SRE disables paging rules (keeps warnings) and increases dampening window.\\n- **16:41** \xe2\x80\x93 Baseline store reads show frequent \xe2\x80\x9cbaseline not found\xe2\x80\x9d with fallback to default thresholds.\\n- **16:46** \xe2\x80\x93 Canary config rolled back to previous 3-model set; alert volume drops.\\n- **17:02** \xe2\x80\x93 Incident declared resolved; follow-up investigation assigned.", "score": 0.298497, "rank": 108, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "2e615fe1-08e1-5708-8bc2-6ac939ff2358", "search_unit_id": "2e615fe1-08e1-5708-8bc2-6ac939ff2358", "block_from_id": "cdf4547a-f291-5150-8a52-5e428cf23ee2", "block_to_id": "4666e94d-af90-5fd1-ae22-f3a1eb902904", "section_path": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Escalation > Severity levels (Support/CS view)", "document_id": "2aced3e3-1ed9-50b2-9ef8-a037c40fcfaf", "document_version_id": "5b0b49bf-5c10-5f9e-b6f9-1ae199865977", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_409618a3958944f9b78362ed7e01bb33.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-29", "heading": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Escalation > Severity levels (Support/CS view)", "content": "Severity levels (Support/CS view)\\n\\nUse these severities to standardize response; SRE may map to their incident severities.\\n\\n**SEV-1 (Critical, likely customer-impacting breach)**\\n\\n- Premium tier customer reports sustained throughput below floor OR SRE burn-rate alert is firing.\\n- Clear production impact (e.g., queue buildup, timeouts, user-visible latency spikes).\\n- Immediate paging required.\\n\\n**SEV-2 (High, likely degradation / at-risk of breach)**\\n\\n- Throughput is below expected but not yet confirmed as an SLO breach.\\n- Customer impact is moderate OR limited to a subset of traffic/routes.\\n- Paging may be required depending on tier and duration.\\n\\n**SEV-3 (Moderate, investigation / metrics discrepancy)**\\n\\n- Conflicting metrics (customer vs Redwood dashboards), short-lived dips, or suspected measurement artifacts.\\n- No clear production impact.\\n\\n---", "score": 0.300708, "rank": 109, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "8e9f1e66-1230-5e46-9a60-b07176885bf7", "search_unit_id": "8e9f1e66-1230-5e46-9a60-b07176885bf7", "block_from_id": "f0b82f5c-4a65-5f05-9411-e2edd86ec289", "block_to_id": "26420bd5-4db2-56e6-900b-daaa7c429262", "section_path": "Sup 18421 Enterprise 429 Spike After Priority Routing Rollout > What customer is seeing", "document_id": "89c06a4c-9104-5c7e-b766-1b1af3c247dd", "document_version_id": "c6465792-6750-5999-b72c-4196eb71504b", "document_name": "jira__dsid_1980f45c3fb4455f943a2b030892ae90.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-8", "heading": "Sup 18421 Enterprise 429 Spike After Priority Routing Rollout > What customer is seeing", "content": "What customer is seeing\\n\\n- 429 response body includes Redwood reason codes (examples pasted by customer):\\n  - \\"reason\\": \\"overload_protection_admission\\"\\n  - \\"reason\\": \\"quota_exceeded\\" (less frequent)\\n- Response headers observed:\\n  - Retry-After: 1 (sometimes 2)\\n  - X-Redwood-Request-Id present\\n  - X-Redwood-Throttle-Reason: overload_protection_admission (most common)", "score": 0.314706, "rank": 110, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "db5d3424-8823-576a-ba75-1207e8f0eb24", "search_unit_id": "db5d3424-8823-576a-ba75-1207e8f0eb24", "block_from_id": "64a984fc-6b61-51c6-a7ee-2db1e8bffc12", "block_to_id": "182daf0b-c726-5b2a-95d1-a8c35d4cde9b", "section_path": "20260828 Crucible Inference Sitrep", "document_id": "f3f0dff2-64f2-519c-8ff2-bb1bb02e1099", "document_version_id": "738452de-9c17-5e45-975f-409192701b08", "document_name": "gmail__dsid_5611f05242ae4e6b9ae68d3c3e91bd54.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h1-0", "heading": "20260828 Crucible Inference Sitrep", "content": "20260828 Crucible Inference Sitrep\\n\\nSource type: gmail\\nDocument ID: dsid_5611f05242ae4e6b9ae68d3c3e91bd54\\nRelease: v1.0.0\\nBenchmark: EnterpriseRAG-Bench synthetic upstream data\\nRe: Critical: inference latency spike + 5xx affecting throughput (Crucible Health)\\n\\nFrom: Julia Adams <julia@cruciblehealth.com>\\nTo: Stephanie Nguyen <stephanie_nguyen@redwood.com>\\nDate: 2026-08-28T08:12:00-07:00\\nSubject: Critical: sudden latency spike and 500s on inference endpoints\\n\\nHi Stephanie,\\n\\nWe started seeing a large increase in end-to-end latency and a burst of 5xx errors on the /v1/generate endpoint at ~07:55 PDT. Throughput dropped by ~40% for our primary production inference queue and several batches timed out. This is causing timeouts in our user-facing flow and triggering throttles on our side.\\n\\nSymptoms observed:\\n\\n- Median latency jumped from ~120ms to ~900ms between 07:55\xe2\x80\x9308:05\\n- 500 responses: ~8% of requests in the window\\n- Throughput fall from 250 req/s to ~150 req/s for the Crucible production key\\n\\nWe attached recent logs (request IDs included) and a CSV snapshot of KPIs. Please escalate \xe2\x80\x94 this is blocking a critical demo at 10:00 PDT.\\n\\nSample request IDs: 6a4f9b2c, 6a4f9b45, 6a4f9c01\\n\\nAttachments: request_logs_20260828.zip, detailed_kpi_snapshot.csv\\n\\nThanks,\\nJulia Adams\\nHead of Integrations, Crucible Health\\n\\nFrom: Stephanie Nguyen <stephanie_nguyen@redwood.com>\\nTo: Marcus Lin <marcus_lin@redwood.com>, Hannah Schmitt <hannah_schmitt@redwood.com>, Laura Bennett <laura_bennett@redwood.com>, Sergio Alvarez <sergio_alvarez@redwood.com>\\nDate: 2026-08-28T08:25:00-07:00\\nSubject: Fwd: Critical: sudden latency spike and 500s on inference endpoints\\n\\nTeam \xe2\x80\x94 forwarding urgent customer report from Crucible Health (Julia).\\n\\nQuick asks: \\n1) Marcus, can infra/serving triage immediately (metrics + recent deploys + autoscaler behavior)?\\n2) Hannah, please own customer comms and schedule an executive sync at 09:00 PDT. They have a demo at 10:00 and want any mitigation now.\\n3) Laura / Sergio, finance/econ may need to approve priority capacity if we recommend burst dedicated capacity.\\n\\nI attached the logs Julia sent. Will coordinate a brief 30-min sync once Marcus confirms an ops hypothesis.\\n\\nAttachments (from customer): request_logs_20260828.zip, detailed_kpi_snapshot.csv\\n\\n\xe2\x80\x94 Stephanie Nguyen\\nAccount Executive, Redwood Inference\\nstephanie_nguyen@redwood.com\\n+1 415-555-0108\\n--- Forwarded message ---\\n> From: Julia Adams <julia@cruciblehealth.com>\\n> Date: 2026-08-28T08:12:00-07:00\\n> Subject: Critical: sudden latency spike and 500s on inference endpoints\\n> To: Stephanie Nguyen <stephanie_nguyen@redwood.com>\\n> \\n> [original message above]\\nFrom: Marcus Lin <marcus_lin@redwood.com>\\nTo: Stephanie Nguyen <stephanie_nguyen@redwood.com>, Hannah Schmitt <hannah_schmitt@redwood.com>, Laura Bennett <laura_bennett@redwood.com>\\nCc: Sergio Alvarez <sergio_alvarez@redwood.com>\\nDate: 2026-08-28T08:30:00-07:00\\nSubject: Re: Fwd: Critical: sudden latency spike and 500s on inference endpoints\\n\\nLooking at the log snippet and metrics in the linked Drive: two quick hypotheses \xe2\x80\x94 autoscaler oscillation under sudden burst + one failing shard (GPU OOM or degraded kernel) causing head-of-line blocking.\\n\\nImmediate actions I can kick off now:\\n\\n- Push an emergency scale to the dedicated pool serving Crucible (add +20% capacity) and temporarily increase max concurrency limits to reduce queueing.\\n- Route new requests to the 2ndary model variant (same family, lower context) as an immediate fallback to reduce per-token work.\\n- Isolate problematic instance(s) and drain to avoid repeated 5xx.\\n\\nI requested the SRE-run to execute scale and are running a quick health sweep across nodes for OOMs and kernel failures. ETA for first measurable improvement: ~8\xe2\x80\x9312 minutes after scale.\\n\\nI\'ll post an ops note to the Jira incident (INC-9821) and attach a short action plan.", "score": 0.317716, "rank": 111, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "1f7af1a5-ba9e-5e5a-af6f-4c7984e60d02", "search_unit_id": "1f7af1a5-ba9e-5e5a-af6f-4c7984e60d02", "block_from_id": "79d5f2dd-1ccc-5085-9cef-2caa44984d4a", "block_to_id": "e53151e6-16e3-52b0-9de7-924ff02db4da", "section_path": "Burst Capacity Retro Notes", "document_id": "055d3522-4e6b-5ab7-a0f7-0224afbd508e", "document_version_id": "f52eceb0-167c-5e90-84d2-0bc653ae49c8", "document_name": "google_drive__dsid_95c7aa5238f94570a8e0a8684736b666.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "list-19", "heading": "Burst Capacity Retro Notes", "content": "- Symptom: customer asked why burst line item didn\xe2\x80\x99t match dashboard usage; we found ~1.8\xe2\x80\x932.3% overcount in a specific retry pattern.\\n- Root cause: metering event emitted pre-idempotency; retried requests emitted duplicate usage increments.\\n- Fix: idempotency keys + reconciliation checks.\\n- Ref: GitHub:redwood/pr-24108, Jira:SUP-28488\\n\\nD) Noisy neighbor scare (tail latency increase for a non-burst tenant)\\n\\n- Symptom: p95/p99 latency increase for a non-burst tenant correlated with another tenant\xe2\x80\x99s burst window.\\n- What we learned: even if pool isolation is \xe2\x80\x9clogical,\xe2\x80\x9d shared control-plane/routing and some shared fleet components can cause tail amplification under saturation.\\n- Mitigation used: reduced global cap + increased cooldown for bursting tenant + forced routing fallback for that tenant for 30 min.\\n- Ref: Jira:SUP-28455\\n\\nE) Support confusion: 429s when burst cap reached (expected but poorly messaged)\\n\\n- We returned 429/overload when both baseline+burst caps were reached; some customers expected queuing instead.\\n- For GA we need crisp guidance: recommended retry/backoff strategy and what will/won\xe2\x80\x99t queue.\\n- Ref: Jira:SUP-28503\\n\\nGuardrails that were too strict (beta feedback)\\n1) Cooldown windows were overly punitive for spiky-but-legit workloads\\n\\n- Current behavior: once a tenant hits max burst ratio, cooldown kicks in even if they drop below quickly.\\n- Effect: denial bursts after short spikes; customers perceive \xe2\x80\x9cflaky burst.\xe2\x80\x9d\\n- Proposal: cooldown should be based on sustained saturation (e.g., N seconds over threshold) or make cooldown proportional to burst duration, not a flat window.\\n\\n2) Max burst ratio default (x1.5) was too conservative for \xe2\x80\x9cweekly batch\xe2\x80\x9d customers\\n\\n- Several beta tenants expected x2\xe2\x80\x93x3 for short windows (10\xe2\x80\x9330 min) and were willing to pay.\\n- But we must pair higher ratios with stronger fairness + fleet safety + SLO gates.\\n\\n3) Admission determinism vs latency tradeoff\\n\\n- We enforced determinism by evaluating multiple signals synchronously; in some cases this added controller latency and increased timeouts.\\n- Suggestion: determinism requirement should be: \xe2\x80\x9csame inputs => same decision\xe2\x80\x9d but inputs can be snapshot-based (cached) within a bounded TTL.\\n\\nGuardrails that were too lax (or missing)\\n1) Retry amplification guard\\n\\n- When customers got denials, some clients retried immediately with high concurrency.\\n- We need an explicit \xe2\x80\x9cretry-after\xe2\x80\x9d / backoff hint tied to denial reason codes, and optionally server-side throttling at gateway.\\n\\n2) Alerting gaps for \xe2\x80\x9ctelemetry freshness\xe2\x80\x9d and \xe2\x80\x9cadmission controller dependency health\xe2\x80\x9d\\n\\n- We lacked a strong, paging-quality indicator for telemetry freshness degradation (it showed in graphs but didn\xe2\x80\x99t page until customers complained).\\n- Admission controller dependency p99 did not have an error-budget aligned alert.\\n\\n3) Fairness under mixed priority tiers needs stronger anti-starvation proofs\\n\\n- We introduced priority tiers late in beta; we didn\xe2\x80\x99t run enough multi-tenant stress tests for gold/silver combos.\\n- Need explicit starvation bound (e.g., min guaranteed tokens) and test harness enforcement.\\n\\n4) Config propagation / console staleness\\n\\n- One customer ticket: burst limit updated but Console showed stale value for too long.\\n- Root cause likely caching/prop delay; not a safety issue but it undermines trust.\\n- Ref: Jira:SUP-28541\\n\\nKey metrics from beta (rough, from dashboards)", "score": 0.291504, "rank": 112, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "2eb4b252-68ef-539a-af06-30d955d63cc2", "search_unit_id": "2eb4b252-68ef-539a-af06-30d955d63cc2", "block_from_id": "0925f911-fc30-57e5-81fe-e947937af9eb", "block_to_id": "f5bf24d4-b979-5a2e-ae4b-6b3d1f7fcadf", "section_path": "Sup 28463 Double Charged For Retries", "document_id": "f32b26b3-6dd1-521a-8be7-4ece35353c67", "document_version_id": "87f5b416-505d-5ae8-8d73-fff770f45c01", "document_name": "jira__dsid_2f1f0358b85148f19d38f619891bfb15.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h1-0", "heading": "Sup 28463 Double Charged For Retries", "content": "Sup 28463 Double Charged For Retries\\n\\nSource type: jira\\nDocument ID: dsid_2f1f0358b85148f19d38f619891bfb15\\nRelease: v1.0.0\\nBenchmark: EnterpriseRAG-Bench synthetic upstream data\\nAcme AI reports being double-charged tokens on retry/fallback path (streaming) in Hosted API\\n\\nIssue summary:\\nAcme AI reports that token usage on invoice preview is higher than their own aggregation of the Usage API for the same time window. They believe they are being billed twice when (a) their client retries after timeouts/5xx and/or (b) Redwood performs a server-side fallback retry during streaming.\\n\\nImpact:\\n\\n- Customer-reported overcharge risk and trust hit; potential credit request.\\n- Meter integrity risk: duplicate usage events on retry/fallback path may skew billing, pricing experiments, and gross margin model.\\n\\nCustomer evidence (from Support):\\n\\n- Spike in billed tokens on 2026-02-06 around 19:00\xe2\x80\x9321:00 UTC.\\n- Correlates to elevated 502/timeout errors and client retry behavior.\\n- Requests primarily via OpenAI-compatible /v1/chat/completions with stream=true.\\n\\nEnvironment:\\n\\n- Prod, region us-east\\n- Models: llama-3.1-70b primary, fallback variant enabled by routing policy\\n\\nSuspected failure mode:\\nUsage event dedupe/idempotency for streaming retry/fallback path is insufficient. When an initial attempt emits partial tokens and then triggers an internal retry/fallback (or when the gateway retries before completion), both attempts may be marked billable and included in the billing aggregation, resulting in double-counting for a single logical client request.\\n\\nInitial triage links:\\n\\n- Slack: sources/slack/support/1770083321-customer-reporting-double-charged-retries.json\\n- Related Eng work: pr-27463 (retry double-count fix), perf-canary metering scenarios (pr-912)", "score": 0.289735, "rank": 113, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "781870d5-debd-5c2c-b230-a93aa740c4c1", "search_unit_id": "781870d5-debd-5c2c-b230-a93aa740c4c1", "block_from_id": "e7e81127-efe5-530a-b1b7-256c4ee5a009", "block_to_id": "5ea079cb-0fef-5bcc-825d-a75df45a30d0", "section_path": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Definition > Burn-rate alerting > Alert routing", "document_id": "19c5a655-72be-5ec3-9113-0a8f67b72c9f", "document_version_id": "2e39e4c6-4649-5fd6-9e3d-ebf763331245", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_e33c122264214846a8b7df7701d31482.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-90", "heading": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Definition > Burn-rate alerting > Alert routing", "content": "Alert routing\\n\\n- Page: `#eng-oncall` and oncall rotation \xe2\x80\x9cSRE - Inference Platform\xe2\x80\x9d\\n- Auto-attach runbook link: \xe2\x80\x9cDedicated Throughput SLO Escalation\xe2\x80\x9d\\n- Auto-include fields: `deployment_id`, `customer_name` (if available), `tier`, `region`, current `observed_tps`, `committed_tps`, top rejection reasons, saturation signals", "score": 0.289387, "rank": 114, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "de86786d-f40b-5884-89f4-9d8399ae0699", "search_unit_id": "de86786d-f40b-5884-89f4-9d8399ae0699", "block_from_id": "5a698eaa-0691-536a-9270-379ee96a445c", "block_to_id": "5a698eaa-0691-536a-9270-379ee96a445c", "section_path": "Sup 864999 Sliced Ingest Priority Class Throttle Config", "document_id": "d9afbdee-1747-5299-966e-78fdaa3c1cf3", "document_version_id": "7d1ff66b-6a87-5e79-a4fb-df1cc60b4124", "document_name": "jira__dsid_5f3a672da4974781a5577b0f3d4993e9.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "p-13", "heading": "Sup 864999 Sliced Ingest Priority Class Throttle Config", "content": "3) Medium-term: devs to backport fix to rate-limiter that prevents borrow from reading stale burst-pool state; target patch 1.14.1.\\n4) Medium-term: instrument edge proxy to always emit X-RateLimit headers even when returning 429 due to callback fanout backpressure.\\n5) Follow-up: run controlled load test with customer after config change and collect traces for 3 consecutive peak windows.", "score": 0.284762, "rank": 115, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "b9038493-5fb3-5ff1-adfd-a65a81e2fffc", "search_unit_id": "b9038493-5fb3-5ff1-adfd-a65a81e2fffc", "block_from_id": "46e6fcf0-e199-5b3d-af0c-ea67bc0ab9e1", "block_to_id": "ce50aab8-e958-5bb3-b105-184243cd579f", "section_path": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > Common customer questions + approved responses > \xe2\x80\x9cCan you increase my limits?\xe2\x80\x9d", "document_id": "d1667e56-b0c3-5fb4-a769-9f13a8679e2f", "document_version_id": "a9986e4a-2cf0-5373-a739-d5611a7a824c", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_ca42a8f18d784c879c5a6fcfe3a484c0.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-62", "heading": "Overload Errors And Retry Guidance > Common customer questions + approved responses > \xe2\x80\x9cCan you increase my limits?\xe2\x80\x9d", "content": "\xe2\x80\x9cCan you increase my limits?\xe2\x80\x9d\\n\\nGuidance:\\n\\n- Confirm whether they are hitting **429** (likely limit-related) vs **503** (regional/model saturation).\\n- If 429 is persistent at normal traffic levels, route to CS for capacity/tier discussion.\\n- If 503 is persistent, treat as reliability issue and escalate to Eng.", "score": 0.297601, "rank": 116, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "2cc9377f-67a0-5bcf-8183-ece562049b82", "search_unit_id": "2cc9377f-67a0-5bcf-8183-ece562049b82", "block_from_id": "9146d58d-26d8-5d0c-9c47-fc24004dd6ab", "block_to_id": "be7cf20c-a3a1-5637-b882-4edf8254663f", "section_path": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Escalation > Templates (copy/paste) > Template B \xe2\x80\x94 Confirmed incident + engaged on-call", "document_id": "2aced3e3-1ed9-50b2-9ef8-a037c40fcfaf", "document_version_id": "5b0b49bf-5c10-5f9e-b6f9-1ae199865977", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_409618a3958944f9b78362ed7e01bb33.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-91", "heading": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Escalation > Templates (copy/paste) > Template B \xe2\x80\x94 Confirmed incident + engaged on-call", "content": "Template B \xe2\x80\x94 Confirmed incident + engaged on-call\\n\\nSubject: Update: Dedicated throughput degradation (Deployment {{deployment_id}})\\n\\nHi {{customer_name}},\\n\\nWe\xe2\x80\x99ve confirmed a throughput degradation affecting your Dedicated deployment ({{deployment_id}}). We\xe2\x80\x99ve engaged our Site Reliability Engineering and Infrastructure on-call teams and are actively mitigating.\\n\\nCurrent status:\\n\\n- Impact: {{impact_summary}}\\n- Start time (UTC): {{start_time_utc}}\\n- Mitigation in progress: {{mitigation_summary}}\\n\\nNext update by {{time}}.\\nBest,\\n{{agent_name}}", "score": 0.307823, "rank": 117, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "26bb1371-d4e1-5d4f-b91c-6ef36f6114d5", "search_unit_id": "26bb1371-d4e1-5d4f-b91c-6ef36f6114d5", "block_from_id": "3f85792b-ea6a-5352-afd8-4f9a111c5e47", "block_to_id": "99127fce-0cb1-5909-a804-8ecc038d0acf", "section_path": "Sup 18421 Enterprise 429 Spike After Priority Routing Rollout > Customer notes", "document_id": "89c06a4c-9104-5c7e-b766-1b1af3c247dd", "document_version_id": "c6465792-6750-5999-b72c-4196eb71504b", "document_name": "jira__dsid_1980f45c3fb4455f943a2b030892ae90.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-10", "heading": "Sup 18421 Enterprise 429 Spike After Priority Routing Rollout > Customer notes", "content": "Customer notes\\n\\n- They run bursty workloads: 10\xe2\x80\x9330s spikes during market-open events.\\n- Client currently retries immediately with fixed 100ms delay and no jitter.", "score": 0.30195, "rank": 118, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "918b8002-0348-5561-9510-1c4509968098", "search_unit_id": "918b8002-0348-5561-9510-1c4509968098", "block_from_id": "7c4d27a8-8885-563d-a66f-f1026b90f1a7", "block_to_id": "fb09b8d6-3283-5191-b8d8-a2089b059f1e", "section_path": "Postmortem Perf Canary False Positive Alert Storm > Detection", "document_id": "020f768f-b114-5ca7-94ee-64447a4e421c", "document_version_id": "aac8c399-c8c4-5179-91b0-c93e8328a769", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_58bdb9f3bb4241e089f4f2b102765db4.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-12", "heading": "Postmortem Perf Canary False Positive Alert Storm > Detection", "content": "Detection\\n\\n- Detected via automated paging to the on-call channel and incident-bot threads.\\n- Secondary signal: Grafana dashboard showed many \xe2\x80\x9cregression detected\xe2\x80\x9d annotations without corresponding customer latency change.", "score": 0.290558, "rank": 119, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "17d628e9-e2bb-54f0-aaa9-04f6cff1f5c6", "search_unit_id": "17d628e9-e2bb-54f0-aaa9-04f6cff1f5c6", "block_from_id": "947e3463-cf65-52cf-aeba-ec29d28e5d30", "block_to_id": "567b9445-f73d-5d9d-a334-719d01923415", "section_path": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Baseline Computation > Fixed baseline artifact", "document_id": "c055d063-a555-5cdf-b541-fe036031cb1b", "document_version_id": "57c83493-8d9e-5467-903c-46c7977b91fb", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a3f94972391244f38ef773c05926af83.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-35", "heading": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Baseline Computation > Fixed baseline artifact", "content": "Baseline Computation\\n\\nFixed baseline artifact\\n\\n- Source: controlled perf-canary runs using the approved synthetic prompt distribution.\\n- For each baseline key, we store:\\n  - distribution summaries (p50/p95/p99) for each latency metric\\n  - mean and standard deviation where stable enough to be meaningful\\n  - tokens/sec distribution summaries\\n  - run metadata (time range, sample count, canary config version)\\n- Baseline validity: baseline is considered valid only if it meets minimum sample size requirements (see below) and passes sanity checks (no telemetry gaps, no error-rate spikes).", "score": 0.277994, "rank": 120, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "9cf737b0-9887-5e68-a33c-8d788f5fb00b", "search_unit_id": "9cf737b0-9887-5e68-a33c-8d788f5fb00b", "block_from_id": "25bed8be-de77-5220-822a-4ff8b6015326", "block_to_id": "c8d2b4dc-ea00-5696-b452-3e87fe69b1ca", "section_path": "Sup 18421 Enterprise 429 Spike After Priority Routing Rollout", "document_id": "89c06a4c-9104-5c7e-b766-1b1af3c247dd", "document_version_id": "c6465792-6750-5999-b72c-4196eb71504b", "document_name": "jira__dsid_1980f45c3fb4455f943a2b030892ae90.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h1-0", "heading": "Sup 18421 Enterprise 429 Spike After Priority Routing Rollout", "content": "Sup 18421 Enterprise 429 Spike After Priority Routing Rollout\\n\\nSource type: jira\\nDocument ID: dsid_1980f45c3fb4455f943a2b030892ae90\\nRelease: v1.0.0\\nBenchmark: EnterpriseRAG-Bench synthetic upstream data\\nProxima Bank: unexpected spike in 429s after enterprise priority routing rollout (chat + embeddings)", "score": 0.310251, "rank": 121, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "5e6f8b25-19fd-5b68-a18e-c944c4553a15", "search_unit_id": "5e6f8b25-19fd-5b68-a18e-c944c4553a15", "block_from_id": "900ad583-02e0-5141-9bc1-afabc3f7b342", "block_to_id": "23d1cd95-b54f-535d-8d84-9739a8b3e912", "section_path": "Sup 18421 Enterprise 429 Spike After Priority Routing Rollout > Requested outcome", "document_id": "89c06a4c-9104-5c7e-b766-1b1af3c247dd", "document_version_id": "c6465792-6750-5999-b72c-4196eb71504b", "document_name": "jira__dsid_1980f45c3fb4455f943a2b030892ae90.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-26", "heading": "Sup 18421 Enterprise 429 Spike After Priority Routing Rollout > Requested outcome", "content": "Requested outcome\\n\\n- Explain semantics and provide an actionable plan.\\n- If eligible, grant a temporary burst exception with guardrails (time-boxed, monitored), or propose a dedicated capacity option.", "score": 0.283947, "rank": 122, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "3673b29b-cfa7-5efa-8aa9-5ff8dd44023f", "search_unit_id": "3673b29b-cfa7-5efa-8aa9-5ff8dd44023f", "block_from_id": "f48154e7-ac7a-58ae-bcf2-0955daab830c", "block_to_id": "cba6a8ae-ef1b-5d20-827b-830f9be9b71c", "section_path": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Default Thresholds (v1) > Latency (p95 end-to-end)", "document_id": "c055d063-a555-5cdf-b541-fe036031cb1b", "document_version_id": "57c83493-8d9e-5467-903c-46c7977b91fb", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a3f94972391244f38ef773c05926af83.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-52", "heading": "Adr Perf Canary Baseline And Thresholds > Default Thresholds (v1) > Latency (p95 end-to-end)", "content": "Latency (p95 end-to-end)\\n\\n- Warning: p95 