14:07:12  DEBUG    [e4c7d1831c8f4529]  sse_starlette.sse  chunk: b'event: result\r\ndata: {"query": "What is the default time limit for automatically expiring guest or contractor accounts in the plan to set up a temporary contractor seat pool for DossierHQ with Okta provisioning and SSO?", "sections": [{"chunk_id": "40ce4b35-be0e-5a00-a7ab-3d3df18c7a3b", "search_unit_id": "40ce4b35-be0e-5a00-a7ab-3d3df18c7a3b", "block_from_id": "12eeb13c-3d25-5b62-93cf-b4aa13d14ca7", "block_to_id": "378b3cd2-acfe-5b4c-8819-099485832829", "section_path": "Int 827501 Dossierhq Ephemeral Collaborator Pool Scim Okta Billing Mapping", "document_id": "8872fd06-4032-511c-8a96-2c38288a04ee", "document_version_id": "a905678e-378a-5ded-a8ff-1d690e43791b", "document_name": "jira__dsid_1d5a3cde61104276b218c05dc101a546.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h1-0", "heading": "Int 827501 Dossierhq Ephemeral Collaborator Pool Scim Okta Billing Mapping", "content": "Int 827501 Dossierhq Ephemeral Collaborator Pool Scim Okta Billing Mapping\\n\\nSource type: jira\\nDocument ID: dsid_1d5a3cde61104276b218c05dc101a546\\nRelease: v1.0.0\\nBenchmark: EnterpriseRAG-Bench synthetic upstream data\\nDossierHQ: procure ephemeral collaborator pool (120 seats) + SCIM/SSO mapping and billing tags\\n\\nResearch & data labeling sprint in Q2/Q3 needs an ephemeral pool for contractors and seasonal collaborators. Centralized pool reduces per-seat procurement cycles and speeds onboarding. Must support SCIM provisioning from Okta and billing tags so cost is attributed to ENG-RD-03. Security requires guest/contractor account expiry and BYOD policies enforced.\\nConfirm commercial terms with DossierHQ (seat pricing, burst add-on, SOC2 evidence)\\nRaise PO and route to Finance for approval (include cost center ENG-RD-03)\\nSet up SCIM connector in Okta: map groups -> DossierHQ team namespaces and provision admin accounts\\nConfigure SAML SSO (DossierHQ support doc provided) and test sign-in flow with 2 devs\\nEnable automated guest expiry policy: 30-day default with manager approval for extensions\\nVerify billing tags insertion: confirm DossierHQ supports cost center metadata on invoices and webhook notifications\\nPerform security attestation checklist (security team to sign off) before enabling contractor invites\\nRun a small pilot (20 seats) for 2 weeks, collect feedback, then bulk-provision remaining seats\\n2026-03-10 Priya Kapoor: Submitting request to establish a 120-seat ephemeral pool for Q3 contractor work. Need SCIM + SSO + billing tags. Cost to be charged to ENG-RD-03. Attached vendor quote in Confluence.\\n2026-03-11 Procurement (Liam O\'Neill): Acknowledged. I\'ll confirm commercial terms and whether a contract amendment is required for burst seats. PO number will be assigned after Finance approval.\\n2026-03-11 Security (Maya Thompson): Please attach SOC2 report and data processing addendum. We need confirmation on where logs are stored and whether sign-on events are forwarded to our Splunk/Grafana instance.\\n2026-03-12 Finance (Diego Ramirez): Can confirm budget availability pending Manager approval. Please include monthly invoicing cadence and a sample invoice with cost-center line items.\\n2026-03-13 Assignee (Evan Chen): Started Okta SCIM validation against DossierHQ dev tenant. Found one mismatch in attribute mapping for manager_email -> manager. Tagging Security for the log-forwarding question. Pilot target: provision 20 seats 2026-03-25.\\nPurchase order issued and approved by Finance (PO attached to ticket)\\nSCIM provisioning tested and automated groups create accounts with correct role mapping\\nSAML SSO validated in prod with audit events flowing to Splunk/Grafana\\nBilling shows cost-center ENG-RD-03 on sample invoice and monthly invoices\\nSecurity provides sign-off on SOC2/DPA and guest expiry configuration", "score": 0.818676, "rank": 0, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "a90526d6-9770-5f65-be0b-7359dcd0cbdf", "search_unit_id": "a90526d6-9770-5f65-be0b-7359dcd0cbdf", "block_from_id": "69685f00-74df-5b60-8358-03b57a51b1b5", "block_to_id": "0f34d99f-cd84-5c84-b939-4f85c7b4ca15", "section_path": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > Evidence pack contents (prospect-facing) > C) Logging, auditability, and access controls", "document_id": "9e945a2c-4051-5cc3-99ca-a95c267f6bdf", "document_version_id": "c6871709-6afb-56d9-883f-81f2536ab49e", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a9a67d815b07451e90ed8c3d1734d007.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-34", "heading": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > Evidence pack contents (prospect-facing) > C) Logging, auditability, and access controls", "content": "C) Logging, auditability, and access controls\\n\\n7) **Audit log sample (redacted) + field reference**\\n\\n- Includes a redacted JSON sample of key audit log event types and a field glossary.\\n- Typical fields covered:\\n  - event_time, event_type\\n  - actor (user/service principal), auth_method\\n  - resource (project, deployment, model), action\\n  - request_id / trace_id\\n  - admin actions (policy updates, key configuration updates, retention config changes)\\n- Important: samples are representative and may omit environment-specific fields.\\n\\n8) **Audit log export patterns (overview)**\\n\\n- Describes supported export mechanisms and operational considerations (format, delivery options, and recommended controls).\\n- Used during technical deep dives for regulated buyers.\\n\\n9) **SSO/SAML + RBAC overview (one-page)**\\n\\n- High-level explanation of authentication options and role-based access control for the Console and admin functions.\\n- Includes SCIM provisioning support language where applicable.", "score": 0.596932, "rank": 1, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "00c72908-ab8f-5f1f-97f4-a27a4dfc0b59", "search_unit_id": "00c72908-ab8f-5f1f-97f4-a27a4dfc0b59", "block_from_id": "68438571-0bfe-5560-b41e-f4b75e0a3cea", "block_to_id": "1d35e0ad-4bf2-50ed-a750-2102a55b6a3a", "section_path": "Cross Role Tool Access Governance And Self Service Handbook 2027", "document_id": "4495c7a6-685c-5344-90c0-ef0b6cee8521", "document_version_id": "43048def-240f-53d7-bef2-2be51354fc0d", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_d7f0edbaa28147da807b5b85ea495bd1.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "list-17", "heading": "Cross Role Tool Access Governance And Self Service Handbook 2027", "content": "- Emergency access (e.g., incident mitigation) uses the Fast-Track Exception flow:\\n  1) Requester files an incident-tagged access request with justification and incident ID.\\n  2) On-call approver (manager or designated incident commander) may grant a 4-hour temporary token via the Access Portal.\\n  3) Security must be notified within 1 hour and will follow up to convert to a formal exception or revoke.\\n- All emergency grants are logged and retroactively reviewed as part of the incident postmortem.\\n\\n7) Deprovisioning and Offboarding\\n\\n- Standard offboarding timeline:\\n  - Day 0: account disabled for interactive access within 4 hours of HR offboarding signal.\\n  - Day 1: short-lived API tokens invalidated; persistent service accounts rotated if necessary.\\n  - Day 7: cloud console roles removed; scheduled backup of any outstanding owner responsibilities.\\n- Manager responsibility: reassign ownership before final removal.\\n- Audit: people-ops runs weekly deprovisioning reports, security runs monthly verification.\\n\\n8) Periodic Access Reviews\\n\\n- Quarterly review for all high-risk roles.\\n- Biannual review for medium-risk.\\n- Annual review for low-risk.\\n- Review process: automated report produced by Access Portal -> owner validates or initiates deprovisioning -> people-ops enforces changes within 7 business days.\\n\\n9) Tool Onboarding Checklist (for Tool Owners)\\n\\n1. Identify required roles and map to least-privilege sets.\\n2. Document roles in the Role Catalog section of the owner page.\\n3. Define which roles are self-service vs require approval.\\n4. Provide an onboarding runbook with contact points and SLOs.\\n5. Register tool owner and escalation email in the Access Portal.\\n6. Provide a test account and verification steps for the access flow.\\n\\n10) Technical Integration Patterns\\n\\n- Recommended: integrate tool with SSO and SCIM for group sync.\\n- Fallback: if SCIM unavailable, implement an API-based provisioning endpoint with scoped tokens and change tracking.\\n- Example access request payload (JSON):\\n\\n{\\n  \\"requester\\": \\"user@redwood.com\\",\\n  \\"tool\\": \\"grafana-prod\\",\\n  \\"role\\": \\"viewer\\",\\n  \\"justification\\": \\"Oncall rotation\\",\\n  \\"expires_at\\": \\"2026-02-28T23:59:00Z\\"\\n}\\n\\n- Example CLI (Access Portal CLI):\\n\\n$ rwd-access request --tool grafana-prod --role viewer --justification \\"oncall\\"\\n\\n11) Monitoring, Auditing and KPIs\\n\\n- KPIs to track: time-to-provision (median), percent self-service, number of active exceptions, time-to-deprovision after offboard.\\n- Reporting cadence: weekly provisioning dashboard, monthly audit exports (CSV) to security vault.\\n- Audit checks: verify no more than 5% of production-admin grants are older than 30 days without re-approval.\\n\\n12) De-Escalation and Dispute Resolution\\n\\n- If a manager or user disputes a grant or removal, escalate to people-ops with the ticket ID. People-ops will convene: manager, tool owner, and security to adjudicate within 5 business days.\\n\\n13) Sample Runbook: Granting Prod-Read Access (Manager-Approved)\\n\\n1. Manager submits request in Access Portal with business justification.\\n2. Portal routes to tool owner notification and manager approval.\\n3. On approval, provisioning job runs (SCIM or API).\\n4. Access granted; audit log written; notification sent to requester and manager.\\n5. After 90 days, system creates a renewal task for the manager.\\n\\n14) Implementation Roadmap (next 18 months)\\n\\n- Q2 2026: Complete SCIM integrations for top 10 tools.\\n- Q3 2026: Launch delegated team-admin roles for large product groups.\\n- Q4 2026: Build exception analytics dashboard and automatic expiration nudges.", "score": 0.577276, "rank": 2, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "d06140d6-c11d-5d65-9ab3-76d752809832", "search_unit_id": "d06140d6-c11d-5d65-9ab3-76d752809832", "block_from_id": "b7f1ce53-ff8b-527f-a0ce-e8a87effa840", "block_to_id": "3c9f24a2-951a-5714-a11d-4b198bd37044", "section_path": "20250520 Trident Financial Dpa And Sla Redlines", "document_id": "f15d34e1-debf-503d-ad3e-2fd3512fa1f9", "document_version_id": "fb519f86-ed83-52c4-97bd-0a58c4f47f19", "document_name": "gmail__dsid_98d7acbda29a4260a413c8d6da7207a3.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "list-21", "heading": "20250520 Trident Financial Dpa And Sla Redlines", "content": "- Our default is typically \xe2\x80\x9cwithout undue delay\xe2\x80\x9d / 72 hours for certain events; 24 hours is aggressive. We may be able to do 24 hours for confirmed material incidents impacting their data, but need to define \xe2\x80\x9csecurity incident\xe2\x80\x9d and \xe2\x80\x9cconfirmed\xe2\x80\x9d / \xe2\x80\x9cmaterial.\xe2\x80\x9d\\n\\nE) Liability / cap changes\\n\\n- Likely business approval required. Sofia \xe2\x80\x94 pls advise if we should hold the line on cap and carve-outs.\\n\\nF) SLA credits + RTO/RPO\\n\\n- We can discuss service credits; RTO/RPO may be for enterprise DR options only, not base.\\n\\nGoal: send Elaine/Raj a consolidated response today with a redline package.\\n\\nMichael\\nAttachments:\\n\\n- Trident_Redwood_DPA_Redlines_2025-05-19.docx\\n- Trident_Redwood_SLA_Redlines_2025-05-19.docx\\n\\n---\\nFrom: Naomi Feldman <naomi.feldman@redwoodinference.com>\\nTo: Michael Grant <michael.grant@redwoodinference.com>, Sofia Mendes <sofia.mendes@redwoodinference.com>\\nCc: Dr. Aisha Rahman <aisha.rahman@redwoodinference.com>, Vivek Kulkarni <vivek.kulkarni@redwoodinference.com>, Stephanie Nguyen <stephanie.nguyen@redwoodinference.com>, Avery Johnson <avery.johnson@redwoodinference.com>\\nDate: Tue, May 20, 2025 at 11:06 AM\\nSubject: Re: Trident \xe2\x80\x94 DPA/SLA redlines (need positions today)\\n\\nQuick take (security/compliance):\\nA) Residency\\n\\n- For Private (VPC), we can commit that customer content (prompts/outputs) is processed and stored within the deployed region/VPC per the architecture. We should NOT commit that \xe2\x80\x9cno Redwood personnel outside US can ever access.\xe2\x80\x9d Better: access is controlled, logged, and limited to authorized personnel on a need-to-know basis; remote access can be restricted by customer policy in the Private deployment.\\n- Subprocessors: in Private/VPC, this is largely their cloud provider + any Redwood support tooling (if enabled). We should require a defined list / allow updates with notice.\\n\\nB) Retention\\n\\n- Separate: (1) customer content retention vs (2) operational/audit logs.\\n- We can offer \xe2\x80\x9cno training on customer data\xe2\x80\x9d language if not already in the DPA; and for retention, propose \xe2\x80\x9ccustomer-configurable retention settings; default per product; can be set to short windows; deletion upon request subject to legal/operational requirements.\xe2\x80\x9d\\n- 24 hours hard SLA on deletion is tough; propose \xe2\x80\x9cwithin 30 days\xe2\x80\x9d standard, with best efforts for faster.\\n\\nC) Audit logs / SIEM export\\n\\n- We can support audit logging and export. We should counter that 7-year retention is on customer side (their SIEM). We can commit we provide log export and the customer can retain as long as they need.\\n\\nD) Incident notice\\n\\n- Recommend counter: notify without undue delay and in any event within 72 hours of confirmation of a Security Incident affecting Customer Data.\\n- If business wants to concede: 48 hours post-confirmation for \xe2\x80\x9cconfirmed material incidents.\xe2\x80\x9d\\n\\nI can help wordsmith but need Sofia/legal to decide positions.\\n\\nNaomi\\n---\\n\\nFrom: Dr. Aisha Rahman <aisha.rahman@redwoodinference.com>\\nTo: Michael Grant <michael.grant@redwoodinference.com>, Naomi Feldman <naomi.feldman@redwoodinference.com>, Sofia Mendes <sofia.mendes@redwoodinference.com>\\nCc: Vivek Kulkarni <vivek.kulkarni@redwoodinference.com>, Stephanie Nguyen <stephanie.nguyen@redwoodinference.com>, Avery Johnson <avery.johnson@redwoodinference.com>\\nDate: Tue, May 20, 2025 at 11:22 AM\\nSubject: Re: Trident \xe2\x80\x94 DPA/SLA redlines (need positions today)\\n\\n+1 to Naomi\xe2\x80\x99s notes.\\n\\nStrongly recommend we avoid absolute commitments that could be interpreted as \xe2\x80\x9cno cross-border access ever.\xe2\x80\x9d We can commit to:\\n\\n- in-region processing/storage for Customer Content for Private/VPC deployments;\\n- access controls (RBAC), MFA/SSO where applicable, and auditability of admin actions;\\n- audit log export capability so Trident can satisfy long-term retention in their own systems.", "score": 0.525663, "rank": 3, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "00b09821-ff9f-5cbe-8d54-80bbd2528b44", "search_unit_id": "00b09821-ff9f-5cbe-8d54-80bbd2528b44", "block_from_id": "196c4bdf-7ea6-50be-8a09-f5a2394a48e6", "block_to_id": "28755e02-0c1d-5db8-8ffa-d35fc732ac08", "section_path": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Rollout / migration plan", "document_id": "98457c4e-240d-5e44-8a6a-abb94e877555", "document_version_id": "b983f052-79ea-54aa-b5f9-45e0bce342f3", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_8c24fd15fe5b473eb71341751d292b6e.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-88", "heading": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Rollout / migration plan", "content": "Rollout / migration plan\\n\\n- Beta: allow only for tenants with feature flag enabled; strongly recommend cohort key usage.\\n- GA: document cohort key + idempotency best practices; ship dashboard panels that surface intended vs effective mismatch.", "score": 0.505464, "rank": 4, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "c3a8f340-a67d-5b45-bb4b-efdc2b7025de", "search_unit_id": "c3a8f340-a67d-5b45-bb4b-efdc2b7025de", "block_from_id": "99b129ef-8d15-517c-86eb-d4340e79101b", "block_to_id": "a87c3c5f-0094-58d3-b292-ab69db0daf90", "section_path": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 11. Meter versions and change control > 11.2 Shadow billing compare", "document_id": "85b37108-378d-55be-805e-bdcd31101232", "document_version_id": "6851cc1f-d2a1-5421-91d3-d80c5cf3f3e8", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_37befab212ae4ed9be7ebe612d942658.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-123", "heading": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 11. Meter versions and change control > 11.2 Shadow billing compare", "content": "11.2 Shadow billing compare\\n\\nBefore enabling a new meter_version for invoicing:\\n\\n1. Run **shadow billing compare** for at least 7 consecutive days across representative traffic.\\n2. Produce an impact report by account/model/region/endpoint (P50/P95 deltas; outlier accounts).\\n3. Obtain approvals:\\n   - Finance/RevRec (Emily Stone)\\n   - Telemetry Eng owner (Logan Wright)\\n   - Product owner for Hosted API pricing (Nikhil Sharma)\\n   - Legal if contract language is impacted (Michael Grant)", "score": 0.498237, "rank": 5, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "7ced6b7b-6ecb-58af-a8a3-516623e73f0f", "search_unit_id": "7ced6b7b-6ecb-58af-a8a3-516623e73f0f", "block_from_id": "8f1ffe2a-abad-55bc-a70b-096fcc7e97e9", "block_to_id": "965b93e7-7507-5bca-b0a2-042d5a0a698a", "section_path": "Usage Meter Audit 2026 02 Working Notes", "document_id": "323fb7d4-a4c2-55a9-a54b-f678b531daf4", "document_version_id": "f619881c-d439-5370-8b57-5d522b4124b6", "document_name": "google_drive__dsid_dc8da53aae4c4a81be13ae8e519cd42d.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "list-27", "heading": "Usage Meter Audit 2026 02 Working Notes", "content": "- server-side retry/fallback should not increase billed tokens beyond final successful attempt (unless contract says otherwise; Legal prefers explicit definition)\\n- client retry with new idempotency key is a new billable group; with same idempotency key should dedupe\\n\\nDedicated\\n\\n- gpu_seconds: sum over active GPUs assigned to pool * wall-clock seconds, minus explicit \xe2\x80\x9cunassigned\xe2\x80\x9d windows (v2 aims to clarify)\\n\\nWhat we\xe2\x80\x99re validating (audit plan notes)\\n1) Meter logic matches invoice outcomes\\n\\n- For sampled invoices (see drive spreadsheet), reproduce totals from usage events.\\n- Slice by account/day/model/region/endpoint.\\n\\n2) Data completeness + freshness SLOs\\n\\n- Freshness: how long after request until usage event is available in recon export and customer usage API.\\n- Late event policy: how handled after close freeze window.\\n\\n3) Consistency across endpoints\\n\\n- OpenAI-compatible endpoints vs native endpoints should align on token counting and billed vs raw token fields.\\n\\n4) Experiment contamination control\\n\\n- Meter fixes overlap with pricing experiments; analysis must segment pre/post meter change windows.\\n\\nOpen questions (needs answers / owners)\\n[Q1] Token counting alignment (OpenAI-compatible)\\n\\n- Are we using the exact same tokenizer + normalization rules across /v1/chat/completions and native /generate?\\n- Do tool call tokens count as completion tokens? How are they displayed?\\n\\nOwner: Nadia Rahman\\nArtifacts: github:redwood-openai-compat/pr-511-align-token-counting-with-openai-compatible-endpoints, PR-27471\\n\\n[Q2] Streaming partials + disconnects\\n\\n- If client disconnects mid-stream, do we bill generated tokens up to disconnect? Do we bill any minimum?\\n- Are partial chunks counted consistently between runtime logs and metering events?\\n\\nOwner: Logan Wright\\nArtifacts: ENG-4410 (streaming tests), perf-canary pr-912 scenarios\\n\\n[Q3] Idempotency definition + dedupe window\\n\\n- Dedupe key: request_id? idempotency_key + account + endpoint? (risk: collisions vs missed dedupe)\\n- Dedupe window duration: 24h? 7d? (backfills may create duplicates outside window)\\n\\nOwner: Cole Summers\\nArtifacts: PR-27555 (hardening idempotency)\\n[Q4] Cache-hit fields missing in some events\\n\\n- Are cache_hit and billed_prompt_tokens nullable today? If so, what default behavior is invoice using?\\n- Can we safely backfill for Jan/Feb for experiment analysis without changing invoiced totals?\\n\\nOwner: Mei Lin\\nArtifacts: ENG-4412 (backfill), PR-27485 (usage API fields)\\n\\n[Q5] Region dimension correctness\\n\\n- What is the source for region dimension (edge POP vs compute region vs billing region)?\\n- For multi-region fallback, do we attribute to final serving region or initial region?\\n\\nOwner: Logan Wright / Ethan Park\\nArtifacts: PR-27541\\n[Q6] Late events + freeze window policy (RevRec)\\n\\n- Confirm: freeze at T+3 days for month-end close? (thread says tentative)\\n- If late events arrive after freeze, do they roll into next month revenue? Do we credit/adjust?\\n\\nOwner: Emily Stone + Laura Bennett\\nArtifacts: slack:finance/1770061189-month-end-close-usage-freeze-window, revrec impact assessment doc\\n\\n[Q7] Dedicated GPU-seconds v2\\n\\n- Definition of idle: if GPU allocated but no requests, do we bill as reserved capacity or usage?\\n- How do we map gpu_seconds to contract constructs (commit, burst, minimums)?\\n\\nOwner: Rishi Malhotra + Nadia Rahman\\nArtifacts: ENG-4426 / PR-27492\\nSuspected discrepancies (running list)\\nFormat: ID | Account | Product | Period | Symptom | Hypothesis | Severity | Owner | Status/next", "score": 0.471445, "rank": 6, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "fdbbfe44-0b8d-50aa-8d99-e4bd35ccb269", "search_unit_id": "fdbbfe44-0b8d-50aa-8d99-e4bd35ccb269", "block_from_id": "d6778458-e29b-5053-8621-47f54ba3d664", "block_to_id": "5f82c632-07a0-5511-8190-382128144d99", "section_path": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review", "document_id": "be72d63b-333f-51cc-8c3e-5b2af46aaab8", "document_version_id": "d74f559c-4a91-5a53-930c-c8b05d67f4ab", "document_name": "fireflies__dsid_6c4c1c875e704f09b4d791d64d7bc7e5.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "p-6", "heading": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review", "content": "[00:33] Chris Osei: Yep, and I\xe2\x80\x99m Chris, program lead on our side for the marketplace refresh. I\xe2\x80\x99ll keep us on agenda and capture action items.\\n\\n[00:41] Anika Desai: Hi all. Anika, partner engineering. I can talk through entitlement, procurement IDs, and the typical \xe2\x80\x9cgotchas\xe2\x80\x9d we see when SaaS vendors wire up the onboarding.\\n\\n[00:50] Miguel Alvarez: Miguel here. Cloud Commerce metering / billing integrations. Happy to go deep on dimensions, aggregation, rounding, and what reviewers will look for.\\n\\n[01:01] Stephanie Nguyen: Great. Quick context: we\xe2\x80\x99re replacing legacy listings. We now have Hosted API usage-based, Dedicated capacity, and Private deployment. We want the marketplace SKUs to map cleanly to our internal billing meters. The primary issues we want to avoid are dimension mismatches and the buyer gets invoiced for something weird.\\n\\n[01:20] Miguel Alvarez: Yeah. The big thing is consistency. Dimension names and IDs in the offer need to match what you\xe2\x80\x99re sending in metering events. And be careful if you\xe2\x80\x99re changing them later\xe2\x80\x94customers don\xe2\x80\x99t like it and it can break reporting.\\n\\n[01:35] Cole Summers: That\xe2\x80\x99s exactly what we\xe2\x80\x99re trying to nail. Let me outline what we were thinking and you can tell us what\xe2\x80\x99s realistic. For Hosted, we meter \xe2\x80\x9ctokens generated\xe2\x80\x9d and \xe2\x80\x9cinput tokens\xe2\x80\x9d internally, but we can expose it as \xe2\x80\x9ctokens\xe2\x80\x9d total if that\xe2\x80\x99s simpler. We also have embeddings measured as \xe2\x80\x9cembedding units\xe2\x80\x9d (basically per 1K tokens equivalent). For Dedicated, we have reserved GPU capacity, which internally is monthly commit + overage. Private is more of a platform fee plus support tier.\\n\\n[02:05] Miguel Alvarez: Okay. So in Cloud Marketplace, you can do usage-based dimensions and you can do subscription pricing models, and you can also do committed-use style constructs depending on the product type. Most SaaS partners keep it simple: one or a small number of usage dimensions, like \xe2\x80\x9c1K tokens\xe2\x80\x9d or \xe2\x80\x9c1M tokens\xe2\x80\x9d, and then for a base subscription you do a seat or a flat monthly fee.\\n\\n[02:29] Nadia Rahman: Quick question on granularity. If we do \xe2\x80\x9c1K tokens\xe2\x80\x9d as the unit, do you see partners reporting fractional quantities? Like 123.456 units? Or is it recommended to round?\\n\\n[02:41] Miguel Alvarez: You can send decimal quantities, but you should document how you compute it and be consistent. The most common pattern is you aggregate per hour or per day and send integer quantities. But for token meters, lots of folks send decimals, just be explicit. Rounding differences between your UI and the invoice is where support tickets come from.\\n\\n[03:02] Ben Carter: On the onboarding side, we have this subscription -> entitlement -> Redwood org provisioning. One issue we see on AWS is entitlements sometimes take a few minutes to show up. Is that also a thing on GCP?\\n\\n[03:14] Anika Desai: Yep. It\xe2\x80\x99s not usually super long, but there can be propagation delay. The UX expectation is you handle \xe2\x80\x9cpending\xe2\x80\x9d gracefully. Like, don\xe2\x80\x99t show a scary error that says \xe2\x80\x9cyou are not entitled.\xe2\x80\x9d Instead say \xe2\x80\x9cwe\xe2\x80\x99re still syncing your subscription, retry in a few minutes\xe2\x80\x9d and maybe a refresh button.", "score": 0.461264, "rank": 7, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "1767090b-7388-5412-9a51-b0480400b9ad", "search_unit_id": "1767090b-7388-5412-9a51-b0480400b9ad", "block_from_id": "ff071304-db9c-52a3-b816-97b72c0711f9", "block_to_id": "d04701c4-2d87-5e4e-8f14-f275990fd981", "section_path": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Definition > Exclusions (non-counted windows/requests) > Window-level exclusions", "document_id": "19c5a655-72be-5ec3-9113-0a8f67b72c9f", "document_version_id": "2e39e4c6-4649-5fd6-9e3d-ebf763331245", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_e33c122264214846a8b7df7701d31482.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-52", "heading": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Definition > Exclusions (non-counted windows/requests) > Window-level exclusions", "content": "Window-level exclusions\\n\\nThe following 5-minute windows may be excluded (removed from total window count) if conditions are met and are annotated in reporting:\\n\\n- **Provisioning warm-up:** First **15 minutes** after a deployment becomes active.\\n- **Scale event warm-up:** First **10 minutes** after an approved scale-up/scale-down event that changes active capacity.\\n- **Planned maintenance:** Approved maintenance windows with customer notice, tracked via change calendar and incident tooling.", "score": 0.438011, "rank": 8, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "28b86ac8-5dfb-5fc0-9cd5-ebdbfd1f1371", "search_unit_id": "28b86ac8-5dfb-5fc0-9cd5-ebdbfd1f1371", "block_from_id": "d27d4dda-2ee7-5a4d-b690-309c54c3dfea", "block_to_id": "1fea54c4-ad92-5ad3-b7c2-757f6412bb24", "section_path": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Escalation > Service credits / compensation (internal guidance) > Approval path (default)", "document_id": "2aced3e3-1ed9-50b2-9ef8-a037c40fcfaf", "document_version_id": "5b0b49bf-5c10-5f9e-b6f9-1ae199865977", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_409618a3958944f9b78362ed7e01bb33.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-127", "heading": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Escalation > Service credits / compensation (internal guidance) > Approval path (default)", "content": "Approval path (default)\\n\\n1) CS/Support compiles incident details and SRE confirmation.\\n2) SRE provides breach window + summary.\\n3) Product Dedicated confirms entitlement interpretation (tier/exclusions).\\n4) Finance (Laura Bennett / delegate) validates policy compliance and amount.\\n5) Legal (Sofia Mendes / delegate) reviews if there is contract ambiguity or if a non-standard remedy is proposed.\\n6) CS communicates outcome to customer.", "score": 0.434601, "rank": 9, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "1044778b-aba5-5ac0-8911-3896dd080ded", "search_unit_id": "1044778b-aba5-5ac0-8911-3896dd080ded", "block_from_id": "bfdb339e-53f4-5e92-9aa9-dc72fe97f7b2", "block_to_id": "5105a2f3-f346-551e-8f9f-663579e74285", "section_path": "Metering To Invoice Reconciliation Playbook > Cadence > Weekly (control)", "document_id": "5dd49655-30e7-5bb0-9f51-c936960c0c6a", "document_version_id": "c0e0c79d-406f-5fb0-b65c-732ae9489a5c", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_602422b841b94f5c8089555015df0ed8.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-39", "heading": "Metering To Invoice Reconciliation Playbook > Cadence > Weekly (control)", "content": "Weekly (control)\\n\\nGoal: verify stability and trend anomalies.\\n\\n- Reconcile previous 7 days ledger vs usage events.\\n- Sample 5\xe2\x80\x9310 accounts across tiers and 2\xe2\x80\x933 regions.", "score": 0.434142, "rank": 10, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "cd97969a-d664-5c44-b40d-29de8e3aed7c", "search_unit_id": "cd97969a-d664-5c44-b40d-29de8e3aed7c", "block_from_id": "8e6320c4-e6f8-5d54-93df-d1029f47c012", "block_to_id": "57ca5e74-583f-5358-b18f-940f2bd5d127", "section_path": "Entitlements And Purchase Pathways Playbook 2030", "document_id": "98b6d795-d34b-588e-9f1d-b236110b9bba", "document_version_id": "9f88e88a-7404-5ed3-aeef-43374be1410f", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_ba7d1565b0694e359983bccaa4bf5977.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h1-0", "heading": "Entitlements And Purchase Pathways Playbook 2030", "content": "Entitlements And Purchase Pathways Playbook 2030\\n\\nSource type: confluence\\nDocument ID: dsid_ba7d1565b0694e359983bccaa4bf5977\\nRelease: v1.0.0\\nBenchmark: EnterpriseRAG-Bench synthetic upstream data\\nentitlements-and-purchase-pathways-playbook-2030\\nOverview\\n=======\\n\\nPurpose: This playbook consolidates company-wide procedures for entitlements (access and permissions), change governance, data handling expectations, procurement and expense approvals, vendor lifecycle management, and travel approvals. It is intended for all Redwood employees and contractors who request or approve resources, commit changes to production, or manage third-party relationships.\\n\\nScope: Applies to hosted, dedicated, and private deployment teams, platform and infra teams, procurement interactions, people-ops travel arrangements, and any third party that will access Redwood systems or customer data.\\n\\nQuick links: Access Portal (Okta + Access Request), VendorHub (procurement back-end), ExpenseFlow (employee expenses), Legal intake form, Security questionnaire (security.redwood.local).\\n\\nRoles and responsibilities\\n--------------------------\\n\\n- Requestor: person initiating an access, change, purchase, vendor relationship, or travel booking. Responsible for completeness and business justification.\\n- Manager/Approver: direct manager who validates business need and budget availability.\\n- Approving Officer (Finance/Procurement): validates procurement thresholds, tax, and vendor classification.\\n- Security Reviewer: evaluates data access risk and mandatory controls (SOC2, encryption, contract clauses).\\n- Legal Reviewer: reviews contract terms for vendor agreements above threshold or with custom IP/data terms.\\n- Proc Ops (Vendor Onboarding): completes supplier setup, obtains W-9/COI as applicable.\\n\\nPrinciples\\n----------\\n\\n- Least privilege: grant minimal entitlements required for the job and set automatic expiration where practical.\\n- Segregation of duties: approval and provisioning paths must not be owned by the same individual for medium/high risk requests.\\n- Evidence-first approvals: all approvals must be attached to the request in VendorHub or Access Portal.\\n- Risk proportionality: stricter controls for vendors with data access, privileged infra access, or requests above financial thresholds.\\n\\nAccess & permissions (entitlement lifecycle)\\n-------------------------------------------\\n1) Request process\\n\\n- Use the Access Portal (accessible from the Redwood Console > Tools > Access Request).\\n- Provide: justification, target systems (service name and environment), requested role (exact RBAC role), duration, and manager approval.\\n- For long-lived roles (>90 days), supply quarterly business justification and add reviewer in Access Portal.\\n\\n2) Approval matrix (summary)\\n\\n- Day-to-day access to non-prod systems: manager approval.\\n- Prod read-only access: manager + security notification.\\n- Prod privileged/infra access (SSH, K8s-admin, DB write): manager + security + infra-owner approval.\\n- Emergency elevated access: break-glass procedure (see Runbook ID: RG-2026-breakglass).\\n\\n3) Provisioning and deprovisioning\\n\\n- Provisioning is automated via Okta + Terraform runbooks (Infra-IAC). On approval, provisioning SLA is 2 business days for standard requests, 8 hours for emergency sprints.\\n- Access expirations are enforced: default 30 days for contractor requests, 90 days for internal hires unless renewed.\\n\\n4) Periodic review\\n\\n- Quarterly access attestation: team owners must validate active entitlements and remove stale accounts. Failure to respond triggers automated revocation notices.\\n\\nChange management\\n-----------------\\n\\nScope: Any modification to production services, infra provisioning, IAM policies, or customer-facing configuration.\\n\\nChange categories and approval paths:", "score": 0.338831, "rank": 11, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "f7a517ae-0405-52eb-98e5-d8ebc0245abc", "search_unit_id": "f7a517ae-0405-52eb-98e5-d8ebc0245abc", "block_from_id": "3cb20a79-9a89-5d4a-9bb5-689991a4f55f", "block_to_id": "43dd021f-47fd-5237-b674-03547a17dbf7", "section_path": "Cross Role Tool Access Governance And Self Service Handbook 2027", "document_id": "4495c7a6-685c-5344-90c0-ef0b6cee8521", "document_version_id": "43048def-240f-53d7-bef2-2be51354fc0d", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_d7f0edbaa28147da807b5b85ea495bd1.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h1-0", "heading": "Cross Role Tool Access Governance And Self Service Handbook 2027", "content": "Cross Role Tool Access Governance And Self Service Handbook 2027\\n\\nSource type: confluence\\nDocument ID: dsid_d7f0edbaa28147da807b5b85ea495bd1\\nRelease: v1.0.0\\nBenchmark: EnterpriseRAG-Bench synthetic upstream data\\nCross-role Tool Access Governance and Self-Service Handbook\\n\\nSummary:\\n\\nThis handbook defines the governance model, operational runbook, and self-service flows for granting, reviewing, and revoking access to Redwood\xe2\x80\x99s internal tools across roles and teams. It is intended for people-ops, engineering managers, security reviewers, and tool maintainers. The goal is to make tool access predictable, auditable, and fast while minimizing over-permissioning.\\n\\nKey Principles:\\n\\n- Least privilege by default: users receive the minimum role required to do their job.\\n- Role-driven access: we prefer role-based assignments with ephemeral exceptions.\\n- Self-service first, manual oversight when necessary: automate safe, common grants.\\n- Clear ownership: every tool has an owner and an escalation path.\\n- Auditable lifecycle: provisioning, reviews and deprovisioning are tracked centrally.\\n\\n1) Scope and Definitions\\n\\n- Tool: any internal SaaS, self-hosted service, or repo that requires explicit credentials or permissions (examples: AWS consoles, Jira admin groups, prod Grafana, CI pipelines).\\n- Role: a named collection of permissions (e.g., developer-readonly, infra-operator).\\n- Access Request: a request submitted to the Access Portal for a role or tool-specific permission.\\n- Exception: temporary access outside standard roles; must have an expiration and approver.\\n\\n2) Ownership and Responsibilities\\n\\n- Tool Owner (team-specified): maintains the role catalog, documents required permissions, defines safe self-service gates, and responds to escalations.\\n- People Ops: owns onboarding/offboarding orchestration, periodic review cadence, and highest-level policy.\\n- Security: defines acceptable exception policies, approves high-risk tools, and performs audits.\\n- Manager (requester approver): verifies business need and ties access to role/mission.\\n\\n3) Access Model (high level)\\n\\n- Role Catalog: canonical list of roles for common tools; stored in the Access Portal.\\n- Self-Service Roles: low-risk roles that can be assigned instantly (examples: docs-edit, ci-reader)\\n- Request-and-Approve Roles: medium-risk roles requiring manager approval (examples: staging-deploy, metrics-writer)\\n- Manual Approval Roles: high-risk roles requiring security review (examples: prod-admin, billing-admin)\\n\\n4) Standard Provisioning Flow (Self-Service preferred)\\n\\n1. User initiates request via Access Portal (or CLI).\\n2. For self-service roles: immediate grant recorded in audit log; user notified.\\n3. For manager-approval roles: manager receives approval email and must respond within 48 business hours; if approved, provisioning executes automatically.\\n4. For security-approval roles: security reviewer staged; expected SLA is 3 business days.\\n5. All grants include metadata: requester, approver, justification, expiration (if exception), and ticket ID.\\n\\n5) Approval Matrix (sample)\\n\\n| Tool Category | Example Tools | Typical Role Name | Approval Path | Max SLA for Approval | Expiration Policy |\\n|---|---:|---|---|---|---|\\n| Low risk | Docs, Internal Wiki | docs-edit | Self-service | Instant | Persistent (attached to employment) |\\n| Medium risk | Staging CI, Dev DB | staging-deploy | Manager | 48 business hours | 90 days (auto-renew) |\\n| High risk | Production infra, Billing | prod-admin | Manager + Security | 3 business days | 7 days (explicit extension only) |\\n\\n6) Exception and Emergency Access", "score": 0.534349, "rank": 12, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "0831657e-8401-56f0-9627-4cbebab0650d", "search_unit_id": "0831657e-8401-56f0-9627-4cbebab0650d", "block_from_id": "625b5926-73a8-54c5-8b6e-46a2a938a372", "block_to_id": "168dded9-63c3-5978-9a34-302313635e29", "section_path": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index", "document_id": "9e945a2c-4051-5cc3-99ca-a95c267f6bdf", "document_version_id": "c6871709-6afb-56d9-883f-81f2536ab49e", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a9a67d815b07451e90ed8c3d1734d007.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h1-0", "heading": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index", "content": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index\\n\\nSource type: confluence\\nDocument ID: dsid_a9a67d815b07451e90ed8c3d1734d007\\nRelease: v1.0.0\\nBenchmark: EnterpriseRAG-Bench synthetic upstream data\\nABM Pilot: Security Evidence Pack Index (Regulated Workloads)", "score": 0.5253, "rank": 13, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "25561c5f-d513-5ce6-beac-acce49aae704", "search_unit_id": "25561c5f-d513-5ce6-beac-acce49aae704", "block_from_id": "7947130a-10de-592a-ae10-7feb7e2c18a6", "block_to_id": "ee5a375e-e60e-5cac-b642-d6803c644150", "section_path": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > Purpose", "document_id": "9e945a2c-4051-5cc3-99ca-a95c267f6bdf", "document_version_id": "c6871709-6afb-56d9-883f-81f2536ab49e", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a9a67d815b07451e90ed8c3d1734d007.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-2", "heading": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > Purpose", "content": "Purpose\\n\\nThis page is the single index for Redwood Inference\xe2\x80\x99s prospect-facing **Security Evidence Pack** used during the Vertical ABM pilot (and reusable beyond) for regulated workloads (financial services, healthcare, and other compliance-driven buyers).\\n\\nIt describes:\\n\\n- What artifacts are included (and what we do *not* share)\\n- How Sales/SEs request access for a prospect\\n- How Security/GRC shares evidence securely and tracks distribution\\n- Confidentiality rules and common \xe2\x80\x9cgotchas\xe2\x80\x9d (SOC 2 handling, retention/data boundary language)\\n\\nThis page is **restricted** because it references the internal sharing process and controlled artifacts.\\n\\n---", "score": 0.507392, "rank": 14, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "5e83c1dd-76b2-5da9-ba7a-50a42b8dfa61", "search_unit_id": "5e83c1dd-76b2-5da9-ba7a-50a42b8dfa61", "block_from_id": "183c3d39-5691-5fb9-92cc-06f2963e33ec", "block_to_id": "02925590-6b67-5022-b7ac-598c2810964f", "section_path": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > Common requests mapped to artifacts", "document_id": "9e945a2c-4051-5cc3-99ca-a95c267f6bdf", "document_version_id": "c6871709-6afb-56d9-883f-81f2536ab49e", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a9a67d815b07451e90ed8c3d1734d007.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-85", "heading": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > Common requests mapped to artifacts", "content": "Common requests mapped to artifacts\\n\\n- \xe2\x80\x9cSend your SOC 2\xe2\x80\x9d \xe2\x86\x92 SOC 2 Type II report (NDA required)\\n- \xe2\x80\x9cHow do you encrypt data?\xe2\x80\x9d \xe2\x86\x92 Encryption overview diagram + data flow diagram\\n- \xe2\x80\x9cDo you have audit logs we can export?\xe2\x80\x9d \xe2\x86\x92 Audit log sample + export patterns overview\\n- \xe2\x80\x9cWhat do you retain and for how long?\xe2\x80\x9d \xe2\x86\x92 Retention policy statement + (if needed) residency statement\\n- \xe2\x80\x9cDo you support SSO/SAML?\xe2\x80\x9d \xe2\x86\x92 SSO/SAML + RBAC overview (and Security FAQ)\\n\\n---", "score": 0.507047, "rank": 15, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "2c824612-e636-550a-ac74-0c9964a63829", "search_unit_id": "2c824612-e636-550a-ac74-0c9964a63829", "block_from_id": "e98f4e0f-f722-5307-b02c-e778833085f3", "block_to_id": "1a953221-7c0e-56d9-a576-0c9ac7edd877", "section_path": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > When to use this evidence pack", "document_id": "9e945a2c-4051-5cc3-99ca-a95c267f6bdf", "document_version_id": "c6871709-6afb-56d9-883f-81f2536ab49e", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a9a67d815b07451e90ed8c3d1734d007.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-8", "heading": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > When to use this evidence pack", "content": "When to use this evidence pack\\n\\nUse the evidence pack when a prospect:\\n\\n- Requests \xe2\x80\x9cproof\xe2\x80\x9d beyond the Security FAQ (e.g., **SOC 2**, pen test attestation, encryption diagrams)\\n- Is moving into **security review**, **procurement**, **POC**, or **Private/Dedicated architecture** discussions\\n- Requires security documentation before allowing a technical pilot or data access\\n\\nIf the prospect only needs high-level posture statements, start with the **Regulated Workloads Security FAQ** and only escalate to evidence if requested.\\n\\n---", "score": 0.489484, "rank": 16, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "5f1b3e62-ef47-5123-9d26-bafc6514c609", "search_unit_id": "5f1b3e62-ef47-5123-9d26-bafc6514c609", "block_from_id": "0a05eefb-144d-527c-9d1d-2e2a6b91b44a", "block_to_id": "732cb8e4-cd2f-52a7-9b5a-6e95e0538458", "section_path": "Cross Role Tool Access Governance And Self Service Handbook 2027", "document_id": "4495c7a6-685c-5344-90c0-ef0b6cee8521", "document_version_id": "43048def-240f-53d7-bef2-2be51354fc0d", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_d7f0edbaa28147da807b5b85ea495bd1.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "p-37", "heading": "Cross Role Tool Access Governance And Self Service Handbook 2027", "content": "15) Appendix: Common Role Examples\\n\\n- developer-readonly: view-only GitHub repos, dev dashboards.\\n- ci-builder: CI pipeline trigger and artifact write to staging buckets.\\n- infra-operator: deploy to staging, manage infra-as-code prs.\\n- prod-admin: full production console access (high-risk).\\n\\nContact and Escalation\\n\\n- People Ops team: people-ops@redwood.com\\n- Security on-call: security-oncall@redwood.com\\n- Tool owner contact: listed on each tool owner page in the Access Portal\\n\\nChange Log\\n\\n- 2025-03-18: Created by Aisha Patel (initial policy and flows).\\n- 2025-09-10: Added emergency fast-track and SLA clarifications (reviewers: Marcus Liu).\\n- 2026-01-14: Extended roadmap and clarified offboarding SLAs (reviewers: Sofia Ramirez, Ethan Morales).", "score": 0.473366, "rank": 17, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "2469d4d0-949d-5293-a069-fe296d2894fc", "search_unit_id": "2469d4d0-949d-5293-a069-fe296d2894fc", "block_from_id": "f22b9c33-b69b-5f0e-bc85-2d5ba5386aa1", "block_to_id": "67a1d84b-4826-5c08-9e13-0747a4f25110", "section_path": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > Audience and roles", "document_id": "9e945a2c-4051-5cc3-99ca-a95c267f6bdf", "document_version_id": "c6871709-6afb-56d9-883f-81f2536ab49e", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a9a67d815b07451e90ed8c3d1734d007.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-13", "heading": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > Audience and roles", "content": "Audience and roles\\n\\n**Primary users (internal):** AEs, SEs, PMM, Security/GRC.\\n\\n**Owners:**\\n\\n- Evidence pack operations + distribution: **Daniel Carter (Security/Compliance)**\\n- Questionnaires + GRC workflow: **Olga Petrov (GRC)**\\n- External security posture approvals / escalation: **Dr. Aisha Rahman (CISO)**\\n- Security/compliance product alignment: **Naomi Feldman (Security)**\\n- NDA / legal terms and redlines: **Michael Grant (Legal)**\\n\\n---", "score": 0.471576, "rank": 18, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "1e4e71b3-5f51-57f6-bd1d-f572d0d9bbe4", "search_unit_id": "1e4e71b3-5f51-57f6-bd1d-f572d0d9bbe4", "block_from_id": "a05bea6d-5cf4-51c3-98a4-7bbd726a3675", "block_to_id": "20c76424-30df-5d27-9f60-4dfdd25bf32b", "section_path": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > How to request evidence (internal process) > Request submission", "document_id": "9e945a2c-4051-5cc3-99ca-a95c267f6bdf", "document_version_id": "c6871709-6afb-56d9-883f-81f2536ab49e", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a9a67d815b07451e90ed8c3d1734d007.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-60", "heading": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > How to request evidence (internal process) > Request submission", "content": "Request submission\\n\\nSubmit an evidence request with:\\n\\n- Company name + opportunity link (HubSpot)\\n- Primary recipient(s): name, title, email domain\\n- Requested artifacts (from the list above)\\n- Deadline and meeting date (if any)\\n- Any special handling (e.g., \xe2\x80\x9cno email attachments\xe2\x80\x9d, \xe2\x80\x9cupload to their portal\xe2\x80\x9d)\\n\\n**Where to submit:**\\n\\n- Primary: Security/GRC intake (internal) \xe2\x80\x94 route to **Olga Petrov**\\n- Backup: Email **security-evidence@redwoodinference.com** (internal alias)\\n- Coordination: Slack **#eng-security** for urgency only (do not paste artifacts into Slack)", "score": 0.453668, "rank": 19, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "9adc00a6-be46-55a0-b8c2-57aa6fd35f53", "search_unit_id": "9adc00a6-be46-55a0-b8c2-57aa6fd35f53", "block_from_id": "34f5703f-c1e3-5740-9958-da6df7619bb6", "block_to_id": "c1408256-d151-54a6-b958-3235d04779d3", "section_path": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > What we do NOT share (default)", "document_id": "9e945a2c-4051-5cc3-99ca-a95c267f6bdf", "document_version_id": "c6871709-6afb-56d9-883f-81f2536ab49e", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a9a67d815b07451e90ed8c3d1734d007.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-52", "heading": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > What we do NOT share (default)", "content": "What we do NOT share (default)\\n\\nTo reduce risk and keep evidence consistent, the following are **not shared** with prospects unless explicitly approved by Security + Legal:\\n\\n- Raw penetration test report details (tool output, exploit steps, full findings list)\\n- Internal runbooks, on-call procedures, or detailed incident postmortems\\n- Source code, internal architecture docs with sensitive implementation details\\n- Customer lists, identifiable customer configurations, or production screenshots\\n- Any artifact containing secrets, internal hostnames, private IPs, or employee personal data\\n\\nIf a prospect requests any of the above, route to **Olga Petrov** (GRC) and **Michael Grant** (Legal) before responding.\\n\\n---", "score": 0.453668, "rank": 20, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "00569f12-4695-570f-ba94-a9f687015bc0", "search_unit_id": "00569f12-4695-570f-ba94-a9f687015bc0", "block_from_id": "311959b0-6977-5ac9-ae94-08b6b70ca891", "block_to_id": "6f343d30-ae6b-583f-868c-dc5e60696e94", "section_path": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > Standard prospect-facing language (use verbatim where possible) > SOC 2", "document_id": "9e945a2c-4051-5cc3-99ca-a95c267f6bdf", "document_version_id": "c6871709-6afb-56d9-883f-81f2536ab49e", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a9a67d815b07451e90ed8c3d1734d007.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-77", "heading": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > Standard prospect-facing language (use verbatim where possible) > SOC 2", "content": "Standard prospect-facing language (use verbatim where possible)\\n\\nSOC 2\\n\\n\xe2\x80\x9cWe can provide our most recent SOC 2 Type II report under NDA. If you confirm the recipients and NDA status, our GRC team will share a secure, access-controlled link.\xe2\x80\x9d", "score": 0.453668, "rank": 21, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "835e4b28-cf88-50fa-af45-cbf85efa2c04", "search_unit_id": "835e4b28-cf88-50fa-af45-cbf85efa2c04", "block_from_id": "3b6fde36-eb81-51fb-a6a3-0ebe18a83e99", "block_to_id": "af968eb5-e0cb-5267-9ec3-8c7369271498", "section_path": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > Evidence pack contents (prospect-facing) > B) Security architecture and encryption", "document_id": "9e945a2c-4051-5cc3-99ca-a95c267f6bdf", "document_version_id": "c6871709-6afb-56d9-883f-81f2536ab49e", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a9a67d815b07451e90ed8c3d1734d007.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-27", "heading": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > Evidence pack contents (prospect-facing) > B) Security architecture and encryption", "content": "B) Security architecture and encryption\\n\\n4) **Encryption overview diagram**\\n\\n- Covers:\\n  - Encryption in transit (TLS)\\n  - Encryption at rest\\n  - Key management model (including KMS/HSM integration options for Private deployments)\\n- Includes clear scoping language for Hosted vs Dedicated vs Private.\\n\\n5) **Data flow / request processing diagram (serving plane)**\\n\\n- Illustrates request path (API ingress \xe2\x86\x92 routing \xe2\x86\x92 inference runtime \xe2\x86\x92 logging/metrics), with explicit callouts for where content may be processed.\\n- Used to answer \xe2\x80\x9cWhere does data go?\xe2\x80\x9d and \xe2\x80\x9cWhat is persisted?\xe2\x80\x9d questions.\\n\\n6) **Network boundary overview (Dedicated/Private)**\\n\\n- Describes reference topologies (VPC peering / private endpoints / customer-managed networking patterns).\\n- Includes shared responsibility notes.", "score": 0.453668, "rank": 22, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "6ba932e5-e3ac-5178-acc1-a2cbd515131c", "search_unit_id": "6ba932e5-e3ac-5178-acc1-a2cbd515131c", "block_from_id": "5a825b8e-94c9-5a5f-9a40-d09318186cb7", "block_to_id": "4828cc63-2ba8-5b1b-85c5-8ceb7e71074f", "section_path": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > How evidence is shared (distribution rules) > Not approved", "document_id": "9e945a2c-4051-5cc3-99ca-a95c267f6bdf", "document_version_id": "c6871709-6afb-56d9-883f-81f2536ab49e", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a9a67d815b07451e90ed8c3d1734d007.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-71", "heading": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > How evidence is shared (distribution rules) > Not approved", "content": "Not approved\\n\\n- Emailing SOC 2 as an attachment\\n- Uploading to a prospect\xe2\x80\x99s procurement portal without Security + Legal approval\\n- Storing evidence in uncontrolled folders or personal drives", "score": 0.453668, "rank": 23, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "bf7b8c11-b250-57a8-883b-87d00556de9e", "search_unit_id": "bf7b8c11-b250-57a8-883b-87d00556de9e", "block_from_id": "9c66d0db-63a9-5cc1-87d7-16de0856fe04", "block_to_id": "d9338132-8f83-508a-8358-87eaadd6c991", "section_path": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > Evidence pack contents (prospect-facing) > E) Assurance supporting materials (as needed)", "document_id": "9e945a2c-4051-5cc3-99ca-a95c267f6bdf", "document_version_id": "c6871709-6afb-56d9-883f-81f2536ab49e", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a9a67d815b07451e90ed8c3d1734d007.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-46", "heading": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > Evidence pack contents (prospect-facing) > E) Assurance supporting materials (as needed)", "content": "E) Assurance supporting materials (as needed)\\n\\n12) **Pen test attestation letter / executive summary (when available)**\\n\\n- High-level summary appropriate for procurement/security reviewers.\\n- Full reports are generally not shared; exceptions require CISO + Legal approval.\\n\\n13) **Vulnerability management + incident response policy summaries**\\n\\n- Policy summaries suitable for \xe2\x80\x9cdoes your program exist?\xe2\x80\x9d questions.\\n- Not intended to replace detailed internal runbooks.\\n\\n---", "score": 0.453668, "rank": 24, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "238e4ad6-1425-50fc-9f18-bb0556a3b03f", "search_unit_id": "238e4ad6-1425-50fc-9f18-bb0556a3b03f", "block_from_id": "c07c1ffd-685d-5061-8196-cfd8189a2747", "block_to_id": "3b741f46-59a5-50f4-8e65-5697328750f2", "section_path": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > How to request evidence (internal process) > Turnaround targets", "document_id": "9e945a2c-4051-5cc3-99ca-a95c267f6bdf", "document_version_id": "c6871709-6afb-56d9-883f-81f2536ab49e", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a9a67d815b07451e90ed8c3d1734d007.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-65", "heading": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > How to request evidence (internal process) > Turnaround targets", "content": "Turnaround targets\\n\\n- Standard pack (SOC 2 + diagrams + policy statements): **2 business days**\\n- Custom questionnaire + custom evidence mapping: **3\xe2\x80\x935 business days**\\n- Rush requests: same-day only with Security approval and clear business justification\\n\\n---", "score": 0.453668, "rank": 25, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "d406fa30-276e-53f3-9fc0-b949e7aa51bf", "search_unit_id": "d406fa30-276e-53f3-9fc0-b949e7aa51bf", "block_from_id": "e199143d-8e88-5b70-997d-ba86542b6558", "block_to_id": "eda74822-b95f-55cd-8f0b-99c2cbf37426", "section_path": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > Change log", "document_id": "9e945a2c-4051-5cc3-99ca-a95c267f6bdf", "document_version_id": "c6871709-6afb-56d9-883f-81f2536ab49e", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a9a67d815b07451e90ed8c3d1734d007.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-97", "heading": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > Change log", "content": "Change log\\n\\n- 2025-06-06: Updated audit log sample description and export guidance; tightened \xe2\x80\x9cdo not share\xe2\x80\x9d list and distribution rules.\\n- 2025-05-15: Added retention/residency statements and clarified NDA gating for SOC 2.\\n- 2025-04-29: Initial publication for ABM regulated workloads pilot.", "score": 0.453668, "rank": 26, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "4f1e3288-89aa-5951-ba49-c115984a9cd6", "search_unit_id": "4f1e3288-89aa-5951-ba49-c115984a9cd6", "block_from_id": "3f480f37-08c1-5167-b58f-da00f1c723c2", "block_to_id": "3044f3ae-0217-510e-98e8-c60148e3effd", "section_path": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > Evidence pack contents (prospect-facing) > D) Data retention, residency, and data handling statements", "document_id": "9e945a2c-4051-5cc3-99ca-a95c267f6bdf", "document_version_id": "c6871709-6afb-56d9-883f-81f2536ab49e", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a9a67d815b07451e90ed8c3d1734d007.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-41", "heading": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > Evidence pack contents (prospect-facing) > D) Data retention, residency, and data handling statements", "content": "D) Data retention, residency, and data handling statements\\n\\n10) **Data retention policy statement (customer-facing)**\\n\\n- Clarifies retention and deletion behavior for different data categories (e.g., operational metadata vs request content), including scoping by deployment type.\\n- Includes:\\n  - Default retention windows (as applicable)\\n  - Configurable retention controls (where supported)\\n  - Deletion request process and escalation\\n\\n11) **Data residency / region availability statement**\\n\\n- High-level statement of supported regions for Hosted/Dedicated and options for Private.\\n- Includes language on how region selection interacts with support and failover.", "score": 0.453668, "rank": 27, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "243fc1b8-41ae-5cae-94e3-f5339836603d", "search_unit_id": "243fc1b8-41ae-5cae-94e3-f5339836603d", "block_from_id": "76366aee-90bb-5169-9549-dc08515ba3a1", "block_to_id": "e86d67d5-dc92-533a-b071-87e1db3ae212", "section_path": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > Confidentiality, handling, and safe-claim guidance", "document_id": "9e945a2c-4051-5cc3-99ca-a95c267f6bdf", "document_version_id": "c6871709-6afb-56d9-883f-81f2536ab49e", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a9a67d815b07451e90ed8c3d1734d007.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-88", "heading": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > Confidentiality, handling, and safe-claim guidance", "content": "Confidentiality, handling, and safe-claim guidance\\n\\n- Treat all evidence pack artifacts as **Confidential / Controlled**.\\n- Do not modify controlled artifacts (SOC 2, formal statements). If you need a tailored statement, request it via GRC.\\n- Do not make absolute claims (e.g., \xe2\x80\x9cHIPAA certified\xe2\x80\x9d, \xe2\x80\x9cfully compliant with X\xe2\x80\x9d) unless explicitly approved in the Security FAQ.\\n- If a prospect requests a signed attestation, route to **Michael Grant (Legal)** and **Olga Petrov (GRC)**.\\n\\n---", "score": 0.453668, "rank": 28, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "d3945569-c0ff-57e9-a6cb-89ea8e6df83c", "search_unit_id": "d3945569-c0ff-57e9-a6cb-89ea8e6df83c", "block_from_id": "7cf720fd-3280-5679-aad5-739793494b7f", "block_to_id": "55e743c3-872b-5aaf-9736-d282393e113a", "section_path": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > Evidence pack contents (prospect-facing) > A) Compliance reports and attestations", "document_id": "9e945a2c-4051-5cc3-99ca-a95c267f6bdf", "document_version_id": "c6871709-6afb-56d9-883f-81f2536ab49e", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a9a67d815b07451e90ed8c3d1734d007.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-20", "heading": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > Evidence pack contents (prospect-facing) > A) Compliance reports and attestations", "content": "A) Compliance reports and attestations\\n\\n1) **SOC 2 Type II report (most recent period)**\\n\\n- Includes: auditor\xe2\x80\x99s opinion, system description, control activities, and test results for the audit period.\\n- Notes:\\n  - Shared **only under NDA**.\\n  - Distributed via secure, access-controlled link (see \xe2\x80\x9cSharing rules\xe2\x80\x9d).\\n  - Watermarked per recipient.\\n\\n2) **SOC 2 bridge/coverage letter (if required between audit periods)**\\n\\n- Used when a prospect needs an update between SOC 2 reporting periods.\\n- Shared under NDA.\\n\\n3) **Security program overview (one-page)**\\n\\n- High-level summary of security governance, risk management, and control domains.\\n- Suitable for early-stage security conversations.", "score": 0.453668, "rank": 29, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "263f8e8a-fa12-5743-9644-354acec3560b", "search_unit_id": "263f8e8a-fa12-5743-9644-354acec3560b", "block_from_id": "da4d3356-1d63-5bae-8621-69e4f2150f04", "block_to_id": "fbdd4e78-afc2-5953-89af-f5cfad5dc1b2", "section_path": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > How to request evidence (internal process) > Eligibility checklist (before requesting)", "document_id": "9e945a2c-4051-5cc3-99ca-a95c267f6bdf", "document_version_id": "c6871709-6afb-56d9-883f-81f2536ab49e", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a9a67d815b07451e90ed8c3d1734d007.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-57", "heading": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > How to request evidence (internal process) > Eligibility checklist (before requesting)", "content": "How to request evidence (internal process)\\n\\nEligibility checklist (before requesting)\\n\\n1) Confirm the prospect name, domain, and opportunity stage.\\n2) Confirm whether an **NDA is executed** (required for SOC 2 and most controlled artifacts).\\n3) Confirm what the prospect actually needs (avoid oversharing). Ask: \xe2\x80\x9cWhich control domains are you validating and what is the deadline?\xe2\x80\x9d", "score": 0.453668, "rank": 30, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "3d4ea93b-bdeb-50c1-995c-51593f022afb", "search_unit_id": "3d4ea93b-bdeb-50c1-995c-51593f022afb", "block_from_id": "c550f059-8d15-5302-b96a-2fa706be4bc6", "block_to_id": "72aa9312-8383-528e-a9b1-97b98c126008", "section_path": "20250520 Trident Financial Dpa And Sla Redlines", "document_id": "f15d34e1-debf-503d-ad3e-2fd3512fa1f9", "document_version_id": "fb519f86-ed83-52c4-97bd-0a58c4f47f19", "document_name": "gmail__dsid_98d7acbda29a4260a413c8d6da7207a3.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h1-0", "heading": "20250520 Trident Financial Dpa And Sla Redlines", "content": "20250520 Trident Financial Dpa And Sla Redlines\\n\\nSource type: gmail\\nDocument ID: dsid_98d7acbda29a4260a413c8d6da7207a3\\nRelease: v1.0.0\\nBenchmark: EnterpriseRAG-Bench synthetic upstream data\\nRe: Trident Financial Services \xe2\x80\x94 DPA + SLA redlines (Private/VPC)\\n\\nFrom: Elaine Porter <elaine.porter@tridentfs.com>\\nTo: Michael Grant <michael.grant@redwoodinference.com>\\nCc: Raj Mehra <raj.mehra@tridentfs.com>, Maya Chen <maya.chen@tridentfs.com>, Avery Johnson <avery.johnson@redwoodinference.com>\\nDate: Tue, May 20, 2025 at 9:12 AM\\nSubject: Trident Financial Services \xe2\x80\x94 DPA + SLA redlines (Private/VPC)\\n\\nMichael,\\n\\nAttached are Trident\xe2\x80\x99s redlines to (i) the DPA and (ii) the SLA. Key items called out in comments in the docs:\\n\\n1) Data residency: require \xe2\x80\x9cno processing outside the United States\xe2\x80\x9d and restrictions on subcontractors.\\n2) Retention: require 0-day retention by default; deletion within 24 hours of request.\\n3) Audit logs: require export to Trident-controlled SIEM; log retention 7 years.\\n4) Security incident notice: 24-hour notification.\\n5) Liability: increase caps + remove carve-outs.\\n6) SLA: increase service credits; add RTO/RPO language.\\n\\nPlease confirm whether Redwood can agree to these as written, and if not, provide counter language.\\n\\nRegards,\\nElaine Porter\\nSenior Counsel, Technology\\nTrident Financial Services\\nAttachments:\\n\\n- Trident_Redwood_DPA_Redlines_2025-05-19.docx\\n- Trident_Redwood_SLA_Redlines_2025-05-19.docx\\n\\n---\\nFrom: Avery Johnson <avery.johnson@redwoodinference.com>\\nTo: Michael Grant <michael.grant@redwoodinference.com>\\nCc: Stephanie Nguyen <stephanie.nguyen@redwoodinference.com>\\nDate: Tue, May 20, 2025 at 9:24 AM\\nSubject: Fwd: Trident Financial Services \xe2\x80\x94 DPA + SLA redlines (Private/VPC)\\n\\nFwd\xe2\x80\x99ing Trident redlines. They\xe2\x80\x99re pushing hard on \xe2\x80\x9cUS-only processing\xe2\x80\x9d + long audit log retention. This is for Private (VPC) deployment.\\n\\nWe need a quick turnaround \xe2\x80\x94 procurement wants a same-week response.\\n\\nAvery\\n---\\nFrom: Michael Grant <michael.grant@redwoodinference.com>\\nTo: Sofia Mendes <sofia.mendes@redwoodinference.com>, Naomi Feldman <naomi.feldman@redwoodinference.com>\\nCc: Dr. Aisha Rahman <aisha.rahman@redwoodinference.com>, Vivek Kulkarni <vivek.kulkarni@redwoodinference.com>, Stephanie Nguyen <stephanie.nguyen@redwoodinference.com>, Avery Johnson <avery.johnson@redwoodinference.com>\\nDate: Tue, May 20, 2025 at 10:03 AM\\nSubject: Trident \xe2\x80\x94 DPA/SLA redlines (need positions today)\\n\\nTeam \xe2\x80\x94 pls review Trident redlines (attached). Need positions + proposed counter language today.\\n\\nTop risk flags / where we likely need to counter:\\nA) Data residency / \xe2\x80\x9cno processing outside US\xe2\x80\x9d\\n\\n- We can generally commit to processing in the customer-selected region for Private/VPC, but need to be careful about support access, telemetry, and backups. We should avoid an absolute \xe2\x80\x9cno bytes ever leave US\xe2\x80\x9d statement.\\n- Ask: Naomi/Aisha \xe2\x80\x94 what can we safely say re: support systems and subprocessors? Any standard language for \xe2\x80\x9csupport may access from outside but data remains in-region\xe2\x80\x9d vs \xe2\x80\x9csupport access limited to US persons\xe2\x80\x9d etc.\\n\\nB) Retention / deletion\\n\\n- They want 0-day retention by default + deletion within 24 hours.\\n- Need to align with product realities: request logs / audit logs retention is configurable; but 0-day might break ops/troubleshooting.\\n- We can offer tight retention for request payloads and define audit logs separately (audit logs are not the same as prompts/responses).\\n\\nC) Audit logs / 7-year retention + SIEM export requirement\\n\\n- We can offer export hooks (customer-controlled destination) + sample schema, but 7-year retention in our systems is a commercial/compliance add-on question and probably \xe2\x80\x9ccustomer retains in their SIEM\xe2\x80\x9d rather than us.\\n\\nD) Incident notice: 24 hours", "score": 0.462583, "rank": 31, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "a83723a0-72a7-5af1-b3ba-8d84e4735006", "search_unit_id": "a83723a0-72a7-5af1-b3ba-8d84e4735006", "block_from_id": "aac3fa7e-ea29-5a00-bef9-f7e5a58991b0", "block_to_id": "f9d26200-f904-5292-8e39-e21213410bc6", "section_path": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 15. Operational checks (required)", "document_id": "85b37108-378d-55be-805e-bdcd31101232", "document_version_id": "6851cc1f-d2a1-5421-91d3-d80c5cf3f3e8", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_37befab212ae4ed9be7ebe612d942658.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-165", "heading": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 15. Operational checks (required)", "content": "15. Operational checks (required)\\n\\nEngineering and Finance must continuously monitor:\\n\\n- Duplicate billing_dedupe_key rate\\n- Null/unknown dimensions (model, region, endpoint)\\n- Drift between aggregated usage events and billing ledger (daily + weekly)\\n- Late-arriving events and backfill volumes\\n\\nSee:\\n\\n- Meter sanity dashboard spec\\n- Reconciliation playbook\\n\\n---", "score": 0.438449, "rank": 32, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "4ddf261d-5dba-529d-a2cd-8f0c44d1cc8c", "search_unit_id": "4ddf261d-5dba-529d-a2cd-8f0c44d1cc8c", "block_from_id": "f9c94cb7-4b8c-57f2-84da-23590ee0f58c", "block_to_id": "854d4a3f-8187-5bde-b2e0-effd62547fed", "section_path": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Summary", "document_id": "98457c4e-240d-5e44-8a6a-abb94e877555", "document_version_id": "b983f052-79ea-54aa-b5f9-45e0bce342f3", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_8c24fd15fe5b473eb71341751d292b6e.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-2", "heading": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Summary", "content": "Summary\\n\\nThis ADR defines how Redwood Hosted assigns requests to **baseline** vs **canary** cohorts during a hosted canary rollout, and how the configured canary percentage is enforced across regions. The decision prioritizes:\\n\\n- Deterministic, reproducible cohort assignment (especially across retries)\\n- Sticky behavior when customers provide stable identifiers\\n- Minimal coordination/state across regions and data planes\\n- Observability clarity when effective serving differs from intended cohort", "score": 0.429644, "rank": 33, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "e17370ce-da6a-5ebc-a9e7-5268533bbb9e", "search_unit_id": "e17370ce-da6a-5ebc-a9e7-5268533bbb9e", "block_from_id": "10470b72-f499-578e-a14a-0f7dc93d14ba", "block_to_id": "ed63b370-f426-5175-8502-47a10f1de74c", "section_path": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > Standard prospect-facing language (use verbatim where possible) > Retention / data boundaries", "document_id": "9e945a2c-4051-5cc3-99ca-a95c267f6bdf", "document_version_id": "c6871709-6afb-56d9-883f-81f2536ab49e", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a9a67d815b07451e90ed8c3d1734d007.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-82", "heading": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > Standard prospect-facing language (use verbatim where possible) > Retention / data boundaries", "content": "Retention / data boundaries\\n\\n\xe2\x80\x9cWe can share our customer-facing retention statement covering what data is stored for operational purposes and what retention controls apply by deployment type. If you have a specific retention requirement (e.g., X days), we\xe2\x80\x99ll confirm feasibility in the context of your desired deployment (Hosted, Dedicated, or Private).\xe2\x80\x9d\\n\\n---", "score": 0.453668, "rank": 34, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "85491455-d406-503e-a459-b54a0e317d99", "search_unit_id": "85491455-d406-503e-a459-b54a0e317d99", "block_from_id": "505d7a71-906f-51da-8a2f-16bb8623a5f1", "block_to_id": "9221a317-d1d4-5e53-aba4-641e40801628", "section_path": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > How evidence is shared (distribution rules) > Tracking requirements", "document_id": "9e945a2c-4051-5cc3-99ca-a95c267f6bdf", "document_version_id": "c6871709-6afb-56d9-883f-81f2536ab49e", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a9a67d815b07451e90ed8c3d1734d007.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-73", "heading": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > How evidence is shared (distribution rules) > Tracking requirements", "content": "Tracking requirements\\n\\nFor every controlled artifact share (especially SOC 2):\\n\\n- Record the recipients and date shared\\n- Record the NDA status\\n- Attach/share the distribution note in the CRM opportunity (or link to the request record)\\n\\n---", "score": 0.453668, "rank": 35, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "aa536da1-fc3b-537e-9974-380591b801c2", "search_unit_id": "aa536da1-fc3b-537e-9974-380591b801c2", "block_from_id": "5197ce0f-8cfe-5bde-8890-29b5a5c1b8a6", "block_to_id": "afb656de-2641-52ae-a2e0-9bfb19888a2a", "section_path": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > Escalations", "document_id": "9e945a2c-4051-5cc3-99ca-a95c267f6bdf", "document_version_id": "c6871709-6afb-56d9-883f-81f2536ab49e", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a9a67d815b07451e90ed8c3d1734d007.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-91", "heading": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > Escalations", "content": "Escalations\\n\\nEscalate immediately if:\\n\\n- Prospect requests full pen test report or vulnerability details\\n- Prospect requires portal uploads or broad internal distribution\\n- Prospect asks for contractual security commitments not in standard terms (custom SLAs, residency guarantees, audit rights)\\n\\nEscalation contacts:\\n\\n- GRC workflow: **Olga Petrov**\\n- Legal terms/redlines: **Michael Grant** / **Sofia Mendes**\\n- Security exec escalation: **Dr. Aisha Rahman**\\n\\n---", "score": 0.453668, "rank": 36, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "d447b675-e26c-5b56-a4e7-bacc261e36b8", "search_unit_id": "d447b675-e26c-5b56-a4e7-bacc261e36b8", "block_from_id": "43208cac-cea3-507a-ba62-96ccba9ae71c", "block_to_id": "0f5a9819-ddcf-539e-b68f-361309227e43", "section_path": "Metering To Invoice Reconciliation Playbook > Thresholds and SLOs (default) > Correctness/drift", "document_id": "5dd49655-30e7-5bb0-9f51-c936960c0c6a", "document_version_id": "c0e0c79d-406f-5fb0-b65c-732ae9489a5c", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_602422b841b94f5c8089555015df0ed8.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-50", "heading": "Metering To Invoice Reconciliation Playbook > Thresholds and SLOs (default) > Correctness/drift", "content": "Correctness/drift\\n\\nAll drift thresholds are computed at the reconciliation grain.\\n\\n- **Daily drift (Hosted API tokens):** absolute drift \xe2\x89\xa4 **0.25%** AND \xe2\x89\xa4 **$250** per account-day\\n- **Weekly drift:** absolute drift \xe2\x89\xa4 **0.10%** AND \xe2\x89\xa4 **$500** per account-week\\n- **Dedicated GPU-seconds:** absolute drift \xe2\x89\xa4 **0.20%** AND \xe2\x89\xa4 **$500** per account-day", "score": 0.427142, "rank": 37, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "27b02fe4-db1f-5edb-891b-ff913f1eaa97", "search_unit_id": "27b02fe4-db1f-5edb-891b-ff913f1eaa97", "block_from_id": "ea497920-d40d-5b91-8aa7-ead6a9593afe", "block_to_id": "ac4eb2b3-9e72-5092-844f-038dff109102", "section_path": "20250520 Trident Financial Dpa And Sla Redlines", "document_id": "f15d34e1-debf-503d-ad3e-2fd3512fa1f9", "document_version_id": "fb519f86-ed83-52c4-97bd-0a58c4f47f19", "document_name": "gmail__dsid_98d7acbda29a4260a413c8d6da7207a3.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "p-66", "heading": "20250520 Trident Financial Dpa And Sla Redlines", "content": "If helpful, I can jump on a 30-minute working session tomorrow with you + Raj/Maya to walk through the edits line-by-line and align on any remaining gaps.\\n\\nBest,\\nMichael Grant\\nContracting Lead (Legal)\\nRedwood Inference\\nAttachment:\\n\\n- Redwood_Proposed_DPA_SLA_Counters_Trident_2025-05-20.docx\\n\\n---\\nFrom: Raj Mehra <raj.mehra@tridentfs.com>\\nTo: Michael Grant <michael.grant@redwoodinference.com>\\nCc: Elaine Porter <elaine.porter@tridentfs.com>, Maya Chen <maya.chen@tridentfs.com>, Avery Johnson <avery.johnson@redwoodinference.com>\\nDate: Tue, May 20, 2025 at 5:48 PM\\nSubject: Re: Trident Financial Services \xe2\x80\x94 DPA + SLA redlines (Private/VPC)\\n\\nMichael,\\n\\nGot it \xe2\x80\x94 thank you for the quick turn. We\xe2\x80\x99ll review the counter doc tonight.\\n\\nCan you hold time tomorrow 11:00\xe2\x80\x9311:30am PT for a working session? Security will want to focus on (i) the \xe2\x80\x9cin-region\xe2\x80\x9d language and (ii) audit log retention/export responsibilities.\\n\\nRaj Mehra\\nVendor Management\\nTrident Financial Services", "score": 0.415274, "rank": 38, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "035700c2-4813-5359-9760-a57b294c2d92", "search_unit_id": "035700c2-4813-5359-9760-a57b294c2d92", "block_from_id": "1e5902ec-db24-504f-85b0-1511300cf487", "block_to_id": "db036fdd-174e-5959-8c33-d80c405cacf4", "section_path": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 1. Purpose", "document_id": "85b37108-378d-55be-805e-bdcd31101232", "document_version_id": "6851cc1f-d2a1-5421-91d3-d80c5cf3f3e8", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_37befab212ae4ed9be7ebe612d942658.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-2", "heading": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 1. Purpose", "content": "1. Purpose\\n\\nThis page defines the **formal billing meters** for Redwood Inference\xe2\x80\x99s **Hosted API** (including OpenAI-compatible endpoints) and the rules used to compute:\\n\\n- **Request counts**\\n- **Prompt (input) tokens**\\n- **Completion (output) tokens**\\n- **Billed tokens** (the chargeable unit for usage-based pricing)\\n\\nIt also specifies:\\n\\n- Streaming semantics and disconnect behavior\\n- Retries, idempotency, and deduplication requirements (to prevent double-counting)\\n- Tool/function calling token attribution rules\\n- Rounding rules\\n- How meters map to the **billing ledger**, **customer Usage API**, and **invoices**\\n\\nThis spec is used by Engineering (telemetry + billing hooks), Finance/RevRec (reconciliation + close), RevOps/Product (pricing experiments), and Legal (usage definitions in contracts).\\n\\n---", "score": 0.408554, "rank": 39, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "9cf8ff01-c68e-57b1-b689-31cf778a3fe7", "search_unit_id": "9cf8ff01-c68e-57b1-b689-31cf778a3fe7", "block_from_id": "9333e634-1275-5864-b5fe-cd1438d48319", "block_to_id": "e2516875-38f1-5de4-9730-420fba5fc71e", "section_path": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > Standard prospect-facing language (use verbatim where possible) > Audit logs", "document_id": "9e945a2c-4051-5cc3-99ca-a95c267f6bdf", "document_version_id": "c6871709-6afb-56d9-883f-81f2536ab49e", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a9a67d815b07451e90ed8c3d1734d007.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-80", "heading": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > Standard prospect-facing language (use verbatim where possible) > Audit logs", "content": "Audit logs\\n\\n\xe2\x80\x9cWe support audit logging for administrative and access events. We can share a redacted sample and a field glossary so your security team can confirm the level of detail and export patterns.\xe2\x80\x9d", "score": 0.453668, "rank": 40, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "e6f18277-f4fa-5c1d-a58a-6bd157d1f9de", "search_unit_id": "e6f18277-f4fa-5c1d-a58a-6bd157d1f9de", "block_from_id": "4f6a97d6-6456-55c2-9c30-c042cabee61d", "block_to_id": "debd2a85-8435-5fdd-a18b-91772e3f7752", "section_path": "20250520 Trident Financial Dpa And Sla Redlines", "document_id": "f15d34e1-debf-503d-ad3e-2fd3512fa1f9", "document_version_id": "fb519f86-ed83-52c4-97bd-0a58c4f47f19", "document_name": "gmail__dsid_98d7acbda29a4260a413c8d6da7207a3.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "p-44", "heading": "20250520 Trident Financial Dpa And Sla Redlines", "content": "For incident notification: 24h is not something I\xe2\x80\x99d sign as a default. If we make it \xe2\x80\x9cwithin 24 hours of confirmation of a material Security Incident,\xe2\x80\x9d that\xe2\x80\x99s safer but still requires on-call process maturity. Prefer 72h.\\n\\nAisha\\n---\\nFrom: Sofia Mendes <sofia.mendes@redwoodinference.com>\\nTo: Michael Grant <michael.grant@redwoodinference.com>\\nCc: Naomi Feldman <naomi.feldman@redwoodinference.com>, Dr. Aisha Rahman <aisha.rahman@redwoodinference.com>, Stephanie Nguyen <stephanie.nguyen@redwoodinference.com>, Avery Johnson <avery.johnson@redwoodinference.com>\\nDate: Tue, May 20, 2025 at 1:04 PM\\nSubject: Re: Trident \xe2\x80\x94 DPA/SLA redlines (need positions today)\\n\\nCommercial/legal positions:\\nLiability:\\n\\n- Hold line on our standard cap + standard carve-outs. If Trident insists on higher cap, that\xe2\x80\x99s business approval (Stephanie) + likely pricing adjustment.\\n- No removal of exclusion of consequential damages.\\n\\nSLA:\\n\\n- We can offer incremental service credits within a defined monthly cap. Avoid open-ended credits.\\n- RTO/RPO only if we explicitly sell DR/secondary region; otherwise \xe2\x80\x9ccommercially reasonable efforts\xe2\x80\x9d + incident comms.\\n\\nDPA:\\n\\n- Residency: okay to commit to in-region processing/storage for Private/VPC; reject absolute \xe2\x80\x9cno processing outside US\xe2\x80\x9d if it constrains support/metadata.\\n- Subprocessors: standard list + notice/update mechanism.\\n- Retention: counter 0-day default + 24h deletion. Suggest \xe2\x80\x9cconfigurable retention\xe2\x80\x9d and a reasonable deletion timeframe.\\n\\nIf you want, I can consolidate into a short redline memo + draft email to Elaine.\\n\\nSofia\\n---\\nFrom: Michael Grant <michael.grant@redwoodinference.com>\\nTo: Elaine Porter <elaine.porter@tridentfs.com>\\nCc: Raj Mehra <raj.mehra@tridentfs.com>, Maya Chen <maya.chen@tridentfs.com>, Avery Johnson <avery.johnson@redwoodinference.com>\\nDate: Tue, May 20, 2025 at 4:37 PM\\nSubject: Re: Trident Financial Services \xe2\x80\x94 DPA + SLA redlines (Private/VPC)\\n\\nElaine \xe2\x80\x94 thank you. We reviewed and prepared a set of counters that we believe addresses Trident\xe2\x80\x99s core requirements for a Private (VPC) deployment while staying aligned with our operational and security commitments.\\n\\nAttached: Redwood_Proposed_DPA_SLA_Counters_Trident_2025-05-20.docx\\n\\nHigh-level summary of key positions:\\n1) Data residency / processing location\\n\\n- For a Private (VPC) deployment, we can commit that Customer Content (e.g., prompts and outputs) will be processed and stored within the agreed deployment region/VPC.\\n- We proposed edits to avoid an absolute prohibition that could unintentionally restrict necessary support and security operations. We included language that any administrative access is controlled, limited, and auditable.\\n\\n2) Retention / deletion\\n\\n- We proposed separating (a) retention of Customer Content from (b) retention of operational/audit logs.\\n- We can support configurable retention settings consistent with the Private deployment architecture. We countered the \xe2\x80\x9c0-day retention by default\xe2\x80\x9d and \xe2\x80\x9c24-hour deletion\xe2\x80\x9d requirements with a more operationally feasible deletion standard while still meeting the underlying objective (minimizing retention of customer content).\\n\\n3) Audit logs / SIEM export / 7-year retention\\n\\n- We can support audit logging and export so Trident can ingest logs into its SIEM and retain them for 7 years (or longer) under Trident\xe2\x80\x99s control.\\n- We proposed edits so long-term retention is satisfied via export/retention in Trident systems, rather than requiring Redwood to retain logs for 7 years.\\n\\n4) Security incident notice\\n\\n- We proposed notification \xe2\x80\x9cwithout undue delay\xe2\x80\x9d and within a defined window following confirmation of a Security Incident affecting Customer Data.\\n\\n5) Liability and SLA credits\\n\\n- We proposed standard positions on liability and a bounded service credit structure.", "score": 0.431044, "rank": 41, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "e222dd74-ed86-5605-9088-440ef14ee209", "search_unit_id": "e222dd74-ed86-5605-9088-440ef14ee209", "block_from_id": "c0622fc2-94ef-52a3-978b-465316943314", "block_to_id": "68302e4d-0349-5e0d-bed6-8e74422e7053", "section_path": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Status", "document_id": "98457c4e-240d-5e44-8a6a-abb94e877555", "document_version_id": "b983f052-79ea-54aa-b5f9-45e0bce342f3", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_8c24fd15fe5b473eb71341751d292b6e.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-5", "heading": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Status", "content": "Status\\n\\n**Published** (effective for Hosted canary rollouts GA).", "score": 0.41448, "rank": 42, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "e0a491fc-83da-5059-a71c-459374aa7d1a", "search_unit_id": "e0a491fc-83da-5059-a71c-459374aa7d1a", "block_from_id": "e0ec97cd-26b1-55e2-bc5a-ff35958b9277", "block_to_id": "28d3a7ec-7ef8-5952-8670-ef3c25726770", "section_path": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 2. Scope > 2.1 Included surfaces", "document_id": "85b37108-378d-55be-805e-bdcd31101232", "document_version_id": "6851cc1f-d2a1-5421-91d3-d80c5cf3f3e8", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_37befab212ae4ed9be7ebe612d942658.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-9", "heading": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 2. Scope > 2.1 Included surfaces", "content": "2. Scope\\n\\n2.1 Included surfaces\\n\\nThis spec applies to Hosted API endpoints that return model outputs and therefore generate token usage:\\n\\n- Chat / Responses-style generation endpoints\\n- Text completion endpoints\\n- Rerank endpoints **only if** they are billed per token (currently: billed per request; see notes)\\n- Embeddings endpoints are covered by a separate meter spec (batch semantics differ)", "score": 0.393607, "rank": 43, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "ae1803c9-0715-5477-8a2b-1b43d62ea413", "search_unit_id": "ae1803c9-0715-5477-8a2b-1b43d62ea413", "block_from_id": "735ba16f-5d73-5eb6-abdd-8d87d46f3c8c", "block_to_id": "d2708ee8-099f-5336-ab2f-ef31de4097a6", "section_path": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Context / problem", "document_id": "98457c4e-240d-5e44-8a6a-abb94e877555", "document_version_id": "b983f052-79ea-54aa-b5f9-45e0bce342f3", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_8c24fd15fe5b473eb71341751d292b6e.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-7", "heading": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Context / problem", "content": "Context / problem\\n\\nHosted canary rollouts require percentage-based traffic splitting on a Hosted route (e.g., 1% \xe2\x86\x92 5% \xe2\x86\x92 25% \xe2\x86\x92 50% \xe2\x86\x92 100%) while maintaining:\\n\\n- Consistent cohorting so a given end user/conversation does not \xe2\x80\x9cflip\xe2\x80\x9d unpredictably\\n- Correctness under retries, streaming, and regional failover\\n- Clear metrics for baseline vs canary cohorts (including when the system must fall back)\\n\\nConstraints:\\n\\n- Data plane enforcement must be fast and operate without a central \xe2\x80\x9cassignment service.\xe2\x80\x9d\\n- Region routing and model fallback already exist; cohorting must compose cleanly.\\n- Customers may or may not provide stable end-user identifiers.", "score": 0.399317, "rank": 44, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "1401c358-7cb0-5e90-b88c-38646ff8b935", "search_unit_id": "1401c358-7cb0-5e90-b88c-38646ff8b935", "block_from_id": "eed2122d-2bd1-5aef-9fdb-05544d53bcc6", "block_to_id": "7eb86b0c-14bf-5344-99a3-89bc6f8a8aaa", "section_path": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Decision > 6) Fallback behavior and \xe2\x80\x9ceffective cohort\xe2\x80\x9d telemetry", "document_id": "98457c4e-240d-5e44-8a6a-abb94e877555", "document_version_id": "b983f052-79ea-54aa-b5f9-45e0bce342f3", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_8c24fd15fe5b473eb71341751d292b6e.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-49", "heading": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Decision > 6) Fallback behavior and \xe2\x80\x9ceffective cohort\xe2\x80\x9d telemetry", "content": "6) Fallback behavior and \xe2\x80\x9ceffective cohort\xe2\x80\x9d telemetry\\n\\nIf the intended cohort target cannot be served (e.g., canary model unavailable in a region, or hard failure triggers fallback), the system may serve from an alternate target.\\n\\nDefinitions:\\n\\n- **intended_cohort:** baseline | canary (result of cohort function)\\n- **effective_target:** the model/version/config actually used after fallback\\n- **effective_cohort:** baseline | canary, derived from `effective_target` mapping\\n\\nTelemetry requirements (must be emitted on every request):\\n\\n- `rollout_id` (when applicable)\\n- `intended_cohort` (baseline/canary)\\n- `effective_cohort` (baseline/canary)\\n- `fallback_reason` (empty or one of: canary_unavailable, canary_error, capacity, policy_denied, region_failover, other)\\n\\nCustomer-facing metrics views:\\n\\n- Default charts compare **effective_cohort** baseline vs canary (what was actually served).\\n- Secondary diagnostics show intended vs effective mismatch counts (to detect \xe2\x80\x9cphantom canary\xe2\x80\x9d).\\n\\nSafety rule:\\n\\n- If the rollout is in a state requiring canary availability guarantees (e.g., higher steps), policy may deny progressing if `canary_unavailable` is non-zero in any eligible region for a configured lookback window (guardrail; tracked separately).", "score": 0.384153, "rank": 45, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "91db06b9-7c72-5090-94cb-9641fdd42f24", "search_unit_id": "91db06b9-7c72-5090-94cb-9641fdd42f24", "block_from_id": "83347e0e-d4a3-56ca-b0e8-e45e3da9a694", "block_to_id": "862d9d2f-1ca5-517e-b080-a3e39065de57", "section_path": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Decision > 2) Cohort key selection rules (stickiness)", "document_id": "98457c4e-240d-5e44-8a6a-abb94e877555", "document_version_id": "b983f052-79ea-54aa-b5f9-45e0bce342f3", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_8c24fd15fe5b473eb71341751d292b6e.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-20", "heading": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Decision > 2) Cohort key selection rules (stickiness)", "content": "2) Cohort key selection rules (stickiness)\\n\\nThe data plane selects `cohort_key` using the following precedence order:\\n\\n1. **Explicit Cohort Key header (preferred):**\\n   - `X-Redwood-Cohort-Key` (customer-provided stable identifier)\\n   - Recommended values: end_user_id, account_id, conversation_id, or a stable hash thereof\\n\\n2. **Idempotency key (recommended for retries/streaming reconnects):**\\n   - `Idempotency-Key` (if present)\\n   - Intended to remain identical across client retries and stream reconnect attempts\\n\\n3. **Request ID (best-effort fallback):**\\n   - `X-Request-Id` (if present)\\n   - If generated by Redwood, it is unique per attempt and does **not** provide end-user stickiness; it only makes repeated internal handling of the same request deterministic.\\n\\n4. **Non-sticky fallback (last resort):**\\n   - A per-request generated UUID (ensures correct aggregate percentage but no per-user stickiness)\\n\\nConsole/Docs requirements:\\n\\n- Console wizard and docs MUST recommend using `X-Redwood-Cohort-Key` or `Idempotency-Key` for best results.\\n- Console must warn when a customer\xe2\x80\x99s observed request mix suggests low stickiness (optional; informational).", "score": 0.384153, "rank": 46, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "84173d31-e829-5995-bbac-21ddcbccbf52", "search_unit_id": "84173d31-e829-5995-bbac-21ddcbccbf52", "block_from_id": "3e6c010a-d245-5385-8a74-8f47254bf32c", "block_to_id": "da23c2c9-e48c-5aa4-93c9-c9f2cb0672df", "section_path": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Decision > 3) Where cohorting is enforced", "document_id": "98457c4e-240d-5e44-8a6a-abb94e877555", "document_version_id": "b983f052-79ea-54aa-b5f9-45e0bce342f3", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_8c24fd15fe5b473eb71341751d292b6e.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-28", "heading": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Decision > 3) Where cohorting is enforced", "content": "3) Where cohorting is enforced\\n\\nCohorting and split enforcement occur in the **Smart Routing / data plane** after route resolution and policy checks, and before final model selection.\\n\\nOrder (simplified):\\n\\n1. Authenticate request / resolve tenant + route\\n2. Evaluate policy eligibility for rollout (RBAC, guardrails, rollout state)\\n3. Select region per existing routing policies (latency/capacity/affinity)\\n4. **Compute cohort (baseline/canary) in the chosen region\xe2\x80\x99s data plane**\\n5. Select model version/config for the cohort\\n6. Apply existing fallback policies if the chosen target is unavailable", "score": 0.384153, "rank": 47, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "045dec30-5eb4-5ea4-97f4-64033f882ba8", "search_unit_id": "045dec30-5eb4-5ea4-97f4-64033f882ba8", "block_from_id": "e956fb4c-eef7-584b-9252-88521804ad42", "block_to_id": "809fe897-8873-52c0-a05f-9ea71e29097e", "section_path": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 5. Meter catalog (Hosted API) > 5.2 Required dimensions", "document_id": "85b37108-378d-55be-805e-bdcd31101232", "document_version_id": "6851cc1f-d2a1-5421-91d3-d80c5cf3f3e8", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_37befab212ae4ed9be7ebe612d942658.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-31", "heading": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 5. Meter catalog (Hosted API) > 5.2 Required dimensions", "content": "5.2 Required dimensions\\n\\nEvery usage record MUST include the following dimensions (nulls are not allowed unless explicitly marked optional):\\n\\n- `account_id` (required)\\n- `workspace_id` (optional)\\n- `project_id` (optional)\\n- `api_key_id` (required; may be hashed in customer exports)\\n- `endpoint` (required; canonical string, e.g., `chat.completions`, `responses`, `completions`)\\n- `model` (required; resolved to the **billed model** after routing/fallback)\\n- `model_variant` (optional; internal)\\n- `region` (required)\\n- `deployment_mode = hosted` (required)\\n- `pricing_plan_id` (required; ties to packaging/experiment cohorts)\\n- `currency` (required; pricing table lookup)\\n- `meter_version` (required; see \xc2\xa711)\\n- `metered_at` (required; UTC timestamp)\\n\\nOptional but strongly recommended:\\n\\n- `customer_request_tag` (optional; customer-provided metadata)\\n- `experiment_cohort` (optional; when experiment flagging is active)\\n\\n---", "score": 0.37866, "rank": 48, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "78c09b43-2795-5141-b0eb-109409db1481", "search_unit_id": "78c09b43-2795-5141-b0eb-109409db1481", "block_from_id": "c2266ea1-b591-5041-8035-d2d60a408cf1", "block_to_id": "be6a7ae4-3454-5ea9-bccc-59e5c45ab73d", "section_path": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Implementation notes (non-normative)", "document_id": "98457c4e-240d-5e44-8a6a-abb94e877555", "document_version_id": "b983f052-79ea-54aa-b5f9-45e0bce342f3", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_8c24fd15fe5b473eb71341751d292b6e.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-84", "heading": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Implementation notes (non-normative)", "content": "Implementation notes (non-normative)\\n\\n- Data plane should compute cohort as early as possible after route resolution, but after any bypass/policy deny checks.\\n- Keep the hash implementation identical across languages (gateway, edge proxies, and routing services). Add a golden test vector suite.\\n- Add integration tests to validate:\\n  - Stable assignment across retries with same idempotency key\\n  - Step increases are monotonic superset of canary population\\n  - Fallback produces intended/effective mismatch telemetry", "score": 0.384153, "rank": 49, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "3b798578-b7ab-5f20-968b-8fb82526e518", "search_unit_id": "3b798578-b7ab-5f20-968b-8fb82526e518", "block_from_id": "d91df646-bacc-58ab-86bc-51e8c9883fa7", "block_to_id": "7daf57d4-abf9-5213-a808-0c78977111d7", "section_path": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Detailed specification > Exclusions / bypass", "document_id": "98457c4e-240d-5e44-8a6a-abb94e877555", "document_version_id": "b983f052-79ea-54aa-b5f9-45e0bce342f3", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_8c24fd15fe5b473eb71341751d292b6e.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-74", "heading": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Detailed specification > Exclusions / bypass", "content": "Exclusions / bypass\\n\\nCertain traffic must not participate in rollouts:\\n\\n- Health checks, internal synthetic probes, and system-to-system calls used for SLO verification.\\n\\nMechanism:\\n\\n- Internal callers set `X-Redwood-Bypass-Rollout: true` (restricted to Redwood internal auth).\\n- If bypassed, cohorting is skipped and the baseline target is selected; telemetry marks `rollout_bypassed=true`.", "score": 0.384153, "rank": 50, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "47540d60-5c60-5e38-b91d-89dd55c3961f", "search_unit_id": "47540d60-5c60-5e38-b91d-89dd55c3961f", "block_from_id": "8f6bd056-2632-5ac2-81fc-da0ad28e97bb", "block_to_id": "b01b67b6-2fe6-57d0-af6e-b8bfb3778fb4", "section_path": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Detailed specification > Cohort key normalization", "document_id": "98457c4e-240d-5e44-8a6a-abb94e877555", "document_version_id": "b983f052-79ea-54aa-b5f9-45e0bce342f3", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_8c24fd15fe5b473eb71341751d292b6e.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-68", "heading": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Detailed specification > Cohort key normalization", "content": "Detailed specification\\n\\nCohort key normalization\\n\\nTo avoid accidental cohort churn:\\n\\n- Use the cohort key exactly as received (no trimming/case folding), except:\\n  - Reject keys longer than **256 bytes** (return validation error: `cohort_key_too_long`)\\n  - Reject keys containing NUL bytes\\n- Do not log raw cohort keys (treat as potentially sensitive). If needed for debugging, log a truncated hash.", "score": 0.384153, "rank": 51, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "2992f651-874e-5466-9abf-29d13cd7442f", "search_unit_id": "2992f651-874e-5466-9abf-29d13cd7442f", "block_from_id": "3ae041b7-dcbf-5173-a2f5-1dd05dccc244", "block_to_id": "6c84b5a6-a222-575c-b99e-48a803602aa0", "section_path": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 6. Request counting rules (request_count) > 6.1 When a request is counted", "document_id": "85b37108-378d-55be-805e-bdcd31101232", "document_version_id": "6851cc1f-d2a1-5421-91d3-d80c5cf3f3e8", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_37befab212ae4ed9be7ebe612d942658.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-37", "heading": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 6. Request counting rules (request_count) > 6.1 When a request is counted", "content": "6. Request counting rules (request_count)\\n\\n6.1 When a request is counted\\n\\n`request_count = 1` if and only if all conditions below are true:\\n\\n1. The request is accepted by Redwood (passes auth + basic validation).\\n2. The request is not deduplicated by idempotency (see \xc2\xa78).\\n3. The request is **billable** based on response category:\\n   - **2xx** responses: billable.\\n   - **4xx** responses: not billable, except where explicitly stated (none currently).\\n   - **5xx** responses: billable only if tokens were produced and streamed/returned (see \xc2\xa76.3 and \xc2\xa77.4).", "score": 0.37866, "rank": 52, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "8f5ffb85-6f16-505e-8ade-9565f06d7a94", "search_unit_id": "8f5ffb85-6f16-505e-8ade-9565f06d7a94", "block_from_id": "5750d164-fd43-56ba-b8fb-7d17726e40c8", "block_to_id": "2b05bf67-97c6-547f-bcfc-0d052255e2b2", "section_path": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Open questions / follow-ups", "document_id": "98457c4e-240d-5e44-8a6a-abb94e877555", "document_version_id": "b983f052-79ea-54aa-b5f9-45e0bce342f3", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_8c24fd15fe5b473eb71341751d292b6e.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-90", "heading": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Open questions / follow-ups", "content": "Open questions / follow-ups\\n\\n1. Should we expose a first-class `cohort_key` field in the API body (in addition to a header) for SDK ergonomics?\\n2. Should we allow customers to choose \xe2\x80\x9cglobal cohorting before region selection\xe2\x80\x9d mode for strict stickiness (likely requires a stable region selection key)?\\n3. What are the default guardrails for blocking step progression if effective canary rate is far below configured percent due to availability?", "score": 0.384153, "rank": 53, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "a670738f-fefe-5463-a181-f7ec0c4c4415", "search_unit_id": "a670738f-fefe-5463-a181-f7ec0c4c4415", "block_from_id": "9ffd9c8d-e450-5568-8221-3f24756f7279", "block_to_id": "9fb82e4b-e739-5789-814b-f67fbd11f0d5", "section_path": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting", "document_id": "98457c4e-240d-5e44-8a6a-abb94e877555", "document_version_id": "b983f052-79ea-54aa-b5f9-45e0bce342f3", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_8c24fd15fe5b473eb71341751d292b6e.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h1-0", "heading": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting", "content": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting\\n\\nSource type: confluence\\nDocument ID: dsid_8c24fd15fe5b473eb71341751d292b6e\\nRelease: v1.0.0\\nBenchmark: EnterpriseRAG-Bench synthetic upstream data\\nADR: Hosted rollout cohorting and percentage traffic splitting (deterministic + region behavior)", "score": 0.444808, "rank": 54, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "e60f5101-2921-5e08-b8aa-f87b08bb91d4", "search_unit_id": "e60f5101-2921-5e08-b8aa-f87b08bb91d4", "block_from_id": "3769b082-b83a-55ab-acaa-863eb467f3b8", "block_to_id": "a374bd57-1cf1-5bbd-96ee-a9079fd76a18", "section_path": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 4. Definitions > 4.1 Core terms", "document_id": "85b37108-378d-55be-805e-bdcd31101232", "document_version_id": "6851cc1f-d2a1-5421-91d3-d80c5cf3f3e8", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_37befab212ae4ed9be7ebe612d942658.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-19", "heading": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 4. Definitions > 4.1 Core terms", "content": "4. Definitions\\n\\n4.1 Core terms\\n\\n- **Request:** A single client call to a Hosted API endpoint.\\n- **Attempt:** A server-side execution attempt for a request (may include fallback routing or server-side retry).\\n- **Prompt tokens (input_tokens):** Tokens attributable to the serialized input to the model for an attempt.\\n- **Completion tokens (output_tokens):** Tokens generated by the model for an attempt.\\n- **Raw tokens:** input_tokens + output_tokens as observed for an attempt.\\n- **Billed tokens:** Token quantity used for charging. May differ from raw tokens when cache hits or policy-based adjustments apply.\\n- **Cache hit:** A case where a prefix/KV cache or response cache results in reduced compute for the request. Depending on pricing policy, cache-hit tokens may be billed differently (see \xc2\xa77).", "score": 0.37866, "rank": 55, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "3abf3610-5d68-59c7-8049-0414b9947f67", "search_unit_id": "3abf3610-5d68-59c7-8049-0414b9947f67", "block_from_id": "2f686ce6-cb50-5b2b-b675-c0f982fc2ac0", "block_to_id": "01faa3e8-879a-5594-aaee-610a3bf911d2", "section_path": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Decision > 4) How configured percentages apply across regions", "document_id": "98457c4e-240d-5e44-8a6a-abb94e877555", "document_version_id": "b983f052-79ea-54aa-b5f9-45e0bce342f3", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_8c24fd15fe5b473eb71341751d292b6e.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-32", "heading": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Decision > 4) How configured percentages apply across regions", "content": "4) How configured percentages apply across regions\\n\\nConfigured canary percentage is enforced **independently per region**, using the same deterministic function.\\n\\nMeaning:\\n\\n- The system does **not** attempt to maintain an exact global canary percentage across all regions combined.\\n- Each region will converge to the configured percentage for traffic that is routed to that region.\\n\\nRationale:\\n\\n- Avoids cross-region coordination/state and reduces blast radius.\\n- Aligns canary metrics to the region where traffic was actually served.\\n- Prevents \xe2\x80\x9cglobal percentage drift\xe2\x80\x9d caused by sudden regional routing shifts.\\n\\nKnown consequence:\\n\\n- If a single end user is routed to different regions on different requests (e.g., due to region failover or customer not using region affinity), they may land in different cohorts because the cohorting is evaluated after region selection and because the available model set can differ by region.\\n\\nMitigation:\\n\\n- Customers who require strict consistency should use region pinning/affinity (where supported) and provide a stable `X-Redwood-Cohort-Key`.", "score": 0.384153, "rank": 56, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "8e25ba0f-fd31-5a9c-8252-ed8022c3f8dc", "search_unit_id": "8e25ba0f-fd31-5a9c-8252-ed8022c3f8dc", "block_from_id": "cdddae83-5692-58e0-b289-a050b58f8e7a", "block_to_id": "e80e8f6b-5e7c-5af2-89f1-40fd0d4dfd61", "section_path": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 7. Token metering rules > 7.2 Prompt tokens (prompt_tokens)", "document_id": "85b37108-378d-55be-805e-bdcd31101232", "document_version_id": "6851cc1f-d2a1-5421-91d3-d80c5cf3f3e8", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_37befab212ae4ed9be7ebe612d942658.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-51", "heading": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 7. Token metering rules > 7.2 Prompt tokens (prompt_tokens)", "content": "7.2 Prompt tokens (prompt_tokens)\\n\\nPrompt tokens represent the total tokens in the model input for the billed attempt, including:\\n\\n- System/developer/user messages (for chat)\\n- Tool schema/function definitions provided by the client (for tool calling)\\n- Any platform-inserted control tokens required by the serving runtime for the endpoint format\\n\\nRules:\\n\\n- Count prompt tokens once for the billed attempt.\\n- If internal reformatting occurs (e.g., OpenAI-compatible -> canonical), token counting is performed after canonicalization.", "score": 0.37866, "rank": 57, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "14a40d6f-d166-5cbf-a176-e60875883b68", "search_unit_id": "14a40d6f-d166-5cbf-a176-e60875883b68", "block_from_id": "03561cf8-c486-5951-8eb1-7c40f83c0752", "block_to_id": "430afaff-4eef-503d-b77f-8e117464709d", "section_path": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Decision > 1) Deterministic cohorting function", "document_id": "98457c4e-240d-5e44-8a6a-abb94e877555", "document_version_id": "b983f052-79ea-54aa-b5f9-45e0bce342f3", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_8c24fd15fe5b473eb71341751d292b6e.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-12", "heading": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Decision > 1) Deterministic cohorting function", "content": "Decision\\n\\n1) Deterministic cohorting function\\n\\nCohort assignment is computed as a pure function:\\n**cohort = f(route_id, rollout_id, cohort_key)**\\nWhere:\\n\\n- `route_id` is the canonical Hosted route identifier\\n- `rollout_id` is the rollout instance identifier (changes per rollout)\\n- `cohort_key` is a stable string chosen per request (see selection rules below)\\n\\nImplementation:\\n\\n- Hash function: **xxHash64** (or equivalent stable 64-bit non-cryptographic hash) over UTF-8 bytes\\n- Input format (must be stable and unambiguous):\\n  - `\\"v1|\\" + route_id + \\"|\\" + rollout_id + \\"|\\" + cohort_key`\\n- Convert hash \xe2\x86\x92 bucket:\\n  - `bucket = hash64 % 10000` (0\xe2\x80\x939999)\\n- Compare to configured canary percentage:\\n  - `is_canary = bucket < (canary_percent * 100)`\\n  - Example: 5% \xe2\x86\x92 threshold 500\\n\\nRationale:\\n\\n- Pure function = deterministic across restarts, regions, and retries (given same `cohort_key`).\\n- 10,000 buckets provides 0.01% granularity while remaining simple.\\n- Including `rollout_id` avoids \xe2\x80\x9cstickiness\xe2\x80\x9d across separate rollouts (a new rollout should re-sample cohorts).", "score": 0.384153, "rank": 58, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "c72d1d5e-7203-5cc5-80a3-8c5f163ab029", "search_unit_id": "c72d1d5e-7203-5cc5-80a3-8c5f163ab029", "block_from_id": "226b57ca-14c5-5077-9c49-8efd6fdf0fd9", "block_to_id": "869fd3c9-aac6-5514-bd86-883b1e8a3aec", "section_path": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Consequences > Negative / tradeoffs", "document_id": "98457c4e-240d-5e44-8a6a-abb94e877555", "document_version_id": "b983f052-79ea-54aa-b5f9-45e0bce342f3", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_8c24fd15fe5b473eb71341751d292b6e.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-82", "heading": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Consequences > Negative / tradeoffs", "content": "Negative / tradeoffs\\n\\n- Stickiness is only as good as the provided key; without it, customer experience may vary.\\n- Regional independence means some users may move cohorts if they move regions.\\n- Must be careful with metric cardinality (rollout_id + cohort tags); see telemetry guidelines.", "score": 0.384153, "rank": 59, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "d77a8afa-1704-5443-a62a-cb5c229dc416", "search_unit_id": "d77a8afa-1704-5443-a62a-cb5c229dc416", "block_from_id": "03f7b8a1-a20d-5573-97a2-f4de3854685f", "block_to_id": "11ba3080-5226-5ff1-8fa9-3c4060494f4a", "section_path": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Detailed specification > Percent precision and step changes", "document_id": "98457c4e-240d-5e44-8a6a-abb94e877555", "document_version_id": "b983f052-79ea-54aa-b5f9-45e0bce342f3", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_8c24fd15fe5b473eb71341751d292b6e.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-72", "heading": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Detailed specification > Percent precision and step changes", "content": "Percent precision and step changes\\n\\n- Percent is stored as a decimal with two decimal places (0.00\xe2\x80\x93100.00).\\n- Changing percent changes the threshold; users move cohorts deterministically based on their bucket.\\n- This behavior is desired: when going from 5%\xe2\x86\x9225%, the additional 20% is a deterministic superset.", "score": 0.384153, "rank": 60, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "416eacab-9d5d-5a11-baa0-7089f4b629a7", "search_unit_id": "416eacab-9d5d-5a11-baa0-7089f4b629a7", "block_from_id": "c938eff1-8898-515f-addd-82578117cc83", "block_to_id": "beb00a88-7a87-54d8-8fbf-42f9a0980850", "section_path": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Options considered > Option B: Central assignment service (store user\xe2\x86\x92cohort mappings)", "document_id": "98457c4e-240d-5e44-8a6a-abb94e877555", "document_version_id": "b983f052-79ea-54aa-b5f9-45e0bce342f3", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_8c24fd15fe5b473eb71341751d292b6e.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-62", "heading": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Options considered > Option B: Central assignment service (store user\xe2\x86\x92cohort mappings)", "content": "Option B: Central assignment service (store user\xe2\x86\x92cohort mappings)\\n\\n- Pros: strong stickiness; globally consistent\\n- Cons: adds new stateful service; latency and availability risks; heavy operational overhead; complicated privacy considerations\\n- Rejected for GA.", "score": 0.384153, "rank": 61, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "18c8b279-0523-5f62-aa18-78eccdb19ee8", "search_unit_id": "18c8b279-0523-5f62-aa18-78eccdb19ee8", "block_from_id": "e685bdd8-a677-52b4-931d-d8c9f8825ed3", "block_to_id": "35c79d64-c0ea-5ff0-a68a-e9ab35618ed8", "section_path": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Decision > 5) Handling retries, streaming, and internal re-attempts > Streaming", "document_id": "98457c4e-240d-5e44-8a6a-abb94e877555", "document_version_id": "b983f052-79ea-54aa-b5f9-45e0bce342f3", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_8c24fd15fe5b473eb71341751d292b6e.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h4-47", "heading": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Decision > 5) Handling retries, streaming, and internal re-attempts > Streaming", "content": "Streaming\\n\\n- Streaming responses are treated as a single request attempt. Cohort is computed at stream start.\\n- If a client reconnects and expects consistent behavior, it must reuse `Idempotency-Key` (and ideally `X-Redwood-Cohort-Key`).", "score": 0.384153, "rank": 62, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "e3453d50-64f0-5886-b5ca-4c1f0d54e8d7", "search_unit_id": "e3453d50-64f0-5886-b5ca-4c1f0d54e8d7", "block_from_id": "92e1541e-73c5-50fa-8467-150f53a7d7e0", "block_to_id": "dbd67c5d-830c-51bc-aaf2-48132b02fa86", "section_path": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 4. Definitions > 4.2 Identifiers", "document_id": "85b37108-378d-55be-805e-bdcd31101232", "document_version_id": "6851cc1f-d2a1-5421-91d3-d80c5cf3f3e8", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_37befab212ae4ed9be7ebe612d942658.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-22", "heading": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 4. Definitions > 4.2 Identifiers", "content": "4.2 Identifiers\\n\\n- **account_id:** Billing account (maps to contract / invoice recipient).\\n- **workspace_id / project_id:** Sub-account segmentation (if enabled).\\n- **request_id:** Redwood-generated ID for a request.\\n- **idempotency_key:** Customer-provided string for deduplication of retries.\\n- **billing_dedupe_key:** Canonical key used in billing events and ledger to enforce idempotency.", "score": 0.37866, "rank": 63, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "0de31ce6-5e98-5f76-bbc6-447cd6b2c2df", "search_unit_id": "0de31ce6-5e98-5f76-bbc6-447cd6b2c2df", "block_from_id": "9dc476f1-a9ab-5bbe-9643-8f2125e824bf", "block_to_id": "e2ea7e00-3943-58f3-91d7-f70723efcdcb", "section_path": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 5. Meter catalog (Hosted API)", "document_id": "85b37108-378d-55be-805e-bdcd31101232", "document_version_id": "6851cc1f-d2a1-5421-91d3-d80c5cf3f3e8", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_37befab212ae4ed9be7ebe612d942658.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-27", "heading": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 5. Meter catalog (Hosted API)", "content": "5. Meter catalog (Hosted API)\\n\\nThe following meters are produced for every billable request.", "score": 0.37866, "rank": 64, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "9ada93b8-ee98-53c8-a31b-9102589ec9fc", "search_unit_id": "9ada93b8-ee98-53c8-a31b-9102589ec9fc", "block_from_id": "de298fa6-6069-56dd-9a65-89ff29803a88", "block_to_id": "cb653d69-2bbd-5341-ab54-beab32f6e722", "section_path": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > References", "document_id": "98457c4e-240d-5e44-8a6a-abb94e877555", "document_version_id": "b983f052-79ea-54aa-b5f9-45e0bce342f3", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_8c24fd15fe5b473eb71341751d292b6e.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-92", "heading": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > References", "content": "References\\n\\n- Hosted canary rollout workflow design spec: confluence://product-docs/design-specs/hosted-canary-rollout-workflow-design-spec\\n- Rollout orchestrator service: confluence://eng-platform/systems-and-services/rollout-orchestrator-service\\n- Rollout policy precedence ADR: confluence://architecture-and-standards/decision-records/adr-rollout-policy-evaluation-order-and-precedence\\n- Hosted rollouts SLO metrics and alerts: confluence://eng-sre/slo-and-error-budgets/hosted-rollouts-slo-metrics-and-alerts", "score": 0.384153, "rank": 65, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "de8e679b-17a1-589f-a99e-d155fa942a54", "search_unit_id": "de8e679b-17a1-589f-a99e-d155fa942a54", "block_from_id": "00316719-3d98-5780-adce-6f3206d2160a", "block_to_id": "615ed8b1-fd7e-5bd7-b5d4-f26cc3d94988", "section_path": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 3. Principles (must-haves)", "document_id": "85b37108-378d-55be-805e-bdcd31101232", "document_version_id": "6851cc1f-d2a1-5421-91d3-d80c5cf3f3e8", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_37befab212ae4ed9be7ebe612d942658.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-16", "heading": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 3. Principles (must-haves)", "content": "3. Principles (must-haves)\\n\\n1. **Single source of truth for invoicing:** The billing ledger is the authoritative source for invoice totals. Telemetry events are authoritative for debugging and reconciliation.\\n2. **Idempotent billing:** Retries/fallbacks must not produce double-charges when the customer provided a stable idempotency key.\\n3. **Customer-observable semantics:** Usage API must explain billed vs raw tokens and any cache attribution.\\n4. **Consistency across endpoints:** OpenAI-compatible endpoints must align token counting semantics with the canonical Redwood endpoints (modulo model/tokenizer differences).\\n5. **Change control:** Any meter logic change requires shadow compare and Finance approval before impacting invoices.\\n\\n---", "score": 0.37866, "rank": 66, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "7bbf7f4b-03a0-5ce5-821e-5fe48f74b7e9", "search_unit_id": "7bbf7f4b-03a0-5ce5-821e-5fe48f74b7e9", "block_from_id": "385abbe2-b625-50be-8271-0de5fb03417f", "block_to_id": "e1d6763b-8a0a-5c1c-8b25-bdf4a9288c88", "section_path": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Security and privacy considerations", "document_id": "98457c4e-240d-5e44-8a6a-abb94e877555", "document_version_id": "b983f052-79ea-54aa-b5f9-45e0bce342f3", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_8c24fd15fe5b473eb71341751d292b6e.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-86", "heading": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Security and privacy considerations", "content": "Security and privacy considerations\\n\\n- Treat `X-Redwood-Cohort-Key` as customer data; do not store raw values.\\n- Audit logs should record that a rollout split is enabled and its configured percentage, but must not include per-request cohort keys.", "score": 0.384153, "rank": 67, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "9a06010a-8ec5-52d9-a48b-5e40faaaa90a", "search_unit_id": "9a06010a-8ec5-52d9-a48b-5e40faaaa90a", "block_from_id": "a4cbe3d4-28ac-531b-8630-6bc77c81fa50", "block_to_id": "4bf0b125-a1be-5c48-95c8-428e8899bfdb", "section_path": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Consequences > Positive", "document_id": "98457c4e-240d-5e44-8a6a-abb94e877555", "document_version_id": "b983f052-79ea-54aa-b5f9-45e0bce342f3", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_8c24fd15fe5b473eb71341751d292b6e.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-79", "heading": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Consequences > Positive", "content": "Consequences\\n\\nPositive\\n\\n- Deterministic and reproducible cohorting without introducing stateful infrastructure.\\n- Supports real-world safe rollouts (sticky cohorts) when customers follow recommended headers.\\n- Clear observability story with intended vs effective cohort tracking.", "score": 0.384153, "rank": 68, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "16c8d608-a1ea-535e-aa8e-875a0f7ba9e2", "search_unit_id": "16c8d608-a1ea-535e-aa8e-875a0f7ba9e2", "block_from_id": "a3a23490-fa0c-54c7-8f68-92ef8575b193", "block_to_id": "efc65ad9-d51d-5e73-8210-be7864129653", "section_path": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Options considered > Option C: Deterministic hashing with stable key (this decision)", "document_id": "98457c4e-240d-5e44-8a6a-abb94e877555", "document_version_id": "b983f052-79ea-54aa-b5f9-45e0bce342f3", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_8c24fd15fe5b473eb71341751d292b6e.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-64", "heading": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Options considered > Option C: Deterministic hashing with stable key (this decision)", "content": "Option C: Deterministic hashing with stable key (this decision)\\n\\n- Pros: no state; deterministic; low latency; supports stickiness when customers provide keys\\n- Cons: stickiness depends on key hygiene; cross-region can vary depending on routing behavior\\n- Accepted.", "score": 0.384153, "rank": 69, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "5087f9c8-1ba1-5889-b5db-a75924f418ee", "search_unit_id": "5087f9c8-1ba1-5889-b5db-a75924f418ee", "block_from_id": "7e70f451-2ac3-54e0-aaeb-f873741d5280", "block_to_id": "6b0aa0c3-f6a0-5ea6-adac-fdd07f552e8b", "section_path": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 2. Scope > 2.2 Exclusions", "document_id": "85b37108-378d-55be-805e-bdcd31101232", "document_version_id": "6851cc1f-d2a1-5421-91d3-d80c5cf3f3e8", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_37befab212ae4ed9be7ebe612d942658.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-13", "heading": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 2. Scope > 2.2 Exclusions", "content": "2.2 Exclusions\\n\\n- Dedicated capacity GPU-seconds meters (separate spec)\\n- Private deployments (metering may be customer-operated; contracts may reference this spec as guidance only)\\n\\n---", "score": 0.37866, "rank": 70, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "8330043b-6a19-5a53-940a-a4400532c7ce", "search_unit_id": "8330043b-6a19-5a53-940a-a4400532c7ce", "block_from_id": "4382a10c-584d-51e2-bb8f-25855934616a", "block_to_id": "244db92c-9ce5-53d6-9a4b-72f7d30089db", "section_path": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 6. Request counting rules (request_count) > 6.3 Streaming and request_count", "document_id": "85b37108-378d-55be-805e-bdcd31101232", "document_version_id": "6851cc1f-d2a1-5421-91d3-d80c5cf3f3e8", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_37befab212ae4ed9be7ebe612d942658.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-43", "heading": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 6. Request counting rules (request_count) > 6.3 Streaming and request_count", "content": "6.3 Streaming and request_count\\n\\nFor streaming endpoints:\\n\\n- `request_count` is emitted once per **deduped request**.\\n- If the client disconnects mid-stream, the request still counts if billing conditions are met (see \xc2\xa77.4).\\n\\n---", "score": 0.37866, "rank": 71, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "35167946-63e3-5b94-8d69-886c27f4ceef", "search_unit_id": "35167946-63e3-5b94-8d69-886c27f4ceef", "block_from_id": "829cdab1-3273-5cf7-8d0e-f3d72e937bf2", "block_to_id": "52ebb209-c1b7-5222-a88a-fa3db7a4ff37", "section_path": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Options considered > Option A: Pure random assignment per request", "document_id": "98457c4e-240d-5e44-8a6a-abb94e877555", "document_version_id": "b983f052-79ea-54aa-b5f9-45e0bce342f3", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_8c24fd15fe5b473eb71341751d292b6e.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-59", "heading": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Options considered > Option A: Pure random assignment per request", "content": "Options considered\\n\\nOption A: Pure random assignment per request\\n\\n- Pros: simple; aggregate percentage correct\\n- Cons: not sticky; terrible for user experience comparisons; retries change cohort; noisy metrics\\n- Rejected.", "score": 0.384153, "rank": 72, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "f830df33-f489-518a-bf4a-e2a47ef91148", "search_unit_id": "f830df33-f489-518a-bf4a-e2a47ef91148", "block_from_id": "3d5ef485-ef51-5445-8d18-c340b4e026b0", "block_to_id": "35515068-ef9e-5872-8da1-56fd93583211", "section_path": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Options considered > Option D: Enforce percentage globally across regions", "document_id": "98457c4e-240d-5e44-8a6a-abb94e877555", "document_version_id": "b983f052-79ea-54aa-b5f9-45e0bce342f3", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_8c24fd15fe5b473eb71341751d292b6e.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-66", "heading": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Options considered > Option D: Enforce percentage globally across regions", "content": "Option D: Enforce percentage globally across regions\\n\\n- Pros: global percentage invariance\\n- Cons: requires coordination/state or cross-region sampling; complex under routing shifts\\n- Rejected for GA. (Can be revisited if we introduce a global routing coordinator.)", "score": 0.384153, "rank": 73, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "4fa3c732-2b4a-5fe1-b7a6-1845f625d14e", "search_unit_id": "4fa3c732-2b4a-5fe1-b7a6-1845f625d14e", "block_from_id": "647e84d1-1de6-56a5-863c-c647463205ac", "block_to_id": "4834b1c2-c23d-584b-810e-1e5a839b7bf8", "section_path": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 5. Meter catalog (Hosted API) > 5.1 Meter names and units", "document_id": "85b37108-378d-55be-805e-bdcd31101232", "document_version_id": "6851cc1f-d2a1-5421-91d3-d80c5cf3f3e8", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_37befab212ae4ed9be7ebe612d942658.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-29", "heading": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 5. Meter catalog (Hosted API) > 5.1 Meter names and units", "content": "5.1 Meter names and units\\n\\n| Meter | Field name (internal) | Unit | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Requests | request_count | count | Counted when request is billable per \xc2\xa76 |\\n| Prompt tokens | prompt_tokens | tokens | Integer tokens for serialized input |\\n| Completion tokens | completion_tokens | tokens | Integer tokens generated |\\n| Raw tokens | raw_tokens | tokens | prompt_tokens + completion_tokens |\\n| Billed tokens | billed_tokens | tokens | Primary pricing unit for Hosted API generation |", "score": 0.37866, "rank": 74, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "1bdc494c-14e3-599c-81f7-af1139adf44a", "search_unit_id": "1bdc494c-14e3-599c-81f7-af1139adf44a", "block_from_id": "b0c676ec-e489-59c0-92f4-ccb0a5d3a07d", "block_to_id": "f31e2bc7-7641-5205-924a-00ecd201f270", "section_path": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests", "document_id": "85b37108-378d-55be-805e-bdcd31101232", "document_version_id": "6851cc1f-d2a1-5421-91d3-d80c5cf3f3e8", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_37befab212ae4ed9be7ebe612d942658.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h1-0", "heading": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests", "content": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests\\n\\nSource type: confluence\\nDocument ID: dsid_37befab212ae4ed9be7ebe612d942658\\nRelease: v1.0.0\\nBenchmark: EnterpriseRAG-Bench synthetic upstream data\\nBilling Meter Spec \xe2\x80\x94 Hosted API Tokens & Requests", "score": 0.423501, "rank": 75, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "e438df88-e50e-5b7d-b48a-1b2de0303e88", "search_unit_id": "e438df88-e50e-5b7d-b48a-1b2de0303e88", "block_from_id": "c2699311-0470-587b-a19e-540aeac3dbd3", "block_to_id": "f72bc7a5-5192-5701-91ab-9d94bcdda335", "section_path": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 6. Request counting rules (request_count) > 6.2 Partial success", "document_id": "85b37108-378d-55be-805e-bdcd31101232", "document_version_id": "6851cc1f-d2a1-5421-91d3-d80c5cf3f3e8", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_37befab212ae4ed9be7ebe612d942658.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-41", "heading": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 6. Request counting rules (request_count) > 6.2 Partial success", "content": "6.2 Partial success\\n\\nIf a request returns partial output (e.g., streaming interrupted after some tokens), it is still counted as a request if it is billable per \xc2\xa77.4.", "score": 0.37866, "rank": 76, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "f9799700-a412-580a-b23e-8ac246d23269", "search_unit_id": "f9799700-a412-580a-b23e-8ac246d23269", "block_from_id": "30293e5b-c5e3-52d1-b296-c27613be624f", "block_to_id": "5d7bbe89-be01-5b07-8141-e5c29d5526f9", "section_path": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Decision > 5) Handling retries, streaming, and internal re-attempts > Internal retries (within a request attempt)", "document_id": "98457c4e-240d-5e44-8a6a-abb94e877555", "document_version_id": "b983f052-79ea-54aa-b5f9-45e0bce342f3", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_8c24fd15fe5b473eb71341751d292b6e.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h4-45", "heading": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Decision > 5) Handling retries, streaming, and internal re-attempts > Internal retries (within a request attempt)", "content": "Internal retries (within a request attempt)\\n\\n- Internal re-attempts must preserve the computed cohort for the request.\\n- Data plane should compute cohort once and propagate it via internal request context to downstream services.", "score": 0.384153, "rank": 77, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "1a29a12a-fe95-51b2-948b-1719db91682d", "search_unit_id": "1a29a12a-fe95-51b2-948b-1719db91682d", "block_from_id": "4a311321-7828-5737-99ee-efa6aaedccf4", "block_to_id": "28eb9156-ffc0-5453-a162-1256b7622f35", "section_path": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Decision > 5) Handling retries, streaming, and internal re-attempts > Client retries", "document_id": "98457c4e-240d-5e44-8a6a-abb94e877555", "document_version_id": "b983f052-79ea-54aa-b5f9-45e0bce342f3", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_8c24fd15fe5b473eb71341751d292b6e.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-42", "heading": "Adr Hosted Rollout Cohorting And Traffic Splitting > Decision > 5) Handling retries, streaming, and internal re-attempts > Client retries", "content": "5) Handling retries, streaming, and internal re-attempts\\n\\nClient retries\\n\\n- If the client supplies the same `Idempotency-Key` across retries, cohort assignment remains stable.\\n- If no idempotency/cohort key is provided, cohort assignment may change between retries (expected).", "score": 0.384153, "rank": 78, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "0202afcb-6bdb-5d77-bd7f-c8dabc6c1475", "search_unit_id": "0202afcb-6bdb-5d77-bd7f-c8dabc6c1475", "block_from_id": "3bf8a4c3-05b0-5866-944e-52f37b3fbb2c", "block_to_id": "62fdd968-9c43-5d3f-a2e6-7b3db87d94e6", "section_path": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Escalation > Customer communications guide > Communication principles", "document_id": "2aced3e3-1ed9-50b2-9ef8-a037c40fcfaf", "document_version_id": "5b0b49bf-5c10-5f9e-b6f9-1ae199865977", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_409618a3958944f9b78362ed7e01bb33.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-77", "heading": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Escalation > Customer communications guide > Communication principles", "content": "Customer communications guide\\n\\nCommunication principles\\n\\n- Use a single owner for customer updates (CS/Support), even if multiple engineering teams are engaged.\\n- Acknowledge impact quickly; avoid speculating on root cause.\\n- Provide time-bound next update (\xe2\x80\x9cnext update in 30 minutes\xe2\x80\x9d).\\n- For Premium accounts, default to more frequent updates and written summaries.", "score": 0.284571, "rank": 79, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "a83d0680-34b9-5735-92f6-b5aca34f901c", "search_unit_id": "a83d0680-34b9-5735-92f6-b5aca34f901c", "block_from_id": "cfacdd9d-af81-5bf7-8ec8-4b2a29d3b767", "block_to_id": "fb1eabd7-2b32-53c5-877e-bf2ca901eb72", "section_path": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review", "document_id": "be72d63b-333f-51cc-8c3e-5b2af46aaab8", "document_version_id": "d74f559c-4a91-5a53-930c-c8b05d67f4ab", "document_name": "fireflies__dsid_6c4c1c875e704f09b4d791d64d7bc7e5.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "p-80", "heading": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review", "content": "[17:07] Miguel Alvarez: That\xe2\x80\x99s okay. The marketplace doesn\xe2\x80\x99t care if you batch, as long as it\xe2\x80\x99s accurate and you can explain your aggregation. Many partners aggregate per hour to reduce volume.\\n\\n[17:19] Cole Summers: We can aggregate per hour per org per dimension. That\xe2\x80\x99s probably a good default.\\n\\n[17:26] Miguel Alvarez: Yes.\\n\\n[17:28] Stephanie Nguyen: Any pitfalls with multi-region? Our hosted API runs in multiple regions. If a GCP marketplace buyer uses us in EU, does billing care?\\n\\n[17:39] Priya Shah: Billing doesn\xe2\x80\x99t care, but listing and security docs might. If you claim data residency, make sure it\xe2\x80\x99s accurate. The marketplace reviewers may look at your data handling statements.\\n\\n[17:52] Chris Osei: We\xe2\x80\x99re preparing a security packet; we\xe2\x80\x99ll align wording.\\n\\n[17:57] Anika Desai: From onboarding side, if you route them to a region selection, ensure it\xe2\x80\x99s clear. Some customers think marketplace implies region-specific. It doesn\xe2\x80\x99t necessarily.\\n\\n[18:10] Ben Carter: We plan to ask region at org creation and default to the closest region, but allow override.\\n\\n[18:18] Stephanie Nguyen: Great.\\n[18:20] (brief crosstalk)\\n\\n[18:22] Stephanie Nguyen: Miguel, can we ask about \xe2\x80\x9cdimension mismatch\xe2\x80\x9d issues you\xe2\x80\x99ve seen? We had one on AWS where our internal meter name didn\xe2\x80\x99t match the marketplace dimension and we billed wrong. We\xe2\x80\x99re trying to prevent it.\\n\\n[18:37] Miguel Alvarez: Yeah, common. It\xe2\x80\x99s usually one of: using display name instead of dimension ID, or typos, or changing the offer and not updating code. The best practice: keep a single source config file for mapping, version it, and add automated tests that validate the dimension list against what\xe2\x80\x99s configured in marketplace.\\n\\n[18:57] Cole Summers: We have a billing catalog config in code. We can add a test that asserts \xe2\x80\x9cthese dimensions are the only allowed ones.\xe2\x80\x9d But we can\xe2\x80\x99t programmatically query the marketplace config easily.\\n\\n[19:10] Miguel Alvarez: You can still do a manual gate before launch: export the dimension list and compare. And once stable, don\xe2\x80\x99t change often.\\n\\n[19:21] Nadia Rahman: We also emit telemetry events in our pipeline. We can alert on unknown dimension errors.\\n\\n[19:28] Miguel Alvarez: Perfect. Set alerts on 4xx responses from metering API.\\n\\n[19:35] Chris Osei: Okay. Let\xe2\x80\x99s do a quick pass on what GCP needs for submission beyond billing: screenshots, support, security docs, etc. Priya?\\n\\n[19:46] Priya Shah: You\xe2\x80\x99ll need: final listing copy, pricing plans configured, support contact info, a privacy policy link, and security documentation depending on the category. For enterprise oriented products, we often see SOC 2 statements, encryption, retention, audit logging. You don\xe2\x80\x99t need to paste the whole report, but your claims must be accurate.\\n\\n[20:10] Stephanie Nguyen: Understood. We\xe2\x80\x99ll provide a marketplace security overview and pointers to evidence under NDA.\\n\\n[20:18] Anika Desai: Reviewers like diagrams. Even a simple data flow diagram helps.\\n\\n[20:23] Ben Carter: We have a diagram showing subscription -> entitlement -> Redwood control plane -> inference runtime. We\xe2\x80\x99ll use it.\\n\\n[20:31] Chris Osei: Back to testing. Anika, you mentioned a test buyer flow. Can you outline quickly? Just so we know if we need special accounts.", "score": 0.262797, "rank": 80, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "e4fb0db7-0d72-513c-864b-54ea9c98779a", "search_unit_id": "e4fb0db7-0d72-513c-864b-54ea9c98779a", "block_from_id": "aa4fd05e-834d-5c0d-94cf-fbfffbf445a6", "block_to_id": "d6208276-c1f2-59e6-8701-5418155a267e", "section_path": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Escalation > Triage checklist (Support/CS) > 2) Confirm the customer is driving enough load", "document_id": "2aced3e3-1ed9-50b2-9ef8-a037c40fcfaf", "document_version_id": "5b0b49bf-5c10-5f9e-b6f9-1ae199865977", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_409618a3958944f9b78362ed7e01bb33.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-51", "heading": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Escalation > Triage checklist (Support/CS) > 2) Confirm the customer is driving enough load", "content": "2) Confirm the customer is driving enough load\\n\\nAsk:\\n\\n- Current request concurrency\\n- Any client-side rate limiting (explicit QPS cap, worker pool limit)\\n- Any recent application deploy or traffic change\\n\\nIf the customer is under-driving load, throughput may be low without being an SLO failure.", "score": 0.250925, "rank": 81, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "0e2f452f-02d5-5d8d-bf48-5b80b9eec835", "search_unit_id": "0e2f452f-02d5-5d8d-bf48-5b80b9eec835", "block_from_id": "7e96897a-87e1-5d5f-8da7-c21e3a39abf0", "block_to_id": "57da432f-3094-5de4-bb54-97076fe76740", "section_path": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review", "document_id": "be72d63b-333f-51cc-8c3e-5b2af46aaab8", "document_version_id": "d74f559c-4a91-5a53-930c-c8b05d67f4ab", "document_name": "fireflies__dsid_6c4c1c875e704f09b4d791d64d7bc7e5.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h1-0", "heading": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review", "content": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review\\n\\nSource type: fireflies\\nDocument ID: dsid_6c4c1c875e704f09b4d791d64d7bc7e5\\nRelease: v1.0.0\\nBenchmark: EnterpriseRAG-Bench synthetic upstream data\\nGCP Marketplace onboarding + billing review (Redwood Inference)\\n\\nRedwood and the GCP Marketplace team reviewed how Redwood\xe2\x80\x99s new marketplace SKUs map to GCP billing dimensions (usage-based token/embedding meters vs subscription/commit dimensions), and what identifiers must be carried end-to-end (consumerId/entitlement) to ensure correct metering. GCP emphasized keeping dimension names stable, ensuring aggregation and rounding are consistent, and making the onboarding flow resilient to entitlement propagation delays. The group aligned on a staging test plan: subscribe with a test buyer account, validate entitlement lookup, provision/link a Redwood org, generate an API key, send test inference traffic, confirm metering events appear with the expected dimensions, and verify cancellation/plan change behavior. Redwood will send a proposed dimension list and sample metering payloads for review.\\nGCP Marketplace SKU dimensions and Cloud Commerce metering\\nEntitlement + account linking expectations\\nOnboarding UX expectations (subscription -> entitlement -> Redwood org provisioning)\\nTesting plan in staging before listing submission\\nRedwood to share final proposed GCP dimension names/IDs per SKU (Hosted/Dedicated/Private + add-ons) for partner review.\\nGCP to confirm any naming/length constraints and whether multiple dimensions per offer are acceptable for Redwood\xe2\x80\x99s packaging.\\nRedwood to run the staging procurement test end-to-end (subscribe -> entitlement -> org link -> first API call -> metering verification) and report results + logs.\\nSchedule a follow-up 30-min review focused on cancellation, plan change, and delayed entitlement propagation edge cases.\\nCole Summers: Send GCP partner team a draft mapping table (internal meter -> GCP dimension) and 2\xe2\x80\x933 example metering payloads by 2026-01-18.\\nBen Carter: Provide screenshots/wireframe of the marketplace onboarding entrypoint and error states (entitlement not found, pending propagation, wrong org) by 2026-01-19.\\nAnika Desai: Confirm recommended aggregation interval + rounding guidance for token-based meters and any API quotas to plan around by 2026-01-20.\\nMiguel Alvarez: Share a reference link / checklist for Cloud Commerce metering validation and how to verify events in the partner console by 2026-01-20.\\nChris Osei: Set up a follow-up session and circulate the staging test plan checklist by 2026-01-17.\\nMeeting header\\nDate: 2026-01-16\\nTime: 10:03 AM PT\\nDuration: 54 min\\nTitle: GCP Marketplace onboarding + billing review (Redwood Inference)\\nAttendees:\\n\\n- Redwood Inference: Stephanie Nguyen, Chris Osei, Cole Summers, Nadia Rahman, Ben Carter\\n- Google Cloud Marketplace: Priya Shah, Anika Desai, Miguel Alvarez\\n\\n[00:00] Stephanie Nguyen: Cool, thanks everyone for making time. This is Stephanie from Redwood, I run a lot of the enterprise motion, and we\xe2\x80\x99ve got Cole and Nadia on billing and metering, Ben on onboarding funnel. We wanted to do a sanity pass on the GCP marketplace requirements, specifically billing dimensions and the onboarding UX expectations before we submit.\\n\\n[00:18] Priya Shah: Sounds good. Thanks Stephanie. Priya here, partner dev manager. Anika and Miguel are the experts on the technical billing side. We saw your email about new SKUs, so today is basically make sure it\xe2\x80\x99s compatible with Cloud Marketplace and Cloud Commerce.", "score": 0.251703, "rank": 82, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "470b6db1-2316-5484-a11f-730215310e6e", "search_unit_id": "470b6db1-2316-5484-a11f-730215310e6e", "block_from_id": "b9418815-8dfc-5cc1-86cc-acc134ee3e3d", "block_to_id": "f3242bfe-4e27-55ca-807f-587a50998081", "section_path": "Metering To Invoice Reconciliation Playbook > Step-by-step reconciliation procedure > Step 8 \xe2\x80\x94 Decide remediation path (forward fix vs adjustment)", "document_id": "5dd49655-30e7-5bb0-9f51-c936960c0c6a", "document_version_id": "c0e0c79d-406f-5fb0-b65c-732ae9489a5c", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_602422b841b94f5c8089555015df0ed8.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-142", "heading": "Metering To Invoice Reconciliation Playbook > Step-by-step reconciliation procedure > Step 8 \xe2\x80\x94 Decide remediation path (forward fix vs adjustment)", "content": "Step 8 \xe2\x80\x94 Decide remediation path (forward fix vs adjustment)\\n\\nUse this decision tree once root cause is identified.\\n\\n**Default policy:** prefer **forward fixes** with clear documentation and audit trail. Avoid retroactive invoice changes.\\n\\nRemediation options:\\n1) **No action** (variance within threshold; document)\\n2) **Forward fix only** (metering bug fixed; applies to future usage)\\n3) **Customer credit** (invoice remains; credit memo or usage credit issued)\\n4) **Invoice adjustment / re-issue** (rare; requires CFO + Legal)\\n5) **Backfill** (usage events/customer export corrected; ledger may or may not change depending on freeze)\\n\\nMinimum documentation for any remediation:\\n\\n- Root cause summary\\n- Impacted period + accounts\\n- Quantified $ impact (best estimate + bounds)\\n- Customer comms plan (Support/Sales owner)\\n- Approvals captured (names + date)\\n\\n---", "score": 0.243933, "rank": 83, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "358bf632-89e6-5aa5-9e2b-7e4ad1ba8d57", "search_unit_id": "358bf632-89e6-5aa5-9e2b-7e4ad1ba8d57", "block_from_id": "81742bb5-fd5d-5f66-a511-bef252bdceb3", "block_to_id": "06515125-ed33-5736-bfd4-5279937b8080", "section_path": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 7. Token metering rules > 7.5 Cache-hit attribution and billed tokens", "document_id": "85b37108-378d-55be-805e-bdcd31101232", "document_version_id": "6851cc1f-d2a1-5421-91d3-d80c5cf3f3e8", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_37befab212ae4ed9be7ebe612d942658.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-66", "heading": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 7. Token metering rules > 7.5 Cache-hit attribution and billed tokens", "content": "7.5 Cache-hit attribution and billed tokens\\n\\nHosted API may apply caching at different layers:\\n\\n- Prefix/KV cache (compute reduction, same semantic output generation)\\n- Response cache (replay exact output)\\n\\nMetering requirements:\\n\\n- Record `cache_hit = true/false` and `cache_type` (optional: `kv_prefix`, `response`, `other`).\\n- Record `raw_tokens` as normal.\\n- Record `billed_tokens` according to active pricing plan policy.\\n\\nCurrent billing policy (default unless overridden in plan):\\n\\n- Cache hits do **not** change `billed_tokens` (billed remains aligned to customer-visible usage). Cache attribution is used for margin analysis and pricing experiments.\\n\\nIf a future plan bills cache hits differently, that plan must:\\n\\n- Explicitly define `cache_billing_policy` (e.g., `full`, `discounted`, `free_prompt`, etc.)\\n- Expose that policy in contract/order form language as required by Legal\\n- Be introduced only with meter_version bump and shadow compare (\xc2\xa711)", "score": 0.233882, "rank": 84, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "768b9e45-99b0-565d-8f72-d3caf446cd14", "search_unit_id": "768b9e45-99b0-565d-8f72-d3caf446cd14", "block_from_id": "2bd7a69d-66d2-508a-beca-31e2f00fdfd5", "block_to_id": "b14cd8f1-c124-5773-a5e1-1bc0fad64ab9", "section_path": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > How evidence is shared (distribution rules) > Approved sharing methods", "document_id": "9e945a2c-4051-5cc3-99ca-a95c267f6bdf", "document_version_id": "c6871709-6afb-56d9-883f-81f2536ab49e", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a9a67d815b07451e90ed8c3d1734d007.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-68", "heading": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > How evidence is shared (distribution rules) > Approved sharing methods", "content": "How evidence is shared (distribution rules)\\n\\nApproved sharing methods\\n\\n- **Secure link** from an access-controlled repository (preferred)\\n  - Recipient-specific access\\n  - Expiring link when possible\\n  - Watermarking for SOC 2", "score": 0.26393, "rank": 85, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "f46c4ba8-acff-59bb-8c27-9220cdeddcb0", "search_unit_id": "f46c4ba8-acff-59bb-8c27-9220cdeddcb0", "block_from_id": "3369b0e3-ccd4-536d-ad1f-a8f23c9764ac", "block_to_id": "3c097507-1408-5e77-886c-dbbc236d4a30", "section_path": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Escalation > Templates (copy/paste) > Template A \xe2\x80\x94 Initial acknowledgement (suspected throughput miss)", "document_id": "2aced3e3-1ed9-50b2-9ef8-a037c40fcfaf", "document_version_id": "5b0b49bf-5c10-5f9e-b6f9-1ae199865977", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_409618a3958944f9b78362ed7e01bb33.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-83", "heading": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Escalation > Templates (copy/paste) > Template A \xe2\x80\x94 Initial acknowledgement (suspected throughput miss)", "content": "Templates (copy/paste)\\n\\nTemplate A \xe2\x80\x94 Initial acknowledgement (suspected throughput miss)\\n\\nSubject: Dedicated deployment throughput investigation (Deployment {{deployment_id}})\\n\\nHi {{customer_name}},\\n\\nThanks for flagging this. We\xe2\x80\x99re investigating reduced throughput on your Dedicated deployment ({{deployment_id}}) in {{region}}.\\n\\nTo help us confirm impact and correlate logs/metrics, could you share:\\n\\n- The approximate start time and whether the issue is ongoing\\n- The model + version you\xe2\x80\x99re using\\n- A representative request ID (or a small set) from the impacted window\\n- Any recent traffic/concurrency changes on your side\\n\\nWe\xe2\x80\x99ll provide the next update by {{time}}.\\nBest,\\n{{agent_name}}", "score": 0.222747, "rank": 86, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "d63fa582-1069-5ed0-949c-73ae9c029e46", "search_unit_id": "d63fa582-1069-5ed0-949c-73ae9c029e46", "block_from_id": "1bc0c0e5-5337-5055-b204-3ff01a9d5ec8", "block_to_id": "d06c3d39-2866-571c-ab50-c35f03c87c79", "section_path": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review", "document_id": "be72d63b-333f-51cc-8c3e-5b2af46aaab8", "document_version_id": "d74f559c-4a91-5a53-930c-c8b05d67f4ab", "document_name": "fireflies__dsid_6c4c1c875e704f09b4d791d64d7bc7e5.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "p-61", "heading": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review", "content": "[13:22] Chris Osei: Yeah. Proposed test checklist:\\n1) Subscribe to plan in GCP Marketplace using test buyer\\n2) Verify entitlement exists (via API/console)\\n3) In Redwood console, run entitlement verification and link/create org\\n4) Generate an API key\\n5) Send a small set of inference requests (chat/text and embeddings)\\n6) Verify metering calls succeed and appear under correct dimension\\n7) Cancel subscription and verify access behavior (grace period?)\\n8) Re-subscribe / plan change and verify entitlements update\\n9) Negative tests: wrong entitlement, cross-org linking, delayed propagation\\n\\n[14:03] Miguel Alvarez: That\xe2\x80\x99s good. On cancellation: define your policy. Some partners cut off immediately, some allow a grace period. But it needs to match your listing terms and how you interpret the entitlement state.\\n\\n[14:18] Cole Summers: Our default is to stop accepting billable traffic if entitlement is inactive, but we may allow a short grace if the cancellation is accidental. For marketplace we probably keep it strict.\\n\\n[14:31] Anika Desai: During review, they\xe2\x80\x99ll test cancel and see if your UI reflects it. If there\xe2\x80\x99s a grace, document it.\\n\\n[14:40] Nadia Rahman: On entitlement state changes, how quickly do we see \xe2\x80\x9cactive -> cancelled\xe2\x80\x9d? That\xe2\x80\x99s a potential race.\\n\\n[14:49] Anika Desai: It\xe2\x80\x99s usually quick, but again, you should poll or have a scheduled sync. Don\xe2\x80\x99t assume immediate.\\n\\n[15:00] Ben Carter: We have a sync job that runs every few minutes and on-demand verification. We can tighten it during onboarding.\\n\\n[15:08] Miguel Alvarez: Just be careful with rate limits. If you have everyone spamming \xe2\x80\x9cverify entitlement\xe2\x80\x9d you might hit quotas. But for normal scale it\xe2\x80\x99s okay.\\n\\n[15:19] Stephanie Nguyen: One more thing: our SKUs include compliance add-ons (like extended audit log retention, compliance pack). Is it common to have add-ons as separate dimensions or separate plans?\\n\\n[15:34] Priya Shah: It depends. If it\xe2\x80\x99s truly an add-on, separate plan can be complicated. Some do \xe2\x80\x9cbundle plans\xe2\x80\x9d (base + add-on). Others handle add-ons off-marketplace. If you want it procured via marketplace, bundling plans is usually easiest.\\n\\n[15:54] Miguel Alvarez: If you do it as a usage dimension, that\xe2\x80\x99s weird for a monthly compliance pack. Better as subscription line item. But you can create a plan called \xe2\x80\x9cHosted + Compliance\xe2\x80\x9d and so on.\\n\\n[16:09] Cole Summers: That increases plan combinatorics. We were trying to keep it minimal.\\n\\n[16:14] Stephanie Nguyen: For enterprise procurement, it might be okay. We can do a small number: Hosted, Hosted+Compliance, Dedicated, Dedicated+Compliance, Private. Something like that.\\n\\n[16:26] Priya Shah: Exactly. Keep it manageable.\\n\\n[16:29] Chris Osei: Let\xe2\x80\x99s capture that as an open decision: add-ons as bundles vs separate plans.\\n\\n[16:35] Ben Carter: If it\xe2\x80\x99s bundled, onboarding can still read plan ID and show the right \xe2\x80\x9cfeatures enabled\xe2\x80\x9d banner.\\n\\n[16:42] Anika Desai: Yep.\\n\\n[16:44] Miguel Alvarez: For dimensions, fewer is better. If you have too many, it\xe2\x80\x99s harder to validate.\\n\\n[16:52] Nadia Rahman: Another metering nuance: we do streaming responses. Tokens get generated over time. We aggregate at end of request. That means we may emit one usage record per request or per batch. Is that okay?", "score": 0.217446, "rank": 87, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "e2a1ef86-f272-548f-81c5-265d56a7e1d3", "search_unit_id": "e2a1ef86-f272-548f-81c5-265d56a7e1d3", "block_from_id": "6f134484-bb33-5444-bbd6-664babff7561", "block_to_id": "6a9abe59-2cc6-5852-b804-0b4ec3bbdd6c", "section_path": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Escalation > What is the Dedicated Throughput SLO (customer-facing summary)", "document_id": "2aced3e3-1ed9-50b2-9ef8-a037c40fcfaf", "document_version_id": "5b0b49bf-5c10-5f9e-b6f9-1ae199865977", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_409618a3958944f9b78362ed7e01bb33.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-7", "heading": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Escalation > What is the Dedicated Throughput SLO (customer-facing summary)", "content": "What is the Dedicated Throughput SLO (customer-facing summary)\\n\\nDedicated Throughput SLO guarantees a **minimum sustained throughput floor** for an eligible Dedicated deployment under defined conditions.\\n\\n**High-level definition (internal summary):**\\n\\n- Metric: throughput measured as **billable output tokens per second**, computed per deployment, aggregated over a rolling window.\\n- Scope: Dedicated deployments only (not Hosted API, not eval/sandbox deployments).\\n- Eligibility: model/version and configuration must be within the customer\xe2\x80\x99s contracted Dedicated configuration (e.g., pinned model family/version, supported max context, supported shape).\\n\\n**Common non-eligibility / exclusions (not exhaustive):**\\n\\n- Customer-side throttling or client concurrency limits causing underutilization.\\n- Customer workload violating sizing assumptions (e.g., contexts above configured limit, unsupported parameter combinations).\\n- Warm-up periods after scale-from-zero or cold start (tier-dependent; see \xe2\x80\x9cWarm pool / startup\xe2\x80\x9d below).\\n- Incidents due to customer networking/VPC misconfig in Private connectivity paths (if applicable).\\n\\nWhen in doubt, treat as potentially eligible, start the triage, and engage SRE.\\n\\n---", "score": 0.21116, "rank": 88, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "98997e59-4396-56a3-8ca6-923da1a39a85", "search_unit_id": "98997e59-4396-56a3-8ca6-923da1a39a85", "block_from_id": "49e91202-e695-532f-ae20-ef4aed919ce2", "block_to_id": "bcaa1799-9702-5bd2-8ddc-92489ec23c08", "section_path": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review", "document_id": "be72d63b-333f-51cc-8c3e-5b2af46aaab8", "document_version_id": "d74f559c-4a91-5a53-930c-c8b05d67f4ab", "document_name": "fireflies__dsid_6c4c1c875e704f09b4d791d64d7bc7e5.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "p-32", "heading": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review", "content": "[06:55] Miguel Alvarez: Dedicated capacity tends to fit better as subscription / contract. Usage metering for \xe2\x80\x9cgpu hours\xe2\x80\x9d works, but you need to align it with what you\xe2\x80\x99re actually providing. If it\xe2\x80\x99s truly reserved, then a monthly subscription fee is clearer. If there\xe2\x80\x99s burst/overage, you can meter overage separately.\\n\\n[07:16] Nadia Rahman: We can do either. Internally we track reserved pool size and then tokens anyway, but the contract is the key. If you recommend subscription for reserved, then our metering is mostly for overage.\\n\\n[07:29] Priya Shah: From a procurement standpoint, enterprise buyers usually prefer a predictable subscription line item. Then usage-based Hosted is more PLG.\\n\\n[07:41] Stephanie Nguyen: That matches what we\xe2\x80\x99re seeing. One thing we\xe2\x80\x99re worried about: we want three clear \xe2\x80\x9cpaths\xe2\x80\x9d in the listing\xe2\x80\x94Hosted, Dedicated, Private. But under the hood GCP constructs are not always \xe2\x80\x9cthree products.\xe2\x80\x9d It\xe2\x80\x99s one listing with plans, right?\\n\\n[07:57] Priya Shah: Exactly. You typically have one product listing and multiple plans. You can describe the three modes in the copy, but technically it\xe2\x80\x99s one product with pricing plans.\\n\\n[08:12] Ben Carter: On the onboarding, would you expect different landing pages for different plans? Like, if someone buys Private, they shouldn\xe2\x80\x99t be dropped into \xe2\x80\x9ccreate API key and call the hosted endpoint\xe2\x80\x9d because their next step is networking and deployment.\\n\\n[08:26] Anika Desai: That\xe2\x80\x99s a good point. Ideally yes: after entitlement, route them to the appropriate next steps. But you can also have a general landing page that asks \xe2\x80\x9cwhat did you purchase?\xe2\x80\x9d or reads the plan from the entitlement.\\n\\n[08:43] Cole Summers: Reading the plan from entitlement is doable if the entitlement includes plan name. We just need to be careful with plan rename.\\n\\n[08:52] Miguel Alvarez: Plan rename is okay if you keep the underlying plan ID stable. Don\xe2\x80\x99t key logic off display name.\\n\\n[09:01] Chris Osei: Noted. We\xe2\x80\x99ll key off ID.\\n\\n[09:05] Stephanie Nguyen: Let\xe2\x80\x99s talk about reviewer expectations on the metering payload. Miguel, can you share what they check?\\n\\n[09:14] Miguel Alvarez: Sure. They\xe2\x80\x99ll check: (1) you\xe2\x80\x99re using the correct API endpoints, (2) the consumerId or entitlement is valid, (3) the dimension is a valid one configured in your offer, (4) the quantity is non-negative and within reason, (5) timestamps are sane (not future by a day), (6) idempotency\xe2\x80\x94you don\xe2\x80\x99t double bill on retry.\\n\\n[09:40] Nadia Rahman: On idempotency: we have an internal \xe2\x80\x9cusage_event_id\xe2\x80\x9d and we can keep a ledger. If we retry, we send the same event id. Is that a pattern you like to see?\\n\\n[09:52] Miguel Alvarez: Yes. Exactly. Store the request ID and mark it as sent. Most of the \xe2\x80\x9cbilling mismatch\xe2\x80\x9d issues are duplicate sends during outages.\\n\\n[10:05] Ben Carter: Do you have any advice on how to validate end-to-end that metering is going to show up for the customer? Like, in AWS we can see it in the buyer account pretty quickly.", "score": 0.206342, "rank": 89, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "0ef7f413-e9d9-5837-ba7f-fcaeacc0c2be", "search_unit_id": "0ef7f413-e9d9-5837-ba7f-fcaeacc0c2be", "block_from_id": "032cd79d-1155-5ae9-82da-1b903b18382d", "block_to_id": "6d39e436-3966-57e6-9640-1f3102ecde09", "section_path": "Metering To Invoice Reconciliation Playbook > Appendix A \xe2\x80\x94 Minimal reconciliation checklist (quick pass)", "document_id": "5dd49655-30e7-5bb0-9f51-c936960c0c6a", "document_version_id": "c0e0c79d-406f-5fb0-b65c-732ae9489a5c", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_602422b841b94f5c8089555015df0ed8.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-155", "heading": "Metering To Invoice Reconciliation Playbook > Appendix A \xe2\x80\x94 Minimal reconciliation checklist (quick pass)", "content": "Appendix A \xe2\x80\x94 Minimal reconciliation checklist (quick pass)\\n\\nUse for weekly spot-checks.\\n1) Confirm freshness SLO pass\\n2) Run ledger vs usage events drift (last 7 days)\\n3) Sample 10 accounts (2 enterprise, 5 mid-market, 3 self-serve)\\n4) Validate top 5 models per account\\n5) Confirm null rate and duplicate rate are under thresholds\\n6) Record pass/fail and open investigations\\n---", "score": 0.21128, "rank": 90, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "4e1de271-3922-5983-9192-38028c593426", "search_unit_id": "4e1de271-3922-5983-9192-38028c593426", "block_from_id": "797bdded-0a3a-51b1-9a43-046c8e6562de", "block_to_id": "216bf5e6-513b-5732-b614-9d58c70f87f6", "section_path": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review", "document_id": "be72d63b-333f-51cc-8c3e-5b2af46aaab8", "document_version_id": "d74f559c-4a91-5a53-930c-c8b05d67f4ab", "document_name": "fireflies__dsid_6c4c1c875e704f09b4d791d64d7bc7e5.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "p-47", "heading": "2026 01 16 Gcp Marketplace Onboarding And Billing Review", "content": "[10:15] Miguel Alvarez: On GCP, you can see metering records in the partner console and via reports, but it\xe2\x80\x99s not always instant. For testing, you want to confirm your API calls succeed and then use the console to verify the usage events are recorded. Anika can share the checklist.\\n\\n[10:33] Anika Desai: Yeah, I can send a link. There\xe2\x80\x99s a \xe2\x80\x9ctest buyer\xe2\x80\x9d flow too. You procure with a test account, then you validate that entitlement is created and your backend can resolve it.\\n\\n[10:47] Stephanie Nguyen: Great. Another question: dimension naming constraints. We saw some limitations around character length and allowed characters.\\n\\n[10:56] Miguel Alvarez: Keep it short, alphanumeric plus underscore usually. Avoid spaces. If you have to include a version, don\xe2\x80\x99t. Better to keep the same dimension and change the price model rather than changing the dimension name.\\n\\n[11:13] Cole Summers: That\xe2\x80\x99s helpful. We were going to do \xe2\x80\x9chosted_tokens_1k\xe2\x80\x9d rather than \xe2\x80\x9chosted_input_tokens_1k\xe2\x80\x9d and \xe2\x80\x9chosted_output_tokens_1k\xe2\x80\x9d to keep it one dimension. We can still show the breakdown in our console for observability, but bill as total tokens.\\n\\n[11:32] Miguel Alvarez: That\xe2\x80\x99s a good compromise.\\n\\n[11:35] Stephanie Nguyen: And embeddings we can keep separate.\\n\\n[11:38] Miguel Alvarez: Yes.\\n\\n[11:40] Chris Osei: I want to make sure we cover onboarding UX expectations. Ben, can you describe what we\xe2\x80\x99re building and get GCP feedback?\\n\\n[11:49] Ben Carter: Sure. So in our console, when you click \xe2\x80\x9cGet started\xe2\x80\x9d, there will be a \xe2\x80\x9cPurchased via marketplace?\xe2\x80\x9d path. If they choose GCP marketplace, we ask them to sign in. Then we try to auto-detect entitlements tied to that user, and if we can\xe2\x80\x99t, we allow them to paste an entitlement ID. If entitlement is found, we show \xe2\x80\x9clink to existing org\xe2\x80\x9d or \xe2\x80\x9ccreate new org.\xe2\x80\x9d After that, we prompt them to create first API key for Hosted, or if the plan is Dedicated/Private, we route to request provisioning steps.\\n\\n[12:24] Anika Desai: That\xe2\x80\x99s pretty aligned with what we like to see. Two things: (1) make it very clear what to do if they\xe2\x80\x99re not the person who purchased. Sometimes procurement buys and engineering uses. So include instructions like \xe2\x80\x9cask the billing admin to invite you\xe2\x80\x9d or \xe2\x80\x9cforward this entitlement ID.\xe2\x80\x9d (2) include a retry experience for pending entitlements.\\n\\n[12:50] Ben Carter: Yep. We have an error state \xe2\x80\x9cEntitlement not found\xe2\x80\x9d that currently suggests contacting support. We can change that to \xe2\x80\x9cmay take a few minutes\xe2\x80\x9d and include refresh.\\n\\n[13:02] Priya Shah: Also include support SLA information and the right support channel for marketplace customers. It doesn\xe2\x80\x99t have to be complex, but the listing should say how to get help.\\n\\n[13:12] Stephanie Nguyen: We\xe2\x80\x99ll incorporate that into listing FAQs. On testing: we need a clear test plan. Chris, we have some draft steps; can we align with GCP?", "score": 0.199992, "rank": 91, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "5900c2b6-db81-566e-93d3-4df758f2b8c3", "search_unit_id": "5900c2b6-db81-566e-93d3-4df758f2b8c3", "block_from_id": "1b0ef4bf-1c4c-5bd9-995c-e8abcb70d76e", "block_to_id": "5feaba5e-2a0b-5679-89f3-6b3d99e35326", "section_path": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Escalation > Warm pool / startup considerations", "document_id": "2aced3e3-1ed9-50b2-9ef8-a037c40fcfaf", "document_version_id": "5b0b49bf-5c10-5f9e-b6f9-1ae199865977", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_409618a3958944f9b78362ed7e01bb33.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-72", "heading": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Escalation > Warm pool / startup considerations", "content": "Warm pool / startup considerations\\n\\nSome tiers include warm pool guarantees (or faster recovery expectations). During triage, identify if the customer is within a warm-up window:\\n\\n- Did the deployment recently scale from zero?\\n- Was there a recent model reload, config change, or node replacement?\\n- Did traffic resume after prolonged inactivity?\\n\\nIf warm-up is suspected, note it explicitly in the customer thread and ask SRE to confirm whether the event is excluded by policy.\\n\\n---", "score": 0.197279, "rank": 92, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "7ad7fa3e-f65e-5590-97d1-e985de4793f2", "search_unit_id": "7ad7fa3e-f65e-5590-97d1-e985de4793f2", "block_from_id": "f4038f70-1166-598a-ac1b-857ef2bae219", "block_to_id": "a2318620-fd88-5b5d-a51e-a6efe75caa19", "section_path": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Escalation > Roles and ownership during an SLO event", "document_id": "2aced3e3-1ed9-50b2-9ef8-a037c40fcfaf", "document_version_id": "5b0b49bf-5c10-5f9e-b6f9-1ae199865977", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_409618a3958944f9b78362ed7e01bb33.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-15", "heading": "Dedicated Throughput Slo Escalation > Roles and ownership during an SLO event", "content": "Roles and ownership during an SLO event\\n\\n**Support/CS (Incident Driver for customer comms)**\\n\\n- Owns the customer thread, updates, and expectation management.\\n- Owns the case record: timeline, impact, and customer confirmations.\\n- Initiates escalation and ensures correct teams are engaged.\\n\\n**SRE On-Call (Technical Incident Lead)**\\n\\n- Confirms whether the SLO is breached per the canonical evaluator.\\n- Drives mitigation and coordinates cross-functional response.\\n- Provides internal/external technical summary for comms.\\n\\n**Infra/Fleet On-Call (Capacity / shape / provisioning owner)**\\n\\n- Addresses capacity constraints, noisy-neighbor isolation issues, node failures, and shape-level performance anomalies.\\n\\n**Observability (as-needed)**\\n\\n- Validates metric correctness (e.g., streaming accounting anomalies), dashboard discrepancies, missing tags.\\n\\n**Product Dedicated (as-needed)**\\n\\n- Confirms entitlement interpretation (tier rules, burst policy, failover posture).\\n- Aligns on customer-facing messaging for edge cases.\\n\\n**Finance/FP&A + Legal (as-needed for credits/comp)**\\n\\n- Approves service credits above policy thresholds and any non-standard compensation.\\n\\n---", "score": 0.19405, "rank": 93, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "a4a5ab98-ac71-5149-90ac-40167effe4de", "search_unit_id": "a4a5ab98-ac71-5149-90ac-40167effe4de", "block_from_id": "a3a62208-9621-5bc5-8801-8cd6026e399f", "block_to_id": "b7cc8d5e-b8cf-5dba-b7ee-a0cb9c078e23", "section_path": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > Evidence pack contents (prospect-facing)", "document_id": "9e945a2c-4051-5cc3-99ca-a95c267f6bdf", "document_version_id": "c6871709-6afb-56d9-883f-81f2536ab49e", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_a9a67d815b07451e90ed8c3d1734d007.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h2-18", "heading": "Abm Pilot Security Evidence Pack Index > Evidence pack contents (prospect-facing)", "content": "Evidence pack contents (prospect-facing)\\n\\nBelow is the standard set of artifacts we can share. Availability depends on the prospect\xe2\x80\x99s stage and whether an NDA is in place.", "score": 0.197426, "rank": 94, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}, {"chunk_id": "5fe984ba-ccbb-5e4c-98b7-db587fd879ec", "search_unit_id": "5fe984ba-ccbb-5e4c-98b7-db587fd879ec", "block_from_id": "dcfd7729-8024-50f2-aecb-6b4bed063005", "block_to_id": "9a204894-e93e-53fb-b5aa-3bca5aec9b73", "section_path": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 4. Definitions > 4.3 \xe2\x80\x9cChargeable event time\xe2\x80\x9d", "document_id": "85b37108-378d-55be-805e-bdcd31101232", "document_version_id": "6851cc1f-d2a1-5421-91d3-d80c5cf3f3e8", "document_name": "confluence__dsid_37befab212ae4ed9be7ebe612d942658.md", "version_no": 1, "page_from": 1, "page_to": 1, "anchor": "h3-24", "heading": "Billing Meter Spec Hosted Api Tokens And Requests > 4. Definitions > 4.3 \xe2\x80\x9cChargeable event time\xe2\x80\x9d", "content": "4.3 \xe2\x80\x9cChargeable event time\xe2\x80\x9d\\n\\nAll Hosted API meters roll up by **UTC usage date**, based on `metered_at` (see \xc2\xa710). Export/UI may render in customer timezone, but the ledger uses UTC for close.\\n\\n---", "score": 0.196078, "rank": 95, "source_id": "b17fa25ce7d34acab77e3ad6267b5159", "source_name": "Project b3086c26ae3c45b6b8710f3c"}], "events": [], "entities": [], "relations": [], "source_hits": [], "summary": "", "citations": [], "answer_status": "pending", "no_answer_reason": null, "exploration_id": null, "stats": {"canonical_index": true, "ready_versions": 81, "requested_top_k": null, "candidate_top_k": 64, "candidate_unit_pool_limit": 1024, "candidate_pool_satur